// Content for The Family Map (node g57, ages 22+, Chapter 7): NEW v2 build to GDD 57 (mechanic-embodying), the
// full-circle callback to My Family Garden (g03): in India you don't just marry a person, you join a family. 84
// researched, India-grounded scenarios across web-you-join → kind-boundaries → couple-team → respect-both-ways →
// when-it-turns-harmful → tools-and-help, each encoded as one of the shared v2 play actions (branch /
// strike-rewrite / sort / reflect / match / spot / role-play): no binary "tap the right card". Lead: branch (your
// move), strike-rewrite (bust the myth), sort. Six modes: the web you join (map the extended family and your new
// place; BOTH partners join each other's families), kind boundaries (respectful limits that protect the couple
// without rejecting anyone; busts "family always knows best" / "interference is love" / "a good bahu never says
// no"), couple as a team (the couple is the primary team; a united, kind front; refuse triangulation; decide
// together, then face family as one), respect both ways (honour elders & culture while keeping your own voice;
// respect is not obedience and flows BOTH ways), when it turns harmful (dowry is illegal under the Dowry
// Prohibition Act 1961; coercive in-law control, threats & dowry harassment are abuse, not friction → Respect at
// Home g56 + help), tools & help. Even-handed and warm; NEVER "cut them off" as a default; honours the value
// placed on family while equipping couples to set kind limits. Links Equal Partners (g55) for the load and Respect
// at Home (g56) where dynamics become abusive. Builds on My Family Garden (g03). India: joint families and in-law
// ties are central and a leading source of marital strain; expectations fall heaviest on the daughter-in-law
// (sons-in-law increasingly adjust too); dowry is illegal; abuse routes to 181, 112 and a counsellor. gameId
// "family-map".
import type { V2GameConfig, Scenario } from "./v2-schema";

const SCENARIOS: Scenario[] = [
  {"id":"fm-001","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"You marry a person, not a family.\"","myth":{"un":"You marry a person, not a family.","re":"In India especially, marrying someone means joining their wider family and being joined to yours.","why":"A partnership lives inside an extended web of relationships."},"relearn":"Marriage joins two families, not just two people; that web is real either way.","persona":"Pooja","source":"you join a family"},
  {"id":"fm-002","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: part of the family web you're joining, or not your concern?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Your partner's parents and siblings"},{"id":"b","text":"A stranger on the street"},{"id":"c","text":"The relatives who'll be at every function"},{"id":"d","text":"A celebrity you follow online"},{"id":"e","text":"Your partner's grandparents and close cousins"},{"id":"f","text":"A neighbour you've never spoken to"}],"bins":[{"id":"web","label":"Your new web","valence":"pos"},{"id":"not","label":"Not your web","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"web","b":"not","c":"web","d":"not","e":"web","f":"not"},"relearn":"Your partner's close family becomes part of your everyday web; strangers and celebrities don't.","persona":"any","source":"mapping the web"},
  {"id":"fm-003","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"finding your place","hook":"You've just married into a big joint family and feel lost about who's who.","steps":[{"prompt":"At breakfast on your first morning, everyone talks at once. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask your partner to map the family together","then":"Over chai, he sketches names and habits, and things start to click.","best":true},{"text":"Smile along and try to piece it together yourself","then":"You catch a few names, but the rest stays a blur by lunchtime."},{"text":"Ask your mother-in-law to explain everyone at once","then":"She rattles off ten names fast. Most of them slip away instantly."},{"text":"Stay quiet and hope you'll pick it up eventually","then":"Breakfast ends and you still can't tell an aunt from a cousin."}],"why":"Mapping it with your partner turns strangers into names you can actually hold onto."},{"prompt":"Days later, a cousin visits and everyone assumes you know her. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask her warmly how she's related to your partner","then":"She laughs and explains, pleased you asked instead of guessing.","best":true},{"text":"Nod along and hope the connection becomes clear later","then":"The visit ends and you still can't place her in the family."},{"text":"Whisper to your partner mid-conversation to check quietly","then":"He whispers back, but you miss half of what the cousin says."},{"text":"Keep your distance for the rest of the visit","then":"She notices the distance, and the visit stays a little awkward."}],"why":"Asking her directly, warmly, closes the gap faster than guessing ever could."},{"prompt":"At the next big gathering, a stranger greets you like family. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Introduce yourself and ask how they're connected","then":"They beam and tell you the whole story. You've made a new tie.","best":true},{"text":"Wait for someone else to introduce you properly","then":"The moment passes, and you're only introduced halfway through the evening."},{"text":"Smile and chat without asking who they are","then":"The conversation goes fine, though you're still unsure who you just met."},{"text":"Slip away to avoid another unfamiliar face","then":"You miss a chance to add one more name to your growing map."}],"why":"Asking directly, warmly, is how the map keeps growing after the first sketch."}],"debrief":"Understanding the web, and your place in it, is the first step to navigating it.","relearn":"Map the family and its unspoken rules together; that's how you find your place."},
  {"id":"fm-004","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each part of the new web to what it really is.","pairs":[{"left":"Your in-laws","right":"Your partner's parents, now family"},{"left":"Your partner's siblings","right":"New brothers or sisters-in-law"},{"left":"The extended relatives","right":"The wider circle at every function"},{"left":"Your partner's grandparents","right":"Elders to honour and get to know"},{"left":"Their cousins your age","right":"Possible friends within the family"}],"relearn":"In-laws, siblings-in-law and the extended relatives are the parts of the family web you join.","persona":"any","source":"who's who"},
  {"id":"fm-005","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"It's only the wife who has to adjust to a new family.\"","myth":{"un":"It's only the wife who has to adjust to a new family.","re":"Both partners join each other's families; increasingly sons-in-law adjust too, and that's healthy.","why":"Joining a family goes both ways, not just one."},"relearn":"Both partners join each other's families; adjusting isn't a one-way, wife-only job.","persona":"Karthik","source":"both ways"},
  {"id":"fm-006","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you're not just gaining a partner, you're gaining a whole web, with a place in it that's yours to shape. How does that land?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["It's a lot","Makes sense","Mine to shape"],"affirm":"Seeing the whole web, and that your place in it is yours to shape, is where navigating it begins.","relearn":"Your place in the new family web is yours to shape, not just inherit.","persona":"any","source":"your place to shape"},
  {"id":"fm-007","cat":"web-you-join","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two myths about joining a family: the lines that aren't true.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Building new relationships over time","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Having to instantly love everyone","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Learning the family's rhythms and ways","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Being owed everyone's full trust on day one","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Letting closeness grow at its own pace","trick":false}],"why":"Instant love and day-one trust are the myths; warmth and trust are built slowly, over time.","relearn":"Joining a family is about building relationships over time, not instant, automatic love.","persona":"any","source":"real vs myth"},
  {"id":"fm-008","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Sneha","source":"different not wrong","hook":"Your partner's family does things very differently from how you grew up.","steps":[{"prompt":"At your first big function with them, the rituals look nothing like yours. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask someone to walk you through what each part means","then":"An aunt happily explains, pleased that you asked instead of assuming.","best":true},{"text":"Follow along and copy whatever everyone else does","then":"You get through it, still unsure what most of it meant."},{"text":"Point out loud how different this is from your own family","then":"A few faces stiffen, as if you'd called their way lesser."},{"text":"Stand back and let the function pass you by","then":"You leave with the same questions you walked in with."}],"why":"Asking what it means turns an unfamiliar custom into something you actually understand."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner's mother invites you to help her cook for the next one. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Join in and ask her to teach you the steps","then":"She lights up, showing you her way, dish by dish.","best":true},{"text":"Join in but quietly do it your own family's way instead","then":"She notices the swap and goes quiet for the rest of the cooking."},{"text":"Say you'd rather just watch this time","then":"You learn a little, though she seems disappointed you didn't join in."},{"text":"Decline, saying your family's version is simpler","then":"She doesn't ask again, and the kitchen stays her space alone."}],"why":"Learning her way, hands on, builds the bond that comparing never will."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks if the two families' customs could ever mix at future events. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'd love to try honouring both, side by side","then":"Your partner smiles, already picturing what that could look like.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's easier to just pick whichever is simpler","then":"Your partner shrugs, unsure whose customs just quietly got dropped."},{"text":"Say your family's version should probably lead","then":"Your partner goes quiet, feeling their own family's ways were ranked lower."},{"text":"Say customs don't really matter to you either way","then":"Your partner isn't sure if you mean that, or just want the topic closed."}],"why":"Wanting to honour both keeps the door open instead of ranking one family over the other."}],"debrief":"Different isn't wrong; family customs vary, and learning them beats judging them.","relearn":"Different family ways are things to learn, not proof that one side is wrong."},
  {"id":"fm-009","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each new in-law tie to a warm way to begin it.","pairs":[{"left":"A reserved mother-in-law","right":"Small, genuine gestures over time"},{"left":"A talkative father-in-law","right":"Listen and ask about his stories"},{"left":"A guarded sibling-in-law","right":"Patience, no forcing closeness"},{"left":"A warm, busy aunt","right":"A quick call to stay in touch"},{"left":"A shy younger cousin-in-law","right":"A light, friendly hello first"}],"relearn":"Each new relationship starts best with patience and small, genuine gestures, not forced closeness.","persona":"any","source":"starting the ties"},
  {"id":"fm-010","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A good marriage means the two families merge into one with no differences.\"","myth":{"un":"A good marriage means the two families merge into one with no differences.","re":"Two families staying distinct, with their own ways, can still be close and respectful.","why":"Closeness doesn't require erasing every difference between families."},"relearn":"Families can stay distinct and still be close; merging into sameness isn't the goal.","persona":"any","source":"distinct yet close"},
  {"id":"fm-011","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a realistic hope for the new family web, or an unfair expectation?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Building warmth over years"},{"id":"b","text":"Everyone agreeing on everything at once"},{"id":"c","text":"Mutual respect from the start"},{"id":"d","text":"Becoming everyone's favourite instantly"},{"id":"e","text":"Getting to know each relative slowly"},{"id":"f","text":"Erasing your own family's ways to fit in"}],"bins":[{"id":"real","label":"Realistic","valence":"pos"},{"id":"unfair","label":"Unfair expectation","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"real","b":"unfair","c":"real","d":"unfair","e":"real","f":"unfair"},"relearn":"Warmth over time and mutual respect are realistic; instant agreement and being everyone's favourite aren't.","persona":"Pooja","source":"realistic hopes"},
  {"id":"fm-012","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: finding your place doesn't mean shrinking to fit, it means taking up your fair space in the web. Agree?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Agree","Taking my space","Not shrinking"],"affirm":"Finding your place means taking up your fair space in the family, not shrinking yourself to fit.","relearn":"Finding your place means taking your fair space, not shrinking to fit the family.","persona":"any","source":"not shrinking"},
  {"id":"fm-013","cat":"web-you-join","type":"role-play","persona":"Sneha","source":"settling in","hook":"You want to be warm and honest with your new family, not pretend you have it all figured out.","setup":"Someone catches you alone at a gathering.","steps":[{"prompt":"A relative asks: \"So, how are you settling in with us?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Still learning your ways, but I'm glad to be here.\"","then":"She smiles. \"It takes time, and that's alright.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine, I guess.\"","then":"She raises an eyebrow, unsure if that means good or not."},{"text":"\"Honestly, it's a lot to take in.\"","then":"She looks a little concerned, and the mood shifts."},{"text":"\"Everything's absolutely perfect, no complaints at all.\"","then":"She smiles but doesn't quite believe you."}],"why":"Naming both the learning and the gladness is warm, honest, and easy to trust."},{"prompt":"She asks: \"Is there anything about our family that still confuses you?\"","options":[{"text":"\"A few customs I'm still learning. I'd love your help.\"","then":"She lights up. \"Ask me anything. I love explaining these things.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Not really. I think I've got it all down.\"","then":"She nods, though you both know that's probably not quite true."},{"text":"\"Some of it feels a bit much, if I'm honest.\"","then":"She goes a little quiet, unsure how to respond to that."},{"text":"\"I'd rather not get into it right now.\"","then":"The offer to help closes, and the moment passes."}],"why":"Naming what's still unclear, warmly, invites her to actually help you learn it."},{"prompt":"Later, as you join them, she tells another relative: \"You know, this one is really trying.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I am. I want to really know this family.\"","then":"The relative smiles. \"That's rare to hear so early on.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Oh, that's kind of you to say.\"","then":"The moment passes pleasantly, without adding much to it."},{"text":"\"I hope it's enough, honestly.\"","then":"It comes out sounding more anxious than you meant it to."},{"text":"\"I'm just doing what anyone would do.\"","then":"The comment brushes past the compliment instead of meeting it."}],"why":"Naming your real wish to know the family, not just fit in, deepens the trust she's already showing."}],"relearn":"A warm, honest answer builds the new ties: glad to be here, and still learning."},
  {"id":"fm-014","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"as people","hook":"You've been so focused on being accepted that you've forgotten to know them as people.","steps":[{"prompt":"At dinner, you realise you've never asked your father-in-law about his own life. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask him a genuine question about his younger days","then":"He brightens and tells you a story you'd never have guessed.","best":true},{"text":"Compliment the meal and keep the small talk light","then":"Dinner stays pleasant, and you still know nothing more about him."},{"text":"Wait for him to bring up something personal first","then":"The evening ends the same way it started, polite and distant."},{"text":"Focus the conversation on your own achievements instead","then":"He nods politely, and the talk stays about you, not him."}],"why":"A genuine question about his life turns a polite dinner into a real conversation."},{"prompt":"Later, your sister-in-law mentions she used to paint but stopped years ago. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask gently why she stopped painting","then":"She pauses, then talks about it more openly than you expected.","best":true},{"text":"Say that's a shame and move the topic along","then":"The comment passes, and you learn nothing more about her."},{"text":"Buy her some paints as a surprise","then":"She thanks you, a little unsure why you jumped straight to a gift."},{"text":"Say nothing, worried it's not your place to ask","then":"The moment passes, and she stays 'the sister-in-law' to you."}],"why":"A real question about what she loved shows her you see her as a person, not a role."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks how you're finding the family lately. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you want to put in more effort to know them","then":"Your partner looks relieved. \"Let's make more room for that.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say everyone still feels mostly like strangers","then":"Your partner nods, though nothing changes after the conversation."},{"text":"Say you're doing everything right to impress them","then":"Your partner looks tired hearing it framed that way."},{"text":"Say you'd rather not talk about the family right now","then":"Your partner drops the topic, and the question sits unanswered."}],"why":"Wanting to know them as people, not just win them over, is what builds real bonds."}],"debrief":"In-laws are individuals to know, not just roles to impress; curiosity builds real bonds.","relearn":"Get to know in-laws as people, not just roles to win over."},
  {"id":"fm-015","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Setting a boundary with family means you're rejecting them.\"","myth":{"un":"Setting a boundary with family means you're rejecting them.","re":"A boundary is about protecting the relationship, not ending it; you can say no and still love them.","why":"Boundaries protect closeness; they don't destroy it."},"relearn":"A boundary protects the relationship; saying no kindly isn't rejection.","persona":"Pooja","source":"boundaries protect"},
  {"id":"fm-016","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Family always knows best, so you should just do as they say.\"","myth":{"un":"Family always knows best, so you should just do as they say.","re":"Elders deserve respect, but you and your partner get to decide your own life together.","why":"Respecting elders doesn't mean handing them every decision."},"relearn":"Respect elders, but the couple still decides their own life; 'they know best' isn't a rule to obey blindly.","persona":"Pooja","source":"knows best myth"},
  {"id":"fm-017","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: the couple's own call to make, or genuinely the wider family's to decide?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"When you two have children"},{"id":"b","text":"The menu for a shared family festival"},{"id":"c","text":"How you spend your own salary"},{"id":"d","text":"A joint family ritual everyone shares"},{"id":"e","text":"Which schools or careers you two choose"},{"id":"f","text":"Hosting the annual family puja together"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"Couple's call","valence":"neutral"},{"id":"family","label":"Shared family call","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"family","c":"couple","d":"family","e":"couple","f":"family"},"relearn":"Your children, salary and your own paths are the couple's call; genuinely shared rituals can be decided together.","persona":"any","source":"whose call"},
  {"id":"fm-018","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"kind boundary","hook":"Your in-laws drop by unannounced often, and you and Aman never get real private time.","steps":[{"prompt":"They arrive again on a Sunday evening, unannounced. What do you do this time?","options":[{"text":"Welcome them warmly, then later plan a boundary with Aman","then":"The visit goes fine, and you and Aman start talking about a heads-up rule.","best":true},{"text":"Welcome them and say nothing, again","then":"The evening passes pleasantly, but the pattern just continues unchanged."},{"text":"Look visibly annoyed so they notice","then":"They sense the mood but aren't sure what actually changed."},{"text":"Make an excuse to cut the visit short","then":"They leave a bit puzzled, unsure what they did wrong."}],"why":"Welcoming them now and planning together keeps the fix kind, not sudden."},{"prompt":"That night, you and Aman talk about setting a boundary. What do you agree on?","options":[{"text":"A warm heads-up call before visits, said together","then":"You both feel good about a rule that's clear and kind.","best":true},{"text":"Let Aman be the only one who says it, since it's his family","then":"You feel less involved in a decision that affects you too."},{"text":"A strict no-visits-without-notice rule, firmly worded","then":"It solves the privacy issue but feels harsher than you both intended."},{"text":"Just hope they figure it out from small hints","then":"Hints don't land, so within days Aman ends up telling her directly instead."}],"why":"A warm, clear ask agreed together protects privacy without feeling like a wall."},{"prompt":"The next weekend, your mother-in-law calls before coming, but sounds a little hurt. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Thank her warmly for calling, and mean it","then":"She relaxes, hearing that the call itself was genuinely welcome.","best":true},{"text":"Explain the whole reasoning again, at length","then":"The long explanation makes the simple ask feel like a bigger deal."},{"text":"Apologise repeatedly for asking in the first place","then":"The over-apologising makes the boundary feel like something to regret."},{"text":"Say nothing about the call. Just let her in","then":"She's left unsure whether the new rule is even wanted."}],"why":"A genuine thank you shows the boundary is about privacy, not rejection."}],"debrief":"A kind, clear boundary, set together, protects the couple without rejecting anyone.","relearn":"Set kind boundaries together and warmly; silence turns into resentment."},
  {"id":"fm-019","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"match","hook":"Match a harsh way to set a limit with its kinder version.","pairs":[{"left":"“Stop interfering in our lives.”","right":"“We love your input; this one's ours.”"},{"left":"“You're always here.”","right":"“We'd love a heads-up before visits.”"},{"left":"“Mind your own business.”","right":"“We'll ask you for advice on this.”"},{"left":"“Stop controlling everything.”","right":"“We'd like to choose this as a couple.”"},{"left":"“Leave us alone for once.”","right":"“We'd love a quiet evening, just us two.”"}],"relearn":"The same boundary can be said with warmth instead of harshness.","persona":"any","source":"kinder version"},
  {"id":"fm-020","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Interference is just love, so you have to accept all of it.\"","myth":{"un":"Interference is just love, so you have to accept all of it.","re":"Concern can come from love and still cross a line; you can receive the love and decline the overreach.","why":"You can honour the love behind it and still keep the boundary."},"relearn":"Care and overreach can both be true; accept the love, keep the boundary.","persona":"Sneha","source":"love and limit"},
  {"id":"fm-021","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two walls that just shut people out, not the kind boundaries.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"“We'll decide this together, and tell you once we have.”","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Cold silence and avoiding them for weeks","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"“We love visits, just call first.”","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Cutting off all contact after one small argument","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"“This one's ours to decide, with love.”","trick":false}],"why":"Cold weeks of silence and cutting off after one small row are walls; a kind boundary names the limit warmly.","relearn":"A kind boundary is warm and clear; cold cut-offs and silent freeze-outs are walls, not boundaries.","persona":"any","source":"boundary not wall"},
  {"id":"fm-022","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a boundary said with warmth, 'we love you, and this is ours to decide', is the strongest kind. Make sense?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Makes sense","Warm and firm","I'll try it"],"affirm":"The strongest boundary pairs warmth with clarity: love them, and still hold the line.","relearn":"The strongest boundary is warm and firm together: 'we love you, and this is ours.'","persona":"any","source":"warm and firm"},
  {"id":"fm-023","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"holding under push","hook":"A relative keeps pushing you and Divya for a decision you two aren't ready to make.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks again, more firmly this time. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'We hear you. We'll decide together'","then":"She nods, a little impatient, but accepts the answer for now.","best":true},{"text":"Say you're leaning one way to satisfy her","then":"She takes the lean as the real answer and starts telling others."},{"text":"Cave and blurt out a rushed decision","then":"The words are out before you're ready, and now you and Divya must deal with them."},{"text":"Get defensive about being asked at all","then":"The sharp tone makes the next conversation harder than it needed to be."}],"why":"Buying time together holds the boundary without a fight or a cave."},{"prompt":"A week later, she reaches you both together and asks where things stand. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say together: 'We're talking it through. We'll tell you together.'","then":"She's a little frustrated, but the consistent answer holds.","best":true},{"text":"Give a softer, different-sounding answer to keep the peace","then":"The mismatched answers make the couple sound unsure or split."},{"text":"Say you personally are leaning toward yes","then":"The relative now expects a yes and repeats it as fact."},{"text":"Ask her to stop calling about it","then":"She feels shut out and brings it up through someone else instead."}],"why":"The same steady answer, from either of you, keeps the boundary firm."},{"prompt":"You two finally reach your own decision, in your own time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell her together, warmly, when you're actually ready","then":"She hears a clear, confident answer and respects it.","best":true},{"text":"Mention it casually before you're both ready to make it official","then":"The decision gets treated as final before you meant it to be."},{"text":"Wait for her to ask again before saying anything","then":"The delay lets her assume the worst in the meantime."},{"text":"Announce it through someone else to avoid the talk","then":"She hears it secondhand and feels a little sidelined."}],"why":"Sharing the decision on your own timeline keeps it genuinely yours."}],"debrief":"You can hold a boundary kindly under pressure without caving or fighting.","relearn":"Hold the line kindly under pressure: 'we'll decide together and tell you.'"},
  {"id":"fm-024","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a healthy boundary, or actually shutting family out unkindly?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Asking for a call before visits"},{"id":"b","text":"Blocking their number out of spite"},{"id":"c","text":"Keeping your finances your own"},{"id":"d","text":"Refusing to ever see them again over a small slight"},{"id":"e","text":"Saying yes to many family things, no to a few"},{"id":"f","text":"Going silent for months to punish them"}],"bins":[{"id":"healthy","label":"Healthy boundary","valence":"pos"},{"id":"unkind","label":"Unkindly shutting out","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"healthy","b":"unkind","c":"healthy","d":"unkind","e":"healthy","f":"unkind"},"relearn":"Asking for notice, keeping finances, and saying yes-and-sometimes-no are healthy; spite-blocking and silent punishment aren't.","persona":"any","source":"healthy vs shut-out"},
  {"id":"fm-025","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"role-play","persona":"Pooja","source":"kitchen boundary","hook":"Your mother-in-law offers strong opinions on how you should run your kitchen.","setup":"She stands beside you, watching you cook, ready to correct.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"You should always temper the dal that way, not like that.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I love learning from you, Ma, but let me try it my way too.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Fair enough. Taste is taste,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"Please just let me do it myself.\"","then":"She steps back. \"Fine, I was only trying to help,\" she says."},{"text":"\"Okay, I'll do it your way.\"","then":"She says: \"Good, that's much better,\" and keeps watching."},{"text":"\"You always have an opinion about everything I do, don't you?\"","then":"She says: \"I was only saying,\" and the kitchen goes quiet."}],"why":"Welcoming her warmth while holding your own way honours her without surrendering it."},{"prompt":"The next week she says: \"You've changed my son's favourite recipe again.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I've added my own touch. We both like it this way.\"","then":"She pauses. \"Oh, what did you change?\" she asks, curious.","best":true},{"text":"\"It's basically the same recipe as before. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"She says: \"If you say so,\" not fully convinced."},{"text":"\"He can tell me himself if he minds.\"","then":"She says: \"Maybe I'll ask him myself, then.\""},{"text":"\"I'll go back to the old recipe, then.\"","then":"She says: \"There, that's better,\" pleased with the change."}],"why":"Naming it as your own touch, confidently, keeps your kitchen genuinely yours."},{"prompt":"Months later, she says: \"Actually, I liked what you did with the dal last time.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you, Ma. That means a lot.\"","then":"She smiles. \"You're welcome. You've got your own touch.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"See, I told you it was fine.\"","then":"She says: \"Well, I still had my doubts,\" a little defensive."},{"text":"\"You didn't like it before, though.\"","then":"She says: \"That was different,\" and the moment cools."},{"text":"\"I guess I got lucky that time.\"","then":"She says: \"Don't sell yourself short,\" a bit puzzled."}],"why":"Simply thanking her lets a good moment stay good, without reopening old ground."}],"relearn":"You can welcome the warmth and still hold your own way, in the same kind sentence."},
  {"id":"fm-026","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A good daughter-in-law never says no to family.\"","myth":{"un":"A good daughter-in-law never says no to family.","re":"Respect and a kind 'no' can coexist; saying no to overreach doesn't make you a bad bahu.","why":"Self-respect and family respect aren't opposites."},"relearn":"A kind 'no' is compatible with respect; never saying no isn't the measure of a good bahu.","persona":"Pooja","source":"kind no"},
  {"id":"fm-027","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Sneha","source":"keep a self","hook":"Your partner's family expects you to drop your own plans whenever they call.","steps":[{"prompt":"They call about a small gathering, right when you had your own plan. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say warmly, 'I can't make it this time, next time for sure'","then":"They're a little disappointed, but understand and move on.","best":true},{"text":"Cancel your plan quietly, just this once","then":"This 'once' quietly becomes the pattern for every future call."},{"text":"Go along with it, but mention afterward how much you actually gave up","then":"The comment leaves everyone a bit uncomfortable, including you."},{"text":"Ignore the call and deal with it later","then":"They assume you're avoiding them, which isn't quite true."}],"why":"A warm decline protects your own plan without rejecting the family."},{"prompt":"The next month they call again, and you actually want to go this time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say yes happily, glad it works out","then":"You have a genuinely good time, no resentment attached.","best":true},{"text":"Say yes, but frame it as a big sacrifice to them","then":"They thank you profusely, though you didn't actually give anything up."},{"text":"Say yes and quietly cancel another plan too","then":"You've stacked two sacrifices instead of one, without noticing."},{"text":"Overcommit to make up for last time","then":"You promise more than you can sustainably give."}],"why":"Saying yes freely, when you actually want to, keeps the connection genuine."},{"prompt":"A friend asks how you keep up with your partner's big family. What do you tell them?","options":[{"text":"'I say yes often, and I'm learning to keep some plans my own'","then":"Your friend nods. 'That actually sounds sustainable.'","best":true},{"text":"'Honestly, I'm exhausted most weekends. What can you even do'","then":"Your friend looks a little concerned, sensing it's costing you."},{"text":"'I don't mind. I just go whenever they call'","then":"Your friend raises an eyebrow. 'All of it? Doesn't that add up?'"},{"text":"'I try to avoid them when I can'","then":"Your friend looks surprised. 'Avoid them? I thought you liked them.'"}],"why":"Naming the balance out loud shows it's a real, sustainable choice, not luck."}],"debrief":"You can say yes to family often and still keep some plans as your own.","relearn":"Keep some plans your own; saying yes always, at your own cost, erases you."},
  {"id":"fm-028","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: boundaries aren't built once, they're re-set gently, again and again, as people test them. Ready for that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Ready","Again and again","That's real"],"affirm":"Boundaries are maintained, not set once; re-stating them gently over time is normal and healthy.","relearn":"Boundaries are re-set gently over time, not built once and forgotten.","persona":"any","source":"re-set gently"},
  {"id":"fm-029","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"In a disagreement with family, you should always side with your own parents.\"","myth":{"un":"In a disagreement with family, you should always side with your own parents.","re":"Your partner and you are the primary team; you decide together, then face the wider family as one.","why":"The couple is the core unit decisions run through."},"relearn":"The couple is the primary team; decide together first, not parents-first by default.","persona":"Karthik","source":"couple is the team"},
  {"id":"fm-030","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"no triangulation","hook":"Your mother keeps telling you your wife Divya does things wrong, hoping you'll agree.","steps":[{"prompt":"Over chai your mother says, 'She never plans well.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say kindly, 'We're happy with how we manage things, Ma'","then":"Your mother pauses, then changes the subject to the weather.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and let the comment pass","then":"Your mother takes the silence as agreement and repeats it later."},{"text":"Half agree, 'She could plan a bit better, I guess'","then":"Your mother brightens and starts a longer list of complaints."},{"text":"Defend Divya loudly and accuse her of favouritism","then":"Your mother looks hurt and the chai turns tense and quiet."}],"why":"A calm, united answer closes the complaint without hurting your mother."},{"prompt":"A few days later your mother calls just to vent about Divya again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Listen briefly, then say, 'Let's talk about something else, Ma'","then":"She sighs, then tells you about a neighbour's wedding instead.","best":true},{"text":"Let her vent fully without saying anything","then":"The call ends, but she feels encouraged to call again with more."},{"text":"Agree just to get off the phone quickly","then":"She hangs up satisfied, and the pattern repeats next week."},{"text":"Tell her to stop calling you about this","then":"She goes quiet and calls your sister to complain about you instead."}],"why":"Redirecting kindly ends the venting without shutting her out."},{"prompt":"That evening, you decide whether to tell Divya about the calls. What do you say to her?","options":[{"text":"Tell her honestly, and say you're a team on this","then":"Divya relaxes. 'Thank you for telling me instead of hiding it.'","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing so she doesn't worry","then":"Divya later hears about it from your sister and feels left out."},{"text":"Downplay it as 'nothing, just Ma being Ma'","then":"Divya senses something is off and stops asking about it."},{"text":"Ask her to be nicer to your mother so calls stop","then":"Divya feels blamed for something she didn't do."}],"why":"Being honest with your partner keeps the couple a team, not two secrets."},{"prompt":"At the next family dinner your mother tries the same complaint, this time in front of everyone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say warmly, 'We're good, Ma, promise', and change topic","then":"The table moves on, and Divya squeezes your hand under it.","best":true},{"text":"Stay silent and hope someone changes the subject","then":"The silence stretches until Divya changes it herself, alone."},{"text":"Laugh it off as a joke","then":"The joke lands wrong and Divya forces a smile through dinner."},{"text":"Ask your mother to apologise right there","then":"The table freezes and your mother looks cornered in front of everyone."}],"why":"A warm, quick close protects Divya in the room without a scene."}],"debrief":"Don't let a relative triangulate you against your partner; the couple stands together.","relearn":"Refuse triangulation: defend your partner kindly rather than siding against them."},
  {"id":"fm-031","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a united-front move, or something that quietly undermines the couple?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Agreeing your stance privately before family"},{"id":"b","text":"Letting family hear you blame your partner"},{"id":"c","text":"Backing your partner, debriefing later"},{"id":"d","text":"Making big calls with parents, not your partner"},{"id":"e","text":"Giving relatives one calm, shared answer"},{"id":"f","text":"Promising a relative something secretly"}],"bins":[{"id":"united","label":"United front","valence":"pos"},{"id":"undermine","label":"Undermines","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"united","b":"undermine","c":"united","d":"undermine","e":"united","f":"undermine"},"relearn":"Aligning, backing each other and one answer are a united front; blame or secret promises undermine it.","persona":"any","source":"united or not"},
  {"id":"fm-032","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A husband torn between his mother and his wife should just keep them both happy somehow.\"","myth":{"un":"A husband torn between his mother and his wife should just keep them both happy by pleasing each in turn.","re":"He doesn't have to choose sides; he stands with his partner as a team while still respecting his mother.","why":"Partnership-first isn't the same as disrespecting a parent."},"relearn":"He isn't 'torn': he stands with his partner as a team and respects his mother; both at once.","persona":"Karthik","source":"not torn"},
  {"id":"fm-033","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match a family-pressure moment to the couple-team response.","pairs":[{"left":"Relatives push a big decision on the spot","right":"'We'll talk and come back to you'"},{"left":"One partner is being singled out","right":"The other steps in to back them"},{"left":"Plans clash between two families","right":"The couple agrees its own plan first"},{"left":"A relative asks you to keep a secret","right":"You decline, keeping no side-deals"},{"left":"A parent runs your partner down","right":"You warmly close the wedge"}],"relearn":"The couple-team response: decide together, back each other, refuse secrets, give one kind united answer.","persona":"any","source":"team responses"},
  {"id":"fm-034","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"Sneha","source":"private then united","hook":"You and your partner disagree about hosting Diwali this year, right in front of the whole family.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your uncle asks you both to settle it on the spot. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say, 'We'll step out, talk, and tell you together'","then":"You both step into the kitchen, and the table waits quietly.","best":true},{"text":"Answer for both of you before they can speak up","then":"Your partner's face tightens, though they say nothing yet."},{"text":"Argue your side loudly to win in front of everyone","then":"Your uncle looks uncomfortable and the room goes tense."},{"text":"Say 'whatever they want' and give up your view","then":"Your partner feels unsupported, even though you 'won' the peace."}],"why":"Pausing to talk privately protects both your view and the partnership."},{"prompt":"Minutes later, alone for a moment, your partner says, 'That was tense. We need to actually sort this out.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'Let's sort out hosting now, properly'","then":"Your partner softens. 'Okay. Let's figure it out together.'","best":true},{"text":"Apologise but avoid discussing hosting at all","then":"The apology lands, but the actual disagreement stays unresolved."},{"text":"Point out they've overruled you before too","then":"The talk turns into a bigger argument about old habits."},{"text":"Say nothing and wait for the tension to pass","then":"The silence stretches and neither of you brings up hosting."}],"why":"Taking it seriously and solving the real issue rebuilds trust fast."},{"prompt":"Word comes that your uncle wants a proper answer from you both. What's your next move?","options":[{"text":"Walk back in together and give one honest answer","then":"Your uncle nods, glad to hear it from both of you, and dinner moves on.","best":true},{"text":"Send your partner back in alone to answer for both of you","then":"Your partner delivers it solo, feeling like this is theirs to carry."},{"text":"Text the family group your answer instead of saying it aloud","then":"Your aunt reads it aloud, and the moment feels oddly cold."},{"text":"Wait for someone to ask again before saying anything","then":"The unanswered question hangs over the rest of the evening."}],"why":"Answering together, even if it is 'not yet', shows the family you are one team."},{"prompt":"A week later your mother brings up the hosting question again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give one calm answer together","then":"She nods, satisfied it's a real decision and not just talk.","best":true},{"text":"Let your partner answer alone while you stay quiet","then":"Your mother notices only one of you speaking for the couple."},{"text":"Get irritated that it keeps being asked again","then":"The irritation reads as tension between you two, not with her."},{"text":"Change the plan slightly to please her, without telling your partner","then":"Your partner is surprised later to hear the plan shifted without them."}],"why":"One steady answer from both of you shows the couple is aligned."},{"prompt":"Later that week, your partner asks how you felt about being put on the spot at the family dinner. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'It stung a bit, but let's sort things out before family'","then":"Your partner nods. 'Same. Let's agree things before family, next time.'","best":true},{"text":"'I'm fine. It was only dinner, so please don't worry'","then":"Your partner senses you're brushing it off and drops the topic."},{"text":"'You should have backed me up a lot faster at the table'","then":"Your partner gets defensive and the evening ends a little flat."},{"text":"Bring up an old, unrelated argument from last year instead","then":"The conversation drifts away from what actually happened at dinner."}],"why":"Naming the sting honestly, without blame, helps the team learn for next time."}],"debrief":"Disagree in private, decide as a team, present one answer; don't fight in front of family.","relearn":"Resolve couple disagreements privately, then face family as one team."},
  {"id":"fm-035","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the couple is the new first team. That isn't disloyal to your parents, it's what marriage means. Sit right?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Sits right","New first team","Still respect parents"],"affirm":"Making the couple the primary team isn't disloyalty to parents; it's the core of a partnership.","relearn":"The couple becomes the first team, while still honouring parents; that isn't disloyalty.","persona":"any","source":"first team"},
  {"id":"fm-036","cat":"couple-team","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"back them warmly","hook":"While Divya takes a call outside, your aunt at dinner is clearly building up to something.","setup":"Your aunt looks straight at you, waiting for a reaction.","steps":[{"prompt":"Aunt says: \"She's a bit too modern for this family, isn't she?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I actually love that about her. We're figuring life out together.\"","then":"Aunt raises an eyebrow. \"Well. If you say so.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe you're right, but she's still lovely.\"","then":"Aunt says: \"See, even you agree a little,\" pleased."},{"text":"\"Every family's different, I guess.\"","then":"Aunt shrugs. \"Well, each to their own.\""},{"text":"\"She'll settle down eventually.\"","then":"Aunt smiles. \"That's more like it.\""}],"why":"Backing Divya warmly, out loud, closes the wedge before it opens."},{"prompt":"Your uncle joins in: \"Modern is fine, but does she cook?\"","options":[{"text":"\"She's wonderful, and we split the kitchen between us.\"","then":"Uncle blinks. \"Fair enough,\" then turns to talk cricket.","best":true},{"text":"\"She's learning, slowly.\"","then":"Uncle nods. \"See, I knew it.\""},{"text":"\"Let's change the subject, Uncle. Have you tried the paneer?\"","then":"Uncle grins. \"Ha, you're dodging,\" and the question about Divya hangs."},{"text":"\"Depends on the day.\"","then":"Uncle takes the vague answer. \"So sometimes not, then.\""}],"why":"A plain, warm answer that includes Divya refuses the trap without a fight."},{"prompt":"Divya comes back from a phone call and asks quietly: \"Did they say something while I was out?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Aunty called you too modern. That's not how I see you. We'll talk after.\"","then":"She squeezes your hand. \"Thank you for telling me straight.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing worth repeating. It was just family chatter.\"","then":"She says: \"Okay,\" but her eyes stay unsettled."},{"text":"\"Just the usual aunty stuff. Please ignore it.\"","then":"She says: \"Which usual stuff, exactly?\""},{"text":"\"Don't worry about it. Enjoy your dinner.\"","then":"She smiles, but says: \"I'm still curious, you know.\""}],"why":"Telling her honestly, without alarming her, keeps the couple a team."},{"prompt":"At the next dinner, the same aunt tries again, more gently: \"No offence, just an observation.\"","options":[{"text":"\"None taken. We're happy, Aunty.\"","then":"Aunty smiles, a little disarmed. \"Well, good, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's fine. We're used to it.\"","then":"Aunty says: \"Used to it? Interesting,\" and keeps going."},{"text":"\"Please, not this again.\"","then":"Aunty says: \"I was only asking.\" The table tenses anyway."},{"text":"\"Divya can answer that herself.\"","then":"Aunty turns to Divya. \"Well? Go on then.\""}],"why":"A warm, direct answer every time keeps your partner from carrying it alone."}],"relearn":"Backing your partner warmly in the room keeps the team solid and ends the wedge."},
  {"id":"fm-037","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a decision the couple should make as a team, or one to share with the elders too?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"How you split chores and money"},{"id":"b","text":"Hosting a shared family puja"},{"id":"c","text":"Whether and when to have kids"},{"id":"d","text":"Planning a joint family holiday"},{"id":"e","text":"Where the two of you decide to live"},{"id":"f","text":"Organising a big family wedding together"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"Couple-team call","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shared","label":"Share with elders","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"shared","c":"couple","d":"shared","e":"couple","f":"shared"},"relearn":"Chores, money, kids and where you live are the couple's; shared pujas, joint holidays and a big wedding can be planned with elders too.","persona":"Pooja","source":"team vs shared"},
  {"id":"fm-038","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Keeping secrets from your partner to protect your family is loyalty.\"","myth":{"un":"Keeping secrets from your partner to protect your family is loyalty.","re":"The couple-team runs on honesty between you two; hidden family deals erode the trust the team needs.","why":"Transparency with your partner is what keeps the team strong."},"relearn":"The couple runs on honesty; hiding family matters from your partner weakens the team, not protects it.","persona":"any","source":"team honesty"},
  {"id":"fm-039","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"no hidden deals","hook":"Your father asks you to commit to funding your cousin's wedding, without telling your wife Divya.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says, 'Just say yes. We'll sort the money later.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say, 'We decide money together. Let me ask Divya first'","then":"Your father looks surprised, then says, 'Fair enough. Go ahead.'","best":true},{"text":"Say yes now and mention it to Divya later","then":"Your father is happy, but you're already dreading the conversation."},{"text":"Say no outright without any explanation","then":"Your father feels brushed off and brings it up again another day."},{"text":"Say you'll think about it and put off talking to Divya","then":"The silence drags, and your father assumes you'll agree eventually."}],"why":"Naming the couple's rule early keeps the decision honest and shared."},{"prompt":"A few days later, you finally sit down with Divya to talk about your cousin's wedding. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Lay out the ask honestly and ask what she thinks","then":"Divya appreciates hearing it straight, and you decide together calmly.","best":true},{"text":"Soften it so it sounds like a small favour","then":"Divya agrees to the softened version, unaware of the real amount."},{"text":"Tell her you're already leaning toward yes","then":"Divya feels her opinion is just a formality, not a real choice."},{"text":"Wait for a quieter week to bring it up with her","then":"The days pass, and your father asks whether you have an answer yet."}],"why":"An honest, full picture lets the couple decide with the real facts."},{"prompt":"Your father calls, wanting a number he can tell the family. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Settle it with Divya first, then give him one answer together","then":"Your father hears it from both of you and respects the number.","best":true},{"text":"Let Divya call alone, so you don't have to be there","then":"Your father assumes Divya is the one deciding, not both of you."},{"text":"You tell him alone so Divya isn't 'blamed'","then":"Your father later brings it up to Divya directly, confused who decided."},{"text":"Text him a number instead of a real conversation","then":"Your father reads it flatly, and it feels transactional to him."}],"why":"Delivering the decision together keeps it a couple decision, not one person's fault."},{"prompt":"Months later your father asks for a bigger favour the same way, quietly to you alone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say kindly: 'We decide together, every time'","then":"He nods, and waits to hear from both of you.","best":true},{"text":"Agree quickly since it worked out fine last time","then":"Divya finds out afterward and feels the rule only applied once."},{"text":"Get annoyed that he's asking the same way again","then":"The annoyance comes out sharper than you meant, and he goes quiet."},{"text":"Ask Divya to just trust your judgement this time","then":"She agrees, but notices the team habit slipping."}],"why":"Repeating the same honest rule keeps money decisions the couple's, every time."}],"debrief":"Big commitments go through the couple-team together, never hidden from your partner.","relearn":"Keep money and big commitments transparent between partners, not hidden for family."},
  {"id":"fm-040","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'we'll decide together and let you know' is the most useful sentence in a joint family. Worth memorising?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth it","Memorising it","So useful"],"affirm":"'We'll decide together and let you know' protects the couple-team kindly in almost any family pressure.","relearn":"One sentence carries the couple-team: 'we'll decide together and let you know.'","persona":"any","source":"the useful sentence"},
  {"id":"fm-041","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each couple-team habit to what it protects.","pairs":[{"left":"Aligning before family events","right":"Fewer surprises and public clashes"},{"left":"Debriefing afterward","right":"Repair and learning as a team"},{"left":"One united answer to pressure","right":"Trust that you've got each other"},{"left":"Keeping money decisions shared","right":"No hidden deals that break trust"},{"left":"A steady, repeatable team line","right":"No fresh fight at every demand"}],"relearn":"Aligning before, debriefing after, sharing money, one answer and a steady line are the habits that keep a couple solid in a big family.","persona":"any","source":"team habits"},
  {"id":"fm-042","cat":"couple-team","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that break the couple-team under family pressure.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Checking in privately before answering relatives","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Letting relatives split you by flattery","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Backing each other in the room","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Blaming your partner to look good","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Giving one calm, shared answer","trick":false}],"why":"Letting flattery split you and blaming your partner break the team; checking in, backing each other and a shared answer keep it.","relearn":"Stay a team: align privately and back each other; don't let flattery split you or blame your partner.","persona":"Sneha","source":"stay a team"},
  {"id":"fm-043","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Respecting elders means obeying them in everything.\"","myth":{"un":"Respecting elders means obeying them in everything.","re":"Respect means honouring and listening to elders; it doesn't mean surrendering every choice to them.","why":"You can deeply respect someone and still disagree."},"relearn":"Respect means honouring and listening, not obeying in everything; you can respect and still choose.","persona":"Pooja","source":"respect not obedience"},
  {"id":"fm-044","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Sneha","source":"honour then choose","hook":"An elder gives you advice you disagree with, and you don't want to be disrespectful.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your grandfather advises you to take a certain job offer over another. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank him sincerely, consider it, then decide for yourself","then":"He nods, pleased you took the advice seriously either way.","best":true},{"text":"Say yes to his pick straight away, mostly to avoid disappointing him","then":"He is pleased, and you spend the week doubting whether the choice was yours."},{"text":"Dismiss it as old-fashioned thinking","then":"The dismissal stings him, even though your reasoning may be sound."},{"text":"Avoid the topic whenever he brings it up","then":"Avoiding him leaves the disagreement unspoken and unresolved."}],"why":"Receiving advice with real respect and still deciding yourself honours you both."},{"prompt":"A week later, he asks which job you've settled on. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell him honestly, and thank him for taking the time to share his view","then":"He listens, and respects the honesty even where he would have chosen differently.","best":true},{"text":"Soften the news so it sounds closer to his advice","then":"The softened version leaves him with a slightly false picture."},{"text":"Avoid answering directly, hoping he'll drop it","then":"The dodge doesn't last. He finds out anyway and brings it up directly."},{"text":"Say you're 'still deciding', even though you've actually already chosen","then":"The small untruth doesn't hold. He hears the truth soon after, and brings it up directly."}],"why":"An honest, grateful answer keeps the respect real instead of just polite."},{"prompt":"He says, 'I only want the best for you, so I keep wondering about your choice.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'I hear you, and I still feel good about my choice'","then":"He nods slowly. 'Well, it's your life. I hope it works out.'","best":true},{"text":"Change the subject to avoid prolonging it","then":"The disagreement gets parked, unresolved, instead of actually settled."},{"text":"Explain your reasoning at length to convince him","then":"The long explanation starts to feel like you're seeking his approval."},{"text":"Say maybe he's right, just to smooth things over","then":"The comment quietly walks back a decision you were sure of."}],"why":"Standing by your choice warmly, without needing him to agree, respects both of you."},{"prompt":"Months later the job is going well, and he mentions it warmly at a family gathering. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Thank him warmly for the advice he gave you","then":"He beams, glad you still valued his input.","best":true},{"text":"Let the moment pass without mentioning the advice","then":"A chance to close the loop warmly quietly slips by."},{"text":"Remind everyone at the table that you were right to go your own way","then":"The comment turns a warm moment into a small point being made."},{"text":"Downplay how well things are actually going","then":"Downplaying it takes away from a genuinely good update he'd enjoy hearing."}],"why":"Thanking him for the advice, regardless of the outcome, honours the relationship fully."}],"debrief":"You can receive elders' advice with real respect and still make your own decision.","relearn":"Honour the advice sincerely, then decide for yourselves; both can coexist."},
  {"id":"fm-045","type":"match","hook":"Match each elder's offering to the respectful way to receive it.","pairs":[{"left":"Hard-won life wisdom","right":"Listen genuinely, take what fits"},{"left":"An outdated expectation","right":"Decline kindly, without scorn"},{"left":"A warm tradition","right":"Join in where it's meaningful to you"},{"left":"A worried warning","right":"Thank them, then weigh it yourself"},{"left":"A generous offer","right":"Accept warmly, or decline gently"}],"relearn":"Respect both ways means hearing wisdom, declining the outdated kindly, joining what's meaningful, weighing warnings and handling offers gracefully.","persona":"any","source":"receiving respectfully","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-046","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If you keep your own views, you're being a disrespectful, modern troublemaker.\"","myth":{"un":"If you keep your own views, you're being a disrespectful, modern troublemaker.","re":"Holding your own values respectfully is healthy; respect flows both ways between generations.","why":"Having a voice and showing respect aren't opposites."},"relearn":"Keeping your own values respectfully isn't disrespect; respect is meant to flow both ways.","persona":"Pooja","source":"voice and respect"},
  {"id":"fm-047","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: respect flowing both ways, or respect demanded only one way?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Elders' experience is valued"},{"id":"b","text":"Only the young must adjust, never elders"},{"id":"c","text":"The couple's choices are also respected"},{"id":"d","text":"Disagreement from the young is 'insolence'"},{"id":"e","text":"Both sides can own a mistake"},{"id":"f","text":"Elders are above every question"}],"bins":[{"id":"both","label":"Both ways","valence":"pos"},{"id":"one","label":"One way only","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"both","b":"one","c":"both","d":"one","e":"both","f":"one"},"relearn":"Valuing elders and the couple's choices, with apologies on both sides, is mutual; all the adjusting and silence on one side isn't.","persona":"any","source":"both ways or one","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-048","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the goal isn't to win against elders, it's a relationship where both generations feel respected. Resonate?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Resonates","Both respected","Not winning"],"affirm":"The aim is mutual respect across generations, not defeating the elders or being defeated by them.","relearn":"The goal is mutual respect across generations, not winning against elders.","persona":"any","source":"mutual respect"},
  {"id":"fm-049","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two that are fear dressed up as respect, not the real thing.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Listening because you value their experience","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Agreeing only because you're scared to speak","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Disagreeing politely and still being warm","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Going silent in dread of their anger","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Weighing their advice, then choosing","trick":false}],"why":"Scared agreement and dread-driven silence are just fear; valuing experience and warm disagreement are real respect.","relearn":"Real respect includes honest disagreement; silent fear isn't respect.","persona":"Sneha","source":"respect not fear","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-050","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Razia","source":"join and opt out","hook":"Your in-laws follow a tradition that doesn't fit your beliefs, but want you to join.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law asks if you'll join the whole three-day ritual. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Join the parts that feel right and skip the rest","then":"She nods and shows you which parts matter most to her.","best":true},{"text":"Agree to join everything so nobody asks again","then":"You go through every step, quietly uneasy the whole time."},{"text":"Say you're busy that whole week","then":"She looks disappointed and stops mentioning it to you at all."},{"text":"Refuse outright and explain your beliefs don't match","then":"The room goes quiet, and she doesn't bring it up again."}],"why":"Joining part of it keeps the bond warm without asking you to fake belief."},{"prompt":"Later, an aunt starts talking about how the old ways matter. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you value the old ways and take part in your own way","then":"The aunt nods slowly. 'Fair enough,' she says, and moves on.","best":true},{"text":"Say you always try to keep everyone happy","then":"The aunt says that's the right attitude, and expects it going forward."},{"text":"Say nothing and let her keep talking","then":"The aunt keeps talking, and you leave without saying what you think."},{"text":"Tell her it's an outdated custom anyway","then":"She stiffens, and the comment gets repeated around the family."}],"why":"Saying you take part in your own way answers warmly without opening a bigger debate."},{"prompt":"The next big family function comes around, ritual and all. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Join what feels right, and skip the rest kindly","then":"Your mother-in-law nods, and the rhythm feels easy now.","best":true},{"text":"Join fully this time to avoid another conversation","then":"You go along with it, feeling the same old unease."},{"text":"Skip the whole function to sidestep the topic","then":"You miss the day entirely, and she wonders if something's wrong."},{"text":"Announce loudly that you never do the full ritual","then":"The declaration lands harder than you meant, and eyes turn to you."}],"why":"Joining what fits keeps the bond easy, without asking you to fake belief."}],"debrief":"Take part in a tradition warmly where you can, and decline the rest respectfully.","relearn":"Join traditions where you can and opt out kindly; honour the bond and yourself."},
  {"id":"fm-051","type":"match","hook":"Match a respectful phrase to the situation it fits.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Thank you, that helps; we'll think it over.\"","right":"Receiving unsolicited advice"},{"left":"\"We see it differently, with respect.\"","right":"A genuine disagreement"},{"left":"\"We'd love to do it our way this time.\"","right":"A choice that's yours to make"},{"left":"\"We'll keep the heart of it, our version.\"","right":"A tradition you'll adapt"},{"left":"\"I'd still like to finish my thought.\"","right":"Being talked over"}],"relearn":"Respectful phrasing lets you receive advice, disagree, choose, adapt a custom and ask to be heard, all without contempt.","persona":"any","source":"respectful phrasing","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-052","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"In-laws can never really become close, loving family.\"","myth":{"un":"In-laws can never really become close, loving family.","re":"Many in-law bonds grow into genuine warmth and support over time, with patience on both sides.","why":"These relationships can become some of life's most supportive."},"relearn":"In-law bonds can grow into real, loving family with time and patience; the cynical version isn't fixed.","persona":"Pooja","source":"bonds can grow"},
  {"id":"fm-053","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: an elder who feels respected is far more open to your boundaries. Respect and boundaries work together. See it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I see it","They work together","Worth remembering"],"affirm":"Respect and boundaries reinforce each other: a respected elder is more open to your limits.","relearn":"Respect and boundaries reinforce each other; a respected elder hears your limits better.","persona":"any","source":"respect aids boundaries"},
  {"id":"fm-054","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"role-play","persona":"Sneha","source":"career advice","hook":"An elder shares strong career advice you don't plan to take.","setup":"Over tea, she leans in with her opinion.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"Take that stable government job your uncle mentioned.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you for caring. I've thought hard, and I'm going my own way on this.\"","then":"She studies you, then says, \"Well, you always did know your own mind.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe. I'll think about it.\"","then":"She takes that as a yes and starts asking your uncle about the details."},{"text":"\"I don't really want to talk about this right now.\"","then":"She looks a little hurt, and brings it up again at the next visit."},{"text":"\"You don't understand my life at all, so please just stop telling me what to do.\"","then":"She stiffens. \"I was only trying to help,\" she says quietly."}],"why":"Gratitude plus a clear boundary lets her hear you without feeling dismissed."},{"prompt":"A week later she calls and asks: \"Have you thought more about what I said?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I have, and I'm still choosing my current path. It's the right fit for me.\"","then":"\"Alright,\" she says. \"I just want you settled and happy.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm still thinking it over.\"","then":"She takes it as progress and brings it up again the following week."},{"text":"\"I've been really busy lately. Honestly, I haven't had much time to think it over.\"","then":"She offers to send you the job details again, just in case."},{"text":"\"Please stop asking about this. It's my decision.\"","then":"She goes quiet on the phone, clearly stung by the sharpness."}],"why":"Repeating the same warm, firm line calmly closes the loop without a fresh argument."},{"prompt":"At the next family gathering, she tells a relative: \"They'll come around to the government job eventually.\" What do you say?","options":[{"text":"\"Actually, I've chosen my own path, and I'm happy with it.\"","then":"The relative nods, and she looks a little surprised but lets it be.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about that here.\"","then":"The comment stands uncorrected, and the relative assumes it's settled."},{"text":"\"She exaggerates sometimes.\"","then":"It brushes past the moment, but the misunderstanding stays alive."},{"text":"\"Why do you keep telling people that, when it isn't even true anymore?\"","then":"She looks embarrassed in front of the relative, and the mood turns awkward."}],"why":"Correcting it warmly, in the moment, stops the story from setting in as fact."}],"relearn":"Gratitude plus a clear, warm 'my own way' honours the elder and your autonomy at once."},
  {"id":"fm-055","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"stay open","hook":"You catch yourself resenting your in-laws and writing them off entirely.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law calls just to chat. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer warmly, and keep the call a comfortable length","then":"She sounds pleased, and the call ends easily for both of you.","best":true},{"text":"Answer, but stay short and distant throughout","then":"She notices the coolness and the call ends a little flat."},{"text":"Let it go to voicemail again","then":"She leaves a message, and the gap between you grows a little more."},{"text":"Answer and list everything that's bothered you lately","then":"She goes quiet, caught off guard by the sudden flood."}],"why":"A warm, ordinary call keeps the door open without costing you anything."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later she invites you over for tea, just the two of you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go, and ask her about her own life for once","then":"She lights up telling you about her college days. It's a good hour.","best":true},{"text":"Go, but keep the visit as brief as you can","then":"You leave quickly, and the visit feels more like a duty."},{"text":"Decline, saying you're too busy this month","then":"She says she understands, though the invitation doesn't come again for a while."},{"text":"Go and use the visit to air old grievances","then":"The tea turns tense, and she seems smaller by the end."}],"why":"Asking about her life builds real closeness, not just polite duty."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks how things feel with your in-laws these days. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you're choosing to stay open, even on the harder days","then":"Your partner nods, glad to hear you're still trying.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's fine, though you're still keeping your distance","then":"Your partner nods, unsure if anything has actually shifted."},{"text":"Say you've basically given up trying","then":"Your partner looks disappointed, having hoped it was getting easier."},{"text":"Say everything's absolutely perfect now, glossing right over the hard parts","then":"Your partner senses you're not being fully honest."}],"why":"Naming the effort honestly, not a tidy verdict, keeps you and your partner aligned."}],"debrief":"Keep the door open to the bond even as you hold boundaries; don't write the relationship off.","relearn":"Hold boundaries and stay open to the bond; writing in-laws off ends what could grow."},
  {"id":"fm-056","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A little dowry is just tradition, so it's fine.\"","myth":{"un":"A little dowry is just tradition, so it's fine.","re":"Dowry is illegal in India, giving, taking and demanding it are all offences under the Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961.","why":"The law bans dowry whatever the custom is called."},"relearn":"Dowry, giving, taking or demanding, is illegal under the Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961, not just 'tradition'.","persona":"Razia","source":"dowry is illegal"},
  {"id":"fm-057","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Boundary problems and abuse from in-laws are the same thing.\"","myth":{"un":"Boundary problems and abuse from in-laws are the same thing.","re":"Normal friction is about limits and adjusting; abuse is a pattern of control, threats, dowry harassment or violence, and needs help.","why":"There's a clear line between everyday friction and abuse."},"relearn":"Everyday friction differs from abuse, a pattern of control, threats or dowry harassment; abuse needs help.","persona":"Reshma","source":"friction vs abuse"},
  {"id":"fm-058","type":"sort","persona":"Reshma","source":"friction or red flag","hook":"Sort: ordinary family friction to work through, or a red flag that needs help?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Disagreeing about visit frequency"},{"id":"b","text":"Demands for money or gifts under threat"},{"id":"c","text":"Differing festival customs"},{"id":"d","text":"Controlling your movements, phone or money"},{"id":"e","text":"A clash over how to spend a shared weekend"},{"id":"f","text":"Being threatened if you don't obey"}],"bins":[{"id":"friction","label":"Work-through friction","valence":"pos"},{"id":"redflag","label":"Red flag, get help","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"friction","b":"redflag","c":"friction","d":"redflag","e":"friction","f":"redflag"},"relearn":"Visit, custom and weekend clashes are friction; threats for money, control and intimidation are red flags needing help.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-059","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Razia","source":"name dowry harassment","hook":"Your in-laws begin pressuring your family for money and gifts after the wedding.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother mentions your in-laws hinted about a car as a 'gift'. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell your partner plainly this is dowry","then":"He goes quiet, then says he'll speak to his parents directly.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself it's probably just a hint, not a real demand","then":"The hint sits unspoken, and it's easy to imagine you're overthinking it."},{"text":"Assume your mother is just being anxious about nothing","then":"You brush it off, though the hint doesn't actually go away."},{"text":"Keep it between you and your mother for now","then":"It stays a quiet worry between the two of you, unspoken to anyone else."}],"why":"Naming it plainly to your partner means you face it together, not alone."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner says his parents told him a car is 'normal' in their family. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Name it plainly as illegal, and reach a counsellor","then":"Your partner agrees, and you both note a counsellor's number that evening.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself maybe it really is just how this family does things","then":"The thought makes the demand feel almost ordinary, though it hasn't changed."},{"text":"Wonder if you're making too much of a small thing","then":"The self-doubt makes it harder to bring up again."},{"text":"Keep it just between you two for now, without telling anyone else","then":"The pressure stays a private worry, unshared with anyone who could help."}],"why":"Naming the law together, and reaching a counsellor, backs the couple's refusal with real support."},{"prompt":"A relative says every family goes through 'some of this'. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say plainly that demanding dowry is illegal","then":"The relative looks surprised, and it isn't repeated at the next gathering.","best":true},{"text":"Wonder if the relative is right and it's just normal","then":"The comment feels a little more convincing each time you hear it."},{"text":"Tell yourself you're probably overreacting","then":"The self-doubt makes the whole thing feel smaller than it is."},{"text":"Ask the relative how other families settled it","then":"The relative describes families who 'gave a little', and the demand starts to sound negotiable."}],"why":"Naming it as illegal, not tradition, is what actually shuts the pressure down."}],"debrief":"Post-wedding money demands are dowry harassment, which is illegal and never your fault; name it and get help.","relearn":"Post-wedding money demands are illegal dowry harassment, never your fault; name it and seek help."},
  {"id":"fm-060","type":"spot","persona":"Reshma","source":"coercive control","hook":"Spot the two signs that in-law conflict has crossed into coercive control.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Disagreeing about how to host a festival","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Being cut off from your own money and friends","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Being threatened if you don't obey","trick":true},{"id":"d","text":"Differing tastes in food or routine","trick":false},{"id":"e","text":"Working out a fair share of chores","trick":false}],"why":"Festival clashes, differing tastes and sorting chores are normal; cutting you off from money and friends, and threats, are control.","relearn":"Being isolated from money and people, or threatened, is coercive control, not ordinary conflict.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-061","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"What happens inside the family is private, so no one should ever get help.\"","myth":{"un":"What happens inside the family is private, so no one should ever get help.","re":"Privacy never covers abuse; you have every right to seek help when control, threats or violence appear.","why":"Abuse isn't a private matter to be hidden."},"relearn":"Privacy doesn't cover abuse; you can always seek help when there's control, threats or violence.","persona":"Reshma","source":"not a private matter"},
  {"id":"fm-062","type":"match","persona":"Reshma","source":"help routes","hook":"Match each India help route to when it's for.","pairs":[{"left":"Women's Helpline 181","right":"In-law abuse or dowry harassment"},{"left":"Police / emergency 112","right":"Immediate danger or violence"},{"left":"Respect at Home (g56) + a counsellor","right":"Naming and planning around abuse"},{"left":"Women in distress 1091","right":"A woman who feels unsafe right now"},{"left":"Childline 1098","right":"A girl about to be married off"}],"relearn":"181, 1091, 112, 1098 and a counsellor are real routes when family conflict turns to abuse, dowry harassment or danger.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-063","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Razia","source":"believe and support","hook":"A friend confides that her in-laws control her every move and threaten her.","steps":[{"prompt":"Over coffee, she admits she's scared to say no to her mother-in-law. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Listen, believe her, and say it's not her fault","then":"She exhales, like she's been holding that in for months.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her exactly what she should do about it","then":"She nods, but feels pushed rather than supported."},{"text":"Say every marriage has hard patches like this","then":"She goes quiet, hearing it as something she should just accept."},{"text":"Change the subject to something lighter","then":"She laughs with relief at the lighter talk, then comes back to what's worrying her on her own."}],"why":"Believing her, and saying it's not her fault, is what lets her keep talking honestly."},{"prompt":"She says she's not sure if it 'counts' as something serious. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it counts, and gently share a helpline she can call","then":"She writes the number down quietly, relief crossing her face.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her to leave immediately for her own safety","then":"She freezes, not ready to hear something that big yet."},{"text":"Reassure her it's probably not as bad as it seems","then":"She second-guesses what she just told you, and pulls back a little."},{"text":"Say you're not sure either, and leave it there","then":"She's left as unsure as before, with nowhere to turn."}],"why":"Naming it seriously and offering a real number respects her pace while still helping."},{"prompt":"A week later, she says she's scared to bring it up with anyone else. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Offer to go with her to a counsellor whenever she's ready","then":"She squeezes your hand. \"Maybe. Just knowing you'd come helps.\"","best":true},{"text":"Tell her she has to tell her parents right away","then":"She feels cornered, and the offer of support feels like pressure."},{"text":"Promise to keep it just between the two of you forever","then":"She feels safer for now, but stays without any real support around her."},{"text":"Say you'll handle it for her if she wants","then":"She isn't ready for someone else to take over her story."}],"why":"Offering to go with her, at her pace, gives real support without taking over her choice."}],"debrief":"When someone describes control and threats, believe them, say it's never their fault, and offer help, never 'just adjust'.","relearn":"Believe someone describing control and threats, and offer help instead of telling them to adjust."},
  {"id":"fm-064","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: kind boundaries handle the everyday, but control, threats or dowry demands aren't boundary problems, they need help. Clear?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Clear","That's different","Get help then"],"affirm":"Boundaries handle ordinary friction; control, threats and dowry demands are abuse that needs help, not negotiation.","relearn":"Control, threats and dowry demands aren't boundary problems; they're abuse that needs help.","persona":"Reshma","source":"beyond boundaries"},
  {"id":"fm-065","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Anti-dowry and cruelty laws are only ever misused, so they don't really protect anyone.\"","myth":{"un":"Anti-dowry and cruelty laws are only ever misused, so they don't really protect anyone.","re":"These laws exist to protect people from real, serious harm; misuse can occur, but the protections are genuine and important.","why":"Protections being imperfect doesn't make them fake."},"relearn":"Anti-dowry and cruelty laws offer real protection from serious harm, even though misuse is debated.","persona":"Reshma","source":"real protection"},
  {"id":"fm-066","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: when a home stops feeling safe, telling one trusted person is a strong first step, not a betrayal of the family. Sit right with you?","prompt":"What's your read?","options":["Sits right","Telling is a step","Not betrayal"],"affirm":"Speaking to one trusted person when home feels unsafe is a brave first move, not a betrayal of anyone.","relearn":"Telling one trusted person when home turns unsafe is a strong first step, never a betrayal.","persona":"Reshma","source":"telling isn't betrayal"},
  {"id":"fm-067","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Reshma","source":"reach out anyway","hook":"You're unsure whether what you're going through at home 'counts' as serious enough.","steps":[{"prompt":"Late one night, after another tense evening at home, you pick up your phone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call a counsellor or 181 anyway","then":"The person on the line listens without needing you to be certain first.","best":true},{"text":"Wait a while longer to see if it gets clearer","then":"Weeks pass and nothing gets clearer, so one evening you call 181 after all."},{"text":"Tell yourself it isn't bad enough to call yet","then":"You push the thought away, but it returns on the next tense evening, and you call 181."},{"text":"Decide every marriage feels like this","then":"The thought is a small comfort. The unease stays, and a few days later you call 181."}],"why":"Reaching out doesn't need certainty first; a counsellor can help you make sense of it."},{"prompt":"The counsellor asks you to describe what's been happening at home. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Describe it honestly, even the parts that feel small","then":"She tells you the small parts matter too, and helps you see a pattern.","best":true},{"text":"Downplay it, worried it isn't bad enough to mention","then":"She senses there's more, and gently asks you to say a little more."},{"text":"Focus only on the one big incident","then":"The smaller, steady pattern around it goes unmentioned."},{"text":"Apologise for taking up her time with something small","then":"She reassures you that you're not taking up her time, and waits."}],"why":"Being honest about even the small parts is what lets a real pattern come into focus."},{"prompt":"She suggests a next step, and you're unsure if you're ready. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say what you're comfortable with now","then":"She adjusts the plan to your pace, and it feels manageable, not rushed.","best":true},{"text":"Agree to everything she suggests right away","then":"You agree, though part of you isn't sure you're ready for all of it yet."},{"text":"Say you'll think about it and never follow up","then":"The thread quietly goes cold, and the uncertainty returns."},{"text":"Ask her to just decide for you","then":"She gently explains it has to be your own pace, not hers."}],"why":"Naming your own pace keeps you moving forward without being pushed faster than you're ready for."}],"debrief":"You don't have to be certain it's 'serious enough' to reach out; it's never your fault, and help can start with uncertainty.","relearn":"You can seek help while still unsure; don't wait until it's 'bad enough'."},
  {"id":"fm-068","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"pre-visit check-in","hook":"Before a big family visit, you want to avoid the usual blow-ups.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks what's on your mind the morning of the visit. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Run a two-minute check-in, and agree your line on the likely topics","then":"You both walk in already aligned, and the visit feels lighter.","best":true},{"text":"Say you're fine and figure it out as it comes","then":"You walk in unprepared, and the first tricky topic catches you off guard."},{"text":"Ask your partner to simply follow your lead today, without much discussion","then":"Your partner feels sidelined rather than part of the plan."},{"text":"List every worry you have about the visit","then":"Your partner starts the day anxious rather than steady."}],"why":"A short check-in aligns you both before you even walk in the door."},{"prompt":"Midway through the visit, a topic you didn't expect comes up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Glance at your partner and give a calm answer together","then":"You handle it steadily, and your partner picks up the thread.","best":true},{"text":"Wing an answer on your own and hope it lands well","then":"It comes out a little uneven, since you hadn't talked it through."},{"text":"Stay quiet and let your partner handle it alone, unsure how to help","then":"Your partner fields it solo, unsure if you're backing them up."},{"text":"Deflect the topic loudly to shut it down","then":"The room goes a bit tense, and the topic just resurfaces later."}],"why":"A shared, calm answer handles the unexpected without either of you scrambling alone."},{"prompt":"On the drive home, your partner asks how the visit felt for you. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say what helped, and suggest a check-in before every visit","then":"Your partner agrees. \"Two minutes before we go. Let's try it,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"Say it went fine because the family was in a good mood today","then":"Your partner nods, and neither of you learns what actually helped."},{"text":"Change the subject to dinner plans, since the drive is for relaxing","then":"The drive turns to dinner plans, and nothing gets learned for next time."},{"text":"Say the visit was exhausting no matter what you do","then":"Your partner feels a bit deflated about the effort you both made."}],"why":"Naming what helps turns it into a habit instead of a one-off."}],"debrief":"A two-minute pre-visit check-in, agreeing your line, prevents most family blow-ups.","relearn":"Align before visits with a quick couple check-in; don't wing the hard topics."},
  {"id":"fm-069","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each tool to the job it does.","pairs":[{"left":"The warm 'no'","right":"Decline without rejecting"},{"left":"The couple check-in","right":"Agree your line before family time"},{"left":"The repeatable team line","right":"Answer as one, every time"},{"left":"Knowing the family map","right":"Feel placed, not powerless"},{"left":"The help routes","right":"When friction becomes abuse"}],"relearn":"The warm no, the check-in, the team line, the map and the help routes are the core tools of navigating family kindly and safely.","persona":"any","source":"the toolkit"},
  {"id":"fm-070","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"the repeatable line","hook":"A relative pushes you to justify a private decision. You want a warm, firm, repeatable line.","setup":"He brings it up again at the dinner table.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"Why haven't you two decided on this yet? What's taking so long?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We've thought about it, and we're happy with our choice.\"","then":"He pauses and says, \"Alright,\" though he looks like he has more to say.","best":true},{"text":"\"It's complicated. Let me walk you through everything we've weighed.\"","then":"He takes that as an invitation to offer more advice."},{"text":"\"Can we talk about something else?\"","then":"He lets it go for now, but brings it up again later that same evening."},{"text":"\"Ugh, why do you always ask me that?\"","then":"He looks taken aback, and mutters that he was only asking."}],"why":"Answering plainly and warmly, without over-explaining, keeps the pressure from creeping back."},{"prompt":"Later that evening, he tries again: \"Surely you've had more time to think by now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you for caring. We're happy with our choice, and that's our answer.\"","then":"He nods slowly. \"Fair enough,\" he says, and lets it be.","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe we'll reconsider that. We'll see.\"","then":"He takes it as an opening and keeps the conversation going."},{"text":"\"I'd rather not get into it.\"","then":"He fills the pause with his own guesses about what's going on."},{"text":"\"Please stop pushing. We're not discussing this.\"","then":"He looks stung, and the table goes a little quiet."}],"why":"Restating your answer plainly keeps the story consistent and low effort to hold."},{"prompt":"At the next family dinner, he tries once more: \"So, really, what's the holdup?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're happy with our choice. That's our answer.\"","then":"He laughs. \"Fair enough,\" he says warmly.","best":true},{"text":"\"We haven't really talked about it lately.\"","then":"He takes that as an opening and asks for an update soon."},{"text":"\"Have you tried the mutton today? It's absolutely amazing.\"","then":"It works for tonight, but the question just waits for the next dinner."},{"text":"\"Can you please just accept this and move on?\"","then":"The sharper tone surprises him, and dinner turns a bit tense."}],"why":"Staying steady, dinner after dinner, is what finally settles the question for good."}],"relearn":"A warm, firm, repeatable line ends the pressure without a fight, every time."},
  {"id":"fm-071","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a tool that protects the relationship, or a habit that quietly damages it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Agreeing your stance privately first"},{"id":"b","text":"Venting about in-laws to everyone but them"},{"id":"c","text":"A warm, clear 'no'"},{"id":"d","text":"Silent resentment that builds for months"},{"id":"e","text":"Backing each other in the room"},{"id":"f","text":"Cutting them off at the first clash"}],"bins":[{"id":"tool","label":"Protective tool","valence":"pos"},{"id":"damage","label":"Quietly damaging","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"tool","b":"damage","c":"tool","d":"damage","e":"tool","f":"damage"},"relearn":"Aligning privately, the warm no and team-backing protect the bond; gossip, silent resentment and snap cut-offs corrode it.","persona":"any","source":"tool or damage"},
  {"id":"fm-072","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"There's nothing you can actually do about family dynamics, you just endure them.\"","myth":{"un":"There's nothing you can actually do about family dynamics, you just endure them.","re":"You have real tools, alignment, kind boundaries, repeatable lines and help routes, that change the dynamic over time.","why":"Family dynamics are workable, not just endurable."},"relearn":"You're not powerless; alignment, boundaries, lines and help routes genuinely shift family dynamics over time.","persona":"any","source":"not powerless"},
  {"id":"fm-073","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can't control how relatives act, but you can control your alignment, your boundaries and your warmth. Enough to start?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Enough","I can do that","Starting there"],"affirm":"You can't control relatives, but your alignment, boundaries and warmth are yours, and they're enough to start.","relearn":"Control what's yours, alignment, boundaries and warmth; that's enough to change the dynamic.","persona":"any","source":"what's yours"},
  {"id":"fm-074","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"team debrief","hook":"After a tense family event, you and your partner feel raw and disconnected.","steps":[{"prompt":"You get home and the car ride has been silent. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Suggest debriefing gently, asking what worked and what didn't","then":"Your partner exhales, relieved someone opened the door to talk.","best":true},{"text":"Wait for your partner to bring it up first","then":"Neither of you says anything, and the tension just sits there."},{"text":"Suggest just going to bed and forgetting about it until morning","then":"The tiredness wins for tonight, but the rawness lingers into tomorrow."},{"text":"Start listing what your partner did wrong tonight","then":"Your partner gets defensive, and the raw evening turns into a fight."}],"why":"Opening the debrief gently invites repair instead of more silence."},{"prompt":"The next day, your partner brings up a moment that caught them off guard the night before. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Listen fully, and ask what could help next time","then":"Your partner feels heard, and the two of you land on a small plan.","best":true},{"text":"Agree quickly just to smooth things over","then":"The quick agreement skips past what your partner actually needed to say."},{"text":"Change the subject to something lighter","then":"Your partner's feeling goes unaddressed, even though the mood lifts briefly."},{"text":"Point out a moment where you felt caught off guard too","then":"The conversation turns into a tally of who had it worse."}],"why":"Listening and asking what helps turns one hard night into a real fix."},{"prompt":"A similar family event is coming up next month. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Suggest agreeing a plan together, and a check-in before it","then":"You both walk in steadier, knowing what you'd each do.","best":true},{"text":"Assume you'll both just know what to do","then":"The old, tense pattern from last time slips back in."},{"text":"Avoid mentioning the earlier event at all","then":"The lesson from last time gets lost, unused."},{"text":"Worry out loud, more than once, that it'll go just as badly as before","then":"Your partner starts the day anxious instead of steady."}],"why":"Agreeing a plan and checking in again makes the repair actually stick."}],"debrief":"Debriefing kindly after family events repairs the couple and builds skill for next time.","relearn":"Debrief as a team after tense events; blame just imports the family's tension into the couple."},
  {"id":"fm-075","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match a tricky moment to the tool that fits it.","pairs":[{"left":"Unsolicited advice","right":"\"Thanks, we'll think it over.\""},{"left":"A surprise demand","right":"\"We'll decide together and tell you.\""},{"left":"A repeating nosy question","right":"One warm, repeatable line"},{"left":"A heated couple disagreement","right":"Settle it privately first"},{"left":"Signs of control or threats","right":"Reach 181 or a counsellor"}],"relearn":"Each moment has a tool: a warm deflect, the couple line, a repeatable line, private alignment, or a help route.","persona":"Reshma","source":"moment to tool"},
  {"id":"fm-076","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that won't sustain a lifelong family relationship.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Small, warm boundaries kept consistently","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"One huge confrontation to 'sort it all out'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Aligning as a couple again and again","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Going cold and silent for weeks at a time","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Assuming good intent where you can","trick":false}],"why":"One big blow-up and long cold silences rarely fix a lifelong relationship; consistent boundaries and alignment do.","relearn":"Sustainable family navigation is consistent small boundaries and alignment, not one big confrontation or going cold.","persona":"Sneha","source":"sustainable approach"},
  {"id":"fm-077","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you've mapped the web, learned the warm 'no', and the team line. The family map is yours now. Ready to use it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Ready","It's mine now","Let's go"],"affirm":"With the web mapped, the warm no, the couple line and the help routes, you can navigate family with confidence.","relearn":"You hold the family map now: the web, the warm no, the couple line and help routes.","persona":"any","source":"the map is yours"},
  {"id":"fm-078","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"the one principle","hook":"You want one principle to carry into every family situation from here.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks what rule you'd both actually want to live by. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say: honour them, protect us, and get help if it turns harmful","then":"Your partner nods slowly. 'That actually covers everything,' they say.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll just handle each situation as it comes","then":"Without a shared rule, each new situation gets solved from scratch."},{"text":"Say you'll try to please everyone, always","then":"Your partner looks uneasy, sensing that goal will wear you both down."},{"text":"Say the rule should really just be to keep family at more of a distance","then":"Your partner worries the rule leans too far the other way."}],"why":"One shared principle gives you both a compass instead of starting fresh each time."},{"prompt":"A tricky moment with family comes up soon after. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Talk it through together and decide out loud, as a team","then":"It settles the moment quickly, and you both feel steadier for it.","best":true},{"text":"Improvise alone, without checking in with your partner","then":"You wing it, and good intentions don't quite turn into an answer."},{"text":"React the way you normally would","then":"The old, reactive pattern shows up instead of a calmer one."},{"text":"Protect the two of you so hard that you shut family out entirely","then":"Family feels pushed away, more than the moment called for."}],"why":"Talking it through together, out loud, turns an idea into a habit."},{"prompt":"Months later, a friend asks how you two deal with family pressure. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Offer them this rule: honour them, protect us, get help if it turns harmful","then":"The friend writes it down, wanting to try it in their own family.","best":true},{"text":"Say you just got lucky with an easier situation","then":"The friend assumes it won't work for their harder situation."},{"text":"Say you don't really have a system, and it just works out","then":"The friend leaves without anything useful to actually try."},{"text":"Say it took a lot of hard fights to get here","then":"The friend feels discouraged before even trying the idea."}],"why":"Putting it in one clear line makes it easy to pass on."}],"debrief":"One principle carries the map: honour them, protect the couple, and get help if it turns harmful.","relearn":"Lead with warmth and clarity: honour them, protect us, get help if it turns harmful."},
  {"id":"fm-079","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"set the tone","hook":"You and your partner have never quite agreed on an approach to family visits.","setup":"You sit down together to talk it through.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"How do you want us to handle my family?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's stay warm with them, decide the big things together, and back each other up.\"","then":"Your partner smiles. \"I like that. Let's actually do that,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's just figure it out as things come up, I guess, without much of a plan.\"","then":"Your partner agrees, though nothing specific actually gets decided."},{"text":"\"Whatever you think is best, honestly.\"","then":"Your partner feels like they're carrying the plan alone."},{"text":"\"Let's just keep them at more of a distance.\"","then":"Your partner looks uneasy, worried it's more than they wanted."}],"why":"Setting a shared tone, warm and aligned, gives you both something concrete to hold onto."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, a decision about a family visit comes up. Your partner asks: \"So how do we handle this one?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's decide this together first, then tell them as one.\"","then":"Your partner nods. \"Right, let's do that now,\" they say, already settling in to talk.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's just wing this one. It's not a big deal.\"","then":"You end up deciding it separately, and the old pattern creeps back."},{"text":"\"You handle this one. I trust your judgement.\"","then":"Your partner ends up deciding alone, which isn't really what either of you wants."},{"text":"\"Let's just say no to everything from now on, to keep it simple.\"","then":"Your partner feels the tone has shifted colder than intended."}],"why":"Deciding together first, then speaking as one, keeps you a team."},{"prompt":"Months later, your partner asks: \"What do you think we should keep doing with my family?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Deciding together first, then telling them as one.\"","then":"Your partner grins. \"Yes, that's it. Together first, then them,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"\"I guess things mostly settle down on their own, somehow.\"","then":"Your partner isn't sure whether there's a plan or it's just chance."},{"text":"\"Let's see how the next visit goes.\"","then":"Your partner nods, though nothing specific is agreed for next time."},{"text":"\"Mostly I keep quiet whenever your family starts in.\"","then":"Your partner looks stung, unsure whether that was a fact or a complaint."}],"why":"Naming what helps, and choosing to keep it, keeps you both using it."}],"relearn":"Setting a shared tone, warm, aligned, mutually backed, beats lurching from blow-up to blow-up."},
  {"id":"fm-080","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two responses that drop your voice instead of keeping it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Thank you, we'll weigh it and decide for ourselves.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Obeying silently to avoid any friction","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Listening fully, then making your own call","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Saying a yes you don't mean to keep the peace","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"With respect, we see this differently.\"","trick":false}],"why":"Silent obedience and an unmeant yes drop your voice; thanking, listening and disagreeing kindly honour the elder and keep it.","relearn":"Honour the elder and keep your voice; silent obedience isn't the only respectful option.","persona":"Sneha","source":"honour and voice","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-081","type":"sort","persona":"Reshma","source":"couple or help","hook":"Sort: handle as a couple, or this needs outside help now?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Mismatched expectations about visits"},{"id":"b","text":"Threats to your safety"},{"id":"c","text":"Tense but ordinary disagreements"},{"id":"d","text":"Money demanded with intimidation"},{"id":"e","text":"Deciding how to split festival time"},{"id":"f","text":"Being cut off from your own family"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"Handle as a couple","valence":"pos"},{"id":"help","label":"Needs help now","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"help","c":"couple","d":"help","e":"couple","f":"help"},"relearn":"Visit mismatches, ordinary tension and festival timing are couple-level; threats, intimidating demands and isolation need help now.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-082","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"role-play","persona":"Razia","source":"shut down dowry","hook":"A relative has a habit of hinting for more than what's given.","setup":"He brings it up again, casually, over tea.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"Your family will keep sending nice things now and then, won't they?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Dowry is illegal. Our families won't be doing that.\"","then":"He raises his eyebrows, then quietly changes the subject.","best":true},{"text":"\"Sorry. I hope we haven't seemed ungrateful.\"","then":"He smiles. \"Not at all. Just don't forget us at Diwali.\""},{"text":"\"That's not really something I want to discuss.\"","then":"He hears it as discomfort rather than a clear no."},{"text":"\"I don't know. That's between the families.\"","then":"He treats it as a question still open to negotiation."}],"why":"Naming dowry as illegal and declining plainly shuts the demand down without leaving a door open."},{"prompt":"A little later, he says: \"It's just a small tradition, nothing serious.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Small or not, it's still illegal, and we're not doing it.\"","then":"He shrugs, seeing the answer isn't going to change.","best":true},{"text":"\"I suppose a little tradition never hurt anyone.\"","then":"He brightens, hearing exactly the opening he wanted."},{"text":"\"I hear you, but let's not talk about it today.\"","then":"He assumes today just isn't the right day to push, not that the answer is no."},{"text":"\"You should really ask my parents directly about that.\"","then":"He says he might just do that, and keeps sitting right where he is."}],"why":"Holding firm and naming the law closes the 'just a tradition' reframing he's trying."},{"prompt":"That evening, your partner asks: \"Was my uncle on about gifts again?\"","options":[{"text":"\"He was. Let's say no together, and ask a counsellor.\"","then":"He nods. \"Together. I'll find someone we can call this week.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It was nothing. I don't want trouble with your family.\"","then":"He hesitates. \"Okay. But tell me if he keeps pushing.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe a few gifts are just how families show respect.\"","then":"He shrugs. \"Maybe. I don't want a fight with him either.\""},{"text":"\"I can handle him myself. You stay out of it.\"","then":"He looks unsure. \"If you're sure. I'd rather we did it together.\""}],"why":"Bringing your partner in means you don't face it alone, and none of this is your fault."}],"relearn":"Naming dowry as illegal shuts it down; it's never your fault, and a counsellor or 181 can help if it doesn't stop."},
  {"id":"fm-083","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a green-flag family habit to keep, or a pattern to change?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Talking issues through early and kindly"},{"id":"b","text":"Letting things fester silently"},{"id":"c","text":"Backing each other in the room"},{"id":"d","text":"Competing over whose family is 'right'"},{"id":"e","text":"Debriefing gently after a tense visit"},{"id":"f","text":"Keeping score of every past slight"}],"bins":[{"id":"keep","label":"Green-flag habit","valence":"pos"},{"id":"change","label":"Pattern to change","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"keep","b":"change","c":"keep","d":"change","e":"keep","f":"change"},"relearn":"Talking early, backing each other and debriefing are habits to keep; festering, family-vs-family scorekeeping and slight-counting need changing.","persona":"any","source":"keep or change"},
  {"id":"fm-084","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that slowly poison a lifelong family bond.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Repairing quickly after a clash","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Keeping score of every past slight","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Assuming good intent where you can","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Bringing up old grudges in every fight","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Saying sorry first when you've slipped","trick":false}],"why":"Scorekeeping and dragging up old grudges slowly poison the bond; quick repair and good-faith assumptions sustain it.","relearn":"Quick repair and good-faith assumptions sustain family bonds; keeping score and reviving old grudges erode them.","persona":"Pooja","source":"keep it healthy"},
  {"id":"fm-900","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If your mother and your wife clash, a good son backs his mother every time.\"","myth":{"un":"If your mother and your wife clash, a good son backs his mother every time.","re":"You're not picking a winner; you and your partner sort it as a team, then honour your mother kindly.","why":"Standing with your partner isn't disrespect to a parent."},"relearn":"You don't 'back' one over the other; the couple decides together, then honours the elder.","persona":"Karthik","source":"not a winner"},
  {"id":"fm-901","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Whatever the elders decide for the couple, the couple should just accept.\"","myth":{"un":"Whatever the elders decide for the couple, the couple should just accept.","re":"Elders' wishes are heard with respect, but the couple makes the final call on its own life together.","why":"The couple is the unit that lives with the decision."},"relearn":"Hear the elders, then the couple decides; respect doesn't mean handing over the final call.","persona":"any","source":"final call"},
  {"id":"fm-902","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A wife who backs her husband against his relatives is turning him against family.\"","myth":{"un":"A wife who backs her husband against his relatives is turning him against family.","re":"Backing your partner warmly in a tense room is being a team, not turning anyone against family.","why":"A united couple lowers conflict instead of stoking it."},"relearn":"Backing your partner is teamwork, not poisoning them against family.","persona":"any","source":"not poison"},
  {"id":"fm-903","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"It's fine to vent about your partner to your parents; they're family.\"","myth":{"un":"It's fine to vent about your partner to your parents; they're family.","re":"Routine venting to your parents hands them a one-sided story and quietly recruits them against your partner.","why":"Family then 'defends' you and the couple gets cornered."},"relearn":"Take couple problems to your partner first, not a running complaint to your parents.","persona":"Pooja","source":"venting recruits"},
  {"id":"fm-904","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A good daughter-in-law never disagrees with her husband in front of his family.\"","myth":{"un":"A good daughter-in-law should silently agree with whatever her husband says before his family.","re":"You can flag a disagreement gently in the room, or save it for later; silence isn't the only respectful option.","why":"A team has two voices, not one speaking for both."},"relearn":"You don't have to vanish your view in front of family; team it up, openly or later.","persona":"Pooja","source":"two voices"},
  {"id":"fm-905","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If relatives are around, a husband should let his wife handle them alone; it's her job.\"","myth":{"un":"If relatives are around, a husband should let his wife handle them alone; it's her job to manage his family.","re":"His family is a shared web you both joined; he stands beside her, he doesn't outsource the relatives to her.","why":"Both partners joined each other's families."},"relearn":"Managing his relatives isn't her solo job; he's beside her as a team.","persona":"Karthik","source":"not outsourced"},
  {"id":"fm-906","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Once you've decided something as a couple, you can still quietly undo it to please a relative.\"","myth":{"un":"Once you've decided something as a couple, you can still quietly undo it to please a relative.","re":"Quietly reversing a shared decision behind your partner's back breaks the trust the team runs on.","why":"A team needs the answer to stay the answer."},"relearn":"Don't secretly undo a joint decision to please family; reopen it together if needed.","persona":"any","source":"stays the answer"},
  {"id":"fm-907","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Letting your mother set the rules for your home keeps the peace.\"","myth":{"un":"Letting your mother set the rules for your home keeps the peace.","re":"The couple sets the rhythm of its own home; a peace built on handing that over breeds quiet resentment.","why":"Resentment is louder later than a kind boundary now."},"relearn":"The couple runs its own home; peace by surrender just stores up resentment.","persona":"Pooja","source":"own rhythm"},
  {"id":"fm-908","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A real man doesn't 'check with the wife'; he just decides.\"","myth":{"un":"A real man doesn't 'check with the wife' in front of family; he just decides on the spot.","re":"'Let us talk and get back to you' is strength, not weakness; deciding together is what a team does.","why":"Solo on-the-spot calls leave a partner exposed."},"relearn":"'We'll discuss and get back' is the strong move, not deciding alone to look decisive.","persona":"Karthik","source":"strong move"},
  {"id":"fm-909","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If your partner and your sister fall out, stay neutral and stay out of it.\"","myth":{"un":"If your partner and your sister fall out, the safest thing is to stay neutral and stay out of it.","re":"Silent 'neutrality' leaves your partner alone in the room; you can be fair to your sister and still stand with your partner.","why":"Fairness and teamwork aren't opposites."},"relearn":"Don't hide behind 'neutral'; back your partner while being fair to your sister.","persona":"Karthik","source":"not neutral"},
  {"id":"fm-910","cat":"couple-team","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Whoever's parents we're visiting, that partner gets to decide everything that week.\"","myth":{"un":"Whoever's parents we're visiting, that partner gets to decide everything that visit.","re":"You're a team in both houses; you still align as a couple even when you're guests in one side's home.","why":"Being on home turf doesn't dissolve the team."},"relearn":"Visiting one side's parents doesn't hand that partner solo control; you stay a team.","persona":"any","source":"both houses"},
  {"id":"fm-911","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"money wedge","hook":"Over chai, your father seems to be building up to something about Divya.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says, 'She does like to shop.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say warmly, 'We manage our money together, Papa'","then":"Your father nods slowly and moves on to another topic.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh it off without correcting him","then":"He takes the laugh as agreement and repeats the comment later."},{"text":"Grumble along a little to bond with him","then":"He brightens and starts listing more things Divya buys."},{"text":"Defend her sharply, calling the comment unfair","then":"The chai turns awkward and your father goes quiet, hurt."}],"why":"A warm, united answer closes the wedge without a fight."},{"prompt":"A week later he brings it up again, asking to see your bank statement. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decline kindly: 'That's between us, Papa. We're fine'","then":"He looks a little put out, but doesn't push further.","best":true},{"text":"Show him a summary to prove there's no problem","then":"He studies it and finds one more thing to comment on."},{"text":"Get defensive and ask why he doesn't trust you","then":"The conversation turns tense and nobody feels heard."},{"text":"Agree just to end the awkward moment","then":"He takes it as an invitation to ask again next month."}],"why":"Keeping finances private protects the couple's space without disrespect."},{"prompt":"That evening Divya asks if your father has said anything about her spending. What do you tell her?","options":[{"text":"Tell her honestly, and how you handled it","then":"Divya relaxes. 'Thanks for telling me straight.'","best":true},{"text":"Say it's nothing to avoid upsetting her","then":"She later hears it from someone else and feels kept in the dark."},{"text":"Suggest she spend a bit less just to be safe","then":"Divya feels blamed for a comment she never even heard."},{"text":"Joke about it to lighten the mood","then":"The joke lands oddly and Divya isn't sure how serious it was."}],"why":"Honesty with your partner keeps trust intact, even about small comments."},{"prompt":"At the next family gathering your father tries a lighter version of the comment in front of relatives. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Smile and say, 'We've got it handled, Papa'","then":"The relatives move on, and Divya catches your eye, relieved.","best":true},{"text":"Change the subject without addressing it","then":"It works this time, but the comment lingers unanswered."},{"text":"Stay quiet, hoping it passes unnoticed","then":"Divya feels exposed, unsure if you'll speak up at all."},{"text":"Correct him firmly, right there at the table","then":"The room goes still, and your father looks embarrassed publicly."}],"why":"A light, firm close protects your partner in the room without a scene."}],"debrief":"Decline the wedge kindly; money is a couple matter, not a thing to bond over by complaining.","relearn":"Refuse the money wedge: close it warmly, and keep the couple's finances the couple's."},
  {"id":"fm-912","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"baby question","hook":"At a cousin's wedding, relatives ask loudly when you two will finally have a baby.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt asks first, in front of the whole table. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'That's our decision, in our own time'","then":"Your aunt nods and moves the conversation to the food.","best":true},{"text":"Say 'soon, hopefully' to end it quickly","then":"Your aunt takes it as a promise and mentions it again later."},{"text":"Joke that your partner is holding things up","then":"Your partner forces a smile, but looks uncomfortable beside you."},{"text":"Say nothing and change the subject yourself","then":"The silence feels awkward and someone else repeats the question."}],"why":"One calm, shared line keeps the decision yours and ends the pressure kindly."},{"prompt":"Later, another relative catches you both together and asks again. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say together: 'This is our decision, in our own time.'","then":"The relative shrugs, satisfied it's a shared answer, not a dodge.","best":true},{"text":"Give a softer, different answer this time","then":"The two answers don't quite match, and word gets around."},{"text":"Say 'ask my partner, not me'","then":"Your partner is put on the spot alone, and the pressure doubles."},{"text":"Get visibly annoyed at being asked again","then":"The annoyance becomes the story relatives tell each other later."}],"why":"Answering together, plainly, shows the couple decides this, not the room."},{"prompt":"At the next family function, a different relative tries the question again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer together, calmly, once more","then":"The relative gets the message and stops asking, for now.","best":true},{"text":"Finally explain your actual plans to make them stop","then":"Explaining opens the door to more questions about the details."},{"text":"Avoid the function next time to dodge the topic","then":"Skipping events doesn't stop the question. It just delays it."},{"text":"Snap that it's nobody's business","then":"The sharp tone becomes gossip faster than the original question did."}],"why":"A steady, repeated line closes the topic without needing anger or an explanation."}],"debrief":"Kids are the couple's call; give one calm, shared answer rather than splitting.","relearn":"On having children, present one united answer; it's the couple's decision, not the room's."},
  {"id":"fm-913","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"side channel","hook":"Your sister calls your partner directly to change a plan the two of you already agreed on.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner tells you about the call. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say, 'Let's call her back together. We decided this as a couple.'","then":"Your sister is surprised, but agrees to talk it through with both of you.","best":true},{"text":"Let your partner handle it alone since they took the call","then":"Your partner handles it alone; your sister calls again a few days later, wanting you both this time."},{"text":"Call your sister yourself and leave your partner out","then":"Your partner feels sidelined, and your sister later calls back with your partner included too."},{"text":"Ignore it and hope the original plan just stays","then":"Your sister assumes silence means the new version is fine, then calls again to confirm with you both."}],"why":"Looping in together closes the side-channel and keeps the plan the couple's."},{"prompt":"Your sister raises the change again, this time with both of you on the line. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Step in evenly: 'We talked it over. We're keeping the plan'","then":"Your sister hears one clear answer from both of you and lets it go.","best":true},{"text":"Let your partner answer alone, to avoid seeming controlling","then":"Your sister keeps pressing your partner, who starts to waver."},{"text":"Interrupt and answer for your partner every time","then":"Your partner feels talked over instead of backed up."},{"text":"Agree to think about the change to end the call","then":"Your sister calls again next week, expecting movement."}],"why":"Answering evenly as a pair shows this decision belongs to both of you."},{"prompt":"A month later your sister tries the same move, calling your partner about a different plan. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask her kindly to loop you both in from now on","then":"Your sister agrees, a little sheepish, and starts including you both.","best":true},{"text":"Let it slide again since it's a small plan this time","then":"The pattern continues, one small plan after another."},{"text":"Get short with your sister about calling separately","then":"The sharp reply makes future conversations tenser than needed."},{"text":"Tell your partner to just stop answering her calls","then":"Your partner feels caught between you and your sister."}],"why":"Naming the pattern kindly, once, stops the side-channel for good."}],"debrief":"Close side-channels by looping in as a couple; don't renegotiate solo.","relearn":"Bring back-channel asks back to the couple together, rather than renegotiating solo."},
  {"id":"fm-914","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"exposed at dinner","hook":"At dinner your mother-in-law corrects your partner sharply, and the table goes quiet.","steps":[{"prompt":"Everyone's eyes turn to you. What do you do first?","options":[{"text":"Step in gently for your partner, and talk to her later privately","then":"Your partner exhales, and the table relaxes as dinner moves on.","best":true},{"text":"Stay quiet so you don't upset your mother-in-law at the table","then":"Your partner sits through the rest of dinner feeling exposed."},{"text":"Correct her back sharply, right at the table","then":"Your mother-in-law goes stiff and dinner turns cold and formal."},{"text":"Change the subject loudly to move past it","then":"The topic shift works, but your partner still feels unbacked."}],"why":"Backing your partner gently now keeps them from feeling alone at the table."},{"prompt":"After dinner, you find a quiet moment with your mother-in-law. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say gently: 'I know you mean well. Can we talk about it?'","then":"She softens. 'I didn't mean it that way. Tell me.'","best":true},{"text":"Avoid the topic entirely, just to keep the peace tonight","then":"The same kind of correction happens again before long."},{"text":"List everything she's ever said wrong","then":"She feels attacked and the conversation turns defensive fast."},{"text":"Ask your partner to talk to her instead","then":"Your partner feels like they have to fight their own battle alone."}],"why":"A gentle, direct talk protects the elder's dignity while naming the real issue."},{"prompt":"That night, your partner brings up how dinner felt. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Ask what would help most next time, and offer a quick line","then":"Your partner feels heard, and you both feel readier for next time.","best":true},{"text":"Say you didn't want to embarrass your mother-in-law in front of everyone","then":"Your partner understands, but still feels the sting of it."},{"text":"Say they're being too sensitive about one comment","then":"Your partner goes quiet, feeling dismissed on top of everything."},{"text":"Promise to always take your partner's side from now on","then":"The promise feels big and vague, and neither of you quite trusts it."}],"why":"Asking what would help, then offering something concrete, turns the moment into a plan."},{"prompt":"At the next family dinner, your mother-in-law starts to correct your partner again, more gently. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Speak up warmly: 'We're happy with how we do it, Ma'","then":"She nods, and the moment passes without anyone feeling small.","best":true},{"text":"Wait to see if she stops on her own","then":"She continues, and your partner tenses up again, waiting."},{"text":"Laugh it off to keep things light","then":"The laugh doesn't quite land, and your partner feels unsupported."},{"text":"Leave the table quietly until the moment fully passes","then":"Your partner is left to handle it alone, again."}],"why":"A ready, warm line protects your partner in the moment, every time it's needed."}],"debrief":"Back your partner in the room, and handle the elder privately and kindly.","relearn":"Don't leave your partner alone under fire; back them now, then address the elder kindly later."},
  {"id":"fm-915","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"dont tell","hook":"A cousin pulls you aside with, 'don't tell your partner, but...' and a juicy family complaint.","steps":[{"prompt":"The cousin waits, expecting you to lean in. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'We don't keep these from each other. Tell us both'","then":"The cousin backs off, a little surprised, and doesn't share it after all.","best":true},{"text":"Listen quietly, planning to decide later whether to share it","then":"You end up holding a secret you didn't ask for."},{"text":"Say you're not interested in gossip at all","then":"The cousin feels shut down and starts avoiding you a little."},{"text":"Accept the secret to hear what's going on","then":"You now know something your partner doesn't, and it sits uneasily."}],"why":"Declining the secret up front keeps the couple from being split by gossip."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, the same cousin tries the 'don't tell your partner' line again, about something new. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say kindly: 'Honestly, we don't do secrets, sorry'","then":"The cousin lets it go, a little disappointed but respectful.","best":true},{"text":"Agree just this once since they seem upset","then":"One exception becomes an opening for the next secret too."},{"text":"Ask them to tell your partner themselves instead","then":"The cousin never does, and the secret just sits unresolved."},{"text":"Change the subject without answering clearly","then":"The cousin assumes you'll come back and ask to hear it later."}],"why":"A clear, kind no closes the door instead of leaving it half open."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks if anything's been going on with your cousin lately. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell them plainly what the cousin tried","then":"Your partner laughs. 'Good to know. Thanks for telling me.'","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing happened, since it felt small","then":"Your partner later hears about it from someone else and feels odd."},{"text":"Make it sound like a bigger deal than it was","then":"Your partner gets more worried about the cousin than the situation needs."},{"text":"Ask your partner what they think the secret might be","then":"You spend the evening guessing instead of just letting it go."}],"why":"Telling it plainly, even lightly, keeps no secrets between the two of you either."}],"debrief":"'Don't tell your partner' is triangulation bait; keep no family secrets from your team.","relearn":"Refuse 'keep this from your partner' deals; the couple shares family matters, not hides them."},
  {"id":"fm-916","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"booked trip","hook":"Your parents book a big family trip and assume you and Divya will both come, without asking first.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother calls, excited, to confirm the dates. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'Let us look at our dates and confirm together'","then":"Your mother sounds a little surprised, but says, 'Sure, let me know.'","best":true},{"text":"Say yes instantly so she isn't disappointed","then":"You commit Divya to dates she hasn't even seen yet."},{"text":"Say you'll come but Divya is 'probably busy'","then":"Your mother now expects to hear a firm no from Divya later."},{"text":"Avoid answering and let the call end unclear","then":"Your mother assumes silence means yes and books your seats."}],"why":"Checking with Divya first keeps the trip a shared decision, not a solo yes."},{"prompt":"That night you show Divya the dates. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Lay out the dates honestly and ask what she thinks","then":"Divya checks her calendar and you decide together, calmly.","best":true},{"text":"Say your mother will be upset if she says no","then":"Divya feels pressured into agreeing rather than choosing freely."},{"text":"Say you already told your mother it's likely a yes","then":"Divya feels the decision was made without her, even if unspoken."},{"text":"Suggest you go without her if dates don't work","then":"Divya feels like the plan matters more than doing it together."}],"why":"An honest, open ask lets Divya decide with you, not around her."},{"prompt":"Your mother calls again to settle the trip details. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call her back together and share your answer warmly","then":"Your mother is glad to hear from both of you, and the plans move forward.","best":true},{"text":"Let Divya handle the call alone","then":"Your mother assumes Divya is the reluctant one in the couple."},{"text":"Send a text instead of calling","then":"Your mother reads it flatly and calls back, a little hurt."},{"text":"Wait for her to ask again before replying","then":"The unanswered question makes her anxious about the booking."}],"why":"Delivering the answer together shows it was a shared decision, not a favour."}],"debrief":"Pause to align as a couple before committing, rather than an auto-yes for them.","relearn":"Big plans go through the couple first: 'we'll check and confirm together', warmly."},
  {"id":"fm-917","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"reinforcement","hook":"Mid-argument with your partner, your mother walks in from the next room.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says, 'I always said you were right about this!' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say, 'Thanks Ma, we'll sort this,' and step away","then":"Your mother looks a little surprised, then lets you two go.","best":true},{"text":"Let her keep talking while you both stay quiet","then":"Your partner feels outnumbered, two against one."},{"text":"Welcome her backup to win the argument","then":"The argument turns into you and your mother against your partner."},{"text":"Snap at her to leave, in front of your partner","then":"The sharp tone startles everyone, and the room turns awkward."}],"why":"Stepping away keeps the disagreement a couple matter, not a family front."},{"prompt":"Alone with your partner again, they say: 'See, this is what happens.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'You're right. Let's finish talking this through'","then":"Your partner relaxes slightly, and you get back to the real issue.","best":true},{"text":"Apologise for your mother but change the subject","then":"The apology helps, but the original disagreement stays unresolved."},{"text":"Defend your mother's intentions instead of the issue","then":"Your partner feels like the point is being missed entirely."},{"text":"Say nothing, hoping the moment just passes","then":"The silence leaves the argument hanging, unfinished."}],"why":"Returning to the real disagreement shows your partner it still matters to you."},{"prompt":"Later, you sit down with your mother about what happened earlier. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Explain that couple disagreements are something you two handle yourselves","then":"She nods slowly. 'Fair. I just want to help.'","best":true},{"text":"Say sorry without explaining why","then":"She's left unsure what she did wrong and may repeat it next time."},{"text":"Tell her she should never get involved, ever","then":"The harsh line makes her feel unwelcome rather than informed."},{"text":"Avoid the conversation and hope she forgets","then":"She brings it up again the next time you two argue."}],"why":"A kind, clear explanation teaches the boundary without pushing her away."}],"debrief":"Keep couple disagreements between the two of you, not a parent as reinforcement.","relearn":"Resolve couple conflicts privately; don't recruit a parent to outnumber your partner."},
  {"id":"fm-918","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"salary the family way","hook":"Your in-laws want your salary handled 'the family way'; your husband Aman looks at you, unsure.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your father-in-law says, 'In this house, the salary goes into one account.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'We'll talk about how we run our money and come back'","then":"He nods, expecting an answer soon, and the topic pauses.","best":true},{"text":"Agree on the spot so the in-laws are happy","then":"You've handed over a couple decision before talking to Aman at all."},{"text":"Say Aman decides these things, not you","then":"Aman is put on the spot alone, without your input beside him."},{"text":"Refuse outright, right there at the table","then":"Your father-in-law looks stung, and the room goes tense fast."}],"why":"Buying time together keeps the money decision the couple's, not the room's."},{"prompt":"Later, alone with Aman, he asks what you actually want to do. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Share your honest view and ask for his too","then":"You two land on a system you both feel good about, together.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll go along with whatever he prefers","then":"Aman decides alone, unsure if it's really what you wanted."},{"text":"Say his family's way is clearly wrong","then":"The conversation turns into criticising his parents instead of deciding."},{"text":"Avoid the topic since it feels awkward","then":"The unanswered question sits between you for days."}],"why":"An honest, two-way talk builds a system that's actually yours together."},{"prompt":"Your father-in-law brings up the salary account again over tea, wanting your answer. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Settle it with Aman, then tell his parents together, kindly","then":"They hear one calm answer from both of you and accept it.","best":true},{"text":"Let Aman tell them alone since it's his family","then":"His parents assume you had no real say in the decision."},{"text":"Avoid telling them and just do it your way quietly","then":"They find out later and feel it was hidden from them."},{"text":"Soften the answer so it sounds closer to what they wanted","then":"The vague answer leaves the door open for them to push again."}],"why":"A clear, shared answer settles the matter instead of leaving it half open."},{"prompt":"Months later, Aman says his mother raised the salary with him alone. He told her you two would talk it over. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank him, and agree how you'll both answer if it comes up again","then":"You both feel steady, ready to answer the same way as a team.","best":true},{"text":"Feel left out that he handled it without you","then":"The moment creates a small rift, even though nothing went wrong."},{"text":"Tell him he should have called you in right away","then":"Aman feels criticised for handling it fine on his own."},{"text":"Ask him to just avoid seeing her alone from now on","then":"The rule feels heavy and hard for Aman to actually keep."}],"why":"Backing each other's steady answers, even apart, keeps the team strong."}],"debrief":"How the couple runs its money is the couple's call; take it back to the team, kindly.","relearn":"Money systems are a couple decision; ask for time and decide together rather than surrender it."},
  {"id":"fm-919","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"comparison bait","hook":"A relative keeps comparing your partner unfavourably to your bhabhi, watching for your reaction.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says, 'Bhabhi manages the kitchen so well.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'We're happy with how we do things, thanks'","then":"She raises an eyebrow, then finally drops the comparison.","best":true},{"text":"Half agree to avoid seeming defensive","then":"She takes the half agreement as an opening to compare more."},{"text":"Compare your bhabhi back, to even the score","then":"The comparison turns into a small family rivalry you didn't want."},{"text":"Stay silent and let the comment sit","then":"The comment sits there, unanswered, and the silence feels heavier."}],"why":"Declining the comparison warmly ends the game without starting a rivalry."},{"prompt":"At the next gathering, she tries a new comparison, this time in front of your partner. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say lightly, 'We don't really compare. We just do us.'","then":"Your partner relaxes, hearing you close it without a fight.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh it off without saying anything to close it","then":"The laugh reads as agreement, and the comments keep coming."},{"text":"Pull your partner aside and complain about the relative","then":"Nothing changes at the gathering itself, just a private grumble."},{"text":"Tell your partner to ignore it. It's not a big deal","then":"Your partner feels like you're not really backing them up."}],"why":"Closing the comparison lightly, in the moment, protects your partner right there."},{"prompt":"Weeks later she brings it up again to someone else, and it gets back to you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Have a kind, direct word with her, just the two of you","then":"She's a little embarrassed, but the comparisons stop after that.","best":true},{"text":"Let it go since it wasn't said to your face this time","then":"The comments continue, just out of earshot now."},{"text":"Vent about her to other relatives","then":"The gossip spreads further and the original comment gets lost in it."},{"text":"Confront her loudly at the next big function","then":"The public confrontation embarrasses everyone, including your partner."}],"why":"A kind, private word addresses the real pattern without a public scene."}],"debrief":"Decline comparison bait warmly; don't half-agree against your partner.","relearn":"Shut down 'compare to bhabhi' bait kindly; back your partner instead of trading insults."},
  {"id":"fm-920","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"festival clash","hook":"Both sets of parents want you home for the same festival day, and each is calling you separately.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother calls first, assuming you're coming to her. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'Let me talk to my partner and we'll decide together'","then":"She's a little surprised, but agrees to wait for your answer.","best":true},{"text":"Say yes to her before hearing from your partner","then":"You've told her yes without checking first, and now need to loop your partner in."},{"text":"Say you'll try to split the day between both homes","then":"The rushed plan falls apart once the actual timings clash."},{"text":"Avoid answering until the other side calls too","then":"Both sides end up waiting, unsure what's actually happening."}],"why":"Deciding as a couple first keeps the plan honest instead of a rushed promise."},{"prompt":"You and your partner sit down to figure it out. What matters most in the decision?","options":[{"text":"What's fair to both families, decided together","then":"You land on a plan that feels balanced, and you both feel good about it.","best":true},{"text":"Whichever parents would be more upset if skipped","then":"The decision leans one way out of guilt, not real fairness."},{"text":"Whichever home has the better food and company","then":"It's an easy pick, but it quietly favours one side every year."},{"text":"Flip a coin to avoid an actual discussion","then":"The coin decides, but neither of you feels sure it was fair."}],"why":"Weighing fairness together makes the plan one you both actually stand behind."},{"prompt":"You've decided. What do you do next?","options":[{"text":"Each of you tells your own parents the same shared plan","then":"Both sides hear one consistent plan, and the day is settled calmly.","best":true},{"text":"Let one partner announce it to both families","then":"One side feels like the news came from 'the other family', not from you both."},{"text":"Wait for them to notice you haven't confirmed","then":"The delay makes both sides anxious and a little annoyed."},{"text":"Change the plan again if either side sounds upset","then":"The plan keeps shifting, and nobody feels sure what's actually happening."}],"why":"One consistent plan, told by each of you, ends the tug-of-war for good."}],"debrief":"Settle the clash as a couple, then both relay the same plan.","relearn":"Festival clashes: agree as a couple first, then each carries the shared answer to their parents."},
  {"id":"fm-921","cat":"couple-team","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"flat money","hook":"Your father offers you money for a flat, 'just between us, no need to tell Divya'.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says, 'This is from me to you. Keep it simple.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'That's so kind, Papa. Let's all talk it through'","then":"He pauses, then says, 'Alright, bring her along, no problem.'","best":true},{"text":"Take it quietly, planning to mention it later","then":"Weeks pass, and you still haven't found the right moment to tell her."},{"text":"Say yes and keep it to yourself as your own saving","then":"The money sits outside the couple's shared plans, unspoken."},{"text":"Refuse the money entirely without explaining why","then":"Your father feels hurt, unsure why his help was turned down."}],"why":"Including Divya keeps a big offer transparent instead of a private secret."},{"prompt":"At some point, you talk to Divya about the offer. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Share it fully, including the 'don't tell her' part","then":"Divya appreciates hearing it in full. 'Thanks for telling me.'","best":true},{"text":"Mention the offer but leave out the secrecy request","then":"Divya senses something's slightly off but can't say what."},{"text":"Ask her opinion before deciding how much to reveal","then":"The conversation starts on unsure footing, half information at a time."},{"text":"Downplay the amount so it seems like a small gesture","then":"Divya agrees based on a picture that isn't quite accurate."}],"why":"Telling the whole story, including the ask for secrecy, keeps trust intact."},{"prompt":"Your father brings up the money again and asks what you have decided. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decide together with Divya first, then tell your father","then":"He hears one clear answer from both of you and respects it.","best":true},{"text":"Let Divya tell him alone","then":"Your father assumes Divya is the one setting conditions on his gift."},{"text":"Keep the decision vague so it can change later","then":"Nobody, including your father, knows quite where things stand."},{"text":"Accept it fully without discussing terms together","then":"The money arrives, but the couple never actually discussed it."}],"why":"A shared decision, delivered together, keeps a generous offer from becoming a secret."}],"debrief":"Big money decisions run through the couple openly, never as a hidden side-deal.","relearn":"Keep family money offers transparent; the couple decides together, not in secret."},
  {"id":"fm-922","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each: a united-front move, or one that quietly undermines the couple?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Agreeing your answer privately before facing relatives"},{"id":"b","text":"Saying 'ask them, it's their fault' to relatives"},{"id":"c","text":"Backing your partner, debriefing later"},{"id":"d","text":"Promising a relative something behind their back"},{"id":"e","text":"Giving one calm answer together"},{"id":"f","text":"Letting family pit you two against each other"}],"bins":[{"id":"united","label":"United front","valence":"pos"},{"id":"undermine","label":"Undermines","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"united","b":"undermine","c":"united","d":"undermine","e":"united","f":"undermine"},"relearn":"Aligning, backing each other and one answer unite you; blame and secret deals undermine it.","persona":"any","source":"united or not 2"},
  {"id":"fm-923","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: which calls are the couple's own, and which are genuinely shared with elders?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Whether and when to have children"},{"id":"b","text":"Hosting the family's annual puja together"},{"id":"c","text":"How you two split money and chores"},{"id":"d","text":"Planning a big joint-family wedding"},{"id":"e","text":"Where the two of you live and work"},{"id":"f","text":"A shared ancestral-property arrangement"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"Couple's own call","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shared","label":"Share with elders","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"shared","c":"couple","d":"shared","e":"couple","f":"shared"},"relearn":"Kids, money/chores and where you live are the couple's; pujas, a joint wedding and shared property go with elders too.","persona":"Pooja","source":"team vs shared 2"},
  {"id":"fm-924","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each line: a team line that holds you together, or one that splits you?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"'We'll talk and get back to you together'"},{"id":"b","text":"'Honestly, blame, not me'"},{"id":"c","text":"'Let us decide this as a couple first'"},{"id":"d","text":"'Fine, whatever you all want is fine with me'"},{"id":"e","text":"'We're a team on this one'"},{"id":"f","text":"'Don't tell my partner, but I agree with you'"}],"bins":[{"id":"team","label":"Team line","valence":"pos"},{"id":"splits","label":"Splits the couple","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"team","b":"splits","c":"team","d":"splits","e":"team","f":"splits"},"relearn":"Lines that say 'we' and buy time keep the team; lines that blame your partner or cut a secret deal split you.","persona":"any","source":"team lines sort"},
  {"id":"fm-925","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a healthy way to handle a relative's complaint, or triangulation?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Bringing the complaint to your partner directly"},{"id":"b","text":"Agreeing with the relative behind their back"},{"id":"c","text":"Inviting you all to talk it out together"},{"id":"d","text":"Carrying secret messages between the sides"},{"id":"e","text":"Defending your partner kindly"},{"id":"f","text":"Letting the relative make you the go-between"}],"bins":[{"id":"healthy","label":"Healthy","valence":"pos"},{"id":"triangle","label":"Triangulation","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"healthy","b":"triangle","c":"healthy","d":"triangle","e":"healthy","f":"triangle"},"relearn":"Take complaints to your partner, talk openly, defend them; secret-siding and ferrying messages is triangulation.","persona":"any","source":"triangulation sort"},
  {"id":"fm-926","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a habit that keeps the couple solid in a big family, or one that wears it down?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A quick check-in before family events"},{"id":"b","text":"Springing surprise commitments on your partner"},{"id":"c","text":"Debriefing kindly after a tense visit"},{"id":"d","text":"Keeping a tally of who 'won' each clash"},{"id":"e","text":"Repeating one steady team line under pressure"},{"id":"f","text":"Going silent and stewing after a fight"}],"bins":[{"id":"keeps","label":"Keeps you solid","valence":"pos"},{"id":"wears","label":"Wears it down","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"keeps","b":"wears","c":"keeps","d":"wears","e":"keeps","f":"wears"},"relearn":"Check-ins, debriefs and a steady line keep you solid; ambushes, scorekeeping and silent stewing wear it down.","persona":"Karthik","source":"habits sort"},
  {"id":"fm-927","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: who hears it first when a family issue lands, partner or relative?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"You're upset by something your in-laws said"},{"id":"b","text":"Your aunt asks you to relay a complaint"},{"id":"c","text":"A big spending request from your parents"},{"id":"d","text":"Your cousin wants a secret about your partner"},{"id":"e","text":"A clash you feel about a ritual"},{"id":"f","text":"A relative baiting you to side against them"}],"bins":[{"id":"partner","label":"Partner first","valence":"pos"},{"id":"decline","label":"Decline / loop in","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"partner","b":"decline","c":"partner","d":"decline","e":"partner","f":"decline"},"relearn":"Hurt, big asks and ritual clashes go to your partner first; relayed complaints, secrets and side bait you decline.","persona":"Pooja","source":"who first sort"},
  {"id":"fm-928","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a kind united front, or a cold wall pretending to be one?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"'We've decided together, and we'd love your blessing'"},{"id":"b","text":"Stonewalling relatives with icy silence"},{"id":"c","text":"Holding the decision warmly, explaining once"},{"id":"d","text":"'Our way or we don't come at all'"},{"id":"e","text":"Honouring the elders while keeping your call"},{"id":"f","text":"Cutting off anyone who disagrees by default"}],"bins":[{"id":"kind","label":"Kind united front","valence":"pos"},{"id":"wall","label":"Cold wall","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"kind","b":"wall","c":"kind","d":"wall","e":"kind","f":"wall"},"relearn":"A united front holds the decision warmly and seeks blessing; icy silence, ultimatums and cutting people off are a cold wall.","persona":"any","source":"front vs wall"},
  {"id":"fm-929","cat":"couple-team","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each phone moment: as a couple, or react alone?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Saying 'let me check with my partner too' before agreeing"},{"id":"b","text":"Committing you both before asking your partner"},{"id":"c","text":"Telling a relative you'll both reply tomorrow"},{"id":"d","text":"Promising a yes to end the call fast"},{"id":"e","text":"Telling your partner about the call right after"},{"id":"f","text":"Hiding that the relative even called"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"As a couple","valence":"pos"},{"id":"alone","label":"React alone","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"alone","c":"couple","d":"alone","e":"couple","f":"alone"},"relearn":"Checking in, buying time and telling your partner keep calls shared; committing both or hiding the call cut them out.","persona":"any","source":"phone sort"},
  {"id":"fm-930","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each family-pressure moment to the couple-team response.","pairs":[{"left":"Relatives push a big decision on the spot","right":"'We'll talk and get back to you'"},{"left":"One partner is singled out at the table","right":"The other steps in to back them"},{"left":"Two families' plans clash on a date","right":"The couple agrees its own plan"},{"left":"A relative shares a 'don't tell' secret","right":"You decline, keeping no side-deals"},{"left":"Your parent vents about your partner","right":"You warmly close the wedge"}],"relearn":"The couple-team response: align, back each other, decline secrets, give one united answer.","persona":"any","source":"team responses 2"},
  {"id":"fm-931","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each couple-team signal to what it tells a partner.","pairs":[{"left":"A heads-up before a visit","right":"No ambush in the room"},{"left":"Sitting beside, not across","right":"We face family together"},{"left":"Saying 'we', not 'I'","right":"Decisions are ours"},{"left":"Defending them when absent","right":"Loyalty out of earshot"},{"left":"Naming their wins upward","right":"Proud of this team"}],"relearn":"Heads-ups, sitting together, saying 'we', defending them when absent and praising them upward are small signals that a partner isn't alone.","persona":"any","source":"team signals"},
  {"id":"fm-932","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each undermining move to the trust it damages.","pairs":[{"left":"Blaming your partner to relatives","right":"Their safety to be open with you"},{"left":"A secret deal behind their back","right":"Honesty the team runs on"},{"left":"Leaving them alone under fire","right":"The feeling you've got each other"},{"left":"Carrying messages between sides","right":"Being a couple, not a go-between"},{"left":"Auto-siding with your parents","right":"That the couple decides first"}],"relearn":"Blame, secrets, abandonment, message-carrying and parents-first each damage a specific thread of couple trust.","persona":"any","source":"undermine match"},
  {"id":"fm-933","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each tricky line a relative might say to a calm couple-team reply.","pairs":[{"left":"'Don't you agree your partner's too modern?'","right":"'I love that about them, actually'"},{"left":"'Decide right now, we can't wait'","right":"'We'll discuss and get back to you'"},{"left":"'Just keep this between us, beta'","right":"'We don't keep things from each other'"},{"left":"'Your bhabhi would never do that'","right":"'We're happy with our own way'"},{"left":"'Side with your own family, na'","right":"'We're a team; we'll sort it'"}],"relearn":"Each pressure line has a warm, united reply that backs your partner without a fight.","persona":"any","source":"reply match"},
  {"id":"fm-934","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each couple check-in question to what it sorts out before a family visit.","pairs":[{"left":"'What's the one thing we won't budge on?'","right":"Your shared non-negotiable"},{"left":"'What can we be flexible about?'","right":"Where you can happily give"},{"left":"'What's our line if they push kids?'","right":"A ready answer to the question"},{"left":"'Who steps in if one of us is cornered?'","right":"A plan to back each other"},{"left":"'When do we step out to regroup?'","right":"An exit to realign mid-visit"}],"relearn":"A quick pre-visit check-in sorts your non-negotiable, your flex and how you'll back each other.","persona":"Karthik","source":"checkin match"},
  {"id":"fm-935","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each scenario to whether it's the couple's call or a genuinely shared family decision.","pairs":[{"left":"When you two have children","right":"The couple decides, in its own time"},{"left":"Hosting the family's annual puja","right":"Planned together with the elders"},{"left":"How you split your salaries","right":"The couple's own money call"},{"left":"Planning a joint-family wedding","right":"Shared across both families"},{"left":"Where the two of you live","right":"Yours as a couple to decide"}],"relearn":"Kids, money and where you live are the couple's; pujas and joint weddings are shared with elders.","persona":"Pooja","source":"call match"},
  {"id":"fm-936","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each tense moment to the team move that defuses it.","pairs":[{"left":"A surprise demand at a function","right":"'We'll get back to you' together"},{"left":"A relative running your partner down","right":"A warm, firm defence"},{"left":"Being pulled into a secret","right":"Declining the side-deal"},{"left":"A clash blowing up at the table","right":"Stepping out to regroup privately"},{"left":"Both parents wanting the same day","right":"One agreed plan from the couple"}],"relearn":"Buy time, defend warmly, decline secrets, step out to regroup, and bring one agreed plan; each defuses a different squeeze.","persona":"any","source":"defuse match"},
  {"id":"fm-937","cat":"couple-team","type":"match","hook":"Match each 'choosing sides' worry to why standing as a team isn't that.","pairs":[{"left":"'Backing my wife betrays my mother'","right":"You can honour both at once"},{"left":"'A united front feels cold'","right":"It's warm; you still seek blessing"},{"left":"'Deciding together cuts elders out'","right":"You still hear their advice"},{"left":"'Defending my partner starts a war'","right":"It actually lowers the conflict"},{"left":"'Saying we'll decide later is rude'","right":"It's the kindest, steadiest reply"}],"relearn":"Standing as a team honours elders, stays warm, hears advice and lowers conflict; it isn't 'choosing sides'.","persona":"Karthik","source":"sides worry match"},
  {"id":"fm-938","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: deciding the big things together first, before looping anyone else in, is how a couple stays a team. Land for you?","prompt":"What's your read?","options":["Lands for me","Decide together first","Then loop family in"],"affirm":"Settling the major calls as a pair before involving relatives keeps the partnership steady and clear.","relearn":"A couple decides the big things together first, then loops family in; that order keeps the team strong.","persona":"any","source":"decide together first"},
  {"id":"fm-939","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'we'll decide together and let you know' might be the handiest line in a joint family. Worth memorising?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth it","I'll memorise it","So useful"],"affirm":"'We'll decide together and let you know' protects the couple-team kindly in almost any family pressure.","relearn":"One sentence carries the couple-team: 'we'll decide together and let you know.'","persona":"any","source":"useful sentence 2"},
  {"id":"fm-940","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: deciding privately, then giving family one calm answer, saves you both a hundred small fights. Notice that?","prompt":"How does that land?","options":["I notice it","Saves the fights","One calm answer"],"affirm":"Aligning in private and offering one calm answer spares the couple repeated public clashes.","relearn":"Decide privately, then present one calm answer; it heads off the small public fights.","persona":"any","source":"private then calm"},
  {"id":"fm-941","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: backing your partner in the room and being kind to your family aren't opposites. Ever felt forced to pick?","prompt":"Sit with it.","options":["Felt that pull","They aren't opposites","I can do both"],"affirm":"You can back your partner warmly and still honour your family; it was never a real either-or.","relearn":"Backing your partner and honouring family can both be true at once; it isn't either-or.","persona":"any","source":"not opposites"},
  {"id":"fm-942","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: refusing to carry secret messages between your partner and your family is a quiet act of loyalty. Agree?","prompt":"What comes up?","options":["I agree","Quiet loyalty","No more go-between"],"affirm":"Declining to be the family go-between keeps you a couple, not a messenger pulled both ways.","relearn":"Refusing to ferry secret messages keeps the couple a team instead of a divided go-between.","persona":"any","source":"go between reflect"},
  {"id":"fm-943","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a quick check-in before a family visit, even two minutes, can change the whole evening. Try it?","prompt":"Worth a try?","options":["I'll try it","Two minutes","Changes the evening"],"affirm":"A short pre-visit check-in aligns you both, so family pressure meets one steady couple, not a gap.","relearn":"A two-minute check-in before a visit aligns the couple and steadies the whole evening.","persona":"Karthik","source":"checkin reflect"},
  {"id":"fm-944","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: when family pressure rises, stepping out together to regroup isn't weakness, it's teamwork. Ring true?","prompt":"Does it ring true?","options":["Rings true","Regroup together","Not weakness"],"affirm":"Stepping out to regroup mid-pressure lets the couple realign and return as one, calm team.","relearn":"Stepping out together to regroup under pressure is a team move, not running away.","persona":"any","source":"regroup reflect"},
  {"id":"fm-945","cat":"couple-team","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: never making your partner choose publicly between you and their parents is its own kind of gift. Notice it?","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["I notice it","It's a gift","No public choosing"],"affirm":"Not forcing a public choice between you and their parents spares your partner an impossible bind.","relearn":"Don't make your partner publicly choose between you and their parents; spare them that bind.","persona":"any","source":"no public choosing"},
  {"id":"fm-946","cat":"couple-team","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"don't be recruited","hook":"An aunt corners you alone to ask you to 'talk sense into' your wife about her job.","setup":"She lowers her voice, like she's doing you a favour.","steps":[{"prompt":"Aunty says: \"Tell her to quit. A working wife is too much strain on a home.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We decided that together, Aunty. I'm glad she has it.\"","then":"Aunty pauses. \"Well, times have changed,\" she mutters.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll mention it to her. We'll see what happens.\"","then":"Aunty says: \"Good, and let me know when you do.\""},{"text":"\"Honestly, some days I wonder about it too.\"","then":"Aunty brightens. \"See, I knew you'd agree.\""},{"text":"\"I'll stay out of it. Raise it with her yourself.\"","then":"Aunty says: \"Nonsense, you're her husband,\" and keeps pressing."}],"why":"Naming the decision as shared and settled shuts down the ask cleanly."},{"prompt":"Aunty adds, testing another angle: \"Just think of the children someday. Who'll be home?\"","options":[{"text":"\"If and when we have children, we'll decide that together.\"","then":"Aunty says: \"Hmm, if you say so,\" unsure how to argue with that.","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. Someone would need to be home.\"","then":"Aunty says: \"Exactly my point,\" and keeps pushing her view."},{"text":"\"I'll talk to her about it and see what she says.\"","then":"Aunty says: \"Good, do that. Tell me what she says.\""},{"text":"\"That's her call to make, not mine.\"","then":"Aunty says: \"Then I'll ask her directly,\" heading that way."}],"why":"A calm, shared answer closes the topic without leaving room to keep pushing."},{"prompt":"At home your wife asks: \"Did aunty say anything about my job today?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, she raised it. I'll always tell you. It's ours to decide.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Thank you. I would rather hear it from you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing worth mentioning. Just the usual aunty chatter.\"","then":"She says: \"Are you sure? She seemed odd earlier.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe I'll just avoid her for a while until it blows over.\"","then":"She says: \"That won't stop her, honestly.\""},{"text":"\"She means well. Let's just let it go for now.\"","then":"She says: \"Let what go, exactly?\" a little unsure now."}],"why":"Telling her plainly about the ask keeps no secrets between you."}],"relearn":"Declining to be recruited against your partner, and naming that you decided together, shuts a wedge before it opens."},
  {"id":"fm-947","cat":"couple-team","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"buy time line","hook":"A big family plan comes up over dinner, and you and your wife haven't talked it over privately yet.","setup":"Everyone's looking at you two, waiting for an answer.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your uncle says: \"Come on, just say yes. It's a simple plan.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let us talk it through and get back to you.\"","then":"Uncle nods. \"Fair enough. Don't take too long.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I think it should be fine, probably.\"","then":"Uncle says: \"Good, I'll count on that then.\""},{"text":"\"Sure, fine, whatever you all think is best.\"","then":"Uncle beams, already picturing it moving ahead."},{"text":"\"Ask my wife. It's her call.\"","then":"Uncle turns to her. \"Well? What do you say?\""}],"why":"Asking for time together keeps the decision the couple's, not a rushed yes."},{"prompt":"Your wife whispers: \"What do you actually think about this plan?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Honestly, I'm unsure. Let's weigh it properly tonight.\"","then":"She nods. \"Good, let's actually look at it at home.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I don't want to disappoint them after all this.\"","then":"She says: \"That's not really an answer, is it?\""},{"text":"\"Let's just agree. It's easier.\"","then":"She says: \"Easier now, maybe not later.\""},{"text":"\"You decide. I'm fine either way.\"","then":"She says: \"I don't want to decide this alone.\""}],"why":"An honest answer, weighed properly, makes the eventual yes or no a real one."},{"prompt":"Back with the relatives, your uncle asks: \"So? What did you two decide?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll answer you together. Here's where we are.\"","then":"Uncle says: \"Good. Straight and clear. I like that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We're still not fully sure, sorry.\"","then":"Uncle sighs. \"Alright, I'll ask again next week, then.\""},{"text":"\"Whatever works best for the family, really.\"","then":"Uncle says: \"Perfect, I'll move ahead with plans.\""},{"text":"\"My wife will confirm later.\"","then":"Uncle turns to her. \"So it's on you now, is it?\""}],"why":"Answering together, and plainly, shows the family you are one team."}],"relearn":"'We'll talk and get back to you together' buys the couple time to decide as one team."},
  {"id":"fm-948","cat":"couple-team","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"decline grumble","hook":"Your father vents about your wife Divya to you after a long day.","setup":"He leans back, expecting you to join in.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"Honestly, she can be difficult, don't you think?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I hear you, Papa, but we sort our own things out together. We're good.\"","then":"He pauses. \"Fair enough, just checking on you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"She has her moments, I guess.\"","then":"He says: \"See, I'm not the only one who thinks so.\""},{"text":"\"Yeah, honestly, she does annoy me sometimes too, more than I let on.\"","then":"He lights up. \"Finally, someone who agrees with me.\""},{"text":"\"I don't want to talk about this, Papa.\"","then":"He says: \"Fine, fine,\" but looks a little stung."}],"why":"A warm, firm line refuses the wedge without dismissing your father."},{"prompt":"He continues: \"I just worry you're not being heard in that house.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's kind of you, but really, we talk things through fine.\"","then":"He softens. \"Okay, good to know you're both alright.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Sometimes, but it's not a big thing.\"","then":"He says: \"Sometimes is still something, no?\""},{"text":"\"You might be onto something there.\"","then":"He says: \"I knew it. Tell me more.\""},{"text":"\"Please stop worrying about my marriage so much, honestly.\"","then":"He says: \"I'm only trying to help,\" sounding hurt."}],"why":"Reassuring him warmly answers the worry without opening the door to more venting."},{"prompt":"That night Divya asks: \"Did your father say something about me again?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, he did. I'll tell you what he said, and what I said.\"","then":"Divya nods. \"Thanks for telling me straight.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's nothing. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"Divya says: \"Nothing sounds like something, honestly.\""},{"text":"\"He just worries too much. That's all.\"","then":"Divya says: \"About me, or about us?\""},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about your father tonight, please.\"","then":"Divya says: \"Okay,\" but stays a little unsettled."}],"why":"Telling Divya honestly, and what you said, keeps the couple's trust whole."},{"prompt":"At the next dinner, your father tries once more, quietly: \"Just between us, how are things really?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Really, we're good, Papa, and I mean that.\"","then":"He smiles, finally convinced. \"Good, that's all I wanted to hear.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"They're fine, mostly.\"","then":"He says: \"Mostly? Tell me the rest, then.\""},{"text":"\"Can we not do this every time I see you?\"","then":"He says: \"I was only checking on you,\" looking stung."},{"text":"\"Ask Divya, not me.\"","then":"He turns to her. \"Alright then, how are things?\""}],"why":"A clear, warm reassurance closes the pattern for good, kindly."}],"relearn":"Decline to grumble along; a warm 'we sort it together' refuses the wedge without snubbing your father."},
  {"id":"fm-949","cat":"couple-team","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"refuse secret line","hook":"A relative pulls you aside, wanting a favour done quietly.","setup":"They glance around, checking that nobody is listening.","steps":[{"prompt":"They say: \"This is just between us. Don't mention it to your partner.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We don't keep secrets. Let's talk together.\"","then":"They hesitate. \"Alright, fine, bring them in too, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Okay, tell me first and I'll see.\"","then":"They say: \"Good, now here's what I need.\""},{"text":"\"I'm not really comfortable with secrets.\"","then":"They say: \"Fine, forget I said anything,\" and back off."},{"text":"\"Fine, but just this once.\"","then":"They say: \"Perfect, I'll come to you again next time too.\""}],"why":"Declining the secret upfront keeps the couple from being pulled apart quietly."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, the same relative says: \"It's only a small thing. Why involve your partner at all?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Small or not, we share family matters together.\"","then":"They shrug. \"Alright, alright, let's bring them in.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine, I'll just handle it quietly this time.\"","then":"They say: \"Good, that's easier for everyone.\""},{"text":"\"You have a point. Maybe it's not a big deal.\"","then":"They say: \"Exactly, so let's keep it simple.\""},{"text":"\"I'll ask my partner not to mind.\"","then":"They say: \"See, that works fine too.\""}],"why":"Holding the same line, even under a 'small thing' excuse, keeps the couple a team."},{"prompt":"That evening your partner asks: \"Did something happen with them today? You seem quiet.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, actually, let me tell you what happened.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"Thank you for telling me straight away.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's nothing. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"You seem off, though. Are you sure?\""},{"text":"\"Just family stuff, boring really.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"What kind of family stuff, exactly?\""},{"text":"\"I'll tell you some other time.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"Alright, but don't forget, okay?\""}],"why":"Telling your partner straight away keeps the couple's trust whole."}],"relearn":"Refuse 'keep this from your partner' deals; the couple shares family matters, not hides them."},
  {"id":"fm-950","cat":"couple-team","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"step in line","hook":"Your mother-in-law corrects your partner sharply, and the table goes quiet.","setup":"All eyes turn to you, waiting to see what you'll say.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law says: \"That's not how it's done in this house.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We've actually talked about this and we're happy with how we do it.\"","then":"She pauses. \"Oh. Alright, then,\" she says, a little surprised.","best":true},{"text":"\"Everyone has their own way of doing things. That's alright too.\"","then":"She says: \"Well, my way has always worked fine,\" not fully satisfied."},{"text":"\"You're right. We'll do it your way from now on.\"","then":"Your partner says quietly: \"You didn't have to agree with her.\""},{"text":"\"Let's not make this a whole thing at the table.\"","then":"She says: \"I wasn't making it a thing,\" a little defensive."}],"why":"Naming it as a decision you've both already made backs your partner without disrespect."},{"prompt":"Later, alone with you, she says: \"I hope I didn't overstep earlier.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I know you care, Ma. We just do it our own way.\"","then":"She nods. \"Alright. I do still worry sometimes.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's fine. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"She says: \"I still feel bad about earlier, though.\""},{"text":"\"You embarrassed them in front of everyone.\"","then":"She says: \"I didn't mean to. I was only trying to help.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe don't comment at the table next time.\"","then":"She says: \"So I should just stay quiet, then?\""}],"why":"Acknowledging her care while holding your own way keeps the bond warm."},{"prompt":"That night, your partner says: \"I keep thinking about that moment at the table.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thanks for telling me. I've got your back at that table, every time.\"","then":"Your partner relaxes. \"That's all I needed to hear.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I was keeping the peace with Ma. It wasn't about you.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"I get that, but I still felt on my own.\""},{"text":"\"It was only a small comment. She didn't mean anything by it.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"Small to her, maybe. It wasn't small to me.\""},{"text":"\"You're overthinking this a bit.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"Maybe. Or maybe it just mattered to me.\""}],"why":"Promising your backing plainly, without excuses, tells your partner they are not alone."},{"prompt":"At the next dinner, your mother-in-law says gently to your partner: \"Just a small note. That's all.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're happy with how we do it, Ma, promise.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Alright, alright, fair enough.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's talk about it after dinner instead.\"","then":"She says: \"Fine, if that's how you'd like it.\""},{"text":"\"Here we go again.\"","then":"She says: \"I was only making a note,\" a little stung."},{"text":"\"It's fine, Ma. Say what you think.\"","then":"She says: \"Well, since you asked,\" and continues."}],"why":"A quick, warm line every time keeps your partner from feeling exposed again."}],"relearn":"A gentle 'we're happy with how we do it' backs your partner without disrespecting the elder."},
  {"id":"fm-951","cat":"couple-team","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"festival line","hook":"Both your parents and your in-laws are calling about the same festival day, each expecting you both.","setup":"Your phone buzzes again before you've even answered the last call.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother says: \"So you're coming to us this year, right?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're deciding. We'll confirm soon.\"","then":"She sounds unsure. \"Alright, let me know then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Probably, I think so.\"","then":"She says: \"Good, I'll tell everyone you're coming.\""},{"text":"\"Don't worry. I'll just come to you.\"","then":"She says: \"Perfect, we'll see you then.\""},{"text":"\"Let's see how the day goes.\"","then":"She says: \"That's not really an answer, is it?\""}],"why":"One honest, shared answer keeps both families waiting on the same true plan."},{"prompt":"Minutes later your partner's father calls: \"We're expecting you both, isn't it?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're deciding as a couple and we'll confirm with everyone.\"","then":"He says: \"Fair enough. Just don't leave it too late.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We'll split the day between both. I'll tell my partner later.\"","then":"He says: \"Wonderful, we'll expect you for the morning, then.\""},{"text":"\"Yes, of course, we'll be there.\"","then":"He says: \"Wonderful, I'll let everyone know you're both coming.\""},{"text":"\"Ask my partner. I'm not sure.\"","then":"He says: \"I'll just call them directly, then.\""}],"why":"The same honest line to both sides keeps the couple in charge of the plan."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"What matters most to you, picking between them this year?\"","options":[{"text":"\"What's fair to both families, decided together.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"Agreed. Let's actually work that out.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Whichever side would be more upset if we skip.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"That's guilt talking, not fairness.\""},{"text":"\"Whichever is less effort to arrange.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"Easier, maybe, but it favours one side every year.\""},{"text":"\"Let's just decide separately and tell them apart.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"That risks two different answers reaching them.\""}],"why":"Weighing fairness together makes a plan you both actually stand behind."}],"relearn":"One shared line to both sides keeps the couple deciding together, not split across two homes."},
  {"id":"fm-952","cat":"couple-team","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"disagree in room line","hook":"In front of the whole family, your partner says something you quietly disagree with.","setup":"The room goes quiet, waiting for your reaction.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your uncle asks: \"So do you agree with that?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We see it differently. We'll talk it through.\"","then":"Uncle says: \"Fair, no rush then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I mean, sort of, I suppose.\"","then":"Uncle says: \"So you agree, then, good.\""},{"text":"\"No, they've got it wrong. That's not what we agreed.\"","then":"Your partner says quietly: \"That's not what I meant, actually.\""},{"text":"\"Let's not get into it here.\"","then":"Uncle says: \"Into what, exactly?\" still curious."}],"why":"Naming the difference gently and taking it private protects your partner in the room."},{"prompt":"Later, alone, your partner says: \"That was awkward. Can we talk about what happened in there?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sorry that was awkward. I see one part differently. Let's sort it out.\"","then":"Your partner softens. \"Okay. Thanks for telling me straight.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Oh, it was nothing. I mostly agree with you, honestly. Forget it.\"","then":"Your partner frowns. \"Mostly? Which part don't you agree with?\""},{"text":"\"You shouldn't have said that in front of everyone in the first place.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"So this is my fault now?\""},{"text":"\"Let's not go over it again. It's done with.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"It isn't done for me. I still don't know what you think.\""}],"why":"Owning the awkwardness and offering to sort it together keeps you a team even when you disagree."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"So what do you actually think about it?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Honestly, here's where I see it differently. Let's find a middle ground.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"Okay, let's actually talk it through properly.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It doesn't matter now. Let's move on.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"It does matter, though, to me.\""},{"text":"\"I was just trying to keep the peace back there. That's all.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"So where do you actually stand, then?\""},{"text":"\"You should already know what I think.\"","then":"Your partner says: \"I'd rather you just tell me.\""}],"why":"Sharing your honest view, privately, finishes what the public moment interrupted."}],"relearn":"Flag the difference gently and take it private; don't contradict your partner in front of family."},
  {"id":"fm-952b","cat":"couple-team","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that quietly split the couple under family pressure.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Checking in privately before answering relatives","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Letting relatives split you with flattery","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Backing each other in the room","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Blaming your partner to win the family over","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Giving one calm, shared answer","trick":false}],"why":"Letting flattery split you and blaming your partner break the team; checking in, backing each other and a shared answer keep it.","relearn":"Stay a team: align privately and back each other; don't let flattery split you or blame your partner.","persona":"Sneha","source":"stay a team 2"},
  {"id":"fm-953","cat":"couple-team","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that triangulate the couple (a relative trying to split you).","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'Let's all sit and talk this out together'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"'Don't tell your partner I said this, but...'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'We decided this as a couple, here's our plan'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"'Just agree with me, we'll handle them later'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"'I'll bring this up with both of you'","trick":false}],"why":"Asking you to keep a secret and to side against your partner are triangulation; open talk isn't.","relearn":"Triangulation: 'don't tell your partner' and 'side against them' are the wedge; open talk isn't.","persona":"any","source":"spot triangulation"},
  {"id":"fm-954","cat":"couple-team","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two replies that leave your partner exposed in front of the family.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'We're a team on this; we'll decide together'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"'Honestly, that one's not my fault'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'Let's step out a moment and talk'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"(Silence, while a relative scolds your partner)","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"'We've thought about it and we're happy'","trick":false}],"why":"Blaming your partner and staying silent while they're scolded both abandon them; teaming up and a shared answer back them.","relearn":"Don't expose your partner: blaming them or going silent under fire leaves them alone.","persona":"any","source":"spot exposed"},
  {"id":"fm-955","cat":"couple-team","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that hide a family matter from your own partner.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Telling your partner about the call right after","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Making a money promise behind their back","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Bringing a complaint to your partner directly","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Keeping a 'family-only' secret deal from them","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Looping your partner in on a tricky request","trick":false}],"why":"A secret money promise and a hidden 'family-only' deal keep your partner in the dark; telling and looping in keep it honest.","relearn":"Keep no family matter hidden; secret promises and 'family-only' deals erode the team.","persona":"any","source":"spot hidden"},
  {"id":"fm-956","cat":"couple-team","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two 'united front' moves that are actually a cold wall, not teamwork.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'We've decided together, and we'd love your blessing'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Freezing relatives out with icy silence","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Holding your decision warmly, explaining once","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"'Our way or we just won't come at all'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Honouring the elders while keeping your call","trick":false}],"why":"Icy silence and an ultimatum are a cold wall that shuts family out; a warm decision and honouring elders are a real front.","relearn":"A united front stays warm and seeks blessing; silence and ultimatums are a wall.","persona":"any","source":"spot wall"},
  {"id":"fm-957","cat":"couple-team","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two phone-call moves that cut your partner out of a decision.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'Let me check with my partner too before I say yes'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Committing you both before asking them","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'We'll both reply to you tomorrow'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Hiding that the relative even called","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Telling your partner about the call right after","trick":false}],"why":"Committing both of you and hiding the call shut your partner out; checking first and telling them keep it a couple decision.","relearn":"Don't commit your partner or hide a call; check in and tell them, so decisions stay shared.","persona":"any","source":"spot phone"},
  {"id":"fm-958","cat":"couple-team","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two things you say to your parents that recruit them against your partner.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'We had a small disagreement; we're sorting it'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"'You won't believe what they did this time'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'We're good, just figuring out our routine'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"'You were right about them all along'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"'We'll handle it between us'","trick":false}],"why":"Gossiping about your partner and 'you were right about them' enlist family against them; calm, contained updates don't.","relearn":"Don't recruit your parents against your partner; keep couple problems between you.","persona":"Pooja","source":"spot recruit"},
  {"id":"fm-990","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“A good bahu adjusts to everything; she never has a need of her own.”","myth":{"un":"A good bahu adjusts to everything; she never has a need of her own.","re":"Adjusting goes both ways, and having needs doesn't make you a bad bahu, it makes you a person.","why":"A home runs on mutual give-and-take, not one person erasing herself."},"relearn":"Adjusting goes both ways; having a need of your own doesn't make you a bad bahu.","persona":"Pooja","source":"never erasing"},
  {"id":"fm-991","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“If you set a limit with your parents, you're a disloyal son.”","myth":{"un":"If you set a limit with your parents, you're a disloyal son.","re":"Loyalty isn't doing everything they say; you can love and honour them and still hold a couple's limit.","why":"Honour and a clear limit live together; obedience isn't the test of love."},"relearn":"Loyalty isn't blind obedience; you can honour your parents and still hold a limit.","persona":"Karthik","source":"loyal not obedient"},
  {"id":"fm-992","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“If you keep any privacy from in-laws, you must be hiding something.”","myth":{"un":"If you keep any privacy from in-laws, you must be hiding something.","re":"A couple needing some private space is normal and healthy. Privacy isn't secrecy or guilt.","why":"Closeness doesn't mean zero walls; every couple needs a little room of its own."},"relearn":"Privacy isn't secrecy; a couple needing some space of its own is normal and healthy.","persona":"Pooja","source":"space not secrecy"},
  {"id":"fm-993","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“Saying no to a relative once means they'll cut you off forever.”","myth":{"un":"Saying no to a relative once means they'll cut you off forever.","re":"A kind, occasional no rarely ends a real bond; most relationships absorb a respectful limit.","why":"Strong bonds survive a no; if one no ends everything, the bond was already strained."},"relearn":"A kind no rarely ends a real bond; most relationships hold a respectful limit fine.","persona":"Sneha","source":"no won't end it"},
  {"id":"fm-994","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“Keeping the peace means agreeing to everything elders ask.”","myth":{"un":"Keeping the peace means agreeing to everything elders ask.","re":"Real peace is honest, not silent; agreeing to everything just stores up resentment for later.","why":"Swallowed nos pile into bitterness; a kind limit now keeps the peace real."},"relearn":"Peace isn't silent agreement; swallowed nos turn into resentment later.","persona":"Pooja","source":"real peace"},
  {"id":"fm-995","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“A boundary should be dropped on people firmly, the harder the better.”","myth":{"un":"A boundary should be dropped on people firmly, the harder the better.","re":"A boundary lands best warm and clear: softness in tone, firmness in the line, not a slammed door.","why":"Warmth carries the limit further than harshness; you can be kind and still unmovable."},"relearn":"A boundary works best warm and clear, not harsh; firm line, kind tone.","persona":"Karthik","source":"warm and firm"},
  {"id":"fm-996","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“Once you set a boundary, you've handled it; it never needs repeating.”","myth":{"un":"Once you set a boundary, you've handled it; it never needs repeating.","re":"Boundaries get tested and need re-stating gently over time. That's upkeep, not failure.","why":"People forget and push again; a calm repeat is normal maintenance, not a defeat."},"relearn":"Boundaries need gentle re-stating over time; repeating one isn't failure, it's upkeep.","persona":"Pooja","source":"upkeep not failure"},
  {"id":"fm-997","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“If you don't pick up every call instantly, you don't love your family.”","myth":{"un":"If you don't pick up every call instantly, you don't love your family.","re":"Love isn't measured in instant availability; you can answer when you can and still care deeply.","why":"Constant availability burns you out; caring runs deeper than a ringtone."},"relearn":"Love isn't instant availability; you can call back when you can and still care deeply.","persona":"Sneha","source":"not a ringtone"},
  {"id":"fm-998","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“How you and your partner spend your own salary is the whole family's business.”","myth":{"un":"How you and your partner spend your own salary is the whole family's business.","re":"Your earnings are the couple's call; sharing generously is a choice, not an owed obligation.","why":"Generosity given freely is kindness; generosity demanded isn't a budget, it's pressure."},"relearn":"Your salary is the couple's call; you can give generously by choice, not by demand.","persona":"Pooja","source":"your call"},
  {"id":"fm-999","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“A boundary is a wall that shuts people out of your life.”","myth":{"un":"A boundary is a wall that shuts people out of your life.","re":"A boundary limits one behaviour while keeping the door open. It's a fence with a gate, not a wall.","why":"Walls end relationships; gates let love in while holding the line on what hurt."},"relearn":"A boundary is a gate, not a wall; it limits a behaviour without shutting the person out.","persona":"Pooja","source":"gate not wall"},
  {"id":"fm-1000","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“Whoever shouts loudest in the house gets to decide for the couple.”","myth":{"un":"Whoever shouts loudest in the house gets to decide for the couple.","re":"Volume isn't authority; the couple's life is theirs to decide, calmly, no matter who's loudest.","why":"A raised voice is pressure, not a verdict; you can stay calm and still keep your decision."},"relearn":"Volume isn't authority; the couple's decision stands calmly, however loud the room gets.","persona":"Karthik","source":"calm decides"},
  {"id":"fm-1001","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“Letting in-laws decide your festival visits avoids all conflict.”","myth":{"un":"Letting in-laws decide your festival visits avoids all conflict.","re":"Handing over every choice doesn't end conflict. It just hides your needs until they overflow.","why":"Buried needs resurface as resentment; a shared plan beats silent self-erasing."},"relearn":"Handing over every choice doesn't end conflict; it hides needs until they overflow.","persona":"Pooja","source":"plan not erase"},
  {"id":"fm-1002","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“If a limit upsets a relative even a little, you set it wrong.”","myth":{"un":"If a limit upsets a relative even a little, you set it wrong.","re":"A kind limit can still land as a small disappointment. That's allowed; their feelings aren't your fault.","why":"You can't control someone's reaction, only your kindness; mild upset isn't proof of cruelty."},"relearn":"A kind limit can still mildly disappoint; their reaction isn't proof you did it wrong.","persona":"Sneha","source":"mild upset is ok"},
  {"id":"fm-1003","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“A boundary set 'for now' isn't real; only a forever rule counts.”","myth":{"un":"A boundary set 'for now' isn't real; only a forever rule counts.","re":"Boundaries can flex with the season of life: a 'just for now' limit is real and completely valid.","why":"Needs change with newborns, jobs, health; a temporary limit is honest, not weak."},"relearn":"A boundary can be 'just for now' and still be real; limits flex with the season of life.","persona":"Pooja","source":"for-now is real"},
  {"id":"fm-1004","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"“Telling family your plan in advance is rude; just surprise them.”","myth":{"un":"Telling family your plan in advance is rude; just surprise them.","re":"A warm heads-up before a decision or visit is the kind move. It gives people time, not offence.","why":"Surprises breed friction; a little notice honours the relationship and lowers conflict."},"relearn":"A warm heads-up before plans isn't rude; it gives people time and lowers conflict.","persona":"Karthik","source":"heads-up is kind"},
  {"id":"fm-1005","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a kind way to hold a limit, or a cold cut-off that just shuts people out?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"“We'd love a call before you drop by.”"},{"id":"b","text":"Ignoring every message for a month"},{"id":"c","text":"“We'll decide on the baby ourselves, with love.”"},{"id":"d","text":"Blocking them after one argument"},{"id":"e","text":"“Join us Sunday; weekdays we need our own time.”"},{"id":"f","text":"Cold silence so they 'get the hint'"}],"bins":[{"id":"kind","label":"Kind limit","valence":"pos"},{"id":"cold","label":"Cold cut-off","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"kind","b":"cold","c":"kind","d":"cold","e":"kind","f":"cold"},"relearn":"A kind limit names the line warmly and keeps the door open; silence and blocking are cut-offs, not boundaries.","persona":"Pooja","source":"kind vs cut-off"},
  {"id":"fm-1006","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: clearly the couple's own call, or genuinely a shared family decision?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"How you raise your own children"},{"id":"b","text":"Whose home the wedding rituals happen in"},{"id":"c","text":"What you do with your own salary"},{"id":"d","text":"A festival the whole joint family hosts"},{"id":"e","text":"Which city you two live and work in"},{"id":"f","text":"Planning a shared family pilgrimage"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"Couple's call","valence":"neutral"},{"id":"shared","label":"Shared family call","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"shared","c":"couple","d":"shared","e":"couple","f":"shared"},"relearn":"Your kids, salary and where you live are the couple's call; genuinely shared rituals are decided together.","persona":"any","source":"whose call"},
  {"id":"fm-1007","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: 'received the love, kept the limit', or just swallowed the overreach?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"“Thanks Aunty, we've got the plan sorted ourselves.”"},{"id":"b","text":"Cancelling your trip because she frowned"},{"id":"c","text":"“Love your concern; this one's ours to decide.”"},{"id":"d","text":"Agreeing just so she'd stop pushing"},{"id":"e","text":"“We'll take your advice on board and choose together.”"},{"id":"f","text":"Saying yes while quietly seething"}],"bins":[{"id":"kept","label":"Kept the limit","valence":"pos"},{"id":"swallowed","label":"Swallowed it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"kept","b":"swallowed","c":"kept","d":"swallowed","e":"kept","f":"swallowed"},"relearn":"You can receive concern warmly and still hold the line; caving buries resentment.","persona":"Sneha","source":"love and limit"},
  {"id":"fm-1008","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a warm, clear way to say it, or a harsh version of the very same limit?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"“We'd love a heads-up before visits.”"},{"id":"b","text":"“Stop barging in.”"},{"id":"c","text":"“We'll ask when we want advice on this.”"},{"id":"d","text":"“Mind your own business.”"},{"id":"e","text":"“This one's ours, and we love you.”"},{"id":"f","text":"“Stay out of our lives.”"}],"bins":[{"id":"warm","label":"Warm and clear","valence":"pos"},{"id":"harsh","label":"Harsh version","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"warm","b":"harsh","c":"warm","d":"harsh","e":"warm","f":"harsh"},"relearn":"The same limit can be said with warmth and clarity; harsh phrasing wounds without making the line any firmer.","persona":"Pooja","source":"warm vs harsh"},
  {"id":"fm-1009","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: holding a limit kindly under pressure, or caving to end the discomfort?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"“We hear you; we'll decide together and tell you.”"},{"id":"b","text":"Saying yes just so the call ends"},{"id":"c","text":"“Give us a week to think, then we'll share.”"},{"id":"d","text":"Agreeing on the spot to stop the frown"},{"id":"e","text":"“We love you, and this stays our choice.”"},{"id":"f","text":"Folding the moment voices get raised"}],"bins":[{"id":"held","label":"Held kindly","valence":"pos"},{"id":"caved","label":"Caved to comfort","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"held","b":"caved","c":"held","d":"caved","e":"held","f":"caved"},"relearn":"You can buy time and hold the line warmly; caving to stop the discomfort trades your real choice for a moment's quiet.","persona":"Karthik","source":"held vs caved"},
  {"id":"fm-1010","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a boundary that still includes family, or one that walls them out?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"“Come for dinner Sunday; we'll plan the rest.”"},{"id":"b","text":"Skipping every event out of spite"},{"id":"c","text":"“We'd love your recipes; cooked our way.”"},{"id":"d","text":"Refusing all contact over a slight"},{"id":"e","text":"“Visit anytime, just call ahead.”"},{"id":"f","text":"Going silent for months to make a point"}],"bins":[{"id":"includes","label":"Still includes them","valence":"pos"},{"id":"walls","label":"Needless wall","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"includes","b":"walls","c":"includes","d":"walls","e":"includes","f":"walls"},"relearn":"A kind boundary keeps people close; spite and total silence wall them out.","persona":"Pooja","source":"include not wall"},
  {"id":"fm-1011","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a respectful 'no' you can actually say, or a needlessly harsh refusal?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"“Not this time, but thank you for thinking of us.”"},{"id":"b","text":"“Why do you always ask such things?”"},{"id":"c","text":"“We'd rather not, and we really appreciate the offer.”"},{"id":"d","text":"“Stop wasting our time with this.”"},{"id":"e","text":"“We've decided differently; we hope you understand.”"},{"id":"f","text":"“You never listen anyway, so no.”"}],"bins":[{"id":"respect","label":"Respectful no","valence":"pos"},{"id":"harsh","label":"Harsh refusal","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"respect","b":"harsh","c":"respect","d":"harsh","e":"respect","f":"harsh"},"relearn":"A respectful no declines the ask but honours the person; harsh refusals attack the person.","persona":"any","source":"kind no"},
  {"id":"fm-1012","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a healthy couple-boundary, or control dressed up as one?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"“We'll plan visits together, fairly.”"},{"id":"b","text":"“You can't see your own parents.”"},{"id":"c","text":"“Let's handle money as a team.”"},{"id":"d","text":"“Hand me your phone to check.”"},{"id":"e","text":"“We'll share news when ready.”"},{"id":"f","text":"“You need my okay to go out.”"}],"bins":[{"id":"healthy","label":"Healthy limit","valence":"pos"},{"id":"control","label":"Control, not a limit","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"healthy","b":"control","c":"healthy","d":"control","e":"healthy","f":"control"},"relearn":"A boundary protects the couple; cutting off family, phone-checks and permission-to-leave are control.","persona":"Karthik","source":"limit vs control"},
  {"id":"fm-1013","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"cupboard line","hook":"Your mother-in-law reorganises your cupboards every week while you're at work.","steps":[{"prompt":"You come home to find everything moved again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank her, then say you'll keep your shelves your way","then":"She looks a little surprised, but nods and smiles.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and quietly put everything back later","then":"The cycle repeats every week, unspoken and a little tiring."},{"text":"Snap that she should stop touching your things again","then":"A sharp rebuke wounds her and hardens the conflict."},{"text":"Thank her and let her keep doing it","then":"You lose your own system, one shelf at a time."}],"why":"Appreciating the care while naming the limit protects your space kindly."},{"prompt":"A little while later, you notice she's gone quiet, seeming a bit down around the house. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask her to help with something else she'd enjoy","then":"She brightens, glad to help in a way that still feels useful.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and hope she feels better on her own","then":"She stays a little unsure whether she did something wrong."},{"text":"Explain your full reasoning about the cupboards, at length","then":"The long explanation makes a small ask feel bigger than it is."},{"text":"Let her reorganise one cupboard to keep her happy","then":"The one cupboard quietly becomes two, then three again."}],"why":"Giving her a job she enjoys shows she matters to you, and makes room to name the limit kindly."},{"prompt":"A month later, Aman mentions his mother said something about feeling a little out of place in the house. What do you say when he tells you?","options":[{"text":"Suggest a weekly cooking session together instead","then":"She loves the idea, and it becomes a warm weekly habit.","best":true},{"text":"Say she'll get used to the changes eventually","then":"She doesn't, and the quiet hurt lingers a little longer."},{"text":"Feel guilty and open all the cupboards back up","then":"Your space is gone again, along with the limit you hoped to keep."},{"text":"Ask Aman to talk to her in your place instead","then":"She feels like the message is coming from the wrong person."}],"why":"Offering a real, shared way to help keeps her close without giving up your space."}],"debrief":"Appreciate the care, then name the limit warmly; welcome help and still keep your space.","relearn":"Welcome the help and keep your space: thank her, then name the line kindly."},
  {"id":"fm-1014","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"baby pressure","hook":"Relatives keep asking when you two will have a baby, at every single gathering.","steps":[{"prompt":"An aunt asks again at a wedding. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'We'll share happy news when there is some'","then":"She smiles and moves on to asking about the food instead.","best":true},{"text":"'Maybe soon, who knows'","then":"She takes it as a hint and mentions it to other relatives."},{"text":"Get visibly annoyed and walk off","then":"Storming off makes you the topic of gossip for the evening."},{"text":"Give a detailed, honest answer to satisfy her","then":"The detail opens the door to even more personal questions."}],"why":"A calm, repeatable line deflects kindly without owing anyone an explanation."},{"prompt":"At the next function, a different relative tries the exact same question. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give a calm, settled answer, without irritation","then":"The relative accepts it easily, since it sounds settled, not defensive.","best":true},{"text":"Explain that you're 'still deciding' this time","then":"The new phrasing invites follow-up questions about the deciding."},{"text":"Joke that your partner isn't ready","then":"The joke puts your partner in an awkward spot in front of everyone."},{"text":"Try to leave the conversation quickly instead of answering","then":"The abrupt exit doesn't stop the question. It just delays it."}],"why":"Repeating the same steady line, calmly, makes it clearly your standard answer."},{"prompt":"Months later, your mother asks the same question, but gently, alone with you. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"A warm, calm answer, softened for the moment","then":"She nods, understanding it's the couple's own timeline.","best":true},{"text":"Promise her a definite timeline to reassure her","then":"You've now promised something you might not be able to keep."},{"text":"Tell her to stop asking, firmly","then":"She feels hurt, even though the question came from love."},{"text":"Change the subject without acknowledging it","then":"She feels brushed off rather than genuinely answered."}],"why":"One warm, steady line works for a crowd or for your own mother alike."}],"debrief":"A warm, repeatable line closes the topic; your family timeline is the couple's own.","relearn":"Deflect baby pressure with one calm line; the timeline is yours."},
  {"id":"fm-1015","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"the key","hook":"Your in-laws want to keep a key to your flat and let themselves in anytime.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your father-in-law asks for a spare key over dinner. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'We love you visiting. We'd just like a call first'","then":"He looks a little surprised, then nods slowly.","best":true},{"text":"Hand over the key to avoid any awkwardness","then":"He pockets the key, and the next visit comes with no warning at all."},{"text":"Say you'll 'think about it' to delay the ask","then":"He assumes it's a yes coming, and mentions it again soon."},{"text":"Refuse sharply without offering an alternative","then":"The blunt no stings, even though the feeling behind it is fair."}],"why":"Offering a warm alternative protects privacy without shutting the door on them."},{"prompt":"A week later, your father-in-law asks how you would like visits to work from now on. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say kindly that visits are welcome, with a call first","then":"He listens, then agrees to call before he visits.","best":true},{"text":"Say anytime is fine, to keep the peace","then":"It feels easier for now, but the old worry quietly returns."},{"text":"Get irritated that the topic keeps coming up","then":"The irritation lands harder than his question deserved."},{"text":"Change the subject and hope visits sort themselves out","then":"Nothing gets agreed, so the question hangs in the air."}],"why":"Stating a clear, kind arrangement gives him something real to follow."},{"prompt":"The next time he visits, your father-in-law phones ahead first. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Thank him warmly for calling ahead","then":"He relaxes, hearing that the call itself is truly welcome.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing about the call and just let him in","then":"He is left unsure whether the call was appreciated."},{"text":"Apologise for having asked for a heads-up","then":"The apology makes the reasonable ask feel like something to regret."},{"text":"Joke that he's 'finally learned the rules'","then":"The joke lands a little sharper than you meant it to."}],"why":"A genuine thank you shows the call is about closeness, not distance."}],"debrief":"Asking for a heads-up isn't rejection; it protects the couple's space while keeping the welcome warm.","relearn":"A call before visits protects your space without shutting anyone out."},
  {"id":"fm-1016","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"back-channel","hook":"A relative keeps phoning Divya to complain about you, behind your back.","steps":[{"prompt":"Divya tells you about the latest call. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say, 'Let's all talk together, kindly,' and call back","then":"Divya nods, relieved to bring it into the open together.","best":true},{"text":"Let it slide since it's not said to your face","then":"Silence lets the side-channel grow and starts poisoning trust."},{"text":"Ask Divya what exactly was said, in detail","then":"You end up dwelling on the complaint instead of the pattern."},{"text":"Tell Divya to just stop answering those calls","then":"The relative simply tries a different way to reach her instead."}],"why":"An open, kind talk together shuts the back-channel down without a fight."},{"prompt":"A few days later, the relative tries again, this time reaching you directly. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'If something's bothering you, tell us both directly'","then":"The relative pauses, then agrees to speak to you both going forward.","best":true},{"text":"Thank them for caring and leave it there","then":"The thanks doesn't actually change the pattern of separate calls."},{"text":"Point out this isn't the first time","then":"The relative feels called out and the conversation turns defensive."},{"text":"Say you'll pass it on to Divya yourself","then":"The relative senses they can still get to Divya alone next time."}],"why":"Asking them to speak to you both directly sets a clear, kind expectation for next time."},{"prompt":"A month later, the same relative starts complaining to you about Divya. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say warmly: 'Let's talk together, kindly'","then":"The relative, a little sheepish, agrees to include Divya this time.","best":true},{"text":"Listen without saying anything, out of politeness","then":"The one-sided venting continues, just aimed at a different partner now."},{"text":"Defend Divya sharply on the spot","then":"The call turns tense, and the relative feels attacked, not corrected."},{"text":"Agree quietly just to end the call faster","then":"The quiet agreement reads as taking sides against Divya."}],"why":"Holding the same rule for either of you keeps the couple equally protected."},{"prompt":"At the next family gathering, the relative approaches you both warmly, as if nothing happened. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Greet them warmly too, choosing to move forward","then":"The gathering goes easily, and the relationship feels lighter.","best":true},{"text":"Stay a little guarded, just in case","then":"The guardedness creates a small, unnecessary distance at the event."},{"text":"Bring up the old calls to make sure they remember","then":"Reopening it undoes the fresh start you'd both worked toward."},{"text":"Avoid them for most of the gathering","then":"The avoidance reads as unresolved tension to the rest of the family."}],"why":"Meeting them warmly gives the relationship room to heal."}],"debrief":"Triangulation thrives in private complaints; an open, kind talk shuts the back-channel down.","relearn":"Refuse the back-channel: invite an open, kind talk instead."},
  {"id":"fm-1017","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"job advice","hook":"Your father-in-law has strong opinions about whether you should keep working.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says, 'A job like that is too much strain. Leave it.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'I value your view, Papa. This career call is mine to make'","then":"He pauses, surprised by the calm, clear answer.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll think about it to end the topic","then":"He assumes you're genuinely considering leaving your job."},{"text":"Seriously consider quitting just to avoid disappointing him","then":"You almost hand in your notice, just to avoid the guilt."},{"text":"Argue that the job is important, defensively","then":"The defensive tone makes the calm point harder for him to hear."}],"why":"Honouring his concern while holding your own choice respects him without giving in."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later he brings it up again, with more reasons prepared. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Listen fully, then say kindly that it's your choice","then":"He runs out of new arguments, and the topic finally starts to settle.","best":true},{"text":"Agree with a couple of his points to seem open","then":"He hears the agreement as movement toward quitting."},{"text":"Ask Aman to talk to him instead","then":"Aman feels caught between defending you and respecting his father."},{"text":"Stop discussing your job with him entirely","then":"The silence makes him assume something is actually wrong at work."}],"why":"Really listening, then holding steady, shows respect without changing your answer."},{"prompt":"He mentions to Aman that he 'just wants you to be happy'. What do you say when Aman tells you?","options":[{"text":"Say you know that, and you are happy, doing this work","then":"Aman relays it warmly, and his father seems genuinely reassured.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing, hoping the concern fades on its own","then":"The concern doesn't fade. It just goes quiet for a while."},{"text":"Feel a little guilty and second-guess your choice","then":"The guilt creeps in, even though nothing has actually changed."},{"text":"Ask Aman to reassure him without involving you","then":"The reassurance feels secondhand and less convincing to his father."}],"why":"Naming your own happiness plainly reassures him more than avoiding the topic."},{"prompt":"Months later he mentions your job warmly to a relative, having come around. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank him warmly for coming around, genuinely","then":"He smiles, clearly glad you noticed and appreciated it.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and just enjoy the shift quietly","then":"He's left unsure whether the change actually mattered to you."},{"text":"Bring up how hard the earlier pressure was","then":"The comment undoes some of the goodwill of his change of heart."},{"text":"Joke that he finally 'gave up'","then":"The joke lands a little sharper than the warm moment deserved."}],"why":"Acknowledging the change warmly closes the story on a genuinely good note."}],"debrief":"You can listen warmly to an elder and still decide your own work; respect isn't obedience.","relearn":"Honour the advice, keep the choice: respect listens, but it doesn't surrender your career."},
  {"id":"fm-1018","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"weekend rhythm","hook":"Your mother expects you and Divya at her place every single weekend, no exceptions.","steps":[{"prompt":"She calls to confirm this Sunday, as usual. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'Divya and I talked. Two Sundays a month, and you visit us too'","then":"She pauses, then says, 'Alright, let's try that.'","best":true},{"text":"Say yes again, exhausted, but keeping the peace","then":"Saying yes again drains you, and you start dreading the calls."},{"text":"Cancel this Sunday with a vague excuse and call her later","then":"She worries something is wrong, since the excuse was so vague."},{"text":"Suggest she visit you instead, every single week, from now on","then":"The new plan just shifts the same exhausting pace onto her."}],"why":"A fair, shared rhythm keeps the bond close without costing every weekend."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, your mother calls about the weekend: \"You know I love having you both. What's the plan?\"","options":[{"text":"Reassure her warmly and name the Sundays you will visit","then":"She feels included, hearing a specific plan to see you both.","best":true},{"text":"Reassure her but keep the next visit vague and open","then":"The vague plan makes visits feel less like a real promise."},{"text":"Give in and say you'll come every Sunday this month, to keep her happy","then":"Saying yes again sets a quiet precedent, and you wonder if weekends will ever be yours."},{"text":"Explain at length exactly why you both need space some weekends","then":"The long explanation makes a simple choice feel like a debate."}],"why":"A warm reassurance with a real plan makes the rhythm feel secure, not cold."},{"prompt":"Two months in, your mother tells a relative she wishes she saw more of you. What do you do when you hear this?","options":[{"text":"Check in warmly and talk through a plan that works for everyone","then":"She relaxes, hearing you want to find time for her.","best":true},{"text":"Let it go for now, since the rhythm is technically still working out fine","then":"The unspoken worry keeps circulating among relatives."},{"text":"Feel guilty and agree to every weekend","then":"The fair rhythm quietly disappears under the guilt."},{"text":"Ask the relative to correct her instead","then":"The message comes from the wrong person and lands oddly."}],"why":"Checking in directly reassures her that the plan is steady, not a slow goodbye."}],"debrief":"A kind, regular rhythm keeps the bond strong and leaves the couple some room.","relearn":"Set a fair visiting rhythm; closeness needn't cost every weekend."},
  {"id":"fm-1019","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"public comment","hook":"An aunt has strong opinions about how you dress, and dinner gives her an audience.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says, 'That's a bit much for a family dinner.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'I dress the way I'm comfortable with, thanks'","then":"She nods, a little unsure how to respond to the calm answer.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh it off without saying anything real","then":"The laugh reads as agreement, and she comments again later."},{"text":"Explain your outfit in detail, hoping she approves","then":"She listens, then finds another detail to criticise."},{"text":"Snap back sharply in front of everyone","then":"The table goes tense, and the dinner turns awkward for everyone."}],"why":"A calm, clear line holds your boundary without escalating the moment."},{"prompt":"The next family event, she brings a friend and repeats the comment to impress her. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer her calmly, unbothered","then":"The friend looks a little surprised at how easily you hold your ground.","best":true},{"text":"Avoid her section of the room the rest of the evening","then":"Avoiding her doesn't stop the comment from happening again next time."},{"text":"Change your outfit choices for future events, to be safe","then":"Reshaping yourself over one comment hands away your own choices."},{"text":"Complain about her to other relatives instead","then":"The complaint spreads, but nothing changes at the actual gatherings."}],"why":"The same steady answer, every time, shows the comment never actually lands."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, Aman mentions he overheard the comments and asks how you're doing with it. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell him honestly how you're really feeling about it","then":"He feels reassured, glad you told him instead of carrying it alone.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's nothing to avoid worrying him","then":"He senses something and keeps wondering what's actually going on."},{"text":"Leave it to him to fix things with his aunt","then":"He tries, but without knowing what you want, the talk with his aunt goes nowhere."},{"text":"Say you're used to it by now","then":"He nods, but keeps wondering how much it really stings."}],"why":"Being honest with your partner keeps him from guessing what you're carrying."}],"debrief":"A calm, kind line holds your boundary in public without escalating; your choices stay yours.","relearn":"Hold your line warmly in public; you don't owe anyone a wardrobe to avoid a comment."},
  {"id":"fm-1020","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"early announce","hook":"Your family wants to announce your move to a new city before you and Divya have decided.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother says, 'I already told your aunt. It's happening.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'We haven't finalised anything; we'll share once we've decided'","then":"She looks a little embarrassed, and says she'll hold off telling anyone else.","best":true},{"text":"Let the announcement stand to avoid an awkward correction","then":"You're now boxed into a decision you hadn't actually made."},{"text":"Get upset that she spoke too soon","then":"The upset feels bigger than the actual mistake deserved."},{"text":"Confirm it's happening, just to match her story","then":"You've committed to a move neither of you has properly discussed."}],"why":"Pausing the announcement kindly keeps the actual decision yours."},{"prompt":"That evening, Divya asks how far along the decision really is. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Be honest that it's still open, and ask what she thinks","then":"You two talk it through properly, without outside pressure this time.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's basically decided, since family already knows","then":"Divya feels the choice was made for her before she even weighed in."},{"text":"Say you're not sure what you even want anymore","then":"The uncertainty leaves Divya unsure how to help you decide."},{"text":"Blame your mother for making it feel more settled than it is","then":"The blame doesn't move the actual decision forward."}],"why":"An honest check-in keeps the real decision between you two, not the rumour."},{"prompt":"A week later, your aunt calls to 'confirm details' for the move. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Kindly say that nothing's decided yet","then":"She's a little surprised, but says she'll wait to hear properly.","best":true},{"text":"Give vague, half-true details to satisfy her","then":"The vague details spread further and firm up into 'facts'."},{"text":"Avoid her calls until the real decision is made","then":"The avoidance makes the rumour seem more, not less, likely."},{"text":"Ask your mother to correct the rumour for you","then":"The correction comes secondhand and doesn't fully land."}],"why":"A calm, repeated correction keeps the rumour from hardening into fact."},{"prompt":"You and Divya finally decide together, one way or the other. What do you do next?","options":[{"text":"Tell family together, now that it's actually settled","then":"The family hears a clear, confident, joint decision this time.","best":true},{"text":"Let your mother share the news, since she's excited","then":"The announcement feels like it belongs to her more than to you two."},{"text":"Delay telling anyone a bit longer, just in case","then":"The delay leaves family confused about whether it's truly final."},{"text":"Mention it casually, in passing, to one relative","then":"The casual mention spreads unevenly before you can share it properly."}],"why":"Sharing the settled decision together shows it was genuinely yours to make."}],"debrief":"The couple decides first, then shares; that order keeps the choice yours.","relearn":"Decide first, announce later; don't let an early announcement box you in."},
  {"id":"fm-1021","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"the loan","hook":"Your sister-in-law borrows money from you often and never quite repays it.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks again, this time for a slightly bigger amount. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'I can't lend this time, but I can help you budget'","then":"She's surprised, but agrees to sit down and look at her budget.","best":true},{"text":"Lend it again to avoid seeming stingy","then":"Endless lending you can't sustain breeds quiet resentment on both sides."},{"text":"Lend it, but mention the unpaid amounts pointedly","then":"The comment stings, and she avoids you for a while afterward."},{"text":"Say no without offering anything else","then":"The bare no feels cold, even though it's a fair answer."}],"why":"A warm no paired with real help protects your money and the relationship."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, she brings the old unpaid amounts up herself, embarrassed. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Suggest a small monthly repayment and help her plan for it","then":"She relaxes, relieved to have a small plan she can actually keep.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's fine, then bring it up again later anyway","then":"Bringing it back up later undoes the goodwill from saying it was fine."},{"text":"Ask for the full amount back at once, before any more help","then":"She feels cornered by an amount she can't manage, and the talk stalls."},{"text":"Say it never really mattered, to reassure her","then":"The comment brushes past something that did actually matter to you."}],"why":"A small agreed repayment settles the past kindly and keeps the focus on a working plan."},{"prompt":"Months later, another relative asks to borrow money the same way. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give a kind, clear no and offer to help them plan instead","then":"The relative accepts the clear answer, and is glad of the offer.","best":true},{"text":"Lend it since it's a different person this time","then":"The exception quietly makes the next request harder to refuse."},{"text":"Explain your sister-in-law's situation as the reason for saying no","then":"Sharing her story without asking feels like a small betrayal."},{"text":"Avoid the relative until the topic passes","then":"Avoiding them just delays the same conversation to another day."}],"why":"The same steady answer protects your finances consistently, not just once."}],"debrief":"A warm no to a loan can still offer real help; your finances are a fair thing to protect.","relearn":"A warm no to lending can still offer help; protecting your finances isn't stingy."},
  {"id":"fm-1022","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"the name","hook":"Elders insist you name your future child after a relative; you and Divya prefer another name.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your grandmother says, 'It must be his name, when the time comes. It's tradition.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'We love that idea, but we'd like to choose our own name when the time comes'","then":"She looks thoughtful, then nods slowly.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll 'consider it' to soften the moment","then":"She takes the soft answer as real consideration, not a decline."},{"text":"Say you'll probably just go along with it in the end anyway, to keep the peace","then":"Agreeing in advance leaves you both a little uneasy about it already."},{"text":"Say the tradition doesn't matter to you","then":"The comment lands as dismissive of something she genuinely values."}],"why":"Honouring the tradition while keeping your own choice respects her without giving in."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, another elder brings up a different family name to consider. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Thank them warmly, and say you already have a name in mind","then":"They accept it, hearing a settled, steady answer.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll add it to a shortlist of names, just to be polite about it","then":"The 'shortlist' reopens a decision that felt settled."},{"text":"Get a little tired and short with them","then":"The short tone stings, even though the ask itself was gentle."},{"text":"Avoid the topic of names altogether from now on","then":"Avoiding it just invites more suggestions, since nothing's been said."}],"why":"A warm, settled answer shows the name preference is genuinely yours to decide."},{"prompt":"Your grandmother mentions to Divya she is unsure how the family name will be honoured. What do you say when Divya tells you?","options":[{"text":"Suggest keeping the family name as a possible middle name","then":"Divya loves the idea, and you both feel it's a fair way to honour everyone.","best":true},{"text":"Say she'll get over it eventually","then":"The comment doesn't really address her feelings, and she stays a little hurt."},{"text":"Feel guilty about it and reconsider the whole approach you two agreed on","then":"The guilt starts to pull at a decision you and Divya felt good about."},{"text":"Ask Divya to handle telling her instead","then":"The message feels like it's coming from the wrong person."}],"why":"Finding a way to honour the wish keeps your own choice while easing the hurt."},{"prompt":"Months later, at a family function, your grandmother happily tells a cousin about the baby name plans. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let her enjoy the moment and join in warmly","then":"She beams and keeps telling the story, glad to be part of it.","best":true},{"text":"Correct small details in how she describes it","then":"The correction takes a little shine off her excitement."},{"text":"Remind everyone at the table it's mainly your and Divya's decision to make","then":"The reminder makes the moment about credit instead of joy."},{"text":"Feel awkward and change the subject","then":"The awkwardness is noticeable, even though nothing is actually wrong."}],"why":"Letting her enjoy the moment keeps it warm, and the name is still yours to choose."}],"debrief":"Naming your future child is the couple's call: honour the wish, but keep the choice.","relearn":"You can honour a family tradition warmly and still make the naming choice yourselves."},
  {"id":"fm-1023","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"kept private","hook":"Your mother-in-law shares your private marital matters with the neighbours.","steps":[{"prompt":"A neighbour mentions something private you never told her. What do you do first?","options":[{"text":"Stay calm, and plan a gentle, direct word with her","then":"You feel steadier, having decided how to actually address it.","best":true},{"text":"Stop telling her anything from now on, quietly","then":"She notices you have gone quiet, and the sharing carries on regardless."},{"text":"Ask the neighbour exactly what she heard","then":"You spend the conversation gathering details instead of addressing the source."},{"text":"Vent to Aman about his mother, at length","then":"The venting feels good briefly, but nothing actually changes at home."}],"why":"Deciding to address it directly, calmly, is the first real step toward fixing it."},{"prompt":"You find a quiet moment with her. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'Ma, some things should stay between us two'","then":"She looks a little taken aback, then says, 'I didn't think of it that way.'","best":true},{"text":"Ask her generally to 'be more careful' about talking","then":"The vague ask doesn't name what actually needs to change."},{"text":"List every instance she's ever shared something","then":"The long list feels like an attack rather than a simple request."},{"text":"Ask Aman to raise it with her instead","then":"She hears it secondhand and isn't sure how serious it really is."}],"why":"Naming the specific limit, directly and kindly, is the clearest way to protect it."},{"prompt":"She says, 'I only mentioned it because I was proud of you both.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"'I understand that, and I'd still like it kept between us'","then":"She nods. 'Alright. I'll remember that.'","best":true},{"text":"Accept the explanation and drop the actual ask","then":"The pride explains the past, but doesn't protect the future."},{"text":"Say her pride doesn't excuse the sharing","then":"The line feels harsher than needed, given she meant well."},{"text":"Brush it off as fine and leave it there","then":"The boundary gets lost in being reassuring about her feelings."}],"why":"Acknowledging her intention while repeating the ask keeps both the request and the warmth."},{"prompt":"A month later, a different neighbour mentions something new, private again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Address it directly again, calmly and once more","then":"She's embarrassed this time, and the sharing genuinely slows down.","best":true},{"text":"Let it go since you already had the conversation once","then":"Without a repeat, it's unclear whether the message really landed."},{"text":"Stop sharing anything with her at all, even good news","then":"Cutting her out entirely cools a relationship that could still be warm."},{"text":"Complain to Aman that 'nothing ever changes'","then":"The complaint doesn't reach her, though nothing more happens right away either."}],"why":"Repeating the boundary calmly, without giving up, is what makes it actually stick."},{"prompt":"Six months on, she mentions something warm and general about you both to a relative, nothing private. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Feel glad about this small moment, and thank her warmly","then":"She's pleased to be thanked, and the goodwill between you grows.","best":true},{"text":"Stay alert, half-expecting her to slip again","then":"The lingering suspicion keeps a little distance that isn't needed anymore."},{"text":"Point out she's finally learned the rule","then":"The comment turns a good moment into a small dig."},{"text":"Say nothing, just relieved it's over","then":"She never actually hears that the change was noticed and appreciated."}],"why":"Noticing and thanking the change keeps the bond warm now that the line is respected."}],"debrief":"Naming the limit kindly protects your privacy while keeping the bond intact.","relearn":"Name the privacy limit kindly; protect what's private without freezing the bond."},
  {"id":"fm-1024","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"match","hook":"Match each harsh limit with its kinder, clearer version.","pairs":[{"left":"“Stop barging in.”","right":"“We'd love a quick call before you visit.”"},{"left":"“That's none of your business.”","right":"“This one's ours to decide, with love.”"},{"left":"“You're smothering us.”","right":"“We really need a little space of our own.”"},{"left":"“Stop telling us what to do.”","right":"“We'll ask when we'd like your advice.”"},{"left":"“Leave us alone.”","right":"“We'd love some quiet just the two of us tonight.”"}],"relearn":"Every harsh line has a warm, clear twin that holds the same limit without wounding the person.","persona":"any","source":"kinder version"},
  {"id":"fm-1025","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"match","hook":"Match each kind-boundary move with what it actually protects.","pairs":[{"left":"A call before visits","right":"The couple's privacy and prep time"},{"left":"“Our salary is our call”","right":"Financial independence as a team"},{"left":"“We'll decide and tell you”","right":"The couple's right to choose together"},{"left":"“We'd love your recipes, my way too”","right":"Your own space while welcoming help"},{"left":"A repeatable team line","right":"Both partners, from fresh fights each time"}],"relearn":"Each kind boundary protects something real: privacy, independence, choice, space, or the couple itself.","persona":"any","source":"what it protects"},
  {"id":"fm-1026","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"match","hook":"Match each over-stepping comment with a warm, boundary-holding reply.","pairs":[{"left":"“When's the baby coming?”","right":"“We'll share happy news when there is some.”"},{"left":"“You cook it all wrong.”","right":"“I'd love to learn your way, and keep mine too.”"},{"left":"“Why work so late?”","right":"“My job suits us; thanks for caring.”"},{"left":"“Visit us every weekend.”","right":"“We'll come often, and keep some weekends ours.”"},{"left":"“Tell me everything you two discuss.”","right":"“Some things stay just between us, Ma.”"}],"relearn":"A warm reply honours the person and still holds the couple's line.","persona":"Pooja","source":"warm reply"},
  {"id":"fm-1027","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"match","hook":"Match each phrase with whether it builds a bridge or a needless wall.","pairs":[{"left":"“Call before you drop by”","right":"A bridge: limit plus open door"},{"left":"Blocking them out of spite","right":"A wall: shuts the person out"},{"left":"“Join us Sunday, we plan the rest”","right":"A bridge: includes and still chooses"},{"left":"Months of cold silence","right":"A wall: punishes the whole bond"},{"left":"“We'll ask when we want advice”","right":"A bridge: holds the line, keeps the tie"}],"relearn":"Kind boundaries build bridges. They hold a line while keeping the door open; spite and silence build walls.","persona":"any","source":"bridge not wall"},
  {"id":"fm-1028","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"match","hook":"Match each couple-boundary with the family-team value it stands on.","pairs":[{"left":"“We'll decide together first”","right":"The couple is the first team"},{"left":"“Let's talk and get back to you”","right":"One united, repeatable answer"},{"left":"Refusing private side-complaints","right":"No triangulation between us"},{"left":"Backing your partner in the room","right":"Standing as a team, kindly"},{"left":"A warm, clear shared limit","right":"Honouring family and our decision"}],"relearn":"Every couple-boundary rests on a team value: deciding together, one answer, no side-deals, mutual backing.","persona":"Karthik","source":"team value"},
  {"id":"fm-1029","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"match","hook":"Match each blunt refusal with its respectful, relationship-keeping version.","pairs":[{"left":"“No, and stop asking.”","right":"“Not this time, but thank you for thinking of us.”"},{"left":"“That's a stupid idea.”","right":"“We see it differently; here's what we'd prefer.”"},{"left":"“We'll never agree to that.”","right":"“This one isn't for us, and we appreciate the offer.”"},{"left":"“You always interfere.”","right":"“We'd love to handle this part ourselves.”"},{"left":"“Forget it.”","right":"“Let's find a way that works for everyone.”"}],"relearn":"A respectful refusal declines the ask but honours the person.","persona":"Sneha","source":"respectful no"},
  {"id":"fm-1030","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"match","hook":"Match each warm boundary line with the moment it's made for.","pairs":[{"left":"“We'd love a call before you visit.”","right":"Unannounced drop-ins"},{"left":"“This one's ours to decide, with love.”","right":"Pressure on a couple's choice"},{"left":"“We'll ask when we want advice.”","right":"Unsolicited instructions"},{"left":"“We'll share news when there's news.”","right":"The baby question"},{"left":"“Some things stay just between us.”","right":"Private matters leaking out"}],"relearn":"Each warm line is matched to the moment it defuses: a kind boundary fits the situation it's made for.","persona":"Pooja","source":"line for the moment"},
  {"id":"fm-1031","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two that are cold cut-offs, not kind boundaries.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"“We'd love a call before you come over.”","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Blocking their number after one disagreement","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"“We'll decide this together and tell you.”","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Months of icy silence to teach them a lesson","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"“Visit Sunday; we plan the rest ourselves.”","trick":false}],"why":"Blocking after one fight and long icy silence are cut-offs that punish the bond, not warm, open boundaries.","relearn":"A kind boundary stays warm and open; silent freeze-outs and spite-blocks are walls, not limits.","persona":"any","source":"cut-off vs limit"},
  {"id":"fm-1032","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two harsh lines that wound, not the kind ways to hold a limit.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"“We'd love your recipes, and I'll cook my way too.”","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"“Stop barging into our kitchen.”","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"“Thanks for caring; this one's ours to decide.”","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"“Mind your own business for once.”","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"“We'll ask when we'd like your advice.”","trick":false}],"why":"“Stop barging in” and “mind your own business” attack the person; the others hold the same limit warmly.","relearn":"The same limit can be held with warmth; harsh phrasing wounds without making the line any firmer.","persona":"Pooja","source":"harsh vs warm"},
  {"id":"fm-1033","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two that are control, not a healthy couple-boundary.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"“Let's plan both families' visits together, fairly.”","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"“You can't visit your own parents anymore.”","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"“We'll tell family our news once we're ready.”","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"“Give me your phone; I decide who you talk to.”","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"“Let's handle our money as a team.”","trick":false}],"why":"Cutting you off from your parents and policing your phone are control, not boundaries. That routes to help.","relearn":"A boundary protects the couple; isolating you or policing your phone is control, not a limit.","persona":"Karthik","source":"limit vs control"},
  {"id":"fm-1034","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two replies that just cave, not the ones that hold the line.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"“We hear you; we'll decide and let you know.”","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Saying yes on the spot to stop the pushing","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"“Give us a week, then we'll share.”","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Cancelling your plan because she frowned","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"“We love you, and this stays our call.”","trick":false}],"why":"Saying yes to stop the pushing and cancelling because she frowned are caving. They trade your choice for quiet.","relearn":"You can hold a limit warmly under pressure; caving to end discomfort buries your choice.","persona":"Sneha","source":"hold vs cave"},
  {"id":"fm-1035","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that shut family out, not the ones that keep them close.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"“Come for dinner Sunday; we'll plan the rest.”","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"“We're done with this family for good.”","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"“Visit anytime, just give us a call first.”","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Skipping every event to punish them","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"“We'd love your help, our own way too.”","trick":false}],"why":"“Done for good” and skipping everything to punish are walls; a kind boundary holds a line while keeping people close.","relearn":"A kind boundary keeps people in your life; total cut-offs and punishing absences wall them out.","persona":"Pooja","source":"close not cut"},
  {"id":"fm-1036","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two that are pressure dressed as 'love', not genuine care.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"“Take your time; we just want you happy.”","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"“If you loved us, you'd obey without question.”","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"“We're here whenever you'd like advice.”","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"“A good bahu never says no to family.”","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"“Your choice; we'll support whatever you pick.”","trick":false}],"why":"“If you loved us, you'd obey” and “a good bahu never says no” use love as leverage to remove your choice.","relearn":"Real care leaves room for your no; love used to force obedience is pressure, not love.","persona":"any","source":"care vs pressure"},
  {"id":"fm-1037","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two triangulation moves, not the team-keeping ones.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"“If something's wrong, let's all talk kindly together.”","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"“Don't tell your wife, but do this for me.”","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"“We decide together, then answer as one.”","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"“Your husband's the problem; side with me.”","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"“Let's get back to you once we've talked.”","trick":false}],"why":"Secret side-deals and “side with me against your partner” are triangulation; the rest keep the couple a team.","relearn":"Triangulation routes complaints around your partner; a team decides and answers as one.","persona":"Karthik","source":"triangulation"},
  {"id":"fm-1038","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a 'no' to one request isn't a 'no' to the whole person. You can decline and still love them. Sit with that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I can do that","No isn't rejection","Worth trying"],"affirm":"A kind no declines a behaviour, not the bond; you can hold a limit and keep loving the person fully.","relearn":"A no to one ask isn't rejection of the person; you can decline and still love them.","persona":"Pooja","source":"no isn't rejection"},
  {"id":"fm-1039","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: think of a limit you wish you'd set sooner. What stopped you was probably fear of seeming unkind, na?","prompt":"What comes up?","options":["Fear of unkindness","Wanting to keep peace","Habit of adjusting"],"affirm":"Whatever held you back is human; a kind limit is itself an act of care, not unkindness.","relearn":"What stops us setting limits is often fear of seeming unkind; a kind limit is care, not cruelty.","persona":"any","source":"what stops us"},
  {"id":"fm-1040","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'we'll decide together and get back to you' is one line you can reuse for years. Could that help you?","prompt":"How's that sit?","options":["I'll use it","One steady line","Saves a fight"],"affirm":"A repeatable team line lets the couple answer as one without a fresh argument every time.","relearn":"One reusable team line like 'we'll decide together and get back to you' saves a fresh fight each time.","persona":"Karthik","source":"one steady line"},
  {"id":"fm-1041","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: receiving the love and declining the overreach can happen in the same warm breath. Picture it?","prompt":"What do you feel?","options":["I can picture it","Love and limit","Both at once"],"affirm":"You can thank someone for caring and still hold your line. Warmth and a boundary aren't opposites.","relearn":"You can receive the love and decline the overreach in the same sentence; they aren't opposites.","persona":"Pooja","source":"love and limit"},
  {"id":"fm-1042","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a boundary that mildly disappoints someone isn't a failed boundary. You can't control every reaction. Agree?","prompt":"Your honest read?","options":["That's freeing","Not my fault","I'll allow it"],"affirm":"You're responsible for your kindness, not for someone's reaction; mild disappointment is allowed.","relearn":"A kind limit can still disappoint a little; you control your kindness, not their reaction.","persona":"Sneha","source":"reaction isn't yours"},
  {"id":"fm-1043","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: limits flex with the season. A 'just for now' boundary after a baby or a job change is completely real. Makes sense?","prompt":"What's your view?","options":["Makes sense","For-now is real","Seasons change"],"affirm":"A temporary boundary is honest, not weak; needs shift with newborns, jobs and health, and that's okay.","relearn":"A 'just for now' limit is real and valid; boundaries can flex with the season of life.","persona":"any","source":"for-now is real"},
  {"id":"fm-1044","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a kind boundary protects the relationship you want to keep. It's an act of staying, not leaving. Land for you?","prompt":"What lands?","options":["It lands","Staying, not leaving","Protects the bond"],"affirm":"Boundaries aren't a step toward the exit; they're how you protect a bond you want to keep for the long haul.","relearn":"A kind boundary is an act of staying, not leaving; it protects the bond you want to keep.","persona":"Pooja","source":"staying not leaving"},
  {"id":"fm-1045","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: backing your partner warmly in the room isn't choosing sides against family. It's being a team. Sit right?","prompt":"How's that?","options":["Sits right","Team, not sides","I'll back them"],"affirm":"Standing with your partner is teamwork, not betrayal of family; a united, kind front lowers conflict for everyone.","relearn":"Backing your partner in the room is being a team, not choosing sides against family.","persona":"Karthik","source":"team not sides"},
  {"id":"fm-1046","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"role-play","persona":"Pooja","source":"run our home","hook":"Your mother-in-law has strong ideas about how a house should run.","setup":"You come home to find the routine changed again.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"I moved things around. It's more efficient this way.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I love your help, Ma, and I'd like to run our home my own way too.\"","then":"She pauses. \"Alright, fair,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"That's so helpful. Thank you, Ma.\"","then":"She says: \"Good, I'll keep helping then,\" and continues."},{"text":"\"Please stop moving my things.\"","then":"She says: \"I was only trying to help,\" sounding hurt."},{"text":"\"Okay, sure, whatever's easier.\"","then":"She says: \"Good, I knew you'd come around,\" pleased."}],"why":"Welcoming her care while naming the limit protects your home kindly."},{"prompt":"A few days later she brings it up again: \"I only want to help, you know, since you're both so busy.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I know, and I appreciate it. I just like to keep some of it my own way.\"","then":"She relaxes. \"Good, that's all I wanted to hear.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's fine. Just don't do it again.\"","then":"She says: \"So I should just stay out of it, then?\""},{"text":"\"We're not that busy, honestly.\"","then":"She says: \"Well, it still seemed that way to me.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe you're right. We do need more help than I've been willing to admit.\"","then":"She says: \"See, I knew it,\" and keeps rearranging."}],"why":"Acknowledging her intention keeps the moment warm while the limit still holds."},{"prompt":"Aman says: \"Ma told me she's not sure what her place in the house is these days.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's find her a way to help that we're both happy with.\"","then":"Aman says: \"I like that. Let's think of something together.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"That's not true. She does plenty.\"","then":"Aman says: \"That's not really what she meant, though.\""},{"text":"\"She'll come around eventually.\"","then":"Aman says: \"I'm not sure she will, honestly.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe let her help with one small thing again, just to see how it goes.\"","then":"Aman says: \"Okay, but let's watch that it stays small.\""}],"why":"Finding a real way for her to help keeps her close without losing your own system."},{"prompt":"A month later she says: \"I really enjoyed helping you plan the festival menu, by the way.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Me too, Ma. Let's do that again next time.\"","then":"She beams. \"Yes, let's do that. I already have ideas.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"That's good to hear.\"","then":"She says: \"It really was fun for me,\" a little wistful."},{"text":"\"See, that was a better way to help.\"","then":"She says: \"I suppose it was,\" a touch deflated."},{"text":"\"Let's not make it a regular thing, though.\"","then":"She says: \"Oh. Alright, then,\" a little disappointed."}],"why":"Building on the help she enjoyed keeps the relationship warm and the boundary intact."}],"relearn":"Welcome the help and keep your own way in one kind sentence; that's a warm, clear limit."},
  {"id":"fm-1047","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"no secrets","hook":"A relative pulls you aside about a money matter, lowering their voice.","setup":"They lean in, expecting you to agree quietly.","steps":[{"prompt":"They say: \"Just don't mention this to Divya. It's simpler that way.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I don't keep money secrets from her. Let's talk about it together.\"","then":"They hesitate. \"Alright, fine, let's include her, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Okay, tell me the details first.\"","then":"They say: \"Good, here's what I need,\" and continue."},{"text":"\"Fine, but just this small thing.\"","then":"They say: \"Perfect, and next time too, alright?\""},{"text":"\"I'll decide if it's worth telling her.\"","then":"They say: \"That works. Just keep it between us for now.\""}],"why":"Refusing the secret upfront keeps money decisions a shared, honest matter."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, the same relative tries a similar ask about something new: \"It's really not a big deal. Why involve her at all?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Big or small, we handle money together.\"","then":"They shrug. \"Alright, alright, let's bring her in, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll just handle it quietly, then.\"","then":"They say: \"Good, that's easier for everyone.\""},{"text":"\"You might be right. It's pretty small.\"","then":"They say: \"Exactly, so let's just keep it simple.\""},{"text":"\"I'll tell her eventually. Don't worry.\"","then":"They say: \"No rush at all, whenever suits you.\""}],"why":"Repeating the same rule, even under pressure, keeps the couple genuinely a team."},{"prompt":"That evening Divya asks: \"Did something come up with your cousin today?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, let me tell you exactly what they asked.\"","then":"Divya says: \"Thank you for telling me straight away.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing important. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"Divya says: \"You seem a little off, though.\""},{"text":"\"Just some family thing, boring stuff.\"","then":"Divya says: \"What kind of family thing, exactly?\""},{"text":"\"I'll tell you properly another time.\"","then":"Divya says: \"Alright, but don't forget, okay?\""}],"why":"Telling her plainly, the same day, keeps no secrets between the two of you."}],"relearn":"Refusing a secret side-deal keeps the couple a team and shuts triangulation down kindly."},
  {"id":"fm-1048","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"role-play","persona":"Sneha","source":"settle down","hook":"Relatives love asking about your plans, and today's function is no exception.","setup":"An uncle catches you near the buffet table, question ready.","steps":[{"prompt":"He asks: \"So when are you settling down, finally?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm happy as I am. News comes when there's news.\"","then":"He nods, a little surprised. \"Alright, fair enough,\" he says.","best":true},{"text":"\"I don't know, maybe soon, I guess.\"","then":"He says: \"Good, I'll tell your aunty that.\""},{"text":"\"You should ask my mother. She has opinions.\"","then":"He says: \"Good idea. Let me find her.\""},{"text":"\"Why does everyone ask me this?\"","then":"He says: \"We just care. That's all,\" a bit taken aback."}],"why":"A calm, warm line closes the topic without owing anyone your timeline."},{"prompt":"At the next function, an aunt tries a gentler version: \"No pressure, just curious how things are going.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Things are good. I'm in no rush.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Good, that's what matters,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let me tell you everything. Then we can stop the questions.\"","then":"She says: \"Wonderful, go on,\" leaning in, ready to pass it along."},{"text":"\"You know how it is these days.\"","then":"She says: \"I suppose,\" filling in the blanks herself."},{"text":"\"Can we talk about something else?\"","then":"She says: \"Of course, sorry,\" a little taken aback."}],"why":"A warm, honest answer respects the gentler ask while still holding the same line."},{"prompt":"Months later, a cousin asks the same question, half joking: \"Still no news for us?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Still happy, still no news. You'll be the first to know.\"","then":"The cousin laughs. \"Fair, I'll wait then,\" he says.","best":true},{"text":"\"You people never stop asking, do you?\"","then":"He says: \"It was just a joke,\" a little stung."},{"text":"\"Maybe I should hurry up. Everyone keeps asking.\"","then":"He says: \"Don't rush for our sake,\" suddenly awkward."},{"text":"\"There's nothing to tell, obviously.\"","then":"He says: \"Alright, alright, just asking,\" backing off."}],"why":"A light, warm version of the same answer keeps the topic closed without any edge."}],"relearn":"A calm, warm line honours the asker and closes the topic without owing anyone your timeline."},
  {"id":"fm-1049","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"role-play","persona":"Pooja","source":"my friendships","hook":"Your father-in-law has never liked one of your close friends.","setup":"He brings it up seriously, over evening tea.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"That friend isn't good company. You should stop seeing her.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I respect that, Papa. My friendships are mine.\"","then":"He pauses. \"Alright,\" he says, surprised by the calm answer.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll think about seeing her less.\"","then":"He says: \"Good, I think that's wise.\""},{"text":"\"Alright, I'll stop seeing her if you say so.\"","then":"He says: \"Good, I'm glad you understand.\""},{"text":"\"You don't even know her properly.\"","then":"He says: \"I know enough,\" folding his arms."}],"why":"You can honour his worry and still keep your choice. Respect isn't obedience."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, he asks about your friend, gentler this time: \"I only worry about you, you know.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I know, and I appreciate that. I'm still fine.\"","then":"He nods slowly. \"Alright. I trust your judgement, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's fine. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"He says: \"I still worry, though,\" not quite reassured."},{"text":"\"You worry about the wrong things sometimes.\"","then":"He says: \"I only meant well,\" a little stung."},{"text":"\"Maybe I'll see her a little less, for your peace of mind.\"","then":"He says: \"Thank you, that eases my mind.\""}],"why":"Acknowledging his worry while holding steady shows respect without changing your choice."},{"prompt":"Aman says: \"Papa raised your friend with me too, earlier, hoping I'd talk sense into you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thanks for telling me. I'll decide this one, and I'd like your view.\"","then":"Aman nods. \"I told him it's yours to decide. I'm glad you'll hear me out too.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"What did you tell him?\"","then":"Aman says: \"I told him I'd mention it. That's all.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe he has a point, though.\"","then":"Aman says: \"Wait, really? That surprises me.\""},{"text":"\"Tell him to stop bringing it up to you too.\"","then":"Aman says: \"I'll try, but he's persistent.\""}],"why":"Owning the choice while asking for your partner's view keeps the couple, and the decision, aligned."},{"prompt":"Months later, your father-in-law meets your friend at a family event. He says: \"She is lovely. I was wrong to worry.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm glad you two finally met properly, Papa.\"","then":"He smiles. \"Alright, alright, you were right about her.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Told you so, didn't I?\"","then":"He says: \"Yes, yes, you did,\" a touch sheepish."},{"text":"\"So you finally trust my judgement now?\"","then":"He says: \"I suppose I do,\" a little put on the spot."},{"text":"\"Better late than never, I suppose.\"","then":"He says: \"Fair point,\" the warmth dimming slightly."}],"why":"Welcoming the shift warmly lets the relationship actually move forward."}],"relearn":"You can honour an elder's concern and still keep your own friendships; respect isn't obedience."},
  {"id":"fm-1050","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"fair rhythm","hook":"Your mother insists you and Divya visit every weekend; you both need some of your own.","setup":"Your phone rings, and it is her.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks: \"You're both coming this Sunday, right, like usual?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll come two Sundays a month, Ma. We'd love you to visit too.\"","then":"She pauses. \"Oh. I'd like that. Alright, let's try.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Of course, Ma. We'll be there every single weekend.\"","then":"She says: \"Good, see you then, as always.\""},{"text":"\"We'll see how the week goes, Ma. I'll call Sunday.\"","then":"She says: \"So, are you coming or not?\" still unsure."},{"text":"\"Divya wants a break, actually. Every weekend is a lot.\"","then":"She says: \"A break? Is everything alright between you two?\""}],"why":"A fair, shared rhythm keeps you close to her without costing every weekend."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, she calls about the coming Sunday: \"So what is the plan this week?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We can do two Sundays a month, Ma. We'd love to host you too.\"","then":"She says: \"Alright, I'll look forward to that, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Ma, please stop checking in every single week.\"","then":"She says: \"Fine, fine,\" sounding a little brushed off."},{"text":"\"Maybe we'll swing by for a bit, if we can manage it.\"","then":"She says: \"Good, I'll expect you, then.\""},{"text":"\"It's Divya's idea to cut back, Ma. I didn't want to at all.\"","then":"She says: \"Divya said that? Oh, I see,\" her voice cooling."}],"why":"A warm reassurance with a specific plan makes the rhythm feel secure, not distant."},{"prompt":"Your mother says: \"A relative told me you two are pulling away from the family. Is that so?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're not pulling away, Ma. We love seeing you, and we'll make time.\"","then":"She relaxes. \"Good. I don't like hearing it from others,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"Whoever said that doesn't know us. Let's not worry about them, Ma.\"","then":"She says: \"It still worries me, though,\" not reassured."},{"text":"\"I'll go tell that relative to mind their own business, Ma.\"","then":"She says: \"There's no need for that,\" a little alarmed."},{"text":"\"Fine, we'll come every weekend, then. Please stop worrying.\"","then":"She says: \"Oh, wonderful, see you then,\" pleased."}],"why":"Reassuring her directly, and promising time, answers the worry behind the gossip."}],"relearn":"A warm, fair rhythm keeps the bond close while leaving the couple some weekends of its own."},
  {"id":"fm-1051","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"role-play","persona":"Pooja","source":"our finances","hook":"Your finances are apparently everyone's business at family gatherings.","setup":"Your aunt sits down beside you with her tea.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks: \"So how much do you both make together, roughly?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We keep our finances between us, Aunty.\"","then":"She smiles, a little surprised. \"Oh, alright then,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"Um, well, it's around... let me tell you.\"","then":"She leans in. \"Yes, go on, roughly is fine.\""},{"text":"\"Enough to get by, I suppose.\"","then":"She says: \"Is it more than your cousin's, though?\""},{"text":"\"Why do you want to know?\"","then":"She says: \"No reason, just curious,\" a little defensive."}],"why":"A warm, private line holds the boundary without any offence."},{"prompt":"Your aunt keeps at it: \"I only ask to plan the festival gifts properly.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's kind, but really, our finances stay private.\"","then":"She says: \"Alright, fair enough,\" a little disarmed.","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe just a rough range, then.\"","then":"She says: \"Good, that helps a lot, thank you.\""},{"text":"\"You don't need to know that for a gift.\"","then":"She says: \"I was only trying to help,\" a little stung."},{"text":"\"Ask Aman. Maybe he'll tell you.\"","then":"She says: \"Good idea. I'll ask him then.\""}],"why":"Repeating the same warm line, even with a new reason offered, keeps it truly private."},{"prompt":"At the next family event, a different relative asks: \"So, how much do you two actually make?\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's between us. We keep it private, always.\"","then":"The relative laughs. \"Fair enough. Can't blame me for trying.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Ask my aunt. She was curious too.\"","then":"The relative says: \"Oh, I will, thanks for the tip.\""},{"text":"\"Honestly, this family asks too many questions.\"","then":"The relative says: \"Ha, fair point,\" repeating it as a joke later."},{"text":"\"It's really not that much. Don't worry.\"","then":"The relative says: \"So roughly how much, then?\""}],"why":"The same steady, warm line works with every relative who asks, not just the first."}],"relearn":"Your earnings are the couple's own; a warm, private line holds that without offence."},
  {"id":"fm-1052","cat":"kind-boundaries","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"we decided","hook":"In front of family, a relative turns to Divya about a decision the two of you made together.","setup":"Everyone looks at Divya, waiting for her to explain herself.","steps":[{"prompt":"The relative says: \"Why did you make them do that? It wasn't a good idea.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Actually, we decided this together. It was both of us.\"","then":"The relative pauses. \"Oh, I see,\" they say, a little surprised.","best":true},{"text":"\"She just went along with it, really.\"","then":"The relative says: \"So Divya simply went along with it?\" and turns to her."},{"text":"\"It wasn't the best idea, honestly. I'll admit that much.\"","then":"The relative says: \"See, I knew it,\" looking at Divya."},{"text":"\"Ask Divya. It was mostly her call.\"","then":"The relative turns to Divya. \"Well? Was it?\""}],"why":"Naming the decision as shared, right away, backs Divya in the room."},{"prompt":"Later, alone with you, Divya says: \"That got a bit awkward back there.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's not fair to you. I'll always say it was both of us.\"","then":"Divya says: \"Thank you. That means a lot.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I just didn't want it to be awkward.\"","then":"Divya says: \"So it wasn't really for me, then?\""},{"text":"\"You could speak up for yourself a bit more too, you know.\"","then":"Divya says: \"I do, but it doesn't always land.\""},{"text":"\"It's fine. Don't make a big deal of it.\"","then":"Divya says: \"It is a big deal, to me.\""}],"why":"Promising to always name it as shared, going forward, rebuilds her trust in the team."},{"prompt":"A cousin later asks you privately: \"Was that decision really both of yours, or just hers?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Both of ours, equally, start to finish.\"","then":"The cousin says: \"Alright, good to know,\" and the story settles.","best":true},{"text":"\"Mostly hers, but I agreed.\"","then":"The cousin says: \"Ah, so it was her idea, then.\""},{"text":"\"Why does it matter whose idea it was?\"","then":"The cousin says: \"Just curious. That's all,\" still unclear."},{"text":"\"Honestly, I don't remember the details.\"","then":"The cousin says: \"Someone said it was her plan, though.\""}],"why":"A clear, equal answer every time keeps the shared decision genuinely shared."},{"prompt":"Months later, after a similar decision goes well, a relative says: \"That worked out nicely for you both.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're glad. We decided this one together too.\"","then":"Divya smiles. \"That's true, and it feels good to hear you say it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Thanks. It was mostly luck.\"","then":"The relative says: \"Modest as ever,\" and moves on with nobody credited."},{"text":"\"It worked because I kept us on track.\"","then":"The relative says: \"Good for you, then.\" Divya's smile fades a little."},{"text":"\"I mostly just went along with her plan.\"","then":"The relative says: \"So it was her plan, really,\" nodding."}],"why":"Naming both credit and blame as shared, consistently, is what makes 'team' really mean it."}],"relearn":"Backing your partner in the room is teamwork, not picking sides; silence leaves them exposed."},
  {"id":"fm-1080","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A respectful son just stays quiet and never contradicts his parents.\"","myth":{"un":"A respectful son just stays quiet and never contradicts his parents.","re":"You can honour your parents and still voice a different view, kindly and clearly.","why":"Honouring isn't the same as having no voice of your own."},"relearn":"Respect lets you disagree with care; staying silent isn't the only way to honour parents.","persona":"Karthik","source":"respect not silence","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1081","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Elders never have to apologise; respect only flows upward.\"","myth":{"un":"Elders never have to apologise; respect only flows upward.","re":"Respect runs both ways across generations; elders can own a mistake too, and that models real respect.","why":"Respect owed in only one direction isn't respect, it's hierarchy."},"relearn":"Respect flows both ways; an elder owning a mistake teaches it better than demanding it one-way.","persona":"Razia","source":"respect runs both ways","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1082","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If you don't follow an elder's advice exactly, you've insulted them.\"","myth":{"un":"If you don't follow an elder's advice exactly, you've insulted them.","re":"You can thank an elder, weigh their advice sincerely, and still make a different choice.","why":"Taking advice seriously and obeying it are not the same thing."},"relearn":"Honour the advice, then decide for yourselves; choosing differently isn't an insult.","persona":"Pooja","source":"honour then choose","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1083","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Tradition can never change, so just do it the way it's always been done.\"","myth":{"un":"Tradition can never change, so just do it the way it's always been done.","re":"You can keep a tradition's meaning while adapting how you do it, with the family's blessing over time.","why":"Living traditions have always shifted; honouring one allows updating it."},"relearn":"Keep a tradition's meaning, adapt the form together; honouring it doesn't mean freezing it.","persona":"Sneha","source":"tradition you can shape","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1084","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"As long as everyone obeys, the family is respectful and happy.\"","myth":{"un":"As long as everyone obeys, the family is respectful and happy.","re":"Obedience driven by fear isn't respect; a home built on fear isn't the same as one built on respect.","why":"Respect is freely given; fear is extracted, and the two feel very different."},"relearn":"Fear-driven obedience isn't respect; a home on fear differs from a home on respect.","persona":"Pooja","source":"fear isn't respect","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1085","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"In any disagreement with an elder, the younger one is automatically wrong.\"","myth":{"un":"In any disagreement with an elder, the younger one is automatically wrong.","re":"Age doesn't decide who's right; a younger person can hold a fair point and still be respectful.","why":"Wisdom often comes with age, but being older doesn't make every view correct."},"relearn":"Age doesn't settle who's right; a younger person can be both correct and respectful.","persona":"Karthik","source":"both can be right","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1086","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Keeping your own beliefs after marriage disrespects your in-laws' ways.\"","myth":{"un":"Keeping your own beliefs after marriage disrespects your in-laws' ways.","re":"You can hold your own beliefs and still respect your in-laws'; mutual respect doesn't require erasing yours.","why":"Two sets of beliefs can sit side by side with respect on both sides."},"relearn":"You can keep your own beliefs and respect theirs; respect doesn't mean erasing yourself.","persona":"Razia","source":"keeping faith respectfully","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1087","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If you're truly respectful, you'll never push back on an elder's plan for you.\"","myth":{"un":"If you're truly respectful, you'll never push back on an elder's plan for you.","re":"Pushing back warmly on a plan you didn't choose is respectful; it honours both the elder and your own life.","why":"Real respect includes honesty about your own direction."},"relearn":"Warm pushback on a plan you didn't choose is still respect; it honours the elder and your life.","persona":"Sneha","source":"respect deflects pressure","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1088","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Disagreeing with an elder is always rude, no matter how you say it.\"","myth":{"un":"Disagreeing with an elder is always rude, no matter how you say it.","re":"It's how you disagree that matters; warm phrasing that honours the person keeps both the bond and your voice.","why":"Tone and care turn a disagreement into a respectful one."},"relearn":"Disagreeing respectfully, warmly and honouring the person, keeps both the bond and your voice.","persona":"any","source":"disagree warmly","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1089","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"You have to choose between respecting elders and ever saying no to them.\"","myth":{"un":"You have to choose between respecting elders and ever saying no to them.","re":"Respect and a kind boundary go together; respecting an elder can make your 'no' land more gently.","why":"A respected elder hears a limit better than a resented one."},"relearn":"Respect and a kind no aren't opposites; respect can make the boundary land softer.","persona":"Pooja","source":"respect aids the no","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1090","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Honouring your elders and obeying your elders mean exactly the same thing.\"","myth":{"un":"Honouring your elders and obeying your elders mean exactly the same thing.","re":"Honouring is about care, attention and respect; obeying is doing as told, and you can do the first without the second.","why":"You can deeply honour someone whose instruction you don't follow."},"relearn":"Honour is care and respect; obeying is doing as told; you can honour without obeying.","persona":"Razia","source":"honour without obeying","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1091","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Sneha","source":"when will you settle","hook":"You love your extended family, but the marriage question follows you into every gathering.","steps":[{"prompt":"At your cousin's wedding, your aunt asks loudly why you're still not married. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say warmly you know she cares, and you'll get there in your own time","then":"She smiles and moves on to complain about the caterer instead.","best":true},{"text":"Change the subject and hope she drops it","then":"She circles back to the question twice more before dinner."},{"text":"Say nothing at all and let the moment pass awkwardly, hoping nobody notices","then":"The silence draws a few curious looks from nearby relatives."},{"text":"Tell her firmly it's none of her business","then":"She looks stung, and word of the sharp reply spreads before dessert."}],"why":"Receiving her care while holding your pace answers her without inviting more pressure."},{"prompt":"Later a different relative brings up the same question at the sweets table. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer warmly: you'll get there when you're ready","then":"The relative laughs and says, 'Fair, fair,' handing you a laddoo instead.","best":true},{"text":"Give a vague excuse about being too busy","then":"The relative asks a follow-up about exactly what's keeping you busy."},{"text":"Walk away from the sweets table entirely","then":"You dodge the question but miss out on the conversation too."},{"text":"Point out they're unmarried too and ask them back","then":"It turns into an awkward back and forth others start watching."}],"why":"A warm, calm answer, repeated as needed, saves you a fresh fight each time."},{"prompt":"At the next family wedding, the same aunt brings it up again, with your parents nearby. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer warmly and calmly, right in front of everyone","then":"Your parents relax, seeing you handle it without any fuss.","best":true},{"text":"Let your parents answer for you this time","then":"Your parents fumble an answer, and you feel it wasn't really theirs to give."},{"text":"Pretend you didn't hear the question","then":"The aunt just asks louder, and the moment gets more awkward."},{"text":"Snap that everyone needs to stop asking, for good","then":"The sharpness surprises your parents, and the mood turns tense."}],"why":"Staying steady and warm in front of your parents shows them it's genuinely handled."}],"debrief":"Honour the aunt's care and hold your own timeline; a warm line deflects the pressure without a family war.","relearn":"Receive the care and keep your timeline; warmth deflects the pressure without rejecting her."},
  {"id":"fm-1092","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"father's career plan","hook":"Your father assumed you'd join the family business; you've built a different career you love.","steps":[{"prompt":"At dinner your father brings up handing you the business again. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Thank him, and say you love the career you've built instead","then":"He goes quiet for a moment, clearly turning it over.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll think about joining someday","then":"He brightens, assuming it's only a matter of time."},{"text":"Change the subject before he can push further","then":"The topic just resurfaces at the next family dinner."},{"text":"Tell him plainly the business was never really your dream anyway","then":"He looks hurt, hearing it land harder than you meant."}],"why":"Gratitude plus honesty about your path lets him hear it without feeling dismissed."},{"prompt":"A week later, he brings up the business again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say plainly, but gently, that you love your career","then":"He nods slowly. 'I'd rather know the truth,' he says.","best":true},{"text":"Hint that you might reconsider someday, to soften the moment","then":"He brightens, holding onto the idea that it's still possible."},{"text":"Try to steer the talk to something else","then":"He lets it go for the moment, though you sense it isn't finished."},{"text":"Say he never really asked what you actually wanted from your own life","then":"He bristles, hearing it as an accusation rather than an answer."}],"why":"Saying your path plainly, without wavering, is what finally gets heard."},{"prompt":"Your mother mentions your father's been telling relatives you'll 'come around eventually.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Talk to him directly and ask him to tell relatives your actual path","then":"He looks surprised, then agrees to set the story straight.","best":true},{"text":"Let it go entirely, since correcting him feels like more trouble than it's worth","then":"Relatives keep asking you about the business at every gathering."},{"text":"Vent to your mother instead of talking to him","then":"Your mother stays caught in the middle, unsure what to do."},{"text":"Correct him loudly in front of relatives","then":"He's embarrassed publicly, and the real conversation gets harder."}],"why":"Asking him directly fixes the story at its source instead of living with it."},{"prompt":"At a family function your father introduces you and, for once, mentions your actual work with pride. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank him warmly, right there, for saying it that way","then":"He smiles, and the moment settles something between you both.","best":true},{"text":"Just smile and say nothing about it","then":"The moment passes, sweet but a little incomplete."},{"text":"Joke that it took him long enough","then":"The joke lands awkwardly, and he looks unsure how to respond."},{"text":"Bring up how hard the years of his quiet disapproval really were","then":"The warm moment turns heavy, and he looks caught off guard."}],"why":"Meeting his pride with thanks in the moment lets the change actually land."}],"debrief":"Thank him and own your path warmly; honouring him doesn't mean taking the path he planned.","relearn":"Honour his hope and keep your own career; gratitude plus clarity holds both."},
  {"id":"fm-1093","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"fasting tradition","hook":"Your mother-in-law expects you to keep a strict fast you don't observe.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks if you'll join the fast this month. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll join the prayers, and skip the fast itself","then":"She nods, and shows you which prayers matter most to her.","best":true},{"text":"Agree to fast just this once to avoid a fuss","then":"You struggle through the day, quietly resentful by evening."},{"text":"Say you're not feeling well that day","then":"She looks concerned, and you feel a little guilty about the excuse."},{"text":"Say plainly that fasting like that just doesn't make sense to you","then":"She looks taken aback, and the room goes quiet for a moment."}],"why":"Joining the prayers honours the day without asking you to fake belief."},{"prompt":"Later that day, a relative asks how you're managing the fast this year. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say warmly that you join in the way that feels right to you","then":"The relative shrugs. 'Whatever works for you,' she says easily.","best":true},{"text":"Say your mother-in-law already knows, so it's fine","then":"The relative looks unconvinced and asks your mother-in-law directly."},{"text":"Give a vague answer and change the subject","then":"The relative senses you're dodging, and brings it up again later."},{"text":"Say fasting rules are old-fashioned anyway","then":"The relative's face falls, and she quietly stops talking to you."}],"why":"A calm, honest answer about your own way settles it without extra explaining."},{"prompt":"The following year the same fast comes around again. What's your approach?","options":[{"text":"Offer to join the prayers, and skip the fast","then":"Nobody blinks. It becomes simply how you do the day.","best":true},{"text":"Fast fully this time to keep things simple","then":"You go along with it, though it isn't really what you'd choose."},{"text":"Avoid the house that day entirely","then":"You miss the family gathering, and your mother-in-law notices your absence."},{"text":"Announce you'll never observe any of it going forward","then":"The declaration lands harder than the day itself needed."}],"why":"A steady, repeated choice becomes normal, without a fresh debate each time."}],"debrief":"Share what's meaningful about the day, and hold your own line on the fast.","relearn":"Join the meaningful parts and decline the fast kindly, honouring the bond and yourself."},
  {"id":"fm-1094","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Razia","source":"elder dismisses your view","hook":"An elder in your family still treats your opinions as a child's, even now that you're grown.","steps":[{"prompt":"The elder waves off your opinion at dinner: \"You're too young to understand.\" What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Stay calm and say you'd still love to share how you see it","then":"The elder pauses, then actually listens to what you say.","best":true},{"text":"Let it go and stay quiet for the rest of dinner","then":"You leave dinner feeling unheard, though nobody notices."},{"text":"Ask someone else at the table to speak for you","then":"They speak up, but it's not quite what you meant to say."},{"text":"Tell the elder plainly that's a rather old-fashioned way to think","then":"The table falls silent, and the elder looks offended."}],"why":"Staying calm while naming your wish to speak keeps the room and your voice both intact."},{"prompt":"The same elder cuts you off again later while you're mid-sentence. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say gently that you'd like to finish your thought first","then":"The elder blinks, then nods and lets you continue.","best":true},{"text":"Wait for a gap and try to say it fast","then":"You rush through it, and half your point gets lost."},{"text":"Give up on the point entirely","then":"The idea goes unsaid, and you're left thinking about it later."},{"text":"Raise your voice sharply just to be heard over them at all","then":"The table tenses, and the moment turns into a small scene."}],"why":"A gentle, direct ask usually gets you heard without a fight."},{"prompt":"At the next big family gathering, the elder actually asks what you think first. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer warmly and thank them for asking","then":"The elder looks pleased, and the exchange feels easy for once.","best":true},{"text":"Answer briefly, unsure if it's really welcome","then":"The elder waits for more, and the moment feels incomplete."},{"text":"Deflect the question back to someone else","then":"The elder looks a little confused, and the opening passes."},{"text":"Point out that they never used to ask before","then":"The elder looks stung by the reminder, and the mood shifts."}],"why":"Meeting the opening warmly rewards the shift and keeps it going."}],"debrief":"Hold your voice warmly; respect doesn't require swallowing your view to keep a fragile peace.","relearn":"Keep your voice calmly; you can honour the elder and still ask to be heard."},
  {"id":"fm-1095","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"respect runs both ways","hook":"An elder demands respect from you but speaks to you with constant contempt.","steps":[{"prompt":"At a family lunch the elder speaks to you sharply again, right after asking for your respect. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep treating them with dignity and stay steady","then":"The elder seems slightly thrown by your calm, and eases off.","best":true},{"text":"Apologise for whatever set it off, though you did nothing wrong","then":"The elder accepts it as due, and the sharp tone carries on."},{"text":"Ask another relative to intervene on your behalf","then":"The relative shrugs it off, and the elder's tone doesn't shift."},{"text":"Snap back with the same sharpness","then":"The exchange gets louder, and lunch turns tense for everyone."}],"why":"Holding dignity while the elder is sharp models the respect you're asking for."},{"prompt":"A few days later the elder calls and starts the conversation with a sharp comment again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay warm, and gently name that respect goes both ways","then":"There's a pause on the line, then a quieter, 'Fair point.'","best":true},{"text":"Keep the call as short as you can to get off the phone faster","then":"The call ends quickly, but nothing about the pattern shifts."},{"text":"Let the comment pass without a word","then":"The sharp tone carries on through the rest of the call."},{"text":"Hang up mid-sentence","then":"The elder is startled, and calls back annoyed rather than reflective."}],"why":"Naming it warmly, without matching the tone, is more likely to land."},{"prompt":"At the next family event, the elder greets you as usual at first. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Greet them warmly, and stay open to how it goes","then":"The elder seems a little softer, and the first part of the evening passes easily.","best":true},{"text":"Stay guarded until you see how they act","then":"The elder notices the guardedness and starts to pull back too."},{"text":"Bring up every past sharp comment right away","then":"The elder gets defensive, and the evening turns tense fast."},{"text":"Test them with a pointed comment of your own","then":"The elder tenses, and the evening turns awkward."}],"why":"Meeting the greeting warmly gives warmth a chance to take hold."},{"prompt":"Later that night, the elder slips back into an old sharp comment, out of habit. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Note it calmly, without matching the old sharpness","then":"The elder catches themselves. 'You're right, sorry,' they say.","best":true},{"text":"Let it go, since bringing it up again feels like too much","then":"The old pattern quietly starts creeping back in."},{"text":"Point out this is exactly the old pattern coming back","then":"The elder feels caught out, and turns defensive rather than sorry."},{"text":"Answer with a sharp comment of your own","then":"The elder answers in kind, and the evening ends on a sour note."}],"why":"Calling it out gently, again, keeps the old pattern from quietly returning."},{"prompt":"Months later, a younger cousin asks how you handle this elder's sharp moments. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you try to hold your dignity, and name it kindly when it happens","then":"The cousin nods, and says they'll try the same with their own family.","best":true},{"text":"Say you just learned to let most things go","then":"The cousin looks a little disappointed by the advice."},{"text":"Say the elder finally changed on their own","then":"The cousin assumes there's nothing they can do but wait."},{"text":"Say you basically had to fight hard for every single inch of respect","then":"The cousin looks worried, unsure if the relationship is even worth it."}],"why":"A steady, repeated approach, not a single fight, is what actually builds mutual respect."}],"debrief":"Hold dignity and name that respect runs both ways; modelling it beats matching contempt.","relearn":"Respect flows both ways; model dignity and name it, rather than mirroring the contempt."},
  {"id":"fm-1096","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Sneha","source":"unmarried at family event","hook":"Relatives keep pitying you for being single and 'pray you'll be saved' by marriage soon.","steps":[{"prompt":"At a cousin's engagement, a relative says this to your face. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Smile and say warmly that your life already feels full and happy","then":"The relative looks a little surprised, then smiles back.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh it off without really answering","then":"The relative takes the laugh as an invitation to say it again."},{"text":"Walk away to avoid the conversation","then":"The relative mentions it louder to whoever's standing nearby."},{"text":"Argue loudly that single life is actually better than marriage","then":"It turns into a debate, and the relative enjoys the drama."}],"why":"Holding your dignity warmly deflects the pity without starting a scene."},{"prompt":"Later that evening a different relative brings up the same pity. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Smile and say your life feels full and happy","then":"The relative nods, seemingly satisfied, and moves on to other gossip.","best":true},{"text":"Give a vague, joking answer to move past it","then":"The joke gets repeated as if you were actually upset."},{"text":"Pretend you didn't hear the comment","then":"The relative repeats it louder, thinking you missed it."},{"text":"Tell them firmly to mind their own business","then":"The sharpness gets noticed and talked about after the event."}],"why":"A calm, warm answer, repeated as needed, saves a fresh explanation each time."},{"prompt":"At the next big family wedding, your mother overhears the same pity and looks uncomfortable. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Reassure her warmly, right there, that you're genuinely happy","then":"Your mother relaxes, seeing you handle it with ease.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and let your mother handle the awkwardness","then":"Your mother fumbles a defence that doesn't quite land."},{"text":"Avoid your mother for the rest of the evening","then":"Your mother is left worrying about what you're really feeling."},{"text":"Tell your mother she should stop bringing you to these events","then":"Your mother looks hurt, unsure what she did wrong."}],"why":"Reassuring her directly settles the moment for you both, in front of everyone."}],"debrief":"Hold your dignity warmly; you can honour relatives and still decline their pity for your choices.","relearn":"Deflect the pity with warmth; your life is yours to define, not theirs to rescue."},
  {"id":"fm-1097","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"grandfather's strong opinion","hook":"Your grandfather pushes for a baby soon; you two aren't ready.","steps":[{"prompt":"At Sunday lunch he brings it up again in front of everyone. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Thank him for his love, and say the timing is yours and your partner's","then":"He nods, though you can tell he's still hoping.","best":true},{"text":"Say maybe next year, mostly just to make the awkward moment pass for now","then":"He brightens, already counting down to next year."},{"text":"Change the subject to something else","then":"He circles back to it before lunch even ends."},{"text":"Tell him it's really none of his concern","then":"He looks hurt, and the table goes quiet for a moment."}],"why":"Receiving his love while keeping the timing yours honours him without handing over the choice."},{"prompt":"A week later he calls just to ask again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer warmly and gently again, without irritation","then":"He sighs. 'Alright, alright,' he says, sounding a little more settled.","best":true},{"text":"Keep the call short to avoid the topic","then":"He senses you're avoiding him, and calls again sooner than usual."},{"text":"Let your partner take the next call instead, quietly relieved","then":"Your partner fields the same question, feeling it's now their job too."},{"text":"Tell him you'll stop taking his calls if he keeps asking","then":"He goes quiet, and the warmth between you cools a little."}],"why":"Staying warm and steady, even on repeat, keeps the door open without wearing you down."},{"prompt":"At a family function he tells relatives he's 'still waiting.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Gently ask him, privately, to let it be your and your partner's news to share","then":"He looks a little sheepish, and agrees to leave it be.","best":true},{"text":"Let the comment pass since it's mostly harmless","then":"Relatives start asking you both about it at every gathering now."},{"text":"Correct him loudly in front of the relatives","then":"He's embarrassed publicly, and the real conversation gets harder."},{"text":"Leave the function early without explaining why","then":"He's confused and hurt, unsure why you left so suddenly."}],"why":"Asking privately protects both his dignity and your own news."},{"prompt":"At the next big family function, he introduces you both without a single mention of babies. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank him warmly for respecting your pace","then":"He smiles, pleased you noticed, and the ease between you grows.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing, relieved but not wanting to jinx it","then":"The moment passes, sweet but a little unacknowledged."},{"text":"Joke that it's about time he let it go","then":"The joke lands awkwardly, and he looks unsure how to respond."},{"text":"Bring up how much pressure his comments had put on you","then":"The warm moment turns heavy, and he looks caught off guard."}],"why":"Naming the change while it's happening helps a new, gentler habit stick."}],"debrief":"Receive his love and keep the timing as the couple's call; honour him without handing over the choice.","relearn":"Honour his eagerness and hold the decision; some choices stay the couple's, even with a beloved elder."},
  {"id":"fm-1098","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Razia","source":"mother-in-law's cooking rules","hook":"Your mother-in-law insists every meal be her exact way; you'd like room to differ.","steps":[{"prompt":"She corrects how you're making dal for the third time this week. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Cook her classics with care, and ask if you can add one of your own","then":"She pauses, surprised, then says, 'Show me what you make.'","best":true},{"text":"Just do it her way every single time, to avoid yet another correction","then":"You keep cooking her way, quietly wishing you could add your own touch."},{"text":"Avoid cooking when she's around","then":"You start eating out more, and the kitchen stays entirely hers."},{"text":"Tell her it's your kitchen too and take over","then":"She looks pushed out, and dinner that night is tense."}],"why":"Honouring her recipes while asking for room keeps both cooks in the kitchen."},{"prompt":"A few days later, the same question of whose way to cook comes up again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Suggest cooking one meal together, her way and a touch of yours","then":"She's hesitant at first, then curious. 'Alright, let's see.'","best":true},{"text":"Keep quiet and go along with whatever she says this time too","then":"You go along again, the same quiet wish still there."},{"text":"Avoid cooking when she's in the kitchen","then":"You start eating out more, drifting further from the kitchen."},{"text":"Tell her plainly you'll cook your way this time","then":"She looks caught off guard, and dinner turns a little tense."}],"why":"Cooking together, even once, opens the kitchen to both of you."},{"prompt":"A month in, a big family gathering is coming up, and someone needs to plan the menu. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Offer to plan it together with her, blending both styles","then":"You two end up laughing over a small kitchen mishap, side by side.","best":true},{"text":"Let her handle the whole menu, as always","then":"It's appreciated, but the sharing of the kitchen doesn't grow."},{"text":"Offer to handle it all yourself this time","then":"She looks a little hurt, unsure why you'd keep her out."},{"text":"Say the whole thing feels like too much bother","then":"The offer to share cooking quietly falls away."}],"why":"Planning together turns a shared kitchen into a shared bond."}],"debrief":"Honour her recipes and add yours; respect both ways lets two cooks share one kitchen.","relearn":"Keep her classics and make room for yours; honour and your own voice can coexist."},
  {"id":"fm-1099","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"public correction","hook":"An elder corrects you sharply in front of guests over something small.","steps":[{"prompt":"It happens right in the middle of a dinner party. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let it pass gracefully now, and plan to raise it privately later","then":"The dinner moves on smoothly, and no one else seems to notice.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh it off in the moment, just to smooth things over quickly","then":"The laugh covers it for now, but the sting stays with you."},{"text":"Go quiet for the rest of the evening","then":"Guests notice the shift in mood, though no one says anything."},{"text":"Defend yourself right there in front of everyone","then":"The table goes stiff, and the guests exchange glances."}],"why":"Staying graceful in public and raising it later protects both the room and your voice."},{"prompt":"The next morning you find a quiet moment with the elder. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Bring it up gently, and say how it felt without blame","then":"The elder looks surprised, then softens. 'I didn't realise,' they say.","best":true},{"text":"Wait for them to bring it up first","then":"They never do, and the moment quietly passes."},{"text":"Let it go entirely since the dinner's over","then":"The same kind of correction happens again at the next event."},{"text":"Bring up every other time this has happened too, all at once","then":"The elder feels ambushed, and gets defensive instead of listening."}],"why":"Naming how it felt, gently and without blame, is most likely to be heard."},{"prompt":"At the next dinner party, a similar moment comes up again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Handle it calmly, speaking up privately if needed","then":"The moment passes with one calm word, and the evening stays easy.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and hope it doesn't happen again","then":"The chance to shift things quietly slips by again."},{"text":"Compare it out loud to how it went last time","then":"The comparison stings a little, even though it's meant kindly."},{"text":"Test them by bringing up the old topic again","then":"The moment turns tense, and the elder wonders if you're setting them up."}],"why":"A steady, calm approach keeps working, whether or not it's needed this time."}],"debrief":"Stay graceful in the room and speak privately later; respect and your voice both survive.","relearn":"Honour the moment publicly and address it privately; you can keep dignity and still be heard."},
  {"id":"fm-1100","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Sneha","source":"elders ask, then ignore","hook":"Your elders often ask for your view on family matters, then seem to overlook it.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your elders finalise a family decision without any sign your input mattered. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Note warmly that you'd offered a view, and ask it be weighed too","then":"They pause, and one of them says, 'You're right. Let's revisit it.'","best":true},{"text":"Let it go, assuming it probably wasn't a big decision anyway","then":"The same pattern happens again at the next decision."},{"text":"Stop offering opinions when asked from now on","then":"They notice you've gone quiet, but never ask why."},{"text":"Accuse them of never actually listening to you","then":"They get defensive, and the conversation turns into an argument."}],"why":"Asking for your view to be weighed claims the respect that asking you implied."},{"prompt":"A month later they ask your opinion on something else. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Share it honestly, hoping it will be heard this time","then":"This time, your point actually shapes the final decision.","best":true},{"text":"Share it half-heartedly, still unsure it will matter","then":"The moment passes without another word, and things go on as before."},{"text":"Say you'd rather not weigh in this time","then":"They take your silence as genuine indifference."},{"text":"Bring up the earlier decision before even answering the new one","then":"The question gets buried under an old grievance."}],"why":"Speaking up again gives the pattern a chance to change."},{"prompt":"At a family meeting, someone credits an idea that was actually yours to someone else. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say warmly that it was your idea, and you're glad it's being used","then":"The room adjusts easily, and no one seems bothered by the correction.","best":true},{"text":"Let the mix-up go quietly, since the idea is happening anyway","then":"The pattern of your input going unnoticed continues quietly."},{"text":"Mention it to your partner later instead of the room","then":"Your partner sympathises, but nothing changes with the family."},{"text":"Correct them sharply in front of everyone","then":"The room tenses, and the moment overshadows the good idea itself."}],"why":"A warm, direct correction claims credit without making it a bigger issue than it needs to be."}],"debrief":"Ask for your view to count too; respect both ways means being heard, not just consulted.","relearn":"Name that you'd shared a view and ask it be weighed; respect runs both directions."},
  {"id":"fm-1101","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"honour and your faith","hook":"You've married into a family with strong festival traditions of its own.","steps":[{"prompt":"At your first festival with them, they assume you'll follow only their way. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Warmly join in, and mention you'd love to keep one of your own customs too","then":"They look curious, and ask what your family does differently.","best":true},{"text":"Go along with everything for now, planning to bring up yours much later","then":"You keep meaning to mention your own customs, but the right moment doesn't come yet."},{"text":"Quietly do your own thing at home instead","then":"They don't hear about your customs from you, and a small distance grows."},{"text":"Say you won't take part in any of their customs","then":"The room goes tense, and they hear it as turning down the whole family."}],"why":"Joining warmly while naming your own custom keeps both heritages in view from the start."},{"prompt":"A few weeks on, the conversation turns to family traditions in general. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Share one of your family's customs, and invite them to take part","then":"They try a small part of it, and seem genuinely interested.","best":true},{"text":"Stay quiet about your own traditions, and just listen to theirs","then":"The moment passes, and your own customs stay unspoken."},{"text":"Downplay your family's customs as not very important","then":"They take that at face value, and your side quietly fades from view."},{"text":"Remark that your family's customs never get a mention here","then":"They feel accused, and the curiosity closes back up."}],"why":"Sharing a custom, and inviting them in, keeps both families visible."},{"prompt":"Next year, your mother-in-law starts planning the festival again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Propose adding one of your own customs this year","then":"She's curious, and you plan it in together.","best":true},{"text":"Go along with her plan, and let her decide most of the details","then":"The festival leans heavily her way, without much room for yours."},{"text":"Wait for her to ask whether you'd like to add anything","then":"She's busy with the plans and doesn't ask, so your custom stays out again."},{"text":"Insist your family's version should take the lead this time","then":"It turns into a small tug of war over whose way wins."}],"why":"Proposing to blend both, and helping plan it, keeps neither family's way erased."},{"prompt":"A younger cousin, newly married in, asks how you handle blending two families' customs. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you try to embrace theirs while keeping room for a few of your own","then":"The cousin nods, relieved there's a way that doesn't erase either side.","best":true},{"text":"Say you mostly just went along with your in-laws","then":"The cousin assumes that's the only real option and stops asking."},{"text":"Say it eventually stopped mattering to you","then":"The cousin looks a little sad, having hoped for something else."},{"text":"Say honestly you had to fight pretty hard to keep any of your own customs","then":"The cousin looks worried, imagining a bigger battle than there needs to be."}],"why":"Naming an approach that honours both keeps it real for someone else."}],"debrief":"Embrace theirs and keep some of yours; respect both ways means neither heritage gets erased.","relearn":"Adopt much of their way and keep some of yours; honouring them needn't erase you."},
  {"id":"fm-1102","type":"match","hook":"Match each nosy pressure question to a warm, dignified reply.","pairs":[{"left":"\"When are you getting married?\"","right":"\"When it's right for me, Maasi.\""},{"left":"\"Why no kids yet?\"","right":"\"That's between me and my partner.\""},{"left":"\"Aren't you too old now?\"","right":"\"I'm exactly where I want to be.\""},{"left":"\"What will people say?\"","right":"\"They'll find something either way.\""},{"left":"\"Don't you want to settle?\"","right":"\"My life already feels settled.\""}],"relearn":"Warm, dignified replies deflect nosy pressure without insulting the elder asking.","persona":"Sneha","source":"deflecting the pressure","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1103","type":"match","hook":"Match each moment to a line that honours the elder and keeps your voice.","pairs":[{"left":"Unwanted advice","right":"\"Thank you; we'll think it over.\""},{"left":"A real disagreement","right":"\"With respect, we see it differently.\""},{"left":"A choice that's yours","right":"\"We'd love to do this our way.\""},{"left":"A tradition you'll adapt","right":"\"We'll keep the heart of it, our version.\""},{"left":"Being talked over","right":"\"I'd still like to finish my thought.\""}],"relearn":"A respectful line exists for advice, disagreement, your own choices, adapting a custom, and being heard.","persona":"any","source":"honour-and-voice phrases","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1104","type":"match","hook":"Match each respectful move to what it honours.","pairs":[{"left":"Listening because you value them","right":"Honours their experience"},{"left":"Disagreeing warmly and kindly","right":"Honours your own voice"},{"left":"Weighing advice, then choosing","right":"Honours both at once"},{"left":"Caring for them when unwell","right":"Honours the bond"},{"left":"Thanking, then deciding for yourselves","right":"Honours the relationship"}],"relearn":"Respect can honour their experience, your voice, both at once, the bond and the relationship, all without obeying.","persona":"Razia","source":"respect vs obedience","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1105","type":"match","hook":"Match each thing an elder offers to the respectful way to receive it.","pairs":[{"left":"Hard-won life wisdom","right":"Listen closely, take what fits"},{"left":"An outdated demand","right":"Decline kindly, no scorn"},{"left":"A meaningful ritual","right":"Join in where it moves you"},{"left":"A worried warning","right":"Thank them, then decide"},{"left":"A generous offer of help","right":"Accept warmly or decline gently"}],"relearn":"You can listen to wisdom, decline the outdated, join rituals, weigh warnings and handle offers, all with respect.","persona":"Sneha","source":"receiving each offering","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1106","type":"match","hook":"Match each harsh line to its warmer version that still holds your ground.","pairs":[{"left":"\"You're so old-fashioned.\"","right":"\"Times have changed; help us find a middle way.\""},{"left":"\"Stop telling me what to do.\"","right":"\"I value your view; this call is mine.\""},{"left":"\"That's a stupid tradition.\"","right":"\"Help me understand what it means to you.\""},{"left":"\"You never listen.\"","right":"\"I'd love to feel heard on this.\""},{"left":"\"I don't care what you think.\"","right":"\"I hear you, and I see it differently.\""}],"relearn":"The same stance can be said warmly; respectful phrasing keeps both your point and the bond.","persona":"Karthik","source":"warm vs harsh phrasing","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1107","type":"match","hook":"Match each respectful act to who is showing it.","pairs":[{"left":"Asking the younger ones, then truly listening","right":"An elder respecting the young"},{"left":"Weighing an elder's advice sincerely","right":"A young one respecting elders"},{"left":"Owning a mistake to the younger ones","right":"An elder modelling humility"},{"left":"Disagreeing kindly, not rudely","right":"A young one keeping their voice"},{"left":"Deciding some things together","right":"Both sides sharing respect"}],"relearn":"Respect flows both ways: elders listen and own mistakes; the young weigh advice and disagree with care.","persona":"any","source":"respect both directions","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1108","type":"match","hook":"Match each elder request to a reply that honours them and keeps your choice.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Quit your job after marriage.\"","right":"\"I love that you care; I'm keeping my work.\""},{"left":"\"Have children right away.\"","right":"\"We'll choose our own timing.\""},{"left":"\"Move in with us for good.\"","right":"\"We'll visit often; we'll live our own place.\""},{"left":"\"Follow our every custom.\"","right":"\"We'll keep the ones that matter most.\""},{"left":"\"Let us pick your home.\"","right":"\"Your input matters; the choice is ours.\""}],"relearn":"Each request can meet warmth and a clear choice; honouring an elder isn't surrender.","persona":"Razia","source":"honour without surrender","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1109","type":"match","hook":"Match each piece of single-shaming to a kind, confident reply.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Such a waste, still single.\"","right":"\"I'm building a life I love.\""},{"left":"\"Who'll look after you later?\"","right":"\"I'm planning well for myself.\""},{"left":"\"Lower your standards.\"","right":"\"I'd rather wait for the right fit.\""},{"left":"\"People are talking.\"","right":"\"Let them; I'm fine.\""},{"left":"\"Don't you feel lonely?\"","right":"\"I have a full, connected life.\""}],"relearn":"Single-shaming meets a calm, confident reply; you can honour relatives and still decline their pity.","persona":"Sneha","source":"stigma-proof comebacks","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1110","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each one: real respect, or fear wearing respect's mask?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Listening because you value them"},{"id":"b","text":"Agreeing only because you're scared"},{"id":"c","text":"Disagreeing warmly and honestly"},{"id":"d","text":"Staying silent to avoid punishment"},{"id":"e","text":"Weighing advice, then choosing"},{"id":"f","text":"Hiding your real view in dread"}],"bins":[{"id":"respect","label":"Real respect","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fear","label":"Just fear","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"respect","b":"fear","c":"respect","d":"fear","e":"respect","f":"fear"},"relearn":"Valuing, disagreeing warmly and choosing is respect; silence and hidden views from dread are just fear.","persona":"Razia","source":"respect or fear","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1111","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: respect flowing both ways, or demanded only one way?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Elders' experience is valued"},{"id":"b","text":"Only the young must ever adjust"},{"id":"c","text":"The couple's choices count too"},{"id":"d","text":"The young may never disagree"},{"id":"e","text":"Both sides can say sorry"},{"id":"f","text":"Elders are above all questions"}],"bins":[{"id":"both","label":"Both ways","valence":"pos"},{"id":"one","label":"One way only","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"both","b":"one","c":"both","d":"one","e":"both","f":"one"},"relearn":"Valuing elders and the couple's choices, with apologies both ways, is mutual; all the adjusting on one side isn't.","persona":"any","source":"both ways or one way","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1112","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each reply: honours the elder and your voice, or surrenders your voice?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Thank you; we'll decide for ourselves.\""},{"id":"b","text":"Obeying silently to dodge any fuss"},{"id":"c","text":"\"I hear you, and I see it differently.\""},{"id":"d","text":"Saying yes you don't mean, every time"},{"id":"e","text":"Weighing the advice, then choosing"},{"id":"f","text":"Dropping every plan the moment they object"}],"bins":[{"id":"honour","label":"Honours both","valence":"pos"},{"id":"surrender","label":"Surrenders voice","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"honour","b":"surrender","c":"honour","d":"surrender","e":"honour","f":"surrender"},"relearn":"Thanking and deciding for yourselves keeps both; silent obedience and unmeant yeses surrender your voice.","persona":"Sneha","source":"honours or surrenders","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1113","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each line to an elder: respectful disagreement, or contempt?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"With respect, we see it differently.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"That's a ridiculous idea.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Help me understand what it means to you.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"You just don't get it.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"Thank you; we'll think it over.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Stop living in the past.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"respectful","label":"Respectful","valence":"pos"},{"id":"contempt","label":"Contemptuous","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"respectful","b":"contempt","c":"respectful","d":"contempt","e":"respectful","f":"contempt"},"relearn":"You can disagree and ask to understand respectfully; mockery and put-downs cross into contempt.","persona":"Razia","source":"respectful or contemptuous","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1114","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each decision: clearly the couple's own to keep, or genuinely worth honouring elders on?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"When you two have children"},{"id":"b","text":"The menu for a shared festival"},{"id":"c","text":"How you spend your own salary"},{"id":"d","text":"A joint family ritual everyone shares"},{"id":"e","text":"Which career you pursue"},{"id":"f","text":"The date for a family puja together"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"Couple's call","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shared","label":"Honour together","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"shared","c":"couple","d":"shared","e":"couple","f":"shared"},"relearn":"Children, salary and career are the couple's call; shared festivals and pujas are worth deciding with the family.","persona":"any","source":"couple's call or shared","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1115","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each reply to 'when will you marry?': warm deflection, or escalation?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"When it's right for me, Maasi.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"None of your business, ever!\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"I'm happy where I am, thanks.\""},{"id":"d","text":"Storming off in a huff"},{"id":"e","text":"\"I'll get there in my own time.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"You ruined this whole party.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"deflect","label":"Warm deflection","valence":"pos"},{"id":"escalate","label":"Escalation","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"deflect","b":"escalate","c":"deflect","d":"escalate","e":"deflect","f":"escalate"},"relearn":"Warm, dignified lines deflect the pressure; snapping and storming off just escalate into a family scene.","persona":"Sneha","source":"deflect or escalate","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1116","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each one: honouring an elder, or simply obeying them?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Greeting them warmly and listening"},{"id":"b","text":"Doing exactly as told, against your judgement"},{"id":"c","text":"Caring for them when they're unwell"},{"id":"d","text":"Hiding your real choices to comply"},{"id":"e","text":"Thanking them for their concern"},{"id":"f","text":"Cancelling your plans on their command"}],"bins":[{"id":"honour","label":"Honouring","valence":"pos"},{"id":"obey","label":"Just obeying","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"honour","b":"obey","c":"honour","d":"obey","e":"honour","f":"obey"},"relearn":"Greeting, caring and thanking are how you honour an elder; doing as told against your judgement is just obeying.","persona":"Karthik","source":"honour or obey","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1117","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: respect means honouring and listening, not handing over every choice. Does that distinction land?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["It lands","Honour, not obey","Worth holding"],"affirm":"Respect honours and listens; it doesn't require surrendering every choice you get to make.","relearn":"Respect means honouring and listening, not obeying in everything.","persona":"any","source":"respect not obedience","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1118","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you don't owe anyone a timeline for marriage or kids; warmth and your own pace can coexist. See it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I see it","My own pace","No timeline owed"],"affirm":"You can be warm to relatives and still hold your own pace; their pressure isn't a deadline you owe.","relearn":"You owe no one a life timeline; you can stay warm and keep your own pace.","persona":"Sneha","source":"your own pace","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1119","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: respect that only ever flows upward isn't respect, it's hierarchy. Real respect runs both ways. Agree?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Agree","Both ways","Not just upward"],"affirm":"Respect that only flows one way is hierarchy; genuine respect runs in both directions across generations.","relearn":"Respect runs both ways across generations, not in one direction only.","persona":"Razia","source":"both ways","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1120","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can sincerely honour an elder's advice AND still make a different choice. Both are true at once.","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["Both true","Honour and choose","Makes sense"],"affirm":"Honouring advice and following it aren't the same; you can value an elder's view and still choose your own way.","relearn":"You can honour an elder's advice and still make your own decision.","persona":"any","source":"honour and choose","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1121","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: an elder who feels respected is far more open to your boundaries; respect can actually aid your 'no'.","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["True","Respect aids it","Worth remembering"],"affirm":"Respect and a kind boundary reinforce each other; a respected elder hears your limit more openly.","relearn":"Respect and boundaries reinforce each other; respect can help your no land.","persona":"Pooja","source":"respect and boundaries","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1122","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a relationship built on fear isn't the same as one built on respect, even if it looks obedient. Resonate?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Resonates","Not the same","Fear isn't respect"],"affirm":"Obedience from fear can look like respect, but fear and respect are not the same relationship at all.","relearn":"Respect is not fear; a bond built on fear isn't one built on respect.","persona":"Razia","source":"respect not fear","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1123","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can join a tradition warmly AND respectfully opt out of specific parts. Both can be true. See it?","prompt":"How does it land?","options":["I see it","Join and opt out","Both true"],"affirm":"Joining traditions warmly and opting out of specific parts respectfully can sit together; it isn't all-or-nothing.","relearn":"You can join traditions warmly and still opt out of specific things, respectfully.","persona":"Sneha","source":"warmly opt out","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1124","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: keeping your own voice and showing deep respect aren't opposites; the warmest homes hold both. Agree?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Agree","Both at once","Not opposites"],"affirm":"Keeping your voice and showing respect aren't opposites; you can hold both in the same relationship.","relearn":"Keeping your own voice is compatible with deep respect; they aren't opposites.","persona":"any","source":"keep your voice","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1125","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two that are fear dressed up as respect, not the real thing.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Listening because you value their experience","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Agreeing only because you're scared to speak","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Disagreeing politely and staying warm","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Hiding your real view in dread of anger","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Weighing their advice, then choosing","trick":false}],"why":"Scared agreement and views hidden in dread are fear, not respect; the others are genuine respect.","relearn":"Real respect includes honest disagreement; agreement or silence driven by fear isn't respect.","persona":"Sneha","source":"fear masked as respect","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1126","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines where respect is demanded in one direction only.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"You'll respect me, but I owe you none.\"","trick":true},{"id":"b","text":"\"Let's both speak kindly to each other.\"","trick":false},{"id":"c","text":"\"Children must obey; elders need not explain.\"","trick":true},{"id":"d","text":"\"I'll listen to you as you listen to me.\"","trick":false},{"id":"e","text":"\"We can each own our mistakes.\"","trick":false}],"why":"The caught lines demand respect one-way only; respect that flows both ways sounds mutual, not top-down.","relearn":"Respect runs both ways across generations; respect demanded one-way only is just hierarchy.","persona":"Razia","source":"one-way respect","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1127","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two responses that surrender your voice instead of keeping it.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Thank you; we'll decide for ourselves.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Obeying silently to avoid any friction","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Listening fully, then making your own call","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Saying yes you don't mean to keep the peace","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"With respect, we see it differently.\"","trick":false}],"why":"Silent obedience and unmeant yeses drop your voice; the others honour the elder and keep it.","relearn":"Honour the elder and keep your voice; silent obedience and unmeant yeses surrender it.","persona":"any","source":"respect vs surrender","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1128","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that cross from disagreement into contempt.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"With respect, we see it differently.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"That's a ridiculous, backward idea.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Help me understand what it means to you.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"You're too old to get it.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Thank you; we'll think it over.\"","trick":false}],"why":"Mockery and 'too old to get it' are contempt; you can disagree firmly while still being respectful.","relearn":"You can disagree firmly and respectfully; mockery and put-downs cross into contempt.","persona":"Razia","source":"respectful vs contempt","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1129","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two replies to 'when will you marry?' that escalate into a scene.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"When it's right for me, Maasi.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Mind your own life, always!\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I'm happy where I am, thank you.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"You've ruined the whole evening.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I'll get there in my own time.\"","trick":false}],"why":"The caught replies escalate into a fight; warm, dignified lines deflect the pressure without a scene.","relearn":"Warm, dignified lines deflect marriage pressure; snapping just escalates into a scene.","persona":"Sneha","source":"deflect vs escalate","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1130","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that are control, not honouring an elder.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Greeting them warmly and listening","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Doing as told against your own judgement","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Caring for them when they're unwell","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Cancelling your plans on their command","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Thanking them for their concern","trick":false}],"why":"Obeying against your judgement and cancelling on command are control; greeting, caring and thanking honour them.","relearn":"Greeting, caring and thanking honour an elder; obeying against your judgement is control.","persona":"Karthik","source":"honour vs control","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1131","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two that erase you, not the warm ways to honour a tradition.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Joining the parts that mean something to you","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Faking belief you don't hold to please them","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Keeping a custom's heart in your own version","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Dropping every belief of your own to fit in","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Opting out of one part, kindly and clearly","trick":false}],"why":"Faking belief and dropping all your own beliefs erase you; honouring a tradition can keep both it and you.","relearn":"Honouring a tradition can keep both it and you; faking belief or erasing yourself doesn't.","persona":"any","source":"tradition: join vs erase","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1132","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two that treat your voice as if it doesn't count.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Elders ask your view and weigh it","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Elders ask, then act as if you never spoke","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Your kind disagreement is heard out","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Your opinion is waved away as 'insolence'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"A decision the couple makes is respected","trick":false}],"why":"Being consulted then ignored, or waved off as insolence, skips your voice; real respect actually weighs it.","relearn":"Real respect weighs your view; being consulted then ignored skips your voice.","persona":"Razia","source":"heard vs ignored","cat":"respect-both-ways"},
  {"id":"fm-1133","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"role-play","persona":"Sneha","source":"marriage pressure","hook":"Weddings always bring out your Maasi's opinions about your marital status.","setup":"She catches your elbow near the buffet table.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks: \"Beta, why are you still not married? What are you waiting for?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I appreciate you caring, Maasi. I'll marry when it's right for me.\"","then":"She pats your hand. \"Okay, okay, just don't wait too long,\" she says, softer.","best":true},{"text":"\"I don't know, Maasi. It just hasn't happened yet, I suppose.\"","then":"She takes that as an opening and starts listing eligible boys."},{"text":"\"Let's talk about something else, Maasi.\"","then":"She lets it go for now, but brings it up again before the night ends."},{"text":"\"Stop asking me that. It's so annoying.\"","then":"She looks hurt and mutters that she was only asking out of love."}],"why":"Naming her care while holding your pace answers her without inviting more pressure."},{"prompt":"Later she introduces you to a 'suitable boy' anyway. She says: \"Just talk to him. There's no harm in that.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I know you mean well, but I'd rather find my own way there.\"","then":"She sighs, but steps back. \"Your choice, beta,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine. I'll say hello.\"","then":"The hello turns into an hour of small talk you didn't want."},{"text":"\"I should go find my seat, actually.\"","then":"She watches you go, and tells another relative you're being difficult."},{"text":"\"I already told you. I'm really not interested in any of this at all.\"","then":"She looks embarrassed in front of the boy's family nearby."}],"why":"A gentle decline respects her intention without agreeing to something you don't want."},{"prompt":"At the next family wedding, she asks again: \"So, beta, any news yet?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll marry when it's right for me, Maasi.\"","then":"She laughs. \"You never change,\" she says, and lets it be.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm still figuring things out.\"","then":"She takes it as an opening and asks for updates every few weeks now."},{"text":"\"How's your health been lately, Maasi?\"","then":"It works for tonight, but the question waits for the next wedding."},{"text":"\"Can you please just accept this about me?\"","then":"She looks taken aback by the sharp edge in your voice."}],"why":"Repeating the same steady line, wedding after wedding, is what finally settles it."}],"relearn":"A warm, clear line honours the elder and keeps your own voice at the same time."},
  {"id":"fm-1134","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"father's plan","hook":"Your father is hurt you didn't join the family firm.","setup":"He brings it up quietly after dinner.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"I built that firm hoping you'd take it forward one day.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm grateful for everything you built, Papa. This work is the path I love.\"","then":"He nods slowly. \"I hear you,\" he says, though his eyes stay a little sad.","best":true},{"text":"\"I know, Papa, maybe someday.\"","then":"He brightens, though he still looks unsure what you really mean."},{"text":"\"Let's not get into this tonight.\"","then":"He goes quiet, and the same conversation comes up again next week."},{"text":"\"It's my life, not yours, so can you please just drop it already.\"","then":"He looks stung, and the room goes cold for the rest of the evening."}],"why":"Gratitude plus honesty lets him hear the choice without feeling like a rejection of him."},{"prompt":"A few days later he asks: \"I still don't understand. Is there something I did wrong?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Nothing, Papa. This was always going to be my own choice.\"","then":"He looks relieved to hear it wasn't something he did wrong.","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe. I'm not sure.\"","then":"He starts wondering, unnecessarily, where he went wrong."},{"text":"\"It doesn't matter now.\"","then":"He's left carrying the question without an answer."},{"text":"\"Honestly, you pushed too hard, if you really want to know the truth.\"","then":"He looks hurt, and the honest moment turns into a wound instead."}],"why":"Reassuring him plainly closes the guilt without leaving him guessing."},{"prompt":"At a family function he says, with pride: \"This is my child, doing important work.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you, Papa. That means a lot to hear.\"","then":"He smiles, and something between you both visibly eases.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not make a fuss here, Papa.\"","then":"He smiles, but the moment passes a little unfinished."},{"text":"\"Well, it certainly took you long enough to say that, Papa.\"","then":"The joke lands oddly, and he looks unsure how to respond."},{"text":"\"Oh, it's just work, Papa. Nothing special.\"","then":"He smiles anyway, though his pride doesn't quite land."}],"why":"Meeting his pride with simple thanks lets the moment actually land."}],"relearn":"A warm, clear line honours the elder and keeps your own voice at the same time."},
  {"id":"fm-1135","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"role-play","persona":"Razia","source":"elder's outdated demand","hook":"An elder has strong ideas about what a good wife should give up.","setup":"She brings it up during a visit, gently but firmly.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"Once you're married, you should really leave the job and focus on the home.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you for caring about our home. I'm keeping my work.\"","then":"She pauses, then says, \"Well, times have changed, I suppose.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll think about cutting back, maybe.\"","then":"She takes that as agreement and starts planning around it."},{"text":"\"Let's talk about this some other time.\"","then":"She doesn't let it drop. \"Let's just settle it now,\" she says, and keeps going."},{"text":"\"That's a pretty outdated idea, honestly. I won't even discuss it further.\"","then":"She looks offended, and the room goes tense, though she isn't done yet."}],"why":"Gratitude plus a clear stand lets her hear your choice without feeling dismissed."},{"prompt":"She asks: \"And who will manage the household while you're at work?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll manage it together, as a team.\"","then":"She nods slowly. \"As long as it works for you both,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm not really sure yet, but we'll figure it out somehow.\"","then":"She takes the uncertainty as an opening to keep pushing her view."},{"text":"\"That's not really your concern.\"","then":"She looks hurt at being brushed aside so directly."},{"text":"\"Are you saying I can't handle both?\"","then":"The question turns defensive, and the conversation gets tense."}],"why":"Framing it as a shared plan answers her worry without giving up the choice."},{"prompt":"Months later, at a family gathering, she tells a relative you're 'still working, for now.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"\"Actually, I love my work. It's not a phase. It's my path.\"","then":"The relative nods with interest, and the elder just smiles quietly.","best":true},{"text":"\"That's one way to put it, I suppose.\"","then":"The 'for now' framing sticks, and people keep expecting you to quit."},{"text":"\"She likes to worry about things.\"","then":"It brushes past the moment, but the misunderstanding stays alive."},{"text":"\"Why do you keep saying that, like my job is only temporary?\"","then":"She looks embarrassed in front of the relative, and the mood cools."}],"why":"Correcting the framing warmly, in the moment, stops it from becoming the family story."}],"relearn":"A warm, clear line honours the elder and keeps your own voice at the same time."},
  {"id":"fm-1136","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"role-play","persona":"Pooja","source":"opting out of a ritual","hook":"Your mother-in-law wants you to keep a strict fast you don't observe.","setup":"She asks the night before the fast begins.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"Tomorrow's fast is important. You'll join us, won't you?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I love this day. I'll join the prayers, though I won't keep the fast.\"","then":"She nods slowly. \"Alright, the prayers matter most,\" she says, unsure.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll try. We'll see how the day goes.\"","then":"She assumes that means yes, and checks on you all day."},{"text":"\"I might not be feeling well tomorrow, actually.\"","then":"She looks concerned, and you feel a little guilty about the excuse."},{"text":"\"I don't personally believe in that, so I'm just not going to do it.\"","then":"She looks stung, and the kitchen goes quiet for a while."}],"why":"Naming both the yes and the no keeps the day shared without asking you to fake it."},{"prompt":"The next morning, she sees you at breakfast and asks: \"Just to be sure, are you keeping today's fast?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm joining the prayers with you, just not the fast itself.\"","then":"She shrugs it off. \"As long as you're there for the prayers,\" she says.","best":true},{"text":"\"I hope this still works for you.\"","then":"She nods, though it's not fully clear if it's settled between you."},{"text":"\"Let's not make a whole thing of breakfast, okay?\"","then":"She lets it go for now, though the question stays unresolved."},{"text":"\"I'm not doing the fast. Please stop asking.\"","then":"The sharpness surprises her, and breakfast turns quiet."}],"why":"Restating the plan clearly keeps the morning calm and clear."},{"prompt":"At the evening prayers, a relative asks: \"Why aren't you fasting like the rest of us?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I join the prayers, but I keep my own choice on the fast.\"","then":"The relative shrugs. \"Whatever works,\" she says, unbothered.","best":true},{"text":"\"Ask my mother-in-law. She knows.\"","then":"The relative looks unconvinced and brings it up with her directly."},{"text":"\"Let's not get into that right now.\"","then":"The relative keeps guessing, and it follows you for the rest of the evening."},{"text":"\"Fasting rules like that have honestly never made much sense to me.\"","then":"The relative's face falls, and she goes quiet for the rest of the prayers."}],"why":"A calm, clear answer handles the question without extra explaining."},{"prompt":"The following year, she asks again: \"Will you be joining properly this time?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll gladly join the prayers. I'm skipping the fast.\"","then":"Nobody blinks. It's simply how you do the day now.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll keep the full fast this year so you stop asking.\"","then":"You struggle through the day, quietly resentful by evening."},{"text":"\"I think I'll skip the whole day this year.\"","then":"You miss the gathering, and your mother-in-law notices your absence."},{"text":"\"I still don't do fasts. You know that.\"","then":"The reminder lands sharper than the day itself needed."}],"why":"A steady, repeated pattern becomes the normal, so nobody needs to ask again."}],"relearn":"A warm, clear line honours the elder and keeps your own voice at the same time."},
  {"id":"fm-1137","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"being talked over","hook":"At a family meeting an elder keeps cutting you off.","setup":"You try again to make your point.","steps":[{"prompt":"The elder interrupts mid-sentence, saying: \"That's not how it's done.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd love to finish my thought, please.\"","then":"The elder pauses, a little surprised, then nods for you to continue.","best":true},{"text":"\"Um, okay, I guess.\"","then":"The chance doesn't come, and your point goes unsaid for now."},{"text":"\"Never mind. It's fine. Forget it.\"","then":"The idea disappears from the meeting, though you keep thinking about it."},{"text":"\"Would you let me speak for once, please?\"","then":"The elder looks taken aback, and the room goes a little tense."}],"why":"Asking plainly to finish your thought is easy for anyone to grant."},{"prompt":"Later in the same meeting, the elder cuts in again and says: \"Actually, let me just say this first.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just one more moment, please.\"","then":"The elder smiles slightly. \"Go on then,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"\"Excuse me, please let me finish this!\"","then":"You get heard, but the room notices the tension in your tone."},{"text":"\"Go ahead, then. You explain it to them.\"","then":"Someone else finishes your point, differently than you meant it."},{"text":"\"You always do this to me, every single time.\"","then":"The elder bristles, and the meeting's mood shifts noticeably."}],"why":"A warm, brief ask keeps the exchange two way instead of one sided."},{"prompt":"At the next family meeting, the elder pauses mid-interruption and says: \"Sorry, go on.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you for that.\"","then":"The meeting flows more easily, and others start speaking up too.","best":true},{"text":"\"...so anyway, that's my point.\"","then":"The change goes unnoticed, though the meeting does feel smoother."},{"text":"\"Wow, so much better when you let me finish!\"","then":"The comment lands a little awkwardly, even though it's kindly meant."},{"text":"\"This is nice, unlike every other time you've cut me off.\"","then":"The elder feels ambushed, and the improvement stalls."}],"why":"Naming the change warmly encourages the elder to keep making room for you."}],"relearn":"A warm, clear line honours the elder and keeps your own voice at the same time."},
  {"id":"fm-1138","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"role-play","persona":"Sneha","source":"pity for being single","hook":"This relative believes something is missing from your life, and prays about it often.","setup":"She says it gently, holding your hand.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"I pray every day that marriage will save you from this loneliness.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's kind of you. My life feels full and happy.\"","then":"She looks surprised. 'If you say so, beta,' she says, still not quite convinced.","best":true},{"text":"\"Haha, thanks I guess.\"","then":"She takes the laugh as agreement and keeps praying out loud about it."},{"text":"\"So, how's the garden doing this year?\"","then":"She brings it up again before the visit is over."},{"text":"\"I really don't need saving from anything. Please just stop.\"","then":"She looks hurt, saying she only meant it with love."}],"why":"Naming your own happiness plainly closes the pity without a fight."},{"prompt":"Later she tells another relative: \"Still waiting to be saved, poor thing.\" What do you say?","options":[{"text":"\"Actually, I'm doing really well these days.\"","then":"The other relative nods, and your first relative looks thoughtful.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's just get some tea.\"","then":"The story spreads, and more relatives start treating you with pity."},{"text":"\"She exaggerates a little.\"","then":"It brushes past the moment, but the pity keeps circulating."},{"text":"\"Why do you keep talking about me like that, exactly?\"","then":"She looks embarrassed in front of the other relative."}],"why":"Correcting the story directly, warmly, stops it from spreading further."},{"prompt":"At the next family gathering, she says gently: \"Any news yet, beta?\"","options":[{"text":"\"My life is genuinely full, Maasi.\"","then":"She smiles. \"You do seem happy,\" she admits, and lets it be.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm sure it'll happen eventually.\"","then":"She takes it as hope and keeps the prayers coming, louder than before."},{"text":"\"I should go say hi to everyone else.\"","then":"She notices the distance and worries she's upset you."},{"text":"\"Can you please just accept this about me, once and for all?\"","then":"She looks taken aback by the sharpness in your voice."}],"why":"Repeating the same steady answer, gathering after gathering, is what finally settles it."}],"relearn":"A warm, clear line honours the elder and keeps your own voice at the same time."},
  {"id":"fm-1139","cat":"respect-both-ways","type":"role-play","persona":"Razia","source":"one-way respect","hook":"An elder demands respect while speaking to you with contempt.","setup":"Tonight, he starts in again before you've even sat down.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"You young people never show any respect these days.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I do respect you. I'd love for us to speak kindly to each other, both ways.\"","then":"He pauses, caught off guard. \"Fair enough,\" he says quietly.","best":true},{"text":"\"Okay, I'll try to be more respectful.\"","then":"He takes it as an admission of fault, and the sharp tone continues."},{"text":"\"I'd rather not get into this tonight.\"","then":"He carries on regardless, and the sharp tone stays in the room."},{"text":"\"You don't respect me either, so why exactly should I respect you?\"","then":"He bristles, and the exchange turns into a standoff."}],"why":"Naming it warmly, without matching the sharpness, models the respect you're asking for."},{"prompt":"A few days later, out of habit, he says sharply: \"Why must everything be an argument with you?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I noticed that tone again. I'd still like us to be kind to each other.\"","then":"He looks a little sheepish. \"You're right. I'm sorry,\" he says.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm sure that's not what you meant.\"","then":"He doesn't correct himself, and the sharp comment goes unaddressed."},{"text":"\"I think I'll go help in the kitchen.\"","then":"He notices the distance but never connects it to his own tone."},{"text":"\"Well, maybe if you spoke to me nicer, I'd listen better!\"","then":"The exchange gets louder, and the room around you tenses."}],"why":"Calling it out again, gently, keeps the old pattern from quietly returning."},{"prompt":"At a family dinner, he says: \"Good to see you, beta.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's good to see you too.\"","then":"The dinner passes easily, warmer than it's felt in a long while.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's just see how tonight goes.\"","then":"He notices the guardedness and starts to pull back too."},{"text":"\"Remember when you snapped at me last week?\"","then":"He gets defensive, and the warm moment fades fast."},{"text":"\"So, are we actually being nice tonight?\"","then":"He tenses, and the dinner's ease slips away."}],"why":"Meeting his greeting warmly, without testing it, is what lets the evening stay easy."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, a younger cousin asks: \"How do you handle his sharp tone?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I try to keep my dignity, and name it kindly when it happens.\"","then":"The cousin nods, and says they'll try the same with their own elders.","best":true},{"text":"\"I just learned to let most things go.\"","then":"The cousin looks a little disappointed by the advice."},{"text":"\"He changed on his own, eventually.\"","then":"The cousin assumes there's nothing they can do but wait it out."},{"text":"\"Honestly, I had to fight pretty hard for every bit of that respect.\"","then":"The cousin looks worried, imagining a bigger battle than needed."}],"why":"A steady, repeated approach, not a single fight, is what actually builds mutual respect."}],"relearn":"A warm, clear line honours the elder and keeps your own voice at the same time."},
  {"id":"fm-1170","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"align then say it","hook":"Your mother-in-law makes changes around your home when she visits, without asking either of you first.","steps":[{"prompt":"She rearranges your kitchen again while you're both out. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Agree one line with your partner first, then say it to her together","then":"She hears one calm, united voice, and takes it more seriously.","best":true},{"text":"Mention it to her yourself, planning to tell your partner later","then":"She hears it as just your preference, not a shared decision."},{"text":"Let it go this time to avoid a scene","then":"She takes the silence as permission, and carries on just as before."},{"text":"Confront her sharply the moment you notice","then":"She feels attacked, and gets defensive instead of listening."}],"why":"A united line, agreed first, carries more weight than a solo comment."},{"prompt":"At home that night, you and your partner talk about the pattern. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Agree to always raise it together from now on","then":"You both settle on the words together, calmly.","best":true},{"text":"Downplay it so your partner doesn't worry","then":"Your partner shrugs it off, not realising how often it's happening."},{"text":"Mention it only briefly, and change the subject","then":"The conversation moves on before anything is actually settled."},{"text":"Vent angrily about your mother-in-law","then":"Your partner gets defensive of her, and the conversation turns tense."}],"why":"Agreeing on the words together, ahead of time, is what makes the next moment easy."},{"prompt":"The next time she visits, the same kind of change happens again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Speak to her together, calmly and as one voice","then":"She pauses, then nods. 'Alright, I'll check with you both first.'","best":true},{"text":"Let your partner raise it alone while you stay quiet","then":"She takes it as your partner's opinion, not a shared decision."},{"text":"Avoid the topic during the visit","then":"The changes happen again before you even leave."},{"text":"Bring it up sharply, cutting her off mid sentence","then":"She looks hurt, and the visit turns stiff for the rest of the evening."}],"why":"Delivering it together, warmly, is what actually makes the boundary stick."},{"prompt":"Months later, she asks before making any change to your home. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank her warmly for checking first","then":"She smiles, glad the new rhythm is working for everyone.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing, just relieved it's working","then":"The good habit isn't reinforced, so it's easy for it to slip."},{"text":"Remind her how it used to be a problem","then":"The reminder sours a moment that was actually going well."},{"text":"Act surprised she's finally asking","then":"The comment lands as a jab, even though it's meant lightly."}],"why":"Thanking her for the new habit is what keeps it going."}],"debrief":"Align as a couple first, then deliver one warm, united line, not a solo blow-up.","relearn":"Decide together, then say it together: one couple line beats a solo snap."},
  {"id":"fm-1171","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"the repeatable line","hook":"Relatives ask, every single visit, when you'll have a baby. You and your partner are tired of the ambush.","steps":[{"prompt":"Before the next visit, your partner asks how you two should handle the baby question. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Agree one warm, simple line together, and rehearse it","then":"Your partner relaxes. You settle on a calm line about timing and try it once out loud.","best":true},{"text":"Each pick your own warm line and use it","then":"You both walk in warm, though each of you has a different answer ready."},{"text":"Say you'll handle every question yourself","then":"Your partner agrees, though they feel a little left out of it."},{"text":"Plan a fresh excuse for each relative","then":"Your partner isn't sure. The excuses will have to be invented on the spot."}],"why":"One line agreed together means you answer as a team, with no fresh scramble each visit."},{"prompt":"At the visit, relatives ask about a baby, one after another. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give the same calm, warm line each time","then":"The relatives smile. 'Fair enough,' one says, and the topic drops.","best":true},{"text":"Vary your answer a little for each relative","then":"The small differences make people wonder which answer is the real one."},{"text":"Try to avoid the relatives for the rest of the visit","then":"One corners you again anyway before you leave."},{"text":"Point out that they ask every single time","then":"The comment lands sharper than intended, and the relatives look stung."}],"why":"Repeating it exactly, without variation, keeps the story consistent and low key."},{"prompt":"At the next family visit, months later, the question comes up again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give the same calm line, side by side with your partner","then":"By now, relatives barely react, and dinner moves on quickly.","best":true},{"text":"Give a real update, to finally ease the pressure","then":"You share more than you meant to, and the questions only multiply."},{"text":"Excuse yourself to the kitchen until the topic passes","then":"You miss the moment, but the question is still waiting when you return."},{"text":"Snap that the question is exhausting by now","then":"The sharpness gets noticed, and it becomes a small family story."}],"why":"Staying steady, visit after visit, is what finally takes the sting out of the question."}],"debrief":"A rehearsed, repeatable line answers the recurring question as a team, no fresh scramble each visit.","relearn":"For a question that repeats, build one repeatable line together. Don't improvise."},
  {"id":"fm-1172","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"decide then share","hook":"You're moving cities for your job. Your in-laws expect a say before anything is decided.","steps":[{"prompt":"You and your partner have decided. How do you tell them?","options":[{"text":"Say: 'We've decided, and we'd love your blessing'","then":"They're quiet for a moment, then say they need time to take it in.","best":true},{"text":"Ask them what they think before confirming anything","then":"They take the question as an opening to try to change your minds."},{"text":"Let your partner break the news alone","then":"They react mostly to your partner, and you're left out of the moment."},{"text":"Announce it flatly with no room for their feelings","then":"They feel blindsided rather than included, even though the news stands."}],"why":"Sharing the decision warmly, with a request for blessing, honours them while keeping the call yours."},{"prompt":"Your father-in-law calls a few days later, hoping to talk you out of it. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Listen warmly, then hold your plan steady","then":"He sighs. 'I just wanted to say my piece,' he admits.","best":true},{"text":"Agree to reconsider, just to end the call gently","then":"He hears hope in that, and calls again with more arguments."},{"text":"Let the call go to voicemail","then":"He calls again the next day, more insistent than before."},{"text":"Cut the call short before he can say much","then":"He feels shut out, and mentions it to other relatives."}],"why":"Listening first, then holding steady, respects him without reopening the decision."},{"prompt":"On the day you leave, your in-laws seem to have made peace with it. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank them warmly, and promise regular visits","then":"They wave you off with real warmth, already planning the first visit.","best":true},{"text":"Say a quick goodbye and focus on the move","then":"The goodbye feels a little rushed, though nothing is actually wrong."},{"text":"Avoid the topic of visiting, to keep things simple","then":"They're left unsure how connected you'll stay from a distance."},{"text":"Point out how hard the whole process was on you","then":"The comment sours a goodbye that was otherwise going well."}],"why":"Promising to stay connected turns a hard goodbye into a warm one."},{"prompt":"A year later, they visit your new city for the first time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Welcome them warmly, and show them the life you've built there","then":"They leave impressed, telling relatives how well you've settled in.","best":true},{"text":"Keep the visit short to avoid any awkwardness","then":"They leave without really seeing your new life, a bit unsatisfied."},{"text":"Compare your new city unfavourably to home, out of habit","then":"The comment undercuts the very life you wanted them to see."},{"text":"Remind them how hard they made the decision","then":"The comment sours what could have been a warm visit."}],"why":"Welcoming them into your new life turns the hard goodbye into an ongoing bond."}],"debrief":"Big life calls are the couple's; share them warmly and invite blessing. That honours both.","relearn":"The couple decides its big moves, then shares them with warmth, not a veto."},
  {"id":"fm-1173","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"back them in the room","hook":"Your father-in-law has a habit of correcting your partner sharply, and your partner shuts down every time.","steps":[{"prompt":"It happens right at the table, in front of the whole family. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Gently back them, saying you see it differently but you'll talk it over","then":"Your partner's shoulders relax, and the table moves on easily.","best":true},{"text":"Wait until later to bring it up privately","then":"Your partner feels alone in the moment, even if you mean to help later."},{"text":"Stay silent so you don't seem disrespectful","then":"Your partner feels exposed, and the silence sits between you all evening."},{"text":"Correct your father-in-law sharply, right there in front of everyone","then":"The table tenses, and your partner feels caught in the middle."}],"why":"A warm, quick back up in the room shows your partner you're a team, not against family."},{"prompt":"Later that night, your partner brings up how the moment felt. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you noticed, and that you'll always back them in the room","then":"Your partner exhales, relieved to hear it named out loud.","best":true},{"text":"Say it wasn't that big a deal","then":"Your partner feels a little dismissed, even though nothing was intended."},{"text":"Change the subject to something lighter","then":"The moment goes unaddressed, and the sting lingers a while longer."},{"text":"Say plainly that your father-in-law is honestly always like that","then":"It turns into a complaint session rather than support for your partner."}],"why":"Naming the support out loud, later, makes sure your partner actually heard it."},{"prompt":"At the next family dinner, your father-in-law starts to correct your partner again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Step in warmly right away, backing your partner in the room","then":"Your partner meets your eyes, steady, feeling backed.","best":true},{"text":"Wait to see if it escalates before saying anything","then":"By the time you step in, your partner's already gone quiet."},{"text":"Let your partner handle it solo, trusting they can manage","then":"Your partner manages it, but notices you didn't step in."},{"text":"Cut your father-in-law off mid sentence, sharply","then":"The table tenses, and the correction turns into a bigger scene."}],"why":"Stepping in early and warmly protects your partner before the moment lands hard."},{"prompt":"Months later, your partner starts speaking up before you even need to step in. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Cheer them on quietly, and back them if needed","then":"Your partner meets your eyes and finishes their point with ease.","best":true},{"text":"Stay out of it entirely, assuming they've got it","then":"Your partner handles it but wonders if you were paying attention."},{"text":"Step in anyway, out of habit","then":"Your partner feels a little undercut, having wanted to handle it themselves."},{"text":"Check your phone instead of paying attention","then":"Your partner notices you weren't really watching this time."}],"why":"Cheering them on, ready to step in only if needed, shows real trust in their growth."}],"debrief":"Backing your partner warmly in the room isn't choosing sides against family. It's being a team.","relearn":"Hold your partner with a warm, united line; silence in the room exposes them."},
  {"id":"fm-1174","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"refuse the wedge","hook":"A relative pulls you aside to 'tell you what your partner is really like'. Classic triangulation.","steps":[{"prompt":"The relative starts with: \"I just think you should know...\" What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decline it: 'I'd rather hear that with both of us here'","then":"The relative hesitates, then drops the subject entirely.","best":true},{"text":"Listen politely, planning to ignore it later","then":"The words linger longer than you expected, even unwanted."},{"text":"Change the subject quickly, without addressing the attempt at all","then":"The relative tries again later, in a quieter corner."},{"text":"Accuse the relative of trying to stir trouble","then":"The relative denies it and the exchange turns into an argument."}],"why":"Declining the side channel, right away, shuts the wedge before it can open."},{"prompt":"A different relative tries the same thing at the next gathering. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decline calmly and simply","then":"The relative shrugs and says, 'Fair, just thought you'd want to know.'","best":true},{"text":"Hear a little of it before cutting it off","then":"The little bit you heard still plants a small seed of doubt."},{"text":"Pretend you didn't understand what they meant","then":"The relative rephrases and tries a second time, more directly."},{"text":"Tell them to never bring this up again","then":"The sharpness gets repeated around the family as a story."}],"why":"Repeating the same firm line keeps every attempt from wearing you down."},{"prompt":"That evening you tell your partner what's been happening. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell them honestly what's been happening","then":"Your partner relaxes, grateful you're telling them instead of hiding it.","best":true},{"text":"Mention it lightly, without much detail","then":"Your partner senses something but doesn't get the full picture."},{"text":"Mention only the most recent one, leaving out the rest","then":"Your partner gets a partial picture, and still feels a bit blindsided later."},{"text":"Demand your partner do something about their relatives","then":"Your partner feels blamed for something they didn't do."}],"why":"Telling your partner honestly keeps the couple a team against the wedge."},{"prompt":"A relative tries once more, this time saying it's 'for your own good.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give a calm, firm decline, without leaving room for more","then":"The relative finally seems to accept it, and stops trying.","best":true},{"text":"Ask what they mean, just out of curiosity","then":"Curiosity opens the door, and the relative takes the chance to talk."},{"text":"Say you're too busy to talk right now","then":"The relative just waits for a better moment to try again."},{"text":"Tell them plainly that they're clearly trying to cause a problem here","then":"The accusation creates the very tension you were trying to avoid."}],"why":"Holding the line without softening it is what finally makes it stick."},{"prompt":"Months later, your partner asks how you've kept the family from coming between you two. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you try to decline every side channel, together","then":"Your partner nods. 'That's exactly why it's never worked on us,' they say.","best":true},{"text":"Say you just don't listen to gossip much","then":"It sounds simple, though it leaves out the actual habit that protects you both."},{"text":"Say you've mostly just been lucky","then":"Your partner isn't sure what to repeat if it happens to them."},{"text":"Say you've had to shut a fair number of people down over the years","then":"Your partner pictures more conflict than there's actually been."}],"why":"Naming the habit clearly is what makes it repeatable, for you and for others."}],"debrief":"Refuse triangulation by routing it back to the couple, no secret side-channels about your partner.","relearn":"When someone tries to pit you against your partner, decline the side-channel and keep it open."},
  {"id":"fm-1175","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"settle it privately","hook":"A relative keeps coming up between you and your partner, and you don't always agree on how to handle them.","steps":[{"prompt":"The disagreement is getting louder at home. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Agree to settle it privately, just the two of you, before facing family","then":"The heat drops once you agree to work on it together.","best":true},{"text":"Pause the conversation, without agreeing when to return to it","then":"It cools for now, but nobody's sure when it'll come up again."},{"text":"Let it go unresolved and hope it settles itself","then":"The same disagreement resurfaces the next time the relative comes up."},{"text":"Each call your own parents to take your side","then":"Two people quickly become four camps, and the real issue gets lost."}],"why":"Settling it privately first keeps the disagreement from becoming a family spectacle."},{"prompt":"Eventually, away from outside opinions, you and your partner sit down to work it out. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Work out an answer together, then rehearse saying it","then":"You practice it a few times, and it starts to feel natural.","best":true},{"text":"Assume you'll both remember when the moment comes","then":"You move on without settling the exact words."},{"text":"Agree the gist, but leave the wording to each of you","then":"You each plan to say it your own way, and the wording stays open between you."},{"text":"Write out exact words for each other to memorise","then":"You draft the exact lines, though it feels a bit stiff on paper."}],"why":"Working it out together, then practicing it, is what you can actually deliver as one."},{"prompt":"The relative brings up the topic at the next family event. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Deliver the agreed answer together, calmly","then":"The relative accepts it easily, hearing one steady, shared voice.","best":true},{"text":"Let your partner answer alone this time","then":"The relative notices only one of you speaking for the couple."},{"text":"Avoid the topic and hope it doesn't come up again","then":"The tension between you stays, and it follows you into the family event."},{"text":"Bring up the earlier disagreement in front of the relative","then":"The private disagreement spills into the family event, awkwardly."}],"why":"Presenting one shared answer shows the family a united team, not two competing views."}],"debrief":"Resolve couple disagreements privately, then present one answer. Don't outsource the fight to family.","relearn":"Hash out couple disagreements in private; never recruit relatives into them."},
  {"id":"fm-1176","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"please call first","hook":"Your in-laws love spontaneous visits. Your week doesn't always love it back.","steps":[{"prompt":"They arrive again without any warning, mid week. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Welcome them warmly, then later ask for a quick call ahead next time","then":"They seem a little surprised, but agree it makes sense.","best":true},{"text":"Welcome them warmly, and say nothing at all about it this time","then":"The pattern continues, since nothing was actually asked for."},{"text":"Pretend you're not home to avoid the disruption","then":"They wait outside a while, confused, before eventually leaving."},{"text":"Tell them bluntly that dropping in isn't okay","then":"They look hurt, hearing it as a rejection rather than a request."}],"why":"Naming the request warmly, after welcoming them, sets a norm without shutting them out."},{"prompt":"A week later they're about to drop in again, without calling. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call them first, warmly, before they leave the house","then":"They're pleased you reached out, and the visit goes smoothly.","best":true},{"text":"Wait and see if they actually show up this time","then":"They show up anyway, and the old pattern repeats."},{"text":"Text them a reminder about calling ahead, halfheartedly","then":"The reminder goes unanswered, and they show up anyway."},{"text":"Ask your partner to handle telling them, instead of you","then":"Your partner ends up carrying a boundary that was meant to be shared."}],"why":"Reaching out first models the habit you're hoping to build."},{"prompt":"The next time, they call ahead for the first time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank them warmly, and say how much it helps to host them properly","then":"They sound pleased, and start calling ahead as a matter of course.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing, just relieved it happened","then":"The good habit isn't reinforced, so it's easy for it to slip."},{"text":"Mention it only to your partner, not to them","then":"They never learn that the call actually made a difference."},{"text":"Point out, a little pointedly, how much better this is than before","then":"The comparison lands awkwardly, even though it's meant kindly."}],"why":"Thanking them for calling ahead is what makes the new habit stick."},{"prompt":"Months later, they mention to a relative that they 'always call first now.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Smile and agree warmly, glad it's become the new normal","then":"The habit is fully settled, with everyone comfortable in it.","best":true},{"text":"Let the comment pass without responding","then":"The moment passes quietly, though the habit is already holding."},{"text":"Correct them gently that it did take a while to get here","then":"The comment lands as a small jab, even though it's meant honestly."},{"text":"Use the moment to ask for a limit on how long they stay","then":"The praise turns into a new demand, and they look a little wrong-footed."}],"why":"Meeting the settled habit warmly keeps the good pattern going."}],"debrief":"A kind 'please call first' is a boundary that limits a behaviour without shutting the person out.","relearn":"Ask warmly for a heads-up before visits. A clear norm beats a cold wall."},
  {"id":"fm-1177","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Sneha","source":"join in part","hook":"A festival ritual matters deeply to your in-laws but clashes with your work deadline.","steps":[{"prompt":"The ritual and a critical work call fall on the same evening. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Offer what you can: join the main puja, and step out for the call","then":"Your in-laws appreciate you being there for the part that matters most.","best":true},{"text":"Skip the work call to attend the whole ritual","then":"The work call goes unanswered, and it becomes a problem the next day."},{"text":"Skip the ritual entirely to focus on work","then":"Your in-laws notice your absence, and it stings more than expected."},{"text":"Tell your in-laws plainly that work always has to come first","then":"The comment lands harder than you meant, and the room goes quiet."}],"why":"Joining the main part and stepping out for the rest honours both commitments."},{"prompt":"That night, your partner asks how you're feeling about how it went. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Talk honestly about how it went, and plan ahead for next time","then":"Your partner agrees, and you start thinking about how to plan the next one.","best":true},{"text":"Say it was stressful, but there wasn't really another way","then":"Your partner nods, though the same stress is likely to repeat."},{"text":"Say you're still annoyed work and family keep clashing like this","then":"The frustration lingers, without turning into any real plan."},{"text":"Say you'd rather not talk about it tonight","then":"The topic gets set aside, and nothing gets learned from tonight."}],"why":"Naming what worked, and planning ahead, turns tonight's stress into next time's ease."},{"prompt":"Next year the festival comes around again, and work is busy around it. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Plan ahead: join the main puja, and plan work around it","then":"This time there's no scramble, since you planned for it early.","best":true},{"text":"Wait and see if the clash happens before planning","then":"The same last minute scramble happens all over again."},{"text":"Assume the ritual will be shorter this year and skip planning","then":"It runs long again, and you're caught in the same bind."},{"text":"Promise your in-laws and your team you'll be there for everything","then":"Both sides expect all of you, and the promise can't hold."}],"why":"Planning ahead turns a yearly scramble into a smooth, repeatable routine."}],"debrief":"You can join a tradition warmly and opt out of one piece respectfully. A partial yes is a real tool.","relearn":"Honour what you can of a ritual and opt out of one piece kindly, not all-in or all-out."},
  {"id":"fm-1178","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"care, not control","hook":"Your partner's family wants to manage your joint savings 'for your own good.'","steps":[{"prompt":"They ask for access to your joint account 'just to help out.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank them, and say you've got a plan you're happy with","then":"They nod, a little surprised, and don't press further.","best":true},{"text":"Let them look at the account just this once","then":"They start commenting on every transaction after that one look."},{"text":"Avoid answering and hope the topic fades","then":"They bring it up again, assuming you're still deciding."},{"text":"Tell them firmly that it's really none of their business at all","then":"The sharp reply creates a tension that lingers past the conversation."}],"why":"Receiving the care while keeping the plan yours declines the overreach without rejecting them."},{"prompt":"A week later they offer 'advice' on a big purchase you're planning. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Listen warmly, then say the decision is yours and your partner's","then":"They accept it easily, glad you at least heard them out.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll think about their advice","then":"They assume that means you're likely to follow it."},{"text":"Change the subject before they finish","then":"They feel unheard, and bring the advice up again another day."},{"text":"Tell them plainly to stop offering financial opinions altogether","then":"The comment lands harshly, and the next visit feels stiff."}],"why":"Hearing them out, then holding the line, respects them without handing over the decision."},{"prompt":"A relative offers to build you an investment plan 'so you don't make mistakes.' What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank them, and say you already have a plan you trust","then":"They nod, satisfied you've got it covered.","best":true},{"text":"Let them make a few suggestions, just to be polite","then":"They start expecting to be consulted on future money decisions too."},{"text":"Ignore the offer without addressing it","then":"They bring it up again, unsure if you even heard them."},{"text":"Say money advice from family is never welcome","then":"The comment stings, and future conversations feel a little guarded."}],"why":"Naming your own plan closes the offer warmly, without leaving it open-ended."},{"prompt":"Months later, they mention how well you two seem to be managing money on your own. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank them warmly, and share how your own plan is going","then":"They look genuinely pleased, and the topic settles for good.","best":true},{"text":"Say thanks, and quickly change the subject","then":"The moment passes, but you don't get to enjoy it together."},{"text":"Bring up, unprompted, how uncomfortable their earlier offer had felt","then":"The old moment resurfaces, souring what was otherwise a nice comment."},{"text":"Joke that you're relieved they finally backed off","then":"The joke lands sharper than intended, and the mood dips."}],"why":"Naming the plan warmly turns a tense moment into a settled, good one."}],"debrief":"Your salary and savings are the couple's call; receive the love and decline the overreach, warmly.","relearn":"Money is the couple's call: take the care and keep the control."},
  {"id":"fm-1179","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"fix the team","hook":"Your partner's parents often question your plans, and you want to stand as one team.","steps":[{"prompt":"At dinner, your partner goes quiet again while you make the case alone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing in the moment, and raise it kindly in private later","then":"You get through dinner, and there's a clear moment waiting to talk it out.","best":true},{"text":"Nudge your partner under the table to speak up","then":"Your partner glances at you but still stays quiet for the rest of dinner."},{"text":"Keep arguing your case alone for the rest of dinner","then":"You carry the whole conversation, and the pattern repeats untouched."},{"text":"Call your partner out for staying silent, right at the table","then":"Your partner feels ambushed, and the dinner turns tense for everyone."}],"why":"Waiting to raise it privately protects your partner from being called out publicly."},{"prompt":"Later, in private, you bring it up. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Name it kindly: 'I need us to decide and hold it together'","then":"Your partner looks a little relieved someone finally named it plainly.","best":true},{"text":"Ask your partner why they always go quiet","then":"It comes across as a question to defend, rather than a shared fix."},{"text":"Bring it up only vaguely, hoping they'll read between the lines","then":"The moment passes unresolved, and the same risk lingers into next time."},{"text":"Say plainly that you're tired of carrying every argument alone","then":"Your partner feels blamed, and gets defensive instead of reflecting."}],"why":"Naming the pattern kindly, without blame, invites a real fix rather than defensiveness."},{"prompt":"At the next family dinner, a similar moment comes up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Pause for a moment, and let your partner have the first word","then":"Your partner steps in this time, and the two of you sound like a team.","best":true},{"text":"Jump in first, out of habit, before your partner can","then":"The old pattern slips back in, and your partner goes quiet again."},{"text":"Stay quiet too, waiting to see what happens","then":"Nobody speaks up, and the topic hangs awkwardly at the table."},{"text":"Point out loudly that it's your partner's turn to talk","then":"The comment puts your partner on the spot, and it lands badly."}],"why":"Pausing to let your partner step in is what actually breaks the old pattern."},{"prompt":"Months later, at a big family event, real pressure tests how you two handle it now. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Catch your partner's eye, and agree together who speaks first","then":"Your partner nods and steps in, steady.","best":true},{"text":"Assume the pattern is fully fixed and don't check in","then":"The old habit slips back in, just this once, under the pressure."},{"text":"Take over completely to avoid any risk","then":"Your partner feels sidelined again, exactly like before."},{"text":"Remind your partner sharply, right there, to finally speak up","then":"The reminder makes your partner more nervous, not more confident."}],"why":"A quiet check-in before high pressure moments keeps the fixed pattern holding."}],"debrief":"When a partner keeps caving, repair the team in private. The fix is alignment.","relearn":"If your partner keeps caving, name it gently and rebuild the team."},
  {"id":"fm-1180","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"our call as parents","hook":"Your aunt means well, and you want to keep your home your own without hurting her.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt moves your furniture around again without asking. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Affirm her love, and hold the line: 'We love that you care. This is our call.'","then":"She looks a little surprised, then nods. 'Fair enough,' she says.","best":true},{"text":"Let it go, since it's just furniture","then":"She takes it as fine, and starts changing other things too."},{"text":"Quietly move it back yourself, without saying anything to her at all","then":"She notices, and simply tries again a different way next time."},{"text":"Tell her sharply to stop interfering","then":"She looks hurt, and says she was only trying to help."}],"why":"Affirming her care while holding the line lets her feel valued and keeps the home yours."},{"prompt":"A week later she rearranges a different room. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Affirm her love, warmly, and hold your line","then":"She pauses, then says, 'I hear you. I'll ask first from now on.'","best":true},{"text":"Let it slide again, since it's a small thing","then":"The pattern quietly becomes the new normal, one room at a time."},{"text":"Mention it to your partner instead of her","then":"Your partner sympathises, but nothing changes with her directly."},{"text":"Accuse her outright of never respecting your home here","then":"She feels attacked, and gets defensive instead of hearing you."}],"why":"Repeating the same warm line, calmly, is what finally gets it heard."},{"prompt":"The following visit, she asks before changing anything for the first time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Thank her warmly for checking, and say yes to what makes sense","then":"She looks pleased, and the asking becomes her habit going forward.","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing, just relieved she asked","then":"The new habit isn't reinforced, so it's easy for it to slip."},{"text":"Say it's about time she started asking","then":"The comment lands as a jab, even though it's meant lightly."},{"text":"Add a long list of other things she should ask about first","then":"The list turns her thanks into a lecture, and she stiffens."}],"why":"Thanking her for asking reinforces the very habit you wanted."},{"prompt":"Months later, a cousin asks how you got a relative to stop overstepping. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you try to affirm the love, and hold the line, each time","then":"The cousin nods, glad to hear a way that doesn't need a big fight.","best":true},{"text":"Say she just eventually got the hint","then":"The cousin assumes there's nothing to actually do but wait it out."},{"text":"Say you mostly just let the small things go","then":"The cousin isn't sure what to actually try with their own relative."},{"text":"Say you basically had to fight her to get anywhere","then":"The cousin pictures a much bigger battle than there needs to be."}],"why":"Naming the steady, repeated approach is what makes it useful to pass on."}],"debrief":"Decisions about your home are the couple's; hold that warmly and keep the bond.","relearn":"Your home is your call. Affirm the love and hold the line."},
  {"id":"fm-1181","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each family moment to the tool that fits it.","pairs":[{"left":"Unsolicited advice you don't want","right":"A warm 'thanks, we'll think about it'"},{"left":"A recurring nosy question","right":"One repeatable team line"},{"left":"A surprise demand at a gathering","right":"'We'll decide and get back to you'"},{"left":"Heated couple disagreement","right":"Settle it privately first"},{"left":"Control, threats, or a dowry demand","right":"Reach a counsellor or Women Helpline 181"}],"relearn":"Each moment has its tool: a warm deflect, a repeatable line, the couple line, private alignment, or help.","persona":"Razia","source":"moment to tool"},
  {"id":"fm-1182","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each tool to exactly what it protects.","pairs":[{"left":"The warm 'no'","right":"Declining without rejecting the person"},{"left":"The pre-visit check-in","right":"Arriving aligned, not split"},{"left":"The repeatable team line","right":"Answering as one, every time"},{"left":"Knowing your family map","right":"Feeling placed, not powerless"},{"left":"The help routes","right":"Safety when friction becomes abuse"}],"relearn":"Each tool guards something: the bond, your alignment, your unity, your sense of place, or your safety.","persona":"any","source":"tool to what it guards"},
  {"id":"fm-1183","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match the family situation to whether it calls for a boundary or for help.","pairs":[{"left":"In-laws drop in unannounced","right":"A kind heads-up boundary"},{"left":"Pressure to host every weekend","right":"A warm, clear 'no' sometimes"},{"left":"Your money is being controlled","right":"A counsellor or Women Helpline 181"},{"left":"A dowry 'gift' is demanded","right":"Shut it down: it's illegal"},{"left":"Threats if you don't obey","right":"Emergency help on 112"}],"relearn":"Friction calls for a boundary; control, dowry demands, and threats call for help. Match the right one.","persona":"Razia","source":"boundary or help"},
  {"id":"fm-1184","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each warm 'no' to the overreach it gently declines.","pairs":[{"left":"'We've picked our baby's name, thank you'","right":"Naming your child for you"},{"left":"'We'll holiday just the two of us this year'","right":"Joining every trip uninvited"},{"left":"'We'll manage our own budget'","right":"Taking over your finances"},{"left":"'We'll visit when we can, not weekly'","right":"Demanding a fixed weekly visit"},{"left":"'We'll decide schooling together'","right":"Enrolling your kid without asking"}],"relearn":"A warm 'no' names the overreach and the couple's call in one kind line, repeatable for each one.","persona":"any","source":"warm no to overreach"},
  {"id":"fm-1185","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each persona's moment to the tool that helps them most.","pairs":[{"left":"Pooja, swamped in a joint household","right":"A kind boundary on the load"},{"left":"Sneha, fielding 'settle down' pressure","right":"A warm, repeatable deflect"},{"left":"Karthik, leaving his partner exposed","right":"Backing her warmly in the room"},{"left":"Reshma, money and movement controlled","right":"A private route to a counsellor"},{"left":"A couple facing a surprise demand","right":"'We'll decide and get back to you'"}],"relearn":"The right tool fits the person and the moment: a boundary, a deflect, team-backing, help, or the couple line.","persona":"any","source":"persona to tool"},
  {"id":"fm-1186","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each early step to building your family map.","pairs":[{"left":"Learn who's who and how they relate","right":"See the whole web"},{"left":"Notice each in-law as a person","right":"Move past just roles"},{"left":"Spot which customs differ","right":"Learn, don't judge"},{"left":"Find your own fair space in it","right":"Take your place, don't shrink"},{"left":"Mark where friction tips into abuse","right":"Know when to reach for help"}],"relearn":"Mapping the family means seeing the web, the people, the customs, your place, and the line to help.","persona":"any","source":"build the map"},
  {"id":"fm-1187","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each check-in question to what it helps the couple agree before a visit.","pairs":[{"left":"'What topics might come up?'","right":"Anticipate the pressure points"},{"left":"'What's our line on each one?'","right":"Agree one united answer"},{"left":"'What's off the table today?'","right":"Decide what you won't discuss"},{"left":"'How long do we stay?'","right":"Set a shared exit plan"},{"left":"'What's our signal to leave?'","right":"A quiet cue between you two"}],"relearn":"A pre-visit check-in agrees the topics, the line, the limits, the timing, and a signal, so you arrive as one.","persona":"Pooja","source":"the check-in questions"},
  {"id":"fm-1188","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","hook":"Match each unhelpful 'tool' to the healthy move that actually fits.","pairs":[{"left":"Silent treatment","right":"A spoken, warm 'no'"},{"left":"Cutting them off by default","right":"A boundary that keeps the bond"},{"left":"One huge blow-up","right":"Small, consistent limits"},{"left":"Venting to outsiders","right":"Aligning with your partner"},{"left":"Just enduring it forever","right":"Naming it kindly and clearly"}],"relearn":"Misapplied 'tools' damage the bond; the healthy move is warm, spoken, consistent, and aligned.","persona":"any","source":"swap the wrong tool"},
  {"id":"fm-1189","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Sneha","source":"warm and firm","hook":"Your aunt has strong opinions about what a 'proper' relationship should look like by now.","setup":"She brings it up at a family lunch.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks: \"Why aren't you two settling down properly like everyone else?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We've thought it through and we're happy with our choices. Thanks for caring about us.\"","then":"She pauses, then says, \"Well, as long as you're both happy.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We're still figuring some things out.\"","then":"She takes that as an opening and offers more unsolicited advice."},{"text":"\"Can we talk about something else, please?\"","then":"She lets it go for now, but circles back before lunch ends."},{"text":"\"Why does everyone in this family keep interrogating me about this?\"","then":"She looks taken aback, saying she only meant to help."}],"why":"Naming appreciation while holding your own choice answers her without inviting more pressure."},{"prompt":"Later she brings it up again, this time in front of your partner. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"\"We've thought it through, and we're happy with our choices.\"","then":"Your partner nods along, and she seems to accept the steady answer.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not do this in front of everyone.\"","then":"She takes the deflection as discomfort, and asks even more pointedly."},{"text":"\"Go ahead, tell her.\"","then":"Your partner fields it solo, unsure if you're backing them up."},{"text":"\"I already told you. Drop it.\"","then":"The sharp tone surprises her, and lunch turns a little tense."}],"why":"Repeating the same steady answer in front of your partner shows you're a team on it."},{"prompt":"A few months later she mentions to another relative that you two are 'still figuring it out.' What do you say?","options":[{"text":"\"Actually, we've settled it. We're happy with our choices.\"","then":"The relative nods, and your aunt looks a little surprised but drops it.","best":true},{"text":"\"Mm, sure.\"","then":"The story sticks, and more relatives start asking about it too."},{"text":"\"She likes to worry about things.\"","then":"It brushes past the moment, but the misunderstanding stays alive."},{"text":"\"Why do you keep saying that, like it's still somehow unresolved?\"","then":"She looks embarrassed in front of the other relative."}],"why":"Correcting the story directly stops it from becoming the family's version of events."},{"prompt":"At the next big family gathering, she doesn't bring it up, and instead asks: \"How's work going for you both?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Work's going really well, thanks for asking.\"","then":"She listens with real interest, and the visit feels easy.","best":true},{"text":"\"Mm, it's fine I guess.\"","then":"The moment passes, pleasant but a little flat."},{"text":"\"Funny, you usually have more questions than that.\"","then":"She looks unsure whether to take it as a compliment or a jab."},{"text":"\"I figured you'd have brought up the settling down thing by now.\"","then":"The comment reopens exactly the topic that was finally closing."}],"why":"Engaging warmly with the new topic she offers is what lets the peace continue."}],"relearn":"A warm, firm, repeatable line closes the pressure without a fight: honour the care, hold your choice."},
  {"id":"fm-1190","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"talk and get back","hook":"At a gathering a relative springs a demand on you both.","setup":"He catches you both together, near the entrance.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"You two should host the whole family reunion this year, no question.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's big. Let's talk and get back to you.\"","then":"He nods. \"Fair, just let me know soon,\" he says.","best":true},{"text":"\"Um, okay, I guess we can probably do that then.\"","then":"You've committed before actually discussing it with your partner."},{"text":"\"Um, what do you think we should do?\"","then":"Your partner, caught off guard too, ends up agreeing on the spot."},{"text":"\"Absolutely not. That's way too much.\"","then":"He looks surprised by the flat no, before you've even discussed it."}],"why":"Buying time with 'let's talk and get back to you' lets the couple decide together, not alone."},{"prompt":"Later, alone with your partner, they ask: \"So, what do you think we should do?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's figure out what we can actually manage, together.\"","then":"You land on a smaller version you're both genuinely comfortable with.","best":true},{"text":"\"Honestly, I'd feel bad saying no to him.\"","then":"Your partner feels pushed toward a yes they never discussed."},{"text":"\"Honestly, you decide. I don't really mind either way on this one.\"","then":"Your partner ends up deciding alone, which wasn't really the point."},{"text":"\"He shouldn't have even asked like that.\"","then":"The venting fades, and you circle back to actually deciding together."}],"why":"Talking it through together first is what actually protects both of you from overcommitting."},{"prompt":"Later you find him again, and he asks: \"Have you two settled things about the reunion?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We can host just part of it, not all.\"","then":"He looks pleased. \"That works. Thank you both,\" he says.","best":true},{"text":"\"We'll do the whole thing, since you asked us to.\"","then":"You agree to more than you're comfortable with, just to close the topic."},{"text":"\"Go ahead and tell him yourself.\"","then":"He hears it from just one of you, and assumes it's their idea."},{"text":"\"It's too much. Please stop asking.\"","then":"The flat refusal lands harsher than intended, and he looks hurt."}],"why":"Offering a workable middle path respects the ask without giving away too much."},{"prompt":"The next year, he asks again: \"So, are you hosting the reunion this time?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's talk it over, and get back to you.\"","then":"He nods. \"Fair,\" he says, and waits to hear back.","best":true},{"text":"\"Sure, we'll probably do the same as last year.\"","then":"You agree before actually checking if it still works for you both."},{"text":"\"Let's talk about something else entirely for now.\"","then":"He takes the deflection as a yes, and starts planning around it."},{"text":"\"Can you stop springing this on us every year?\"","then":"The frustration lands harder than the moment really called for."}],"why":"Repeating the same line, year after year, keeps the decision the couple's every time."}],"relearn":"'Let's talk and get back to you' buys time to decide as a couple, not a cornered solo yes."},
  {"id":"fm-1191","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Pooja","source":"the warm no","hook":"Hosting has quietly become an every weekend expectation, and you want it to feel doable again.","setup":"Your mother-in-law calls about it.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"Why don't you host everyone every weekend? It would be so nice.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'd love to host once a month. Every weekend is too much for us.\"","then":"She pauses, then says, \"Once a month sounds lovely, actually.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe. Let's see how it goes.\"","then":"She takes that as a yes and starts planning the very next weekend."},{"text":"\"I don't really have time to talk about this now.\"","then":"She brings it up again on the very next call."},{"text":"\"Sure, whatever works for everyone else. I'll just manage somehow, I guess.\"","then":"You agree, already dreading the next several weekends."}],"why":"Offering a kind reason and a real alternative sets a warm boundary without erasing yourself."},{"prompt":"The next weekend she calls: \"So, see you all for lunch as usual?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's make this our once a month, going forward.\"","then":"She sounds a little disappointed, but says, \"Oh right, next time then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I guess this once is fine.\"","then":"Once turns into every weekend, and the pattern quietly sets in."},{"text":"\"Sure, come on over.\"","then":"You're hosting again, and no limit has been set."},{"text":"\"Honestly, every single week is just not happening.\"","then":"The sharper tone catches her off guard, and the call ends awkwardly."}],"why":"Stating your limit gently, and again, is what makes a boundary real."},{"prompt":"Your partner brings it up one evening: \"How do you think the visits have been going?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's name what's working, and reset what's slipped.\"","then":"You both feel more on the same page about visits again.","best":true},{"text":"\"I think it's fine. We don't need to talk about it.\"","then":"Without naming it, the rhythm is left to chance."},{"text":"\"It's mostly been good, honestly.\"","then":"The slips go unaddressed, and the plan keeps quietly drifting."},{"text":"\"She's pushed for more than we can manage almost every time.\"","then":"It turns into a list of grievances rather than a reset."}],"why":"Naming what's working, and resetting what's not, keeps a boundary real over time."}],"relearn":"A warm 'no' gives a kind reason and an alternative: clear, not rude, and never self-erasing."},
  {"id":"fm-1192","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"set the tone","hook":"You and your partner have never quite agreed on how to handle their family.","setup":"You talk it over one quiet evening.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"What's our approach with my family going forward?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's be warm with them, but make the big calls as a couple.\"","then":"Your partner nods firmly. \"That's exactly what I was hoping we'd say,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's just handle each thing as it comes up.\"","then":"Your partner agrees, though nothing specific actually gets decided."},{"text":"\"You know them better. You decide.\"","then":"Your partner ends up carrying decisions that should be shared."},{"text":"\"Let's just keep a bit more distance from your side, from now on, I think.\"","then":"Your partner looks unsure, worried it's more than either of you wanted."}],"why":"A shared tone that is warm, aligned and mutually backed gives you both something concrete to hold onto."},{"prompt":"Soon after, a visit to your own parents comes up. Your partner asks: \"How do we handle this one?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's agree what we want first, then tell them together.\"","then":"Your partner smiles. \"Good, let's sit down and figure it out now,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's just wing this one. Honestly, it's probably going to be fine.\"","then":"You end up deciding it separately, and the old habit creeps back in."},{"text":"\"You handle this one. I trust you.\"","then":"Your partner decides alone, which isn't really what either of you wants."},{"text":"\"Let's just say no to keep it simple.\"","then":"Your partner feels the tone has turned colder than intended."}],"why":"Deciding together first, then speaking as one, keeps you a team."},{"prompt":"Months later, your partner asks: \"My mother wants to know how we decide things now. What should I tell her?\"","options":[{"text":"\"That we decide together first, then answer as one.\"","then":"Your partner nods. \"That's what I'll tell her, then,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"\"That it's none of her business how we decide.\"","then":"Your partner winces. \"I'd rather she felt included, not shut out.\""},{"text":"\"That you mostly decide, and I go along.\"","then":"Your partner looks unsure. \"Is that how you actually want it?\""},{"text":"\"Tell her we'll work it out as things come up.\"","then":"Your partner hesitates. \"That's what used to cause the fights.\""}],"why":"Saying plainly that you decide together, then answer as one, keeps the approach clear to everyone."}],"relearn":"A shared tone that is warm, aligned and mutually backed beats lurching from one blow-up to the next."},
  {"id":"fm-1193","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"both of us here","hook":"A relative tries to get you to gang up on your partner.","setup":"She pulls you aside at a gathering.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"Just between us, don't you think your partner needs to change?\"","options":[{"text":"\"If there's something about us, let's say it with both of us here.\"","then":"She hesitates, then says, \"Fair enough, forget I said anything.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe, but let's not get into it right now.\"","then":"She takes the opening and tries again later, more directly."},{"text":"\"Okay, tell me. Just don't let my partner know we talked.\"","then":"You hear more than you wanted, and it plants a quiet doubt."},{"text":"\"How dare you even try to turn me against my own partner like that.\"","then":"She denies any bad intention, and the exchange turns tense."}],"why":"Naming it aloud, in front of both of you, shuts down the wedge before it can open."},{"prompt":"Later, a different relative tries something similar: \"Just between us, right?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's always talk about this with both of us present.\"","then":"The relative nods and lets it go, seemingly satisfied.","best":true},{"text":"\"Go on. I'm listening.\"","then":"You hear a little more than you wanted, and it lingers."},{"text":"\"I'm not sure what you mean.\"","then":"They rephrase and try again, more plainly this time."},{"text":"\"Everyone needs to stop trying to do this.\"","then":"The sharpness surprises them, and it becomes a small family story."}],"why":"Repeating the same firm line keeps every attempt from wearing you down."},{"prompt":"That night, your partner asks: \"You seem distracted. Everything okay?\"","options":[{"text":"\"A couple of relatives tried talking to me about you privately.\"","then":"Your partner relaxes. \"Thank you for telling me,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"\"Some family stuff came up. I'll tell you later.\"","then":"Your partner senses something, and waits for a later that may not come."},{"text":"\"I'm just tired. Let's talk about something else.\"","then":"Your partner senses you're downplaying it, and later feels blindsided by the details."},{"text":"\"Your family keeps trying to turn me against you, and honestly I'm sick of it.\"","then":"Your partner feels defensive of their family, and it turns into an argument."}],"why":"Telling your partner honestly keeps the couple a team against the wedge."},{"prompt":"Months later, a relative leans in again: \"You must agree with me on this.\"","options":[{"text":"\"With both of us, or not at all. That's simply how we do this.\"","then":"The relative laughs a little. \"Alright, alright,\" they say, giving up.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's just skip this conversation today.\"","then":"The relative assumes it's just timing, and tries again another day."},{"text":"\"Maybe just this once. What did you want to say?\"","then":"The relative takes the opening and says more than you meant to hear."},{"text":"\"I can't believe you're still trying this.\"","then":"The frustration lands harder than the moment calls for."}],"why":"A consistent, repeated answer is what finally makes people stop trying."}],"relearn":"Saying 'with both of us here' out loud refuses triangulation and protects the couple's trust."},
  {"id":"fm-1194","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"honour and decide","hook":"Your father-in-law gives advice you respect but won't follow.","setup":"He shares it over dinner, clearly wanting to help.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"Here's what I'd do in your situation, based on my own experience.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you. That helps. We'll weigh it and decide what's right for us.\"","then":"He nods, satisfied just to have been heard. \"Fair enough,\" he says.","best":true},{"text":"\"Sounds reasonable. We'll see.\"","then":"He takes that as likely agreement and checks in on it again soon."},{"text":"\"That's interesting, thanks.\"","then":"He's left unsure whether his advice actually mattered at all."},{"text":"\"Fine. I suppose we'll just do it your way then, if that's what you want.\"","then":"He isn't sure you meant it, and says he'll ask again once you've had time."}],"why":"Gratitude plus your own decision honours the advice without obligating you to follow it."},{"prompt":"A week later, he asks: \"So, have you two decided what to do?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We thought it through, and we're choosing differently. It helped us decide.\"","then":"He looks a little disappointed, but says, \"At least you thought it over properly.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We're still deciding, honestly.\"","then":"He keeps checking in, assuming a decision is imminent."},{"text":"\"We haven't really had time to think about it.\"","then":"He offers to explain his reasoning again, in more detail."},{"text":"\"Honestly, your advice didn't really apply that well to our own situation.\"","then":"He feels dismissed, even though you meant to explain, not reject."}],"why":"Naming that it helped, even while choosing differently, keeps him feeling valued."},{"prompt":"Months later, your decision turns out well. He says warmly: \"I'm glad it worked out for you both.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you. Your advice actually helped us think it through properly.\"","then":"He beams, glad his input mattered even if you chose differently.","best":true},{"text":"\"Thanks. We got lucky with the timing.\"","then":"The moment passes kindly, though his part in it goes unnamed."},{"text":"\"It really came together well, didn't it?\"","then":"He never learns that his input actually shaped your thinking."},{"text":"\"See, we were right all along not to follow your advice exactly.\"","then":"The comment stings a little, even though it's meant lightly."}],"why":"Crediting his input, warmly, closes the loop and keeps the bond strong."}],"relearn":"You can honour an elder's advice and still make your own decision: gratitude plus your own call."},
  {"id":"fm-1195","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Razia","source":"no dowry, full stop","hook":"Wedding planning has brought old expectations around gifts and money into the open.","setup":"A relative brings it up during planning.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"It's just tradition. A small gift is expected.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're not doing any dowry. It's illegal. Let's drop it.\"","then":"She looks a little taken aback, then says, \"Alright, just mentioning it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe I'm just being too sensitive about this.\"","then":"The self-doubt creeps in, though the request itself hasn't changed."},{"text":"\"Maybe everyone does this. Perhaps it's not worth the fight.\"","then":"The doubt lingers, though nothing is actually promised yet."},{"text":"\"How dare you even suggest that to me.\"","then":"She denies meaning anything by it, and the conversation turns defensive."}],"why":"A dowry hint is shut down, not negotiated. It's never your fault that someone asks."},{"prompt":"A different relative says: \"It's just a small custom. Why make a fuss?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No dowry. That's not something we're doing.\"","then":"The relative shrugs, and moves on to other plans.","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe it really is just a small, harmless custom.\"","then":"The thought sits uneasily, even though nothing's been agreed."},{"text":"\"My friend said it's easier to just go along with it.\"","then":"Repeating that doesn't make the old pressure feel any less real."},{"text":"\"This family is obsessed with money.\"","then":"The comment offends people who had nothing to do with the request."}],"why":"Repeating the same firm no keeps the boundary consistent no matter who raises it."},{"prompt":"Your partner notices you're quiet and asks: \"Everything okay?\"","options":[{"text":"\"A relative hinted at a dowry gift. I want us on the same page.\"","then":"Your partner nods firmly. \"Good, let's keep saying no, together,\" they say.","best":true},{"text":"\"Some relatives said some odd things about gifts.\"","then":"Your partner isn't sure how serious it actually is."},{"text":"\"Something came up about gifts. It's probably nothing.\"","then":"Your partner senses you're downplaying it, and asks more questions later."},{"text":"\"I'll handle it myself so you don't have to worry.\"","then":"Your partner is shut out, and the pressure stays with you alone."}],"why":"Telling your partner keeps you a team against the pressure, not carrying it alone."},{"prompt":"At the next planning meeting, a relative says: \"One small gift would settle everything. Nobody needs to know.\"","options":[{"text":"\"No dowry, ever. If it goes on, we'll see a counsellor.\"","then":"The relative goes quiet, then changes the subject.","best":true},{"text":"\"Ask my parents. They handle these things.\"","then":"The talk moves to the elders, and the demand is now being negotiated."},{"text":"\"I won't mention this to anyone else. It will only cause worry.\"","then":"The relative smiles, and the demand stays alive, hidden from the people who could help."},{"text":"\"Maybe a small gift is just a way of showing goodwill.\"","then":"The idea settles in, and the pressure grows with it."}],"why":"A dowry demand is shut down, never negotiated. Help is there if it continues, and it's never your fault."}],"relearn":"A dowry hint is shut down, never negotiated: giving, taking and demanding it are all illegal."},
  {"id":"fm-1196","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"the team debrief","hook":"After a tense visit, you and your partner are raw.","setup":"You're both quiet in the car on the way home.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner finally says: \"That was a lot tonight.\" What do you say?","options":[{"text":"\"That was a lot. What worked, and what should we do differently?\"","then":"Your partner exhales. \"Okay, let's actually talk about it,\" they say, relieved.","best":true},{"text":"\"Yeah, your family is always like this.\"","then":"Your partner gets a little defensive, and the drive turns quiet again."},{"text":"\"Let's just not talk about any of it tonight, and go straight to bed.\"","then":"The tiredness wins tonight, but the rawness carries into tomorrow."},{"text":"\"You should have backed me up more in there.\"","then":"Your partner feels blamed, and the debrief turns into a fight."}],"why":"Asking what worked and what's next turns a hard night into repair, not blame."},{"prompt":"The next day, your partner says: \"That moment at dinner really caught me off guard.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Tell me more. What would help next time?\"","then":"Your partner feels heard, and you land on a small plan together.","best":true},{"text":"\"Yeah, let's not dwell on it.\"","then":"The quick agreement skips past what your partner actually needed to say."},{"text":"\"Anyway, how was your lunch today?\"","then":"Your partner's feeling goes unaddressed, even as the mood lifts briefly."},{"text":"\"Honestly, a moment caught me off guard too.\"","then":"The conversation turns into a tally of who had it worse that night."}],"why":"Listening and asking what helps turns one hard night into a real fix."},{"prompt":"A similar family visit is coming up soon. Your partner asks: \"Should we plan ahead this time?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, let's make a plan, and check in again before it.\"","then":"You both walk in steadier, remembering exactly what you'd agreed.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm sure we'll just remember what to do.\"","then":"The old, tense pattern from that night starts creeping back in."},{"text":"\"Let's not even talk about that whole night again, honestly.\"","then":"The lesson from that night gets lost before you even need it."},{"text":"\"I just know it's going to go badly again.\"","then":"Your partner starts the visit anxious instead of steady."}],"why":"Reusing the plan, and checking in again, makes the repair actually stick."}],"relearn":"A team debrief asks 'what worked, what's next'. It repairs the couple instead of importing the tension."},
  {"id":"fm-1197","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each move: a tool that protects the bond, or a habit that quietly damages it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Agreeing your line privately before a visit"},{"id":"b","text":"A warm, clear 'no' with a reason"},{"id":"c","text":"Backing your partner in the room"},{"id":"d","text":"Giving the silent treatment for days"},{"id":"e","text":"Venting about in-laws to everyone but them"},{"id":"f","text":"Cutting them off at the first disagreement"}],"bins":[{"id":"tool","label":"Protective tool","valence":"pos"},{"id":"damage","label":"Quietly damaging","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"tool","b":"tool","c":"tool","d":"damage","e":"damage","f":"damage"},"relearn":"Aligning, the warm no and team-backing protect the bond; silence, gossip and snap cut-offs corrode it.","persona":"any","source":"tool or damage"},
  {"id":"fm-1198","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each situation: work it through with a tool, or reach for help?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"In-laws and you clash over festival plans"},{"id":"b","text":"Your partner forgot to back you up once"},{"id":"c","text":"Your phone and money are being controlled"},{"id":"d","text":"A dowry 'gift' is being demanded"},{"id":"e","text":"Disagreement over how often to visit"},{"id":"f","text":"You're threatened if you don't obey"}],"bins":[{"id":"tool","label":"Work it through","valence":"pos"},{"id":"help","label":"Reach for help","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"tool","b":"tool","c":"help","d":"help","e":"tool","f":"help"},"relearn":"Ordinary friction you work through; control, dowry demands and threats are abuse: reach for help.","persona":"Reshma","source":"work it or help"},
  {"id":"fm-1199","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each line: a warm 'no' that keeps the bond, or a harsh 'no' that wounds?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"'We'd love to, just not this weekend'"},{"id":"b","text":"'Stop bothering us about it'"},{"id":"c","text":"'We've decided, but thank you for caring'"},{"id":"d","text":"'Mind your own business'"},{"id":"e","text":"'That one's our call. We hope you understand'"},{"id":"f","text":"'Why can't you ever leave us alone?'"}],"bins":[{"id":"warm","label":"Warm no","valence":"pos"},{"id":"harsh","label":"Harsh no","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"warm","b":"harsh","c":"warm","d":"harsh","e":"warm","f":"harsh"},"relearn":"The same limit can be warm (reason given, person honoured) or harsh; warm keeps the bond intact.","persona":"any","source":"warm or harsh no"},
  {"id":"fm-1200","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each one: a couple's own call, or genuinely shared with the wider family?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"How you spend your salary"},{"id":"b","text":"Whether to have children, and when"},{"id":"c","text":"The date of a shared family festival"},{"id":"d","text":"How to raise your own kids"},{"id":"e","text":"Hosting a joint family ritual together"},{"id":"f","text":"Which city you live in for work"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"Couple's call","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shared","label":"Genuinely shared","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"couple","c":"shared","d":"couple","e":"shared","f":"couple"},"relearn":"Salary, kids, where you live are the couple's call; shared rituals and festival dates you decide together.","persona":"any","source":"couple or shared"},
  {"id":"fm-1201","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each habit: a green-flag pattern to keep, or one to change?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Talking issues through early and kindly"},{"id":"b","text":"Backing each other in front of family"},{"id":"c","text":"Debriefing gently after tense events"},{"id":"d","text":"Keeping score of every past slight"},{"id":"e","text":"Competing over whose family is 'right'"},{"id":"f","text":"Letting resentment fester in silence"}],"bins":[{"id":"keep","label":"Keep it","valence":"pos"},{"id":"change","label":"Change it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"keep","b":"keep","c":"keep","d":"change","e":"change","f":"change"},"relearn":"Talking early, backing each other and debriefing are keepers; scorekeeping, rivalry and festering need changing.","persona":"any","source":"keep or change"},
  {"id":"fm-1202","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each timing: a heads-up that prevents friction, or a silence that breeds it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Telling in-laws your visit plans early"},{"id":"b","text":"Springing a big decision on them last-minute"},{"id":"c","text":"Agreeing your line before the gathering"},{"id":"d","text":"Bottling up an issue for months"},{"id":"e","text":"Flagging a clash kindly when it's small"},{"id":"f","text":"Saying nothing until you explode"}],"bins":[{"id":"early","label":"Early heads-up","valence":"pos"},{"id":"silence","label":"Festering silence","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"early","b":"silence","c":"early","d":"silence","e":"early","f":"silence"},"relearn":"Early, warm heads-ups prevent friction; springing things late or bottling up breeds it. Silence isn't kindness.","persona":"Pooja","source":"early or silence"},
  {"id":"fm-1203","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each one: a sustainable, repeatable move, or a one-off blow-up?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A short check-in before every visit"},{"id":"b","text":"One dramatic confrontation to 'fix it all'"},{"id":"c","text":"The same warm line, used each time"},{"id":"d","text":"Small limits kept consistently"},{"id":"e","text":"A furious group message at midnight"},{"id":"f","text":"Storming out of a function in anger"}],"bins":[{"id":"sustain","label":"Sustainable","valence":"pos"},{"id":"oneoff","label":"One-off blow-up","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"sustain","b":"oneoff","c":"sustain","d":"sustain","e":"oneoff","f":"oneoff"},"relearn":"Consistent check-ins, repeated lines and small limits last; dramatic blow-ups rarely fix a lifelong bond.","persona":"Sneha","source":"sustainable or blow-up"},
  {"id":"fm-1204","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each tool to the job it's actually for.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Pre-visit check-in"},{"id":"b","text":"The warm 'no'"},{"id":"c","text":"The repeatable team line"},{"id":"d","text":"The help routes"},{"id":"e","text":"The family map itself"},{"id":"f","text":"A team debrief after"}],"bins":[{"id":"before","label":"Before & during","valence":"tell"},{"id":"safety","label":"When it's serious","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"before","b":"before","c":"before","d":"safety","e":"before","f":"before"},"relearn":"Most tools shape the everyday: check-in, warm no, team line, map, debrief; help routes are for when it's serious.","persona":"any","source":"which job"},
  {"id":"fm-1205","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"There's nothing you can really do about family dynamics. You just have to endure them.\"","myth":{"un":"There's nothing you can really do about family dynamics. You just have to endure them.","re":"You have real tools, like alignment, kind boundaries, repeatable lines and help routes, that shift the dynamic over time.","why":"Family dynamics are workable, not just endurable; you're not powerless in them."},"relearn":"You're not powerless: alignment, boundaries, repeatable lines and help routes genuinely shift the dynamic.","persona":"any","source":"not powerless"},
  {"id":"fm-1206","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Asking a counsellor for help means the family has failed.\"","myth":{"un":"Asking a counsellor for help means the family has failed.","re":"Bringing in a neutral third person is a tool strong families use early, not a verdict that they have failed.","why":"Outside help is a resource you reach for, not proof anything is broken."},"relearn":"Reaching for a counsellor or mediator is a smart tool, not a sign of failure.","persona":"Pooja","source":"help isn't failure"},
  {"id":"fm-1207","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A good couple should just absorb whatever the family throws at them.\"","myth":{"un":"A good couple should just absorb whatever the family throws at them.","re":"A strong couple decides together and answers as one. Absorbing everything silently only stores up resentment.","why":"Standing as a team lowers conflict; silently absorbing it corrodes the couple from inside."},"relearn":"A couple isn't a sponge: deciding and answering as one beats silently absorbing everything.","persona":"Karthik","source":"not a sponge"},
  {"id":"fm-1208","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"The warm 'no' is just a polite way of being rude to your elders.\"","myth":{"un":"The warm 'no' is just a polite way of being rude to your elders.","re":"A warm 'no' is kind, clear and reasoned. It honours the person while declining the request, the opposite of rude.","why":"Saying no with warmth and a reason respects the elder and keeps your own voice."},"relearn":"A warm 'no' (kind, clear, reason given) is a learnable tool, not rudeness.","persona":"Sneha","source":"warm no isn't rude"},
  {"id":"fm-1209","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If you're having any family trouble, the tool is always a firmer boundary.\"","myth":{"un":"If you're having any family trouble, the tool is always a firmer boundary.","re":"Boundaries fix friction. But when behaviour is controlling or threatening, the right tool is help, not a bigger boundary.","why":"Abuse isn't a boundary problem; the fix is help and safety, not more adjusting."},"relearn":"Boundaries are for friction; when it's control or threats, the right tool is help, not a firmer boundary.","persona":"Razia","source":"right tool for the moment"},
  {"id":"fm-1210","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Cutting the family off is the cleanest way to protect your marriage.\"","myth":{"un":"Cutting the family off is the cleanest way to protect your marriage.","re":"Kind, consistent boundaries protect the marriage while keeping the family. Cutting off by default loses a web worth keeping.","why":"A boundary holds the bond; estrangement is a last resort for real harm, not the everyday tool."},"relearn":"Kind boundaries protect the marriage and keep the family; cutting off isn't the default tool.","persona":"any","source":"boundaries, not cut-off"},
  {"id":"fm-1211","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A couple check-in before a family visit is overthinking it: just go and wing it.\"","myth":{"un":"A couple check-in before a family visit is overthinking it. Just go and wing it.","re":"A two-minute check-in agrees your line on the likely topics, so you arrive aligned instead of split in the moment.","why":"Aligning before you face family prevents most blow-ups; winging it leaves you to be divided."},"relearn":"A quick pre-visit check-in isn't overthinking. It's how you arrive aligned instead of split.","persona":"Pooja","source":"check-in, not wing it"},
  {"id":"fm-1212","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can't control how relatives behave, but your alignment, your boundaries and your warmth are yours. Enough to start with?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["That's enough to start","I can work with that","Those are mine to use"],"affirm":"You can't control relatives, but your alignment, boundaries and warmth are yours, and they're enough to start.","relearn":"Control what's yours: alignment, boundaries and warmth; that's enough to shift the dynamic over time.","persona":"any","source":"what's yours"},
  {"id":"fm-1213","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: which tool do you most want in your pocket for the next family gathering?","prompt":"Pick the one that fits you:","options":["The warm 'no'","The repeatable team line","The pre-visit check-in"],"affirm":"Whichever you pick, it's a real, learnable tool, and you can carry more than one into the room.","relearn":"The warm no, the team line and the check-in are all yours to carry. Choose what fits the moment.","persona":"any","source":"pick your tool"},
  {"id":"fm-1214","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: picture saying a warm, firm 'no' to a relative, and the world not ending. How does that sit?","prompt":"How does it feel?","options":["Lighter than I expected","Still a little scary","Doable with practice"],"affirm":"A warm no feels lighter once you've said it, and it gets easier each time you use the same kind line.","relearn":"The warm no feels big the first time and ordinary by the tenth. It's a skill that grows with use.","persona":"Sneha","source":"saying the no"},
  {"id":"fm-1215","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: knowing one calm line, one trusted person and one helpline number is a kit you can carry into any hard family moment. Feel ready to pack it?","prompt":"What's your read?","options":["I'll pack it","One line, one person, one number","Carry it anywhere"],"affirm":"A steady line, a trusted person and a saved helpline are a small kit that travels with you into any tense moment.","relearn":"Your portable kit is one calm line, one trusted person and one saved helpline number.","persona":"any","source":"pack the kit"},
  {"id":"fm-1216","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: think of one thing you and your partner could agree to back each other on, every time. What comes up?","prompt":"What's on your mind?","options":["Our big decisions","Saying no to overreach","Not splitting in the room"],"affirm":"Whatever you name, agreeing to back each other on it is exactly what makes you a team family can rely on.","relearn":"Pick one thing to always back each other on. That agreement is the heart of the couple team.","persona":"Karthik","source":"back each other on"},
  {"id":"fm-1217","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a tool keeps a relationship healthy. But help is the tool when it turns harmful. Does that line feel clear to you?","prompt":"Where are you with it?","options":["It's clear","Clearer now","I'll hold onto that"],"affirm":"Boundaries and check-ins keep things healthy; when it's control or threats, reaching for help is the right move.","relearn":"Tools keep a relationship healthy; when behaviour is abusive, the right tool is help, not another boundary.","persona":"Reshma","source":"tool vs help"},
  {"id":"fm-1218","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: which feels harder for you right now, the warm 'no', or staying a united team in the room?","prompt":"Sit with it:","options":["The warm 'no'","Staying a team","Both, honestly"],"affirm":"Naming which is harder is the first step, and both get easier with a partner and a little practice.","relearn":"Whichever is harder, naming it lets you practise it on purpose, together.","persona":"any","source":"which is harder"},
  {"id":"fm-1219","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that quietly damage a lifelong family bond.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Small, warm boundaries kept consistently","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"One huge confrontation to 'sort it all out'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Aligning as a couple before each visit","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Giving relatives the silent treatment for weeks","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Assuming good intent where you can","trick":false}],"why":"A big blow-up and weeks of silent treatment damage the bond; warm boundaries and alignment sustain it.","relearn":"Sustainable family navigation is consistent warm boundaries and alignment, not blow-ups or silence.","persona":"Sneha","source":"two damaging moves"},
  {"id":"fm-1220","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that are a harsh 'no', not a warm one.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'We'd love to, just not this weekend'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"'Mind your own business for once'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'That one's our call. Thanks for caring'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"'Stop nagging us about it, seriously'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"'We've decided, but we hope you understand'","trick":false}],"why":"'Mind your own business' and 'stop nagging' wound the person; a warm no gives a reason and honours them.","relearn":"A warm no keeps the bond: reason given, person honoured; a harsh no just wounds.","persona":"any","source":"spot the harsh no"},
  {"id":"fm-1221","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that leave your partner exposed instead of backing them.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'We see it differently, but we'll talk it over'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Going silent while they're criticised at the table","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Quietly agreeing your line beforehand","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Siding with your parents against them","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Holding a united, warm front together","trick":false}],"why":"Going silent and siding with your parents leave your partner alone; backing them warmly keeps the team.","relearn":"Back your partner in the room. Silence and taking the family's side break the team's trust.","persona":"Karthik","source":"spot what exposes them"},
  {"id":"fm-1222","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two situations that need help, not just another boundary.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"In-laws disagree with your festival plans","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Your money and phone are being controlled","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A debate over how often you visit","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"A dowry 'gift' is being demanded","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Different cooking customs to get used to","trick":false}],"why":"Controlled money and a dowry demand are abuse: reach for help; the rest are ordinary friction to work through.","relearn":"Control over money and a dowry demand are abuse and need help; friction over plans and customs you work through.","persona":"Razia","source":"spot what needs help"},
  {"id":"fm-1223","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two 'tools' that are really triangulation traps to refuse.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Deciding privately, then answering as one","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Carrying a relative's secret complaint to your partner","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Asking to hear concerns with both present","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Letting a relative talk you against your partner","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Keeping side-channels out of the couple","trick":false}],"why":"Carrying secret complaints and being turned against your partner are triangulation; keeping it open refuses it.","relearn":"Refuse triangulation: no secret side-deals, no being turned against your partner. Keep it open.","persona":"any","source":"spot the wedge"},
  {"id":"fm-1224","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that import family tension into the couple itself.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Debriefing gently: what worked, what's next","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Blaming each other for how the event went","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Assuming you're on the same side","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Keeping score of whose family behaved worse","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Repairing quickly after a clash","trick":false}],"why":"Blaming each other and keeping family score turn the family's tension into your own; debriefing repairs the couple.","relearn":"Debrief and repair as a team; blaming each other and family scorekeeping import the tension into the couple.","persona":"Pooja","source":"spot what imports tension"},
  {"id":"fm-1225","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two responses that mistake a warm 'no' for surrender.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"'We'll host once a month, not every weekend'","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"'Sure, whatever everyone wants, I'll manage'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"'We've picked our baby's name, thank you'","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"'Okay, I guess we'll just do it your way'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"'We'll decide schooling together as parents'","trick":false}],"why":"'Whatever everyone wants' and 'we'll do it your way' erase the couple's call; a warm no keeps it while staying kind.","relearn":"A warm no holds the couple's call kindly; caving with 'whatever you want' just erases your own voice.","persona":"any","source":"spot the surrender"},
  {"id":"fm-1226","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two signs this has crossed from friction into something that needs help.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"You disagree about visiting frequency","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"You're isolated from your own friends and family","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"You're adjusting to new household customs","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"You're threatened if you don't comply","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"You're learning who's who in a big family","trick":false}],"why":"Isolation and threats are coercive control: abuse that needs help; the rest are ordinary friction.","relearn":"Isolation and threats are abuse: reach for help; disagreeing, adjusting and learning are friction.","persona":"Reshma","source":"spot the line crossed"},
  {"id":"fm-1260","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Marry the right person and the family part takes care of itself.\"","myth":{"un":"Marry the right person and the family part takes care of itself.","re":"You join their whole family too; those ties take real, ongoing effort, not luck.","why":"A good partner doesn't replace the work of building family relationships."},"relearn":"A loving partner is the start, not a shortcut past learning the wider family.","persona":"Pooja","source":"effort not luck"},
  {"id":"fm-1261","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Once you marry in, your own family stops being your family.\"","myth":{"un":"Once you marry in, your own family stops being your family.","re":"You gain a family without losing yours; both can stay part of your life.","why":"Joining a new web doesn't erase the one you came from."},"relearn":"Marriage adds a family to your web; it doesn't delete the one you grew up in.","persona":"Sneha","source":"keep both"},
  {"id":"fm-1262","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A daughter-in-law should know the family's ways from day one.\"","myth":{"un":"A daughter-in-law should know the family's ways from day one.","re":"Nobody arrives knowing a new family's rhythms; learning them takes time, and asking is fine.","why":"Customs are learned by living them, not absorbed instantly on the wedding day."},"relearn":"Learning a new family's ways takes time; not knowing on day one is normal, not a failing.","persona":"Pooja","source":"learning takes time"},
  {"id":"fm-1263","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A son-in-law is just a guest; he doesn't really join her family.\"","myth":{"un":"A son-in-law is just a guest; he doesn't really join her family.","re":"He joins her family too, building real ties there, not just visiting as a guest.","why":"Joining a family goes both ways; the husband joins her side as well."},"relearn":"A husband joins his wife's family for real; he's not a permanent guest there.","persona":"Karthik","source":"he joins too"},
  {"id":"fm-1264","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If you have to work at liking your in-laws, something's wrong.\"","myth":{"un":"If you have to work at liking your in-laws, something's wrong.","re":"Most family bonds are built, not instant; effort early on is normal, not a bad sign.","why":"Relationships grow through time and small gestures, not on first sight."},"relearn":"Warmth with in-laws is usually built over time; needing effort isn't a red flag.","persona":"any","source":"built not instant"},
  {"id":"fm-1265","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Your in-laws are just roles, like the saas and the nanad, not people.\"","myth":{"un":"Your in-laws are just roles, like the saas and the nanad, not people.","re":"Each in-law is an individual with their own story; the roles are only a starting label.","why":"Real relationships form with people, not with the titles they hold."},"relearn":"In-laws are individuals to get to know, not just roles to impress or fear.","persona":"any","source":"people not roles"},
  {"id":"fm-1266","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A close family means everyone always thinks the same way.\"","myth":{"un":"A close family means everyone always thinks the same way.","re":"Closeness holds plenty of differences; people can disagree and still belong together.","why":"Belonging isn't sameness; healthy families make room for different views."},"relearn":"You can join a close family and still hold your own views: closeness isn't sameness.","persona":"any","source":"closeness not sameness"},
  {"id":"fm-1267","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Both families have to drop their own customs and follow just one.\"","myth":{"un":"Both families have to drop their own customs and follow just one.","re":"Two families can keep their own ways and still celebrate together with respect.","why":"Different customs can sit side by side; one side needn't erase the other."},"relearn":"Different family customs are things to honour side by side, not to rank or replace.","persona":"any","source":"side by side"},
  {"id":"fm-1268","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Settling in means becoming exactly like your new family.\"","myth":{"un":"Settling in means becoming exactly like your new family.","re":"You take your fair place as yourself; fitting in isn't erasing who you are.","why":"A family web has room for your own personality, not just a copy of theirs."},"relearn":"Finding your place means taking your fair space as yourself, not dissolving into the family.","persona":"Pooja","source":"yourself in the web"},
  {"id":"fm-1269","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"In a joint family, the couple has no separate life of its own.\"","myth":{"un":"In a joint family, the couple has no separate life of its own.","re":"You can live within a joint family and still have a shared life that's yours as a couple.","why":"Belonging to the bigger household doesn't cancel the couple's own bond and choices."},"relearn":"Even inside a joint family, the couple gets a life and a few choices that are its own.","persona":"Karthik","source":"couple within the joint"},
  {"id":"fm-1270","cat":"web-you-join","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Your spouse's relatives are your problem to win over alone.\"","myth":{"un":"Your spouse's relatives are your problem to win over alone.","re":"You build those ties together; your partner helps you map and meet their family.","why":"Joining a family is a shared project, not a solo audition for approval."},"relearn":"You meet a new family together; winning everyone over isn't a solo job.","persona":"Pooja","source":"together not solo"},
  {"id":"fm-1271","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: in your everyday web, or a wider circle you'll meet rarely?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"In-laws you share a home or city with"},{"id":"b","text":"A distant relative abroad you've never met"},{"id":"c","text":"Your partner's sibling who visits often"},{"id":"d","text":"A great-uncle seen once a decade"},{"id":"e","text":"The cousin who lives next door"},{"id":"f","text":"A relative you meet only at big weddings"}],"bins":[{"id":"every","label":"Everyday web","valence":"pos"},{"id":"wider","label":"Wider, rarer circle","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"every","b":"wider","c":"every","d":"wider","e":"every","f":"wider"},"relearn":"Some in-laws are your daily web; others a wider circle you meet now and then. Both are real, with different effort.","persona":"any","source":"daily vs distant"},
  {"id":"fm-1272","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a fair way to learn a new family, or an unfair burden to put on yourself?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Asking your partner who's who"},{"id":"b","text":"Pretending you already know every name"},{"id":"c","text":"Learning one custom at a time"},{"id":"d","text":"Memorising every ritual before the wedding"},{"id":"e","text":"Letting some closeness grow slowly"},{"id":"f","text":"Being everyone's favourite by week one"}],"bins":[{"id":"fair","label":"Fair on yourself","valence":"pos"},{"id":"unfair","label":"Unfair burden","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"fair","b":"unfair","c":"fair","d":"unfair","e":"fair","f":"unfair"},"relearn":"Asking, learning slowly and letting closeness grow are fair; pretending to know it all or winning everyone instantly is an unfair load.","persona":"Pooja","source":"fair learning"},
  {"id":"fm-1273","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a sign you're building real ties, or just performing the 'perfect' new spouse?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Asking a relative about their life"},{"id":"b","text":"Saying yes to everything to be liked"},{"id":"c","text":"Remembering what matters to them"},{"id":"d","text":"Hiding your real opinions to seem ideal"},{"id":"e","text":"Sharing a little of yourself too"},{"id":"f","text":"Exhausting yourself to win approval"}],"bins":[{"id":"real","label":"Building real ties","valence":"pos"},{"id":"perform","label":"Just performing","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"real","b":"perform","c":"real","d":"perform","e":"real","f":"perform"},"relearn":"Curiosity and sharing yourself build real bonds; saying yes to everything and hiding your views is just performing.","persona":"any","source":"real vs perform"},
  {"id":"fm-1274","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: both partners joining each other's families, or the old one-way idea?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"He learns her parents' ways too"},{"id":"b","text":"Only she has to fit in"},{"id":"c","text":"He stays in touch with her siblings"},{"id":"d","text":"Her family is 'not really his'"},{"id":"e","text":"They visit both homes warmly"},{"id":"f","text":"Adjusting is the wife's job alone"}],"bins":[{"id":"both","label":"Both ways","valence":"pos"},{"id":"oneway","label":"Old one-way idea","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"both","b":"oneway","c":"both","d":"oneway","e":"both","f":"oneway"},"relearn":"Both partners join each other's families; the wife-only 'adjusting' idea is the outdated one.","persona":"Karthik","source":"both join"},
  {"id":"fm-1275","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a healthy way two families relate, or a sign someone's being erased?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Each family keeps its own festivals"},{"id":"b","text":"One side's customs are called 'wrong'"},{"id":"c","text":"They blend a few traditions by choice"},{"id":"d","text":"Your family's ways are mocked"},{"id":"e","text":"Both sides are spoken of with respect"},{"id":"f","text":"You're told to forget where you came from"}],"bins":[{"id":"healthy","label":"Healthy blend","valence":"pos"},{"id":"erased","label":"Someone erased","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"healthy","b":"erased","c":"healthy","d":"erased","e":"healthy","f":"erased"},"relearn":"Two families can stay distinct and respectful; mocking or erasing one side isn't 'becoming close'.","persona":"any","source":"blend not erase"},
  {"id":"fm-1276","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a tie that's genuinely yours now, or someone outside your family web?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Your partner's parents"},{"id":"b","text":"Your partner's old college friend"},{"id":"c","text":"Your sister-in-law"},{"id":"d","text":"A coworker of your spouse"},{"id":"e","text":"Your partner's grandmother"},{"id":"f","text":"A face from your partner's feed"}],"bins":[{"id":"yours","label":"Family now","valence":"pos"},{"id":"outside","label":"Outside the web","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"yours","b":"outside","c":"yours","d":"outside","e":"yours","f":"outside"},"relearn":"Your partner's close relatives become your family; their friends, coworkers and online faces don't.","persona":"any","source":"family vs friends"},
  {"id":"fm-1277","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a kind first move toward a new in-law, or a way to keep them at arm's length?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Asking about their day"},{"id":"b","text":"Only ever speaking when spoken to"},{"id":"c","text":"Offering to help in the kitchen"},{"id":"d","text":"Staying glued to your phone at visits"},{"id":"e","text":"Remembering a small thing they like"},{"id":"f","text":"Treating every visit as a chore"}],"bins":[{"id":"warm","label":"Warm first move","valence":"pos"},{"id":"distant","label":"Arm's length","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"warm","b":"distant","c":"warm","d":"distant","e":"warm","f":"distant"},"relearn":"Small, genuine gestures open a new tie; silence and distance keep it cold.","persona":"any","source":"warm openers"},
  {"id":"fm-1278","cat":"web-you-join","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a realistic timeline for a new family bond, or an instant-result fantasy?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Feeling at ease after many visits"},{"id":"b","text":"Being 'one of them' within a week"},{"id":"c","text":"Inside jokes forming over a year"},{"id":"d","text":"Total trust on the wedding night"},{"id":"e","text":"Trust deepening with shared moments"},{"id":"f","text":"Everyone confiding in you at once"}],"bins":[{"id":"realtime","label":"Real timeline","valence":"pos"},{"id":"instant","label":"Instant fantasy","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"realtime","b":"instant","c":"realtime","d":"instant","e":"realtime","f":"instant"},"relearn":"Ease, inside jokes and trust grow over months and years; instant belonging is a fantasy.","persona":"any","source":"timeline"},
  {"id":"fm-1279","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"first function","hook":"Your first big family function is starting, and you don't recognise half the room.","steps":[{"prompt":"An uncle wraps you in a hug like he's known you for years. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Quietly ask your partner who he is before you reply","then":"Your partner leans in. \"My father's cousin. He's very fond of you already.\"","best":true},{"text":"Hug back warmly and hope it becomes clear later","then":"The hug feels nice, but you're no closer to knowing who he is."},{"text":"Stay near the food table until you feel more ready","then":"You stay comfortable, but the room stays a blur of strangers."},{"text":"Introduce yourself to a few people nearby, hoping it sticks","then":"A few people smile, but half the names blur together anyway."}],"why":"A quiet check with your partner turns a warm stranger into someone you actually know."},{"prompt":"Later, a group photo is called and people are placed by how they're related, not by name. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask your partner to point out who's who as you line up","then":"He quickly names three key people before the photographer calls positions.","best":true},{"text":"Just follow wherever you're pointed to stand","then":"You end up in the frame, still unsure who's beside you."},{"text":"Guess at the seating based on how people are dressed","then":"You guess wrong, and someone gently redirects you."},{"text":"Stand at the back to avoid the mix-up entirely","then":"You avoid the confusion, but miss a chance to place a few more faces."}],"why":"Asking your partner to place people in real time builds the map as it happens."},{"prompt":"That evening, a cousin teases that you still don't know half the family. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Laugh and say you're learning the map one face at a time","then":"The cousin laughs too and starts naming people for you, unprompted.","best":true},{"text":"Say you're trying your best, feeling a bit exposed","then":"The cousin backs off, but the moment leaves you a little rattled."},{"text":"Pretend you actually know everyone already","then":"The cousin tests you with a name, and the pretending falls apart fast."},{"text":"Say you'll never keep track of such a big family","then":"It comes out sounding like a complaint, and a few people overhear it."}],"why":"Owning that you're still learning, lightly, invites help instead of judgement."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, another function comes up. What's your approach this time?","options":[{"text":"Ask your partner for a quick who's-who before you go in","then":"It takes two minutes in the car, and you walk in far more sure of yourself.","best":true},{"text":"Assume you'll recognise enough people once you're there","then":"A few faces blur together, and you're back to guessing by evening."},{"text":"Skip asking, planning to just hang back near your partner","then":"You manage by staying close, though you still learn few names directly."},{"text":"Ask a different relative for a quick briefing instead","then":"They give you a shorter, patchier version than your partner would have."}],"why":"Making the who's-who a habit, before each function, is what actually builds the map."},{"prompt":"At the next wedding, your partner offers a quick who's-who on the way in. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say yes, and ask to make it a routine","then":"He grins. \"Anytime. You're basically family HR now.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you probably don't need it, though you're not sure","then":"You freeze on two names within the first hour."},{"text":"Say you feel bad relying on him for this","then":"He reassures you, though the guesswork lingers a little."},{"text":"Say you'd rather just wing it from now on","then":"You wing it, and spend more of the wedding guessing than enjoying it."}],"why":"Making it a routine, without guilt, is what makes leaning on your partner sustainable."}],"debrief":"A quiet who's-who from your partner turns a sea of strangers into a map you can read.","relearn":"Lean on your partner to learn the family web; you don't have to figure it out alone."},
  {"id":"fm-1280","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"join her side","hook":"Your wife's parents invite you to help with a ritual you don't fully know.","steps":[{"prompt":"Her mother hands you a tray and asks you to join in. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Join in and ask what each step means as you go","then":"You fumble the first step, but she warms to your effort.","best":true},{"text":"Hang back, calling it 'her family's thing'","then":"You watch from the side, and the moment passes you by."},{"text":"Take the tray and copy others without asking","then":"You get through it, though half the meaning is lost on you."},{"text":"Ask your wife to do your part for you","then":"She does it smoothly, but the ritual stays entirely hers, not yours too."}],"why":"Asking what each step means turns a borrowed ritual into one you've actually joined."},{"prompt":"Afterward, her father asks if you found it strange. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it was new, and you're glad to have been there for it","then":"He smiles, pleased. \"Then you'll come for the next one too.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say it was fine, keeping the answer short","then":"He nods, unsure if it actually meant anything to you."},{"text":"Say it reminded you of a similar custom in your family","then":"The talk drifts to your side, and his question goes unanswered."},{"text":"Joke that you were just following instructions","then":"He laughs along, though the moment loses some of its weight."}],"why":"Naming it as meaningful, not just survived, shows you're really joining her family."},{"prompt":"Your wife later asks if you'd like to be part of these rituals going forward. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you want to keep learning their ways, not just this once","then":"She squeezes your hand. \"That means more than you know.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say maybe, depending on how busy things get","then":"She's not certain how much to count on you next time."},{"text":"Say you did it mainly for her sake","then":"She appreciates it, but wishes you'd wanted it for yourself too."},{"text":"Say you'll join if her parents insist","then":"She frowns a little. \"I don't want it to feel like an order.\""}],"why":"Wanting to keep learning shows this wasn't a one-off performance for her."},{"prompt":"Months later, her family plans another ritual you don't know well. What's your approach?","options":[{"text":"Offer to help and ask questions as you go","then":"Her mother hands you the tray without a second thought this time.","best":true},{"text":"Wait to be asked instead of offering","then":"You're included eventually, but a beat later than everyone else."},{"text":"Assume you already know enough from last time","then":"You miss a step, and this ritual works differently from the last."},{"text":"Let your wife lead this one, staying in the background","then":"You're there, but the ritual still feels like hers alone, not yours too."}],"why":"Offering again, and still asking, is how a borrowed ritual becomes a shared one."}],"debrief":"A husband joins his wife's family too; leaning in is how those ties become real.","relearn":"Both partners join each other's families; showing up at her family's rituals builds your place."},
  {"id":"fm-1281","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"different cooking","hook":"Your mother-in-law cooks differently from your mother, and you keep comparing.","steps":[{"prompt":"She serves a dal that tastes nothing like the one you grew up on. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask her to teach you how she makes it","then":"She beams and pulls out her spice box right away.","best":true},{"text":"Eat it politely while silently missing your mother's version","then":"You finish the meal, quietly comparing the whole time."},{"text":"Mention that your mother makes it a different way","then":"She goes a little quiet, taking the comparison as a criticism."},{"text":"Add your own spices to it without saying anything","then":"She notices the change and wonders if you didn't like her cooking."}],"why":"Asking her to teach you turns a comparison into a shared moment instead."},{"prompt":"The next week, she offers to teach you one of her family's recipes. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say yes, and ask about the story behind the dish","then":"She tells you it was her mother's recipe, and her eyes light up.","best":true},{"text":"Say yes, mostly to be polite","then":"You learn the recipe, though the moment stays a little flat."},{"text":"Say you'll stick to your own family's recipes for now","then":"She nods, and the offer doesn't come around again for a while."},{"text":"Say yes, then quietly tweak it to taste like home","then":"You end up with a dish that's neither quite hers nor quite yours."}],"why":"Asking about the story behind a dish turns cooking into a real connection."},{"prompt":"At dinner, your husband asks how you're finding his mother's cooking lately. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'd like to keep learning from her, whatever's happened so far","then":"He looks pleased, and mentions it warmly to his mother later.","best":true},{"text":"Say the food is fine, keeping it neutral","then":"He can't quite tell if it's going well or not."},{"text":"Say you still prefer how your own mother cooks","then":"He goes quiet, unsure how to pass that along kindly."},{"text":"Say you're doing it mostly to keep the peace","then":"He looks a little disappointed it isn't more than that to you."}],"why":"Wanting to keep learning, whatever's happened, is what keeps the connection open."},{"prompt":"Months later, your own mother asks to swap a family recipe with your mother-in-law. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Set it up warmly, glad to bring both kitchens together","then":"The two of them trade notes for an hour on the phone.","best":true},{"text":"Pass the message along but stay out of it yourself","then":"The exchange happens, though it feels a little distant to you."},{"text":"Say it's probably better to keep the kitchens separate","then":"The offer quietly drops, and a nice moment slips away."},{"text":"Worry the two might end up comparing notes on you instead","then":"You hesitate so long that your mother stops asking about it."}],"why":"Bringing both families' kitchens together is what learning to cook her way was building toward."}],"debrief":"Different isn't wrong; asking to learn a new family's ways beats ranking them against your own.","relearn":"Different family customs are things to learn, not proof one side is better."},
  {"id":"fm-1282","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"not instant favourite","hook":"You feel pressure to be everyone's instant favourite in your new joint family.","steps":[{"prompt":"Three weeks in, you're running errands for everyone and feel worn out. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let closeness grow at its own pace, one relationship at a time","then":"You stop rushing, and a few real conversations start to happen.","best":true},{"text":"Keep bending over backwards for everyone at once","then":"You're exhausted by week's end, and nothing feels closer."},{"text":"Focus all your energy on winning over the one who seems toughest","then":"You make progress there, while everyone else feels a little ignored."},{"text":"Pull back from everyone until you feel less overwhelmed","then":"The pressure eases, but so does any chance to actually connect."}],"why":"Pacing yourself with one relationship at a time is sustainable; chasing everyone at once isn't."},{"prompt":"A cousin seems unimpressed by you no matter what you do. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let that one relationship take its own time, without forcing it","then":"You stop watching for her approval, and the tension quietly eases.","best":true},{"text":"Try even harder to win her over specifically","then":"The more you try, the more distant she seems to get."},{"text":"Ask other relatives why she doesn't seem to like you","then":"The question spreads, and it reaches her before you meant it to."},{"text":"Decide she'll just never warm up and stop trying entirely","then":"You give up on it fully, and any small chance closes with it."}],"why":"Letting one slow relationship be slow protects your energy for the ones that are opening up."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks how things are going with the family lately. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'd like to stop rushing and let bonds grow slowly","then":"He nods. \"That sounds a lot less exhausting.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you're just too tired to try as hard anymore","then":"He looks a little concerned, unsure if that's really the reason."},{"text":"Say you've basically given up on a few of them","then":"He looks surprised, having assumed things were improving."},{"text":"Say nothing's really changed and brush the question off","then":"He drops it, though he can tell something's on your mind."}],"why":"Choosing pace over pressure, out loud, gives you permission to stop chasing everyone."},{"prompt":"Six months in, a relative comments you've grown close to some but not others. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say those bonds are just taking their own time, and that's fine","then":"The relative nods, and the comment doesn't sting the way it might have.","best":true},{"text":"Say you're worried you're failing with the ones who feel distant","then":"The relative reassures you, though the worry sticks with you anyway."},{"text":"Say you'll try harder with the ones you're not close to yet","then":"You go back to chasing approval, right where you started."},{"text":"Say some people are simply harder to connect with","then":"It comes out sounding like blame, which wasn't quite what you meant."}],"why":"Trusting the slower bonds to grow in their own time is the whole point of not chasing everyone at once."}],"debrief":"Joining a family is built over time; you can't, and needn't, win everyone at once.","relearn":"Family bonds grow at their own pace; instant universal approval isn't the goal."},
  {"id":"fm-1283","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"beyond the role","hook":"A relative keeps calling you only 'the new bahu', never your name.","steps":[{"prompt":"At a family lunch, she introduces you that way to a guest again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Introduce yourself by name and share a bit about yourself","then":"You mention your name and your work, and the guest asks a real follow-up question.","best":true},{"text":"Correct her by giving just your name and nothing else","then":"The guest gets your name, but the conversation ends there anyway."},{"text":"Let it go and stay quiet through the introduction","then":"The guest walks away knowing only the label, not you."},{"text":"Joke about it to lighten the moment","then":"A few people laugh, but the label sticks around after the laugh fades."}],"why":"Sharing something real about yourself is what turns a label into an actual person."},{"prompt":"Later, the same relative asks what you actually do for work. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell her warmly, and ask a bit about her own life too","then":"She lights up talking about her own younger days, surprised to be asked.","best":true},{"text":"Give a short, flat answer and change the subject","then":"The question fizzles, and the conversation doesn't go further."},{"text":"Downplay your work so you don't seem to be showing off","then":"She nods, though she still doesn't really know what you do."},{"text":"Answer, but steer straight back to family talk","then":"The moment to be known as yourself slips by again."}],"why":"Answering and asking back turns a one-sided introduction into a real exchange."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, she starts using your name sometimes, though not always. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Share something small about yourself when it comes up naturally","then":"Slowly, 'the new bahu' starts giving way to your actual name.","best":true},{"text":"Point out that she still forgets your name sometimes","then":"She apologises, a little embarrassed, and the moment feels tense."},{"text":"Decide the change is good enough and stop making an effort","then":"Her progress slows, and the label still slips out more often than your name."},{"text":"Wait for her to use your name consistently before relaxing","then":"You stay a little guarded, watching and waiting instead of connecting."}],"why":"Staying warm and consistent is what lets a slow change actually take hold."},{"prompt":"At the next big function, she introduces you to a guest by your name. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Notice it warmly and let the moment be simple","then":"The introduction flows naturally, like it always should have.","best":true},{"text":"Make a point of thanking her for finally using your name","then":"It comes out sounding pointed, and the warmth of the moment fades."},{"text":"Wonder if she'll slip back into the old habit later","then":"You spend the rest of the function half-watching for a slip."},{"text":"Say nothing and quietly hope no one else noticed it took this long","then":"The relief passes without you ever really registering the shift."}],"why":"Letting the change land simply is the real sign the label has become a name."}],"debrief":"You're an individual joining a family, not just a role; let them know the real you.","relearn":"In-laws meet you as a role at first; sharing yourself turns the label into a relationship."},
  {"id":"fm-1284","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"two festivals","hook":"Your partner's family does festivals very differently from yours.","steps":[{"prompt":"Their big festival lands the same week as one from your side. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Plan to honour both, even if only in small ways","then":"You manage a bit of each, and both families notice the effort.","best":true},{"text":"Pick whichever family is hosting the bigger event","then":"One family feels celebrated, and the other quietly feels skipped."},{"text":"Skip both to avoid choosing between them","then":"Neither family feels celebrated, and both wonder why you were absent."},{"text":"Insist your own family's festival is the one that matters more","then":"The comment lands badly, and it takes a while to smooth over."}],"why":"Honouring both, even briefly, keeps neither family feeling chosen over the other."},{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law asks if you'll join their full festival next year. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'd love to, and ask what matters most to include","then":"She walks you through the parts she loves best, pleased you asked.","best":true},{"text":"Say yes without asking anything more about it","then":"You show up, but miss the parts that actually matter to her."},{"text":"Say maybe, keeping your options open for now","then":"She isn't sure whether to count on you, and doesn't ask again."},{"text":"Say you'll come only if your own festival isn't also that week","then":"She hears it as a condition, and the warmth of the offer cools."}],"why":"Asking what matters most turns showing up into something that actually means something."},{"prompt":"A year on, both families' festivals fall in the same season again. What's your approach?","options":[{"text":"Start a small tradition of your own that blends a bit of both","then":"Both families end up curious, and a couple of them even join in.","best":true},{"text":"Pick one family over the other this year","then":"One family ends up feeling chosen, and the other quietly loses out."},{"text":"Let your partner decide which to prioritise this time","then":"The decision gets made, but it still feels like a choice between them."},{"text":"Avoid planning ahead and decide at the last minute","then":"You end up rushing between the two, doing neither justice."}],"why":"A blended tradition of your own is what actually lets two families sit side by side."}],"debrief":"Two families can keep their own ways and still celebrate together; blending beats ranking.","relearn":"Different customs can sit side by side; you can honour both families' ways."},
  {"id":"fm-1285","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"take your space","hook":"In the joint family you've stopped speaking up at all to keep peace.","steps":[{"prompt":"At dinner, the talk turns to a topic you actually have an opinion on. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Gently share your view, kindly but clearly","then":"The table pauses. Someone asks for your view again at the next dinner.","best":true},{"text":"Keep quiet like you usually do","then":"The moment passes, and no one learns what you actually think."},{"text":"Say your opinion, but immediately soften it into a joke","then":"People laugh, and your actual view gets lost in the laugh."},{"text":"Wait until later to mention it to your partner privately","then":"Your partner hears it, but the family at the table never does."}],"why":"Speaking up kindly, in the moment, is how you start taking your fair space."},{"prompt":"A few days later, a relative asks you directly what you think about a similar topic. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Share your view plainly, now that you're asked directly","then":"The relative listens properly this time, and takes it in.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'd rather not get into it","then":"The relative shrugs, and the chance to be heard passes again."},{"text":"Answer, but downplay it so it seems unimportant","then":"The point gets across, but doesn't really land as something you meant."},{"text":"Say your partner already knows what you think","then":"The relative is left without hearing it from you directly."}],"why":"Answering plainly when asked directly is how you start actually taking up space."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks how you've been feeling about speaking up at family gatherings. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you're trying not to shrink yourself just to keep the peace","then":"He nods. \"I want you to feel like you can say what you think.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you're not sure it's making any real difference","then":"He reassures you, though you're still not fully convinced."},{"text":"Say you might be pushing too hard lately","then":"He tells you gently that isn't what he's noticed at all."},{"text":"Brush the question off and change the subject","then":"The moment to talk about it passes without being named."}],"why":"Saying out loud that you won't shrink yourself makes it a choice you can keep making."},{"prompt":"At the next big family decision, you feel the old pull to stay quiet. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Speak up with your view before the decision is made","then":"There's a small pause. Then the family actually factors it in.","best":true},{"text":"Stay quiet, assuming it won't change anything anyway","then":"The decision goes ahead exactly as everyone expected it to."},{"text":"Voice your view only after the decision is already final","then":"Everyone listens politely, but the moment to matter has passed."},{"text":"Ask your partner to raise it on your behalf instead","then":"He raises it, but the family still isn't hearing it from you."}],"why":"Speaking up before the decision lands is what taking your fair space actually looks like."}],"debrief":"Finding your place means taking your fair space, not disappearing to avoid all friction.","relearn":"Finding your place means taking up your fair space, not shrinking yourself to fit."},
  {"id":"fm-1286","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"know them","hook":"You've spent months being the perfect new family member, and you barely know anyone's story.","steps":[{"prompt":"At a family function, your partner's grandmother mentions she once taught in a village school. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Get curious and ask her more about it","then":"She lights up and tells you about her very first classroom.","best":true},{"text":"Nod politely and let the comment pass","then":"The function goes on pleasantly, and you learn nothing further."},{"text":"Mention your own school days instead","then":"The talk shifts to you, and her story is quickly forgotten."},{"text":"Keep making sure everyone sees the perfect new member","then":"She smiles warmly at you, but you still know little about her."}],"why":"Real curiosity about her life is what starts an actual conversation."},{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law later asks what you think of her cooking, seeming a little nervous. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you love it, and ask how she learned to cook","then":"She relaxes at the compliment, then tells you a story about her own mother.","best":true},{"text":"Give a quick compliment and move on to something else","then":"She smiles, but the moment stays surface level."},{"text":"Offer detailed feedback on what could be improved next time","then":"She goes quiet, taking it more personally than you meant."},{"text":"Say very little, unsure what she wants to hear from you","then":"The silence makes her more nervous, not less."}],"why":"Asking about her story, not just her cooking, is what turns her into someone you know."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks what has surprised you most about the family so far. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you've been impressing them, not getting to know them","then":"Your partner's face softens. \"Let's make time to really know them.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say they're exactly how you imagined them to be","then":"Your partner smiles, and wonders if you've had time to look closer."},{"text":"Say you've learned what everyone likes, to keep them happy","then":"Your partner nods. \"That sounds like a lot of work for you.\""},{"text":"Say it's too early to have an opinion about anyone yet","then":"The talk moves on, and what you've noticed stays unsaid."}],"why":"Naming that you missed who they are is where real closeness starts."}],"debrief":"Closeness grows from curiosity about their lives, not from performing the perfect new family member.","relearn":"Ask in-laws about their own stories; knowing them matters more than impressing them."},
  {"id":"fm-1287","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"reserved elder","hook":"Your spouse's grandmother seems distant; you wonder if she dislikes you.","steps":[{"prompt":"At a family visit, she barely says more than a greeting to you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep showing up with small, patient warmth","then":"She doesn't say much, but she watches you a little longer this time.","best":true},{"text":"Push for a longer conversation than she seems to want","then":"She grows more clipped, uncomfortable with the sudden effort."},{"text":"Decide her reserve means she dislikes you","then":"You start avoiding her, and the distance only grows."},{"text":"Ask your partner to find out why she seems so cold","then":"Your partner asks, and she seems embarrassed to be discussed at all."}],"why":"Small, patient warmth lets a reserved elder open up without feeling pushed."},{"prompt":"A few visits later, she offers you a plate without being asked. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Accept warmly and thank her simply, without making a fuss","then":"She gives the smallest nod, pleased but not wanting attention on it.","best":true},{"text":"Make a big deal of the gesture in front of everyone","then":"She looks uncomfortable, and shrinks back a little from the attention."},{"text":"Assume it doesn't mean much and move on quickly","then":"You miss noticing a small opening she offered you."},{"text":"Ask her directly why she's suddenly being kinder","then":"The question catches her off guard, and she goes quiet again."}],"why":"Accepting simply, without fuss, respects how she prefers to show warmth."},{"prompt":"Months on, your partner mentions she asked about you when you weren't there. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Feel glad, and keep visiting her without expecting more too soon","then":"Over time, she starts greeting you first, in her own quiet way.","best":true},{"text":"Take it as proof you should visit her more often now","then":"The sudden increase in visits makes her a little more guarded again."},{"text":"Assume the distance is fully gone and stop making an effort","then":"Without the patience, the bond quietly stalls where it was."},{"text":"Mention it to her directly to see how she reacts","then":"She brushes it off, uneasy that it was repeated back to her."}],"why":"Staying patient, even as things improve, is what lets a slow bond keep growing."}],"debrief":"Some bonds open slowly; patient, low-pressure warmth lets a reserved elder come around.","relearn":"New ties start best with patience and small gestures, not forced or instant closeness."},
  {"id":"fm-1288","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Pooja","source":"new household","hook":"You moved cities to your partner's family home and feel like an outsider.","steps":[{"prompt":"On your first morning there, you're unsure when the household even starts its day. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask your partner to walk you through the household's rhythms","then":"He explains the morning routine, and you slot into it far more easily.","best":true},{"text":"Wait silently for someone to guide you","then":"You end up guessing, and the morning feels more awkward than it needed to."},{"text":"Copy whatever your mother-in-law happens to do first","then":"You follow along, though you're still not sure why things happen this way."},{"text":"Keep to your own old routine from before the move","then":"You feel comfortable, but a little out of step with the household."}],"why":"Learning the household's rhythms with your partner helps you find your footing faster."},{"prompt":"A few days in, you're unsure who handles which chores in the house. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask your partner how tasks are usually split","then":"He walks you through it, and you find a spot that fits you too.","best":true},{"text":"Pick tasks at random and hope it's fine","then":"You end up doing some things twice and missing others entirely."},{"text":"Wait to be told exactly what to do","then":"Days pass with you standing a little on the edges of the household."},{"text":"Take over as much as you can to prove you belong","then":"You're exhausted fast, and it doesn't actually make you feel more at home."}],"why":"Understanding how the household already works is what lets you find your own place in it."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks how the new house is feeling after two weeks. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell him honestly what still feels hard, and ask for help","then":"He listens, and offers to show you more of the household's rhythms this weekend.","best":true},{"text":"Say it still feels like someone else's house","then":"He listens, though the conversation doesn't lead anywhere yet."},{"text":"Say you're managing, keeping the real answer to yourself","then":"He takes you at your word, not realising how you actually feel."},{"text":"Say you're not sure you'll ever feel settled here","then":"The comment lands heavier than you meant it to."}],"why":"Naming what's still hard, and asking, lets your partner actually help you settle in."},{"prompt":"A month later, a relative visiting comments you seem right at home already. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you're getting there, bit by bit","then":"The relative smiles, glad to hear it's genuinely going well.","best":true},{"text":"Agree quickly, even though it's not fully true yet","then":"You nod along, though the words don't quite match how you feel."},{"text":"Tell the relative everything that's been hard about the move","then":"The relative listens politely, and the whole list reaches your mother-in-law by evening."},{"text":"Deflect with a joke about still getting lost in the kitchen","then":"It gets a laugh, though the real answer stays unsaid."}],"why":"Naming the real, ongoing process is more honest than pretending you're already fully settled."}],"debrief":"Finding your footing in a new household is built with your partner, not waited for.","relearn":"Map the home's rhythms with your partner; that's how an outsider joins the web."},
  {"id":"fm-1289","cat":"web-you-join","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"your side too","hook":"Your partner barely knows your family: 'they married you, not them'.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your parents invite you both over, and your partner seems unsure why they should come along. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Invite them in warmly, saying your family is theirs too now","then":"Your partner comes along, and your parents light up at the visit.","best":true},{"text":"Go alone, since it's just your side of the family","then":"Your partner stays home, and your parents ask why they never came."},{"text":"Say your partner can join only if they're free","then":"It sounds optional, and your partner reasonably decides to skip it."},{"text":"Agree it doesn't matter and let your partner stay distant","then":"Your family stays half-known to your partner, visit after visit."}],"why":"Inviting your partner in as family, not a guest, is how your two worlds actually connect."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your mother mentions she'd love to get to know your partner better. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Encourage a proper visit soon and help set it up","then":"You plan a get-together, and your parents are excited to have real time with your partner.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll mention it to your partner eventually","then":"The 'eventually' keeps slipping, and no visit gets planned."},{"text":"Say your partner isn't really one for family visits","then":"Your mother quietly stops expecting to see much of them."},{"text":"Change the subject, unsure how to bring it up","then":"The chance to actually connect quietly passes by."}],"why":"Helping set up real time together is what turns knowing about your partner into knowing them."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, your partner mentions wanting to visit your parents. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you're glad, and plan the next visit together","then":"Your parents are surprised and pleased when your partner suggests the date.","best":true},{"text":"Be surprised, since you assumed they wouldn't want to","then":"The visit happens, though your surprise makes it feel a little odd."},{"text":"Let them go without you this time","then":"It goes fine, though you miss being part of the moment."},{"text":"Worry it means something's wrong and ask what prompted it","then":"Your partner reassures you, though the question dampens the warmth a little."}],"why":"Planning it together shows your partner's own wish to connect is genuinely welcome."}],"debrief":"Both partners join each other's families; your spouse joining your side matters too.","relearn":"Both partners join each other's families; bringing your spouse into yours is part of the web."},
  {"id":"fm-1290","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each new relative to a simple way to start knowing them.","pairs":[{"left":"A grandparent-in-law","right":"Ask them to share an old family story"},{"left":"A foodie aunt-in-law","right":"Ask her to teach you a family recipe"},{"left":"A cricket-loving uncle","right":"Watch a match together sometime"},{"left":"A shy teenage cousin","right":"Keep it light, no pressure to bond"},{"left":"A busy sister-in-law","right":"A short, warm message now and then"}],"relearn":"Each in-law opens up to a different small, genuine gesture suited to who they are.","persona":"any","source":"start with each"},
  {"id":"fm-1291","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each myth about joining a family to the truth that replaces it.","pairs":[{"left":"Only the wife adjusts","right":"Both partners join each other's families"},{"left":"You must love everyone at once","right":"Bonds are built slowly over time"},{"left":"Their way is just wrong","right":"Different customs are things to learn"},{"left":"Two families must become one","right":"They can stay distinct and still be close"},{"left":"In-laws are only roles","right":"They are individuals to get to know"}],"relearn":"Each tired myth about marrying into a family has a kinder, truer reality behind it.","persona":"any","source":"myth to truth"},
  {"id":"fm-1292","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each part of the wider web to roughly how often you'll really see them.","pairs":[{"left":"In-laws you live near","right":"Part of everyday life"},{"left":"A sibling-in-law in another city","right":"Calls and the big occasions"},{"left":"Festival-only relatives","right":"A few warm times a year"},{"left":"A cousin-in-law abroad","right":"Mostly weddings and video calls"},{"left":"A great-aunt you rarely meet","right":"The occasional respectful visit"}],"relearn":"The family web has near ties and far ones; knowing which is which sets fair expectations.","persona":"any","source":"near and far"},
  {"id":"fm-1293","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each warm habit to the new tie it helps build.","pairs":[{"left":"Remembering a birthday","right":"Shows a relative they matter to you"},{"left":"Asking about their health","right":"Turns a greeting into real care"},{"left":"Joining one family ritual","right":"Signals you want to belong"},{"left":"Sharing a bit of your own world","right":"Lets them know you back"},{"left":"A patient, unforced hello","right":"Opens a guarded relative gently"}],"relearn":"Small, repeated warmth, not grand gestures, is what slowly knits you into a family.","persona":"any","source":"warm habits"},
  {"id":"fm-1294","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each new term you'll hear to who it points to in the web.","pairs":[{"left":"Saas","right":"Your mother-in-law"},{"left":"Sasur","right":"Your father-in-law"},{"left":"Nanad","right":"Your husband's sister"},{"left":"Devar","right":"Your husband's younger brother"},{"left":"Jeth","right":"Your husband's elder brother"}],"relearn":"Learning who each family term points to is part of mapping the web you've joined.","persona":"Pooja","source":"who's who terms"},
  {"id":"fm-1295","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each realistic hope to why it's fair to expect.","pairs":[{"left":"Warmth growing over years","right":"Bonds are built, not instant"},{"left":"Mutual respect from the start","right":"Respect can be offered on day one"},{"left":"Learning customs slowly","right":"Nobody knows a new family at once"},{"left":"Keeping your own voice","right":"You take your fair space, not none"},{"left":"Honouring both families' ways","right":"Two sides can stay distinct and close"}],"relearn":"Fair hopes for a new family rest on truths: bonds are built, respect is mutual, you stay yourself.","persona":"any","source":"fair hopes why"},
  {"id":"fm-1296","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each thing to whose call it gently is as you join the family.","pairs":[{"left":"Whether you call an aunt 'didi'","right":"Yours, as you settle in"},{"left":"Learning a family festival","right":"Something you and your partner share"},{"left":"How fast you grow close to each in-law","right":"Set by the relationship's own pace"},{"left":"Which relatives are 'your web' now","right":"Your partner's close family, by marriage"},{"left":"Keeping ties to your own parents","right":"Always still yours to hold"}],"relearn":"Joining a family still leaves room for your own pace, your own ties and a few of your own calls.","persona":"any","source":"whose call"},
  {"id":"fm-1297","cat":"web-you-join","type":"match","hook":"Match each early in-law moment to a kind way to handle it.","pairs":[{"left":"You forget a relative's name","right":"Smile and ask again, warmly"},{"left":"A custom confuses you","right":"Quietly ask your partner later"},{"left":"An elder offers strong advice","right":"Thank them, decide together after"},{"left":"You feel shy at a big gathering","right":"Stay close to your partner at first"},{"left":"A relative tests you a little","right":"Stay warm; trust is built over time"}],"relearn":"Awkward early moments are normal; warmth, honesty and your partner's help carry you through.","persona":"any","source":"early moments"},
  {"id":"fm-1298","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: marrying someone means joining a whole web of relatives, and being joined to yours. Sit with that for a second.","prompt":"What comes up?","options":["A lot to take in","Kind of exciting","Makes sense"],"affirm":"You marry into a family, not just a person, and that web becomes part of your life either way.","relearn":"Marriage joins two families, not just two people; the web is real whichever way you feel about it.","persona":"any","source":"the whole web"},
  {"id":"fm-1299","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you don't have to love every new relative on day one. Warmth can grow slowly. How does that sit?","prompt":"Your honest reaction?","options":["Relieved","Still nervous","That's fair"],"affirm":"Family bonds are built over time; letting them grow slowly is normal, not a failing.","relearn":"Joining a family is building relationships over time, not instant, automatic love.","persona":"any","source":"grows slowly"},
  {"id":"fm-1300","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: both of you are joining a new family. It isn't a one-way, wife-only job. What do you think?","prompt":"How does that land?","options":["Long overdue","Agree","Hadn't framed it that way"],"affirm":"Both partners join each other's families; adjusting goes both ways, and that's healthy.","relearn":"Both partners join each other's families; adjusting isn't a one-way, wife-only job.","persona":"Karthik","source":"both of you"},
  {"id":"fm-1301","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: your new family's ways aren't 'wrong' just because they differ from yours. They're new things to learn. Agree?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Agree","Worth trying","Easier said than done"],"affirm":"Different family customs are things to learn, not proof that one side is wrong.","relearn":"Different family ways are things to learn, not proof that one side is wrong.","persona":"any","source":"new to learn"},
  {"id":"fm-1302","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can keep your own family ties and still fully join your partner's. You don't have to choose. Sound right?","prompt":"How do you feel?","options":["Reassuring","Yes, both","I needed to hear that"],"affirm":"You gain a family without losing your own; both can stay part of your web.","relearn":"Marriage adds a family to your web without deleting the one you grew up in.","persona":"Sneha","source":"keep both ties"},
  {"id":"fm-1303","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: two families can stay distinct, with their own ways, and still be genuinely close. Does that feel true to you?","prompt":"Your view?","options":["Feels true","Hope so","Refreshing"],"affirm":"Closeness doesn't need sameness; families can keep their own ways and still be close.","relearn":"Families can stay distinct and still be close; merging into sameness isn't the goal.","persona":"any","source":"distinct and close"},
  {"id":"fm-1304","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: each in-law is a person with a whole story, not just a title to manage. Worth getting curious about?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Definitely","Curious now","Hadn't thought of it"],"affirm":"In-laws are individuals to get to know, not just roles to impress.","relearn":"In-laws are individuals to get to know, not just roles to win over.","persona":"any","source":"people with stories"},
  {"id":"fm-1305","cat":"web-you-join","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you build your place in the new family with your partner. It's a shared project, not a solo audition. How's that?","prompt":"Your honest take?","options":["Less alone","Agree","Good to remember"],"affirm":"Joining a family is something you and your partner do together; you aren't auditioning alone.","relearn":"You meet a new family together; winning your place isn't a solo job.","persona":"Pooja","source":"shared project"},
  {"id":"fm-1306","cat":"web-you-join","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two unfair expectations someone might put on a new daughter-in-law.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Time to learn the family's ways","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Knowing every custom on day one","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A warm welcome as she settles in","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Being everyone's favourite immediately","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Space to ask who's who","trick":false}],"why":"Knowing every custom at once and being an instant favourite are unfair loads; time, welcome and space are fair.","relearn":"Learning a new family takes time; instant mastery and instant adoration are unfair to expect.","persona":"Pooja","source":"unfair on the bahu"},
  {"id":"fm-1307","cat":"web-you-join","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two myths about marrying into a family: the lines that aren't true.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"You join their wider family too","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"You marry a person, not a family","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Both partners join each other's families","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Only the wife ever has to adjust","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"The relatives become part of your web","trick":false}],"why":"'You marry a person, not a family' and 'only the wife adjusts' are the myths; you join a family, both ways.","relearn":"You join a family, not just a person, and both partners adjust. Those are the truths.","persona":"Karthik","source":"two myths"},
  {"id":"fm-1308","cat":"web-you-join","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that treat a different custom as 'wrong' instead of new.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Their festival has its own lovely rhythm","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Their way of doing it is just backward","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Worth asking what each step means","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Our family does it the only proper way","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"A new custom I can learn over time","trick":false}],"why":"Calling their way 'backward' or yours 'the only proper way' judges instead of learns; different isn't wrong.","relearn":"Different family customs are things to learn, not proof one side is wrong or backward.","persona":"any","source":"different not wrong spot"},
  {"id":"fm-1309","cat":"web-you-join","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that wrongly demand you erase yourself to fit the family.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Take your fair space in the home","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Forget where you came from now","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Share a little of yourself too","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Never voice your own opinion again","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Keep your own ties to your parents","trick":false}],"why":"'Forget where you came from' and 'never voice your opinion' demand erasure; finding your place isn't shrinking.","relearn":"Finding your place means taking your fair space, not erasing yourself to fit the family.","persona":"Pooja","source":"don't erase"},
  {"id":"fm-1310","cat":"web-you-join","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that mistake performing for actually building a bond.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Asking a relative about their life","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Saying yes to everything to be liked","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Remembering what matters to them","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Hiding all your real views to seem ideal","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Letting them know the real you","trick":false}],"why":"Saying yes to everything and hiding your views is performing; curiosity and honesty build the real tie.","relearn":"Real bonds come from curiosity and being yourself, not from performing the 'perfect' new spouse.","persona":"any","source":"perform vs build spot"},
  {"id":"fm-1311","cat":"web-you-join","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that wrongly expect instant closeness with new in-laws.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Warmth that grows over many visits","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Best friends with everyone by week one","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Trust deepening through shared moments","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Owed everyone's full confidence at once","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Patience with a reserved relative","trick":false}],"why":"'Best friends by week one' and 'owed full confidence at once' expect instant closeness; bonds are built slowly.","relearn":"Closeness with in-laws grows over time; instant friendship and instant trust aren't owed.","persona":"any","source":"instant closeness spot"},
  {"id":"fm-1312","cat":"web-you-join","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that reduce an in-law to a role instead of a person.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Asking your father-in-law about his work","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"She's just the saas, here to be managed","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Learning what your sister-in-law loves","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"He's only the sasur, nothing more to know","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Hearing a grandparent's old stories","trick":false}],"why":"Treating someone as 'just the saas' or 'only the sasur' flattens a person to a role; in-laws are individuals.","relearn":"In-laws are individuals to get to know, not just roles to manage or impress.","persona":"any","source":"role not person spot"},
  {"id":"fm-1313","cat":"web-you-join","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"both traditions","hook":"A new aunt-in-law is curious about where you come from.","setup":"She pours you tea and pulls her chair closer.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks: \"What do you all do differently at home?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We do it a little differently. I'd love to share, and learn yours too.\"","then":"She smiles. \"I'd like that. Tell me about your side first.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Oh, ours don't really matter now.\"","then":"She looks a little puzzled, unsure why you'd brush it off."},{"text":"\"Honestly, yours make more sense to me already.\"","then":"It comes out sounding like your own family's ways don't count."},{"text":"\"It's complicated. Let's not get into it.\"","then":"The conversation stalls, and the offer to share closes early."}],"why":"Offering to share and to learn keeps both families' ways alive in the room."},{"prompt":"A few days later, she asks: \"Could we maybe try a bit of your family's way sometime?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd love that. Let me show you how we usually do it.\"","then":"She claps her hands, delighted at the idea of trying something new.","best":true},{"text":"\"Only if it's not too much trouble for everyone.\"","then":"She hesitates, unsure if you actually want to or not."},{"text":"\"Maybe, if there's ever a good moment for it.\"","then":"She laughs. \"Then I'll make the moment myself.\""},{"text":"\"Sure, but it's honestly not a big deal either way.\"","then":"The offer lands as less meaningful than it actually is to you."}],"why":"Saying yes warmly turns curiosity into an actual shared tradition."},{"prompt":"At the next function, she tells you in front of everyone: \"We've added your family's custom this year.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you for including it. It means a lot to see it here.\"","then":"She squeezes your hand. \"Both our families belong in this house.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You didn't have to do that.\"","then":"She looks unsure if you're pleased or uncomfortable."},{"text":"\"It's not quite how we usually do it, but that's fine.\"","then":"The correction lands harder than you meant it to."},{"text":"\"That's nice, I suppose.\"","then":"The gesture passes without the warmth it deserved."}],"why":"Naming what it means to you honours the effort she made to include your family's way."}],"relearn":"Honouring both families' ways, sharing yours and learning theirs, builds the bond without erasing you."},
  {"id":"fm-1314","cat":"web-you-join","type":"role-play","persona":"Pooja","source":"forgot the name","hook":"You've forgotten a relative's name at a function and she's noticed.","setup":"She waits for you to greet her properly.","steps":[{"prompt":"The relative says: \"You don't remember me, do you?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sorry, I'm still learning names. Remind me?\"","then":"She laughs. \"Fair enough. It's a big family. I'm Meenal.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Uh... hi, you.\"","then":"She looks a little hurt, and the moment stays awkward."},{"text":"\"Of course I remember, hi!\"","then":"She tests you with a follow-up question, and the pretending shows."},{"text":"\"There are just too many people to keep track of.\"","then":"It comes across as a complaint rather than an honest slip."}],"why":"A warm, honest admission turns a slip into a friendly moment instead of an awkward one."},{"prompt":"She asks: \"So how do you know me, then?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Honestly, I don't yet. How are you connected?\"","then":"She grins and tells you the whole story, happy to be asked.","best":true},{"text":"\"I think we met at the wedding, maybe?\"","then":"She corrects you gently, and the guess feels a little awkward."},{"text":"\"I must have just forgotten. Sorry again.\"","then":"The apology repeats, but you still don't actually learn who she is."},{"text":"\"Does it really matter how we're connected?\"","then":"The question lands oddly, and she seems taken aback."}],"why":"Asking directly how she's connected finally closes the gap, instead of guessing again."},{"prompt":"Later, a cousin you do remember jokes: \"I hear you forgot someone's name today!\"","options":[{"text":"\"I did. I'm still working on knowing everyone.\"","then":"Your cousin laughs. \"Fair enough. At least you're trying.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Please don't bring that up again.\"","then":"The joke gets repeated anyway, now with a bit more edge to it."},{"text":"\"It's a big family. Everyone forgets names sometimes.\"","then":"It comes off a little defensive, more than you intended."},{"text":"\"I know. I felt terrible about it.\"","then":"The moment dwells on guilt instead of moving the conversation forward."}],"why":"Owning the slip, and showing you're trying to know everyone, turns it into a warm story."}],"relearn":"A warm, honest 'I'm still learning everyone' turns a slip into a friendly moment."},
  {"id":"fm-1315","cat":"web-you-join","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"bring him in","hook":"Your husband hasn't met your parents much, and you want to bring him into your family too.","setup":"You catch him before your parents arrive.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"I still feel a bit unsure around your parents.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Come meet them properly. They're your family too.\"","then":"He relaxes a little. \"I'd like that, actually.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You don't really need to know them.\"","then":"He looks a little relieved, and the distance stays exactly where it was."},{"text":"\"They'll warm up eventually. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"He nods, though nothing changes about how he approaches them."},{"text":"\"Just be yourself. It'll be fine.\"","then":"He tries, but still feels like a guest in the room."}],"why":"Naming them as his family too is what actually invites him in, not just reassures him."},{"prompt":"Your father asks him directly: \"So, how did the two of you meet?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Go on. Tell him yourself.\"","then":"Your husband answers, and your father actually leans in to listen.","best":true},{"text":"\"Let me answer that one for him.\"","then":"You answer instead, and your father learns little about him directly."},{"text":"\"He gets a bit shy. Go easy on him.\"","then":"The comment makes the moment more awkward than it needed to be."},{"text":"\"Don't worry. He'll open up eventually.\"","then":"The question passes without really being answered by him."}],"why":"Letting him answer directly is what actually starts building his own bond with your father."},{"prompt":"After the visit, he says: \"I actually enjoyed talking to your dad.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm glad. I want you two to have your own bond.\"","then":"He smiles. \"Me too. Let's do this again soon.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"That's a relief. I was worried it'd be awkward.\"","then":"He nods, though the comment focuses more on your worry than his connection."},{"text":"\"See, I told you they weren't so bad.\"","then":"It comes across as dismissive of how nervous he'd actually felt."},{"text":"\"Good, now you won't need me there every time.\"","then":"It lands a little cold, more like a task completed than a bond built."}],"why":"Naming that you want their bond to be its own, not just through you, is what makes it real."},{"prompt":"Months later, your husband says: \"I invited your parents over this weekend. Hope that's alright.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you. Let's make sure they feel really welcome.\"","then":"Your parents leave saying how at home he made them feel.","best":true},{"text":"\"Oh. You didn't have to do that without asking me.\"","then":"He looks caught off guard, unsure if he did something wrong."},{"text":"\"Let me double check everything's arranged properly.\"","then":"He notices the checking, and it undercuts how much he'd taken on himself."},{"text":"\"Really? I wasn't expecting that from you.\"","then":"He shrugs, a little deflated. \"I thought you'd be pleased.\""}],"why":"Welcoming the idea warmly keeps building the bond he started on his own."}],"relearn":"Both partners join each other's families; inviting your spouse into your side is part of the web."},
  {"id":"fm-1316","cat":"web-you-join","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"teach me the dish","hook":"A mother-in-law makes a dish a new way to you, and you want to show curiosity, not judgement.","setup":"She sets the plate down, watching for your reaction.","steps":[{"prompt":"She asks: \"Is this how you expected it to taste?\"","options":[{"text":"\"This is new to me. Would you teach me?\"","then":"She beams and starts explaining the recipe right at the table.","best":true},{"text":"\"We make it the proper way at my house.\"","then":"Her smile fades, hearing it as a comparison instead."},{"text":"\"It's fine, a bit different from what I'm used to.\"","then":"She nods, unsure if that means she should have done it differently."},{"text":"\"Different, but I'll get used to it, I suppose.\"","then":"It comes across as tolerating the dish rather than enjoying it."}],"why":"Naming it as new and asking to learn shows real curiosity, not comparison."},{"prompt":"Clearing the plates, she asks: \"Would you like to help me make it next time?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, just tell me when you're cooking.\"","then":"She lights up. \"Come by Sunday. We'll make it together.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe, if I have time that day.\"","then":"\"I'll call you Sunday, then,\" she says, not sure you'll come."},{"text":"\"I'll try, though I'm not much of a cook.\"","then":"The offer stays half open, uncertain either way."},{"text":"\"Could you just write the recipe down for me?\"","then":"She hesitates. \"I don't cook from a list, but alright.\""}],"why":"Saying yes plainly, and asking to be included, turns curiosity into an actual shared moment."},{"prompt":"A few days later, she calls you into the kitchen, watches you try a step, and says: \"Not quite. Try again.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Show me again. I want to get this right.\"","then":"She laughs and redoes it patiently, glad you're really trying.","best":true},{"text":"\"Sorry, cooking really isn't my strength.\"","then":"She reassures you, though the moment feels more apologetic than joyful."},{"text":"\"It's close enough, I think.\"","then":"The dish comes out a little off, and the lesson feels incomplete."},{"text":"\"Maybe you should just finish this part.\"","then":"She takes over, and you end up watching instead of learning."}],"why":"Asking to be shown again keeps you learning together instead of stepping back."}],"relearn":"Curiosity treats a different custom as new to learn, not as wrong, and it opens the relationship."},
  {"id":"fm-1317","cat":"web-you-join","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"hear his story","hook":"Your father-in-law wants to tell you a long story about the family's past.","setup":"You're both alone on the porch after dinner.","steps":[{"prompt":"He asks: \"Do you have a minute? I was just remembering something.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd love to hear it. Please go on.\"","then":"He settles back, pleased, and begins from the very beginning.","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe some other time. I'm busy.\"","then":"He nods for now, but brings it up again a little later that evening."},{"text":"\"Sure, if it's quick.\"","then":"He shortens the story, sensing you don't have much time."},{"text":"\"Is this a long one?\"","then":"He pauses, a little unsure if you actually want to hear it."}],"why":"Genuinely asking to hear it invites him to open up fully, not just talk at you."},{"prompt":"Partway through, he pauses and asks: \"Am I boring you with all this?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Not at all. What happened next?\"","then":"He laughs, relieved, and picks the story right back up.","best":true},{"text":"\"A little, but go on if you want to.\"","then":"He hurries through the rest, the warmth of it a bit deflated."},{"text":"\"Could we maybe wrap it up a bit sooner?\"","then":"He shortens the story, and you both feel the moment shrink."},{"text":"\"It's fine, whatever you'd like.\"","then":"The flat answer leaves him unsure whether to continue at all."}],"why":"Asking what happens next keeps him talking, and shows the interest is real."},{"prompt":"Later, your partner says: \"Papa told me he really enjoyed talking with you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd love to hear more of his stories.\"","then":"Your partner smiles. \"He doesn't tell those stories to just anyone.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It was nice, though quite long.\"","then":"Your partner isn't sure if you actually enjoyed it or not."},{"text":"\"I mostly just listened politely.\"","then":"Your partner looks a little surprised, having assumed it went deeper."},{"text":"\"He does love to talk.\"","then":"It comes off dismissive of something that clearly mattered to him."}],"why":"Asking to hear more builds the tie, whatever that first chat was like."}],"relearn":"Listening to an elder's stories is a simple, genuine way to begin a real tie."},
  {"id":"fm-1318","cat":"web-you-join","type":"role-play","persona":"Pooja","source":"who's who help","hook":"At a gathering you feel shy and out of place.","setup":"Your partner leans in, noticing.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"You okay? You've gone quiet.\"","options":[{"text":"\"A bit lost. Could you stay close and help me out?\"","then":"He nods and slips his arm through yours. \"Of course. Stay with me.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm fine. Leave it.\"","then":"He drops it, and you spend the evening feeling more alone."},{"text":"\"I just need a minute alone.\"","then":"You get a moment to breathe, but the room stays unfamiliar."},{"text":"\"Everyone here already knows I'm new. It's fine.\"","then":"He isn't quite sure what you need from him right now."}],"why":"Naming exactly what you need lets your partner actually help you through it."},{"prompt":"A little later, he points to a woman nearby and asks: \"That's my cousin Nisha. Want to meet her?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes please, I'd like that.\"","then":"He walks you over, and the introduction goes warmly.","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe later. I'm not ready yet.\"","then":"The moment passes, and the stranger stays a stranger a while longer."},{"text":"\"You go ahead. I'll just watch for now.\"","then":"You stay on the edges, watching rather than meeting anyone."},{"text":"\"Only if it won't take long.\"","then":"The introduction feels rushed, and little of it sticks with you."}],"why":"Saying yes when he offers is how the introductions actually start happening."},{"prompt":"By the end of the evening, he asks: \"How are you feeling now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Still finding my feet. Stay close next time?\"","then":"He squeezes your hand. \"Always. We're a team at these things.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Still a bit overwhelmed, but okay.\"","then":"He nods, glad you told him, though the evening's already over."},{"text":"\"Fine, I think I managed on my own mostly.\"","then":"He's not sure whether his help actually made a difference."},{"text":"\"Exhausted, honestly. These things are a lot.\"","then":"The evening ends on a tired note rather than a warm one."}],"why":"Asking for his help again, honestly, makes it something you can count on each time."},{"prompt":"At the next big function, your partner gets pulled away, and a relative you now recognise says: \"Come, sit with us a while.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd like that, thank you.\"","then":"You join them, and realise, with some surprise, that you're not lost anymore.","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll just wait here until he's back.\"","then":"You manage, but the evening feels smaller than it could have."},{"text":"\"Let me just go find him first.\"","then":"You end up tagging along after him rather than finding your own footing."},{"text":"\"Maybe just for a minute. I don't know many people.\"","then":"You join hesitantly, though it turns out easier than expected."}],"why":"Saying yes to a friendlier face is what shows how far you've come in this family."}],"relearn":"Asking your partner to help you read the room is how you find your footing, not a weakness."},
  {"id":"fm-1319","cat":"web-you-join","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"open the door","hook":"A reserved sister-in-law has kept her distance, and you want to open the door gently.","setup":"You find yourselves alone in the kitchen for once.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says, a little stiffly: \"I suppose we should talk more, being family now.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Whenever you like, I'd love to know you.\"","then":"She relaxes slightly. \"Okay. Maybe we could start now, actually.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Why are you always so cold to me?\"","then":"She stiffens further, caught off guard by the directness."},{"text":"\"Sure, I guess we're stuck with each other now.\"","then":"The joke lands oddly, and the moment doesn't warm up."},{"text":"\"It's fine if you'd rather not, honestly.\"","then":"She takes the out, and the distance stays exactly as it was."}],"why":"Offering warmth without pressure gives her room to come closer at her own pace."},{"prompt":"A little later, she asks: \"What's it like, moving to a new family?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Still finding my feet, but glad. What about you?\"","then":"She answers, a little surprised anyone asked her something in return.","best":true},{"text":"\"It's fine. Nothing special to say.\"","then":"The conversation runs out of places to go."},{"text":"\"Let me tell you the whole story.\"","then":"She listens politely, though she barely gets a word in."},{"text":"\"Why do you ask?\"","then":"She seems unsure if you actually wanted to share anything."}],"why":"Answering and asking back turns one careful question into an actual conversation."},{"prompt":"At the next gathering, she waves you over and says: \"I saved you a seat.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thank you. I'm really glad you did.\"","then":"She stays the whole evening, talking more than she has in months.","best":true},{"text":"\"Oh, this is new for you, isn't it?\"","then":"The comment makes her self-conscious, and she pulls back a little."},{"text":"\"Thanks, but I said I'd sit with my cousins.\"","then":"\"Oh. Of course,\" she says, and turns back to her plate."},{"text":"\"What made you want to sit here?\"","then":"The question puts her on the spot, and she goes quiet again."}],"why":"Meeting her warmly, without making it a big moment, is what lets the closeness actually hold."}],"relearn":"A patient, no-pressure invitation lets a guarded relative come closer in their own time."},
  {"id":"fm-1350","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Razia","source":"dowry demand is not negotiation","hook":"\"If in-laws ask for a fridge or a scooter, you just negotiate it down politely.\"","myth":{"un":"If in-laws ask for a fridge or scooter, you just negotiate it down politely.","re":"A demand for goods as a marriage condition is a dowry demand, which is illegal under the Dowry Prohibition Act 1961, not a price to haggle.","why":"You shut a demand down; you don't bargain over it."},"relearn":"A demand for goods tied to the marriage is an illegal dowry demand to refuse, not negotiate.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1351","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"control is not love","hook":"\"He checks my phone and decides who I meet because he loves me.\"","myth":{"un":"He checks my phone and decides who I meet because he loves me.","re":"Reading your phone and controlling who you see is coercive control, not love; love doesn't need to police you.","why":"Care respects your freedom; control removes it."},"relearn":"Monitoring your phone and choosing your friends is control, not a sign of love.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1352","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"economic control is abuse","hook":"\"Taking my whole salary and giving me an 'allowance' is just how our home runs.\"","myth":{"un":"Taking my whole salary and giving me an allowance is just how our home runs.","re":"Seizing your earnings and rationing money to you is economic abuse, not normal housekeeping.","why":"Sharing money is mutual; controlling all of it is not."},"relearn":"Taking your earnings and rationing an allowance is economic abuse, not housekeeping.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1353","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"a daughter-in-law's silence","hook":"\"A good bahu just bears the taunts about her dowry quietly.\"","myth":{"un":"A good bahu just bears the taunts about her dowry quietly.","re":"Repeated taunts and harassment over dowry are cruelty, not something to bear; you can name it and seek help.","why":"Enduring harm in silence protects the harm, not you."},"relearn":"Dowry taunts are harassment to name and get help for, not a test of being a good bahu.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1354","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"isolation is a red flag","hook":"\"They stopped me visiting my parents to help me 'settle into' the new family.\"","myth":{"un":"They stopped me visiting my parents to help me settle into the new family.","re":"Cutting you off from your own family and friends is isolation, an early sign of coercive control, not settling in.","why":"Joining a family adds people to your life; it doesn't subtract them."},"relearn":"Being cut off from your parents and friends is isolation and control, not 'settling in'.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1355","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Razia","source":"the home is not above the law","hook":"\"What a husband does inside his own house is nobody else's business.\"","myth":{"un":"What a husband does inside his own house is nobody else's business.","re":"Cruelty by a husband or his relatives is a crime; a closed door doesn't put harm beyond the law.","why":"Privacy protects a family's life, not its violence."},"relearn":"Cruelty at home is a crime; four walls don't place it above the law.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1356","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"threats are not discipline","hook":"\"He only threatens to throw me out when I 'push him too far'.\"","myth":{"un":"He only threatens to throw me out when I push him too far.","re":"Threats to abandon or harm you are abuse and never your fault, whatever was said before; they aren't discipline you earned.","why":"Nothing you do makes a threat your responsibility."},"relearn":"Threats to throw you out are abuse and never your fault, not something you provoked.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1357","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Razia","source":"the fix isn't more adjusting","hook":"\"If the abuse continues, she just needs to adjust a little harder.\"","myth":{"un":"If the abuse continues, she just needs to adjust a little harder.","re":"Abuse doesn't ease with more adjusting; the right response is help and safety, not a better boundary.","why":"You can't compromise your way out of someone else's control."},"relearn":"Abuse calls for help and safety, not more adjusting; that's not a flaw in your effort.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1358","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"asking for help is courage","hook":"\"Calling a helpline about your own family means you've failed at marriage.\"","myth":{"un":"Calling a helpline about your own family means you've failed at marriage.","re":"Reaching out about harm is courage, not failure; a marriage isn't a thing you alone have to hold up.","why":"Naming harm is strength, and you don't carry it alone."},"relearn":"Reaching a helpline about harm at home is courage, not a failed marriage.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1359","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"gifts with strings are a demand","hook":"\"They only 'suggest' what gifts our side should bring, so it isn't dowry.\"","myth":{"un":"They only suggest what gifts our side should bring, so it isn't dowry.","re":"A 'suggestion' that you owe goods as a condition of the match is a dowry demand, illegal under the Dowry Prohibition Act 1961, however softly it's put.","why":"Polite wording doesn't change what's being demanded."},"relearn":"A 'suggested' list of gifts owed for the match is still an illegal dowry demand.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1360","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"men face control too","hook":"\"A son-in-law can't really be controlled or harassed by his wife's family.\"","myth":{"un":"A son-in-law can't really be controlled or harassed by his wife's family.","re":"Coercion, threats and financial control can target anyone; harm isn't decided by which side you married into.","why":"Abuse is about the pattern of control, not the gender of who faces it."},"relearn":"Control and threats can target a son-in-law too; abuse isn't only one side's experience.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1361","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Razia","source":"laws give real protection","hook":"\"Dowry and cruelty laws only ruin families, so no one should use them.\"","myth":{"un":"Dowry and cruelty laws only ruin families, so no one should use them.","re":"These laws give real protection to people in genuine danger; that some cases are debated doesn't make the safety they offer false.","why":"A protection being imperfect isn't a reason to leave someone unprotected."},"relearn":"Dowry and cruelty laws offer genuine protection from serious harm, even where misuse is debated.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1362","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"one bad incident counts","hook":"\"It's only happened a couple of times, so it doesn't count as abuse yet.\"","myth":{"un":"It's only happened a couple of times, so it doesn't count as abuse yet.","re":"Harm doesn't need a long history to count; you can seek help and clarity after the very first time.","why":"You don't have to wait for it to get worse to deserve support."},"relearn":"You don't have to wait for a pattern; you can seek help after the first incident of harm.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1363","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Razia","source":"deny dowry, not your bride","hook":"\"Refusing to give dowry would shame our daughter on her wedding day.\"","myth":{"un":"Refusing to give dowry would shame our daughter on her wedding day.","re":"Declining a dowry demand protects your daughter; giving it is illegal and signals that demands will be met.","why":"Saying no to a demand is on the demander, never on her."},"relearn":"Refusing dowry protects your daughter; the shame belongs to the demand, not to her.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1364","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Reshma","source":"jealousy isn't proof of love","hook":"\"He gets angry if I talk to my brother, which just shows how much he cares.\"","myth":{"un":"He gets angry if I talk to my brother, which just shows how much he cares.","re":"Anger at your ordinary contact with family is controlling jealousy, not proof of love; it shrinks your world.","why":"Love widens the people you're allowed; control narrows them."},"relearn":"Anger over you talking to your own family is controlling jealousy, not a measure of love.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1365","type":"sort","persona":"Reshma","source":"friction or abuse","hook":"Sort: ordinary friction to work through, or harm that needs help?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"In-laws disagree about visit frequency"},{"id":"b","text":"He tracks your phone and where you go"},{"id":"c","text":"Tense words over a festival ritual"},{"id":"d","text":"They demand cash after the wedding"},{"id":"e","text":"You clash about chores"},{"id":"f","text":"Threatened if you don't hand over pay"}],"bins":[{"id":"friction","label":"Friction","valence":"pos"},{"id":"harm","label":"Harm, get help","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"friction","b":"harm","c":"friction","d":"harm","e":"friction","f":"harm"},"relearn":"Visit, ritual and chore clashes are friction; tracking, cash demands and threats are harm needing help.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1366","type":"sort","persona":"Reshma","source":"name the harm","hook":"Sort each behaviour into the kind of harm it is.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Stopping you seeing your parents or friends"},{"id":"b","text":"Taking your salary and rationing you money"},{"id":"c","text":"\"Tell your family to send a gold chain\""},{"id":"d","text":"Reading your phone, tracking your every move"},{"id":"e","text":"\"Where's the scooter your side promised?\""},{"id":"f","text":"Threatening to send you away if you refuse"}],"bins":[{"id":"isolate","label":"Isolation / control","valence":"neg"},{"id":"dowry","label":"Dowry demand","valence":"neg"},{"id":"threat","label":"Threats","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"isolate","b":"isolate","c":"dowry","d":"isolate","e":"dowry","f":"threat"},"relearn":"Cutting you off and controlling money is isolation; demands for gold or a scooter are dowry; 'I'll send you away' is a threat.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1367","type":"sort","persona":"Razia","source":"right help route","hook":"Sort each situation to the help route that fits.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"In-laws harassing you over dowry"},{"id":"b","text":"You're in danger right now"},{"id":"c","text":"You want to name a pattern, plan"},{"id":"d","text":"A girl about to be married off"},{"id":"e","text":"A woman who feels unsafe now"},{"id":"f","text":"You need someone to talk to"}],"bins":[{"id":"women","label":"Women Helpline 181","valence":"tell"},{"id":"emerg","label":"Emergency 112","valence":"tell"},{"id":"distress","label":"In distress 1091","valence":"tell"},{"id":"child","label":"Childline 1098","valence":"tell"},{"id":"counsel","label":"A counsellor","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"women","b":"emerg","c":"counsel","d":"child","e":"distress","f":"counsel"},"relearn":"181 for dowry/in-law abuse, 112 for danger, 1091 in distress, 1098 for a girl married off, a counsellor to plan.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1368","type":"sort","persona":"Reshma","source":"yours to refuse or share","hook":"Sort: a demand to refuse, or an everyday matter to talk through?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Bring 5 lakh or the wedding's off\""},{"id":"b","text":"Whose parents to visit at Diwali"},{"id":"c","text":"\"Hand over your bonus or there's trouble\""},{"id":"d","text":"Agreeing a housewarming budget"},{"id":"e","text":"\"More gold must come from your side\""},{"id":"f","text":"Which relatives to invite over"}],"bins":[{"id":"refuse","label":"Demand: refuse","valence":"neg"},{"id":"talk","label":"Everyday: talk","valence":"pos"}],"key":{"a":"refuse","b":"talk","c":"refuse","d":"talk","e":"refuse","f":"talk"},"relearn":"Cash or gold tied to the marriage is illegal dowry to refuse; visits, budgets and guest lists are everyday talk.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1369","type":"sort","persona":"Reshma","source":"early signs","hook":"Sort each sign: a warning worth noticing, or normal newlywed life?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"He must approve every rupee you spend"},{"id":"b","text":"You're still learning each other's habits"},{"id":"c","text":"He gets cold if you message a friend"},{"id":"d","text":"You sometimes miss your old routine"},{"id":"e","text":"\"No one will believe you anyway\""},{"id":"f","text":"You're nervous meeting his big family"}],"bins":[{"id":"warn","label":"Warning sign","valence":"uhoh"},{"id":"normal","label":"Normal","valence":"pos"}],"key":{"a":"warn","b":"normal","c":"warn","d":"normal","e":"warn","f":"normal"},"relearn":"Controlling every rupee, punishing contact and 'no one will believe you' are warnings; settling in and nerves are normal.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1370","type":"sort","persona":"Reshma","source":"who carries it","hook":"Sort: whose fault is each thing, the person harmed, or the one doing the harm?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"He raised his hand when dinner was late"},{"id":"b","text":"They harassed her until her family paid"},{"id":"c","text":"She finally called a helpline for advice"},{"id":"d","text":"He cut her off from her sister"},{"id":"e","text":"She told a counsellor what was happening"},{"id":"f","text":"They threatened her over a 'small' gift"}],"bins":[{"id":"theirs","label":"On the one harming","valence":"neg"},{"id":"brave","label":"A brave, right step","valence":"pos"}],"key":{"a":"theirs","b":"theirs","c":"brave","d":"theirs","e":"brave","f":"theirs"},"relearn":"Violence, dowry harassment, isolation and threats are on the person harming; reaching for help is a brave, right step.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1371","type":"sort","persona":"Razia","source":"true or myth about the law","hook":"Sort each statement about the law: true, or a myth?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Giving dowry is also an offence"},{"id":"b","text":"A 'small' dowry is fine if both agree"},{"id":"c","text":"In-law cruelty can be a crime"},{"id":"d","text":"Abuse stops once you're married"},{"id":"e","text":"Economic abuse is a recognised harm"},{"id":"f","text":"You need injuries before anyone helps"}],"bins":[{"id":"true","label":"True","valence":"pos"},{"id":"myth","label":"Myth","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"true","b":"myth","c":"true","d":"myth","e":"true","f":"myth"},"relearn":"Giving dowry is an offence, in-law cruelty can be a crime, economic abuse counts; no 'small dowry' or injuries-required.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1372","type":"sort","persona":"Reshma","source":"safe next step","hook":"Sort: a step that builds safety, or a step that just keeps you stuck?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Quietly telling one trusted person"},{"id":"b","text":"Paying the demand to 'keep the peace'"},{"id":"c","text":"Saving a counsellor's number privately"},{"id":"d","text":"Promising to 'try harder' and saying nothing"},{"id":"e","text":"Noting down dates when things happened"},{"id":"f","text":"Waiting alone until it's 'bad enough'"}],"bins":[{"id":"safe","label":"Builds safety","valence":"pos"},{"id":"stuck","label":"Keeps you stuck","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"safe","b":"stuck","c":"safe","d":"stuck","e":"safe","f":"stuck"},"relearn":"Telling someone, saving help routes and noting dates build safety; paying off demands or waiting alone keep you stuck.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1373","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Razia","source":"shut a hint down","hook":"At a family lunch, an aunt hints your parents 'should still send' a gold set.","steps":[{"prompt":"The aunt brings it up again, smiling like it's a small thing. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say plainly that dowry is illegal","then":"The table goes quiet for a second, then moves on to other talk.","best":true},{"text":"Assume it's harmless since everyone else laughs along","then":"The hint blends into the general laughter and goes unanswered."},{"text":"Whisper to your partner to handle it instead","then":"He deflects it vaguely, and the door stays a little open."},{"text":"Tell yourself it's not really your place to say anything","then":"The hint sits there unanswered, and the aunt smiles as if it's settled."}],"why":"Naming it as illegal, plainly, closes the hint instead of leaving it open to try again."},{"prompt":"Your mother asks quietly if she should 'just send something small' to keep the peace. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell her not to send anything, and face it together","then":"She exhales, and you agree to call each other before answering the aunt.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's her decision, whatever she thinks is best","then":"She's left to decide alone, and starts wondering what 'small' would mean."},{"text":"Believe a token gift is just polite, not dowry","then":"The word 'token' makes the demand sound harmless, though it's the same demand."},{"text":"Tell her not to worry about it and drop the topic","then":"She's left carrying the worry alone, without a real answer."}],"why":"Refusing together means neither of you carries the demand alone, and the answer stays no."},{"prompt":"At the next family event, the aunt brings a friend who repeats the same hint. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give a plain, calm answer in front of her friend","then":"The friend looks surprised, and the aunt doesn't try again that day.","best":true},{"text":"Pull the aunt aside privately instead of answering in the room","then":"The private word works for the moment, but the public hint goes unanswered."},{"text":"Get visibly annoyed that it's come up again","then":"The sharpness draws attention, and the actual point gets lost in it."},{"text":"Say it's up to your parents, not you","then":"The friend turns to your mother next, and the pressure shifts onto her."}],"why":"A calm, plain answer in the room is what makes the illegal demand stop, not just a private word."},{"prompt":"Months later, a cousin's wedding planning brings the topic up again in the family group chat. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say plainly it's illegal, and share the 181 number","then":"The group moves on, and a cousin quietly saves the number too.","best":true},{"text":"Assume someone else in the chat will answer it","then":"Nobody does, and a relative who missed the earlier talk raises it again."},{"text":"Reply that a small gift isn't really dowry","then":"Two relatives agree, and the hint starts to sound like a plan."},{"text":"Reply with a joke to keep things light","then":"The joke softens the point until it barely reads as a no anymore."}],"why":"Naming it as illegal, and sharing where to get help, keeps the answer clear for anyone new to it."}],"debrief":"A dowry hint is never your fault; name it as illegal, calmly and plainly, to shut it down.","relearn":"Name a dowry hint as illegal and decline it plainly; laughing it off invites more."},
  {"id":"fm-1374","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Reshma","source":"he controls the money","hook":"Your husband takes your whole salary and gives you a small 'allowance' you must justify.","steps":[{"prompt":"This month, he asks why you needed money for a simple errand. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Name it to yourself as economic control, not normal","then":"Naming it changes how the question lands. It isn't just an errand anymore.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself it's normal to explain every expense","then":"Naming it as normal keeps the questioning feeling routine, not controlling."},{"text":"Remind yourself what happens at home stays private","then":"The questions keep coming, with no one outside the house to talk them over with."},{"text":"Assume you should just need less money","then":"Wanting less becomes the norm, and the control stays out of sight."}],"why":"Naming it as control, plainly to yourself, is the first honest step toward help."},{"prompt":"A friend asks casually if you handle your own money. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell her honestly, and ask if she knows a counsellor","then":"She listens carefully, and shares a number she knows is trustworthy.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's just how your household runs things","then":"The conversation moves on, and the pattern stays exactly as it was."},{"text":"Laugh it off as him being 'careful with money'","then":"The joke makes it sound lighter than it actually feels to you."},{"text":"Change the subject before she asks anything more","then":"The moment to say something honest quietly closes."}],"why":"Telling someone honestly, and asking for help, opens a real door instead of a closed one."},{"prompt":"A week later, you call a counsellor from your phone, in private. What do you say first?","options":[{"text":"Describe the allowance and the questioning, plainly","then":"She confirms gently that this is economic abuse, not budgeting.","best":true},{"text":"Downplay it as just a difference in how you two manage money","then":"The counsellor senses more, and gently asks a few more questions."},{"text":"Ask her to just tell you if it's normal or not","then":"She explains it takes more than a yes or no to understand fully."},{"text":"Apologise for taking up her time over 'just money'","then":"She reassures you it matters, though the apology slows things down."}],"why":"Describing it plainly lets her name the pattern clearly, instead of guessing at it."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, he tightens the allowance further after a disagreement. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go back to the counsellor with what's changed","then":"She helps you see the tightening as part of the same pattern, not a new one.","best":true},{"text":"Blame yourself for the disagreement that set it off","then":"The guilt makes the new limit feel like something you caused."},{"text":"Ask him directly to explain the new tighter rules","then":"He gives reasons that sound fair, though the control stays in place."},{"text":"Tell yourself managing with less is just being sensible","then":"The belief makes the tightening easier to accept, though nothing's actually changed."}],"why":"Going back with what's changed keeps the counsellor's support current with what's really happening."}],"debrief":"Seizing your earnings and rationing money is economic abuse, never your fault; a counsellor or Women Helpline 181 can help.","relearn":"Controlling all your money is economic abuse; name it and reach a counsellor or 181."},
  {"id":"fm-1375","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Reshma","source":"cut off from family","hook":"Your in-laws keep finding reasons you can't visit or call your parents.","steps":[{"prompt":"This week, another reason comes up for why you can't call home. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep your contact and confide in someone you trust","then":"A quiet call to a trusted cousin reminds you that you're not alone with this.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself it's reasonable, since families get busy","then":"The reasoning makes the pattern feel ordinary instead of what it is."},{"text":"Argue loudly about it in the moment","then":"The argument ends the discussion, but the pattern stays exactly the same."},{"text":"Assume you're just being too needy, wanting to call so much","then":"The thought quiets the urge to call, though nothing about the situation has changed."}],"why":"Holding onto your own contact, and confiding in someone, keeps you from being isolated alone."},{"prompt":"A trusted cousin asks how often you actually get to speak to your parents. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell her honestly and ask for her help","then":"She offers to call regularly herself, so you're never fully cut off.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's less than you'd like, but leave it there","then":"She's concerned, but without more detail she isn't sure how to help."},{"text":"Say it's fine, not wanting to worry her","then":"She takes you at your word, and the pattern stays hidden a while longer."},{"text":"Say a new bride is meant to call home less","then":"She wonders if that's true, and lets the subject drop for now."}],"why":"Being honest and asking for a specific kind of help builds a real line back to your family."},{"prompt":"Your mother asks gently why your calls have become so rare. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell her honestly, even if it's hard","then":"She goes quiet, then says, \"I'm glad you told me. We'll find a way.\"","best":true},{"text":"Make an excuse so she doesn't worry","then":"She accepts it, though the real reason stays hidden from her too."},{"text":"Say you've been neglecting her, and apologise","then":"She forgives you at once, and never hears the real reason."},{"text":"Change the subject to avoid the question","then":"The moment to be honest with her passes by unspoken."}],"why":"Telling your mother honestly brings another person in who can help you stay connected."}],"debrief":"Being steadily cut off from your family is isolation, not keeping the peace, and it is never your fault.","relearn":"Keep your link to family; being cut off is isolation, not peace-keeping."},
  {"id":"fm-1376","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Razia","source":"a sister asks for help","hook":"Your sister says her husband threatens her and in-laws demand 'gifts'.","steps":[{"prompt":"She tells you this quietly, watching your face for a reaction. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Believe her, say it's not her fault, share 181","then":"She exhales, relieved you didn't question a single word of it.","best":true},{"text":"Ask her what she might have done to provoke it","then":"She flinches, and starts second-guessing what she just told you."},{"text":"Say every marriage has its difficult moments","then":"She hears it as a reason to stay quiet about the rest."},{"text":"Tell her to leave immediately, right now","then":"She freezes, not ready to make a decision that large today."}],"why":"Believing her without question, and naming it as not her fault, is what real support sounds like."},{"prompt":"She asks if she's overreacting about the 'gift' demands. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say these 'gifts' are dowry, and dowry is illegal","then":"She's surprised to hear it named so clearly, and a little steadier for it.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's probably just how his family talks","then":"She lets the demand slide for now, though the unease doesn't leave her."},{"text":"Say you're not sure. It's hard to say from the outside","then":"She's left with the same uncertainty she came to you with."},{"text":"Tell her to just give them what they're asking for","then":"The demand is met, and a new one follows not long after."}],"why":"Naming the 'gifts' as dowry, which is illegal, helps her trust what she already sensed was wrong."},{"prompt":"A few days later, she says she's been thinking about Women Helpline 181, but she's scared to call alone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Offer to sit with her for the call, when she's ready","then":"She nods slowly. \"Maybe this weekend. With you there, I could.\"","best":true},{"text":"Offer to make the call for her instead","then":"She feels a little pushed aside from her own decision."},{"text":"Tell her she has no choice but to call right now","then":"The pressure makes her retreat rather than move forward."},{"text":"Suggest she ask his parents to calm him down first","then":"She goes pale. His parents are the ones asking for 'gifts'."}],"why":"Offering to sit with her, at her pace, supports her choice without taking it over."},{"prompt":"A little while later, her husband's family senses something has changed and turns colder toward her. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay close, remind her it's not her fault and 181 is there","then":"She holds onto that, and keeps 181 in mind, even as the coldness around her continues.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her she should have expected this reaction","then":"It lands as blame, even though that isn't what you meant."},{"text":"Suggest she try harder to smooth things over with them","then":"She goes back to managing everyone else's feelings but her own."},{"text":"Step back a little, unsure how involved to stay","then":"She feels more alone right when she needed steady support."}],"why":"Staying close, repeating it's not her fault and keeping 181 in reach keeps her from carrying this alone."}],"debrief":"Threats and dowry demands are abuse; believe her, say it's not her fault, share 181.","relearn":"Believe someone facing threats and dowry demands and share 181; don't tell them to 'adjust'."},
  {"id":"fm-1377","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Reshma","source":"unsure it counts","hook":"Nothing's 'bad enough' to name, but you feel watched, rationed and small at home.","steps":[{"prompt":"You catch yourself minimising it again in your own head. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Reach a counsellor or 181 anyway to make sense of it","then":"The voice on the line doesn't ask you to prove anything first.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself it's probably just how marriage feels","then":"The feeling doesn't fade, and a week later you call 181 after all."},{"text":"Wait for something bigger to happen before reaching out","then":"Nothing bigger comes, only more of the same, and one evening you call 181."},{"text":"Keep it entirely to yourself for now","then":"Carrying it alone gets heavier, until one quiet afternoon you call 181."}],"why":"Reaching out doesn't require certainty first; help can meet you where you are."},{"prompt":"The counsellor asks what a typical day looks like for you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Describe an ordinary day honestly, small details included","then":"She notices the pattern in the small details before you fully see it yourself.","best":true},{"text":"Describe only the calmest days you can remember","then":"The picture she gets doesn't quite match how things really feel."},{"text":"Say it's probably nothing, really","then":"She gently says it sounds like more than nothing, and asks a few easy questions."},{"text":"Ask her to just tell you if it sounds bad","then":"She explains she needs to hear more before she can say."}],"why":"An honest, ordinary day tells her more than the one big story you were waiting for."},{"prompt":"After a few more gentle questions, she calls what you've shared a pattern of control. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Let the words land, even if they're hard to hear","then":"It's a relief and a shock at once, to finally have a name for it.","best":true},{"text":"Argue that it can't really be that serious","then":"She listens patiently, giving you time to sit with what she said."},{"text":"Wonder if it's your fault for being too sensitive","then":"She tells you gently that none of this is your fault."},{"text":"Ask her to soften how she said it","then":"She's honest but gentler, though the pattern is still the same."}],"why":"Letting the naming land, even when it's hard, is what lets real help actually begin."}],"debrief":"You don't have to be sure it 'counts', and it's never your fault; a counsellor or 181 can help you name a pattern early.","relearn":"You can reach help while unsure; you needn't wait for it to get undeniable."},
  {"id":"fm-1378","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"his family pressures her side","hook":"Your parents expect a car from your wife's family, and you didn't know.","steps":[{"prompt":"You walk in as your parents raise the car with your wife's family. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Step in and tell your parents plainly: no dowry","then":"The room goes still, and your wife's family looks at you, waiting.","best":true},{"text":"Wait until everyone's left to bring it up privately","then":"Your wife's family leaves believing the demand still stands."},{"text":"Stay quiet, assuming your parents didn't mean it seriously","then":"Your wife's mother later asks your wife if it's really expected."},{"text":"Laugh it off in the moment to defuse the tension","then":"The laugh softens it, but the demand is never actually withdrawn."}],"why":"Stepping in immediately stops your own family's demand from standing unchallenged."},{"prompt":"Later that week, your mother raises the car again when it's just the two of you. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say plainly that dowry is illegal, whoever asks for it","then":"She's quiet for a moment, then says she didn't think of it that way.","best":true},{"text":"Say sorry for the awkwardness, and leave the car unmentioned","then":"She hears only the apology, and the car stays on her mind."},{"text":"Say you'll explain it to her more gently another time","then":"The 'another time' keeps getting pushed further away."},{"text":"Avoid the conversation with her for a few days","then":"The silence leaves the disagreement unresolved between you."}],"why":"Naming the law plainly, without softening it, is what actually changes her mind."},{"prompt":"Your wife asks quietly if you're really on the same page as her about this. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say plainly you're with her, whatever your parents think","then":"She relaxes, hearing you say it without hedging.","best":true},{"text":"Say you mostly want to avoid an awkward scene","then":"She isn't sure if that means you're standing with her."},{"text":"Say your parents will come around eventually","then":"It sounds like a promise for later, not a clear answer now."},{"text":"Say you'd rather not relive the whole conversation","then":"She's left without knowing where you actually stand."}],"why":"Saying plainly that you're with her reassures her you're a team, not just managing the moment."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, your father raises the same expectation quietly, just to you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Refuse plainly, and tell your wife what he said","then":"Your wife appreciates hearing it from you directly, before it reaches her another way.","best":true},{"text":"Handle it quietly yourself without mentioning it to her","then":"She later hears about it from someone else, and wonders why you didn't say."},{"text":"Ask your father to drop it, without saying why","then":"He drops it for now, though the underlying belief stays unchanged."},{"text":"Let it go this time, hoping it won't come up again","then":"The demand resurfaces again a little later, unaddressed."}],"why":"Refusing plainly and telling your wife keeps you a team, with nothing hidden between you."}],"debrief":"A dowry demand from your side is yours to shut down, never her family's fault; stand with your partner, and reach a counsellor if it goes on.","relearn":"If your own family demands dowry, step in; silence makes you part of it."},
  {"id":"fm-1379","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Reshma","source":"threatened over salary","hook":"Your husband says if you don't hand over your bonus, you'll 'regret it'.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says it flatly, watching for how you'll react. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Treat it as a threat, and note what was said","then":"You stay calm outwardly, quietly noting the words to yourself.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself he probably didn't mean it that way","then":"The thought makes the moment easier to get through, though the words stay with you."},{"text":"Argue back just as sharply","then":"The exchange escalates, and the room feels tense for the rest of the evening."},{"text":"Tell yourself a good wife shouldn't keep money apart","then":"The thought makes it feel like duty, though the words keep replaying in your head."}],"why":"Naming a threat as a threat, even quietly to yourself, takes it seriously without escalating it."},{"prompt":"The next day, he acts as if nothing happened. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hold onto what you remember, and mention it to someone you trust","then":"Saying it out loud to someone else makes the threat feel real, not imagined.","best":true},{"text":"Decide it must not have been serious after all","then":"The memory fades, and the pattern has room to repeat."},{"text":"Bring it up with him directly, hoping for an apology","then":"He denies saying it quite that way, and you're left doubting yourself."},{"text":"Ask his mother whether he really meant it","then":"She says all men talk like that under stress, and the words get waved away."}],"why":"Telling someone you trust keeps the threat from being quietly explained away."},{"prompt":"A similar comment happens again a few weeks later. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Name it as a pattern, and reach a counsellor","then":"The counsellor confirms what you already sensed: this is a pattern worth acting on.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself twice isn't really a pattern yet","then":"The wait for a clearer sign keeps stretching a little longer."},{"text":"Wonder if you provoked it by asking about money","then":"The self-blame keeps the focus on you, not on what he said."},{"text":"Confront him alone about it, hoping he'll stop","then":"The confrontation raises the tension without changing what happens next time."}],"why":"Naming a second threat as a pattern, and reaching help, is safer than confronting it alone."},{"prompt":"Days later, you wonder what you'd do if he said it a third time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Make a safety plan with a counsellor or 181","then":"With a plan and a safe person to call, the next moment feels less frightening.","best":true},{"text":"Decide you'll figure it out if it happens","then":"Without a plan, a third time would leave you working it out alone, on the spot."},{"text":"Tell yourself safety plans are only for women who get hit","then":"The worry stays, with nothing ready if he says it again."},{"text":"Ask a neighbour what she'd do in your place","then":"She says to keep the peace and hand the money over, and the fear stays."}],"why":"Planning ahead, with help and a safe person, keeps you safer if it happens again."}],"debrief":"'You'll regret it' over money is a threat, never your fault; take it seriously and reach a counsellor or 181 early.","relearn":"A threat tied to money is abuse to take seriously, never your fault; don't explain it away."},
  {"id":"fm-1380","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Razia","source":"friend wants to call it nothing","hook":"A colleague laughs that her mother-in-law 'only' threw out her things and screamed for hours.","steps":[{"prompt":"She keeps smiling as she describes the screaming. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Gently say that sounds frightening and ask if she's okay","then":"Her smile falters for a second. \"It was, actually. Thank you for asking.\"","best":true},{"text":"Laugh along, since she seems to be laughing too","then":"The laugh keeps the moment light, but the fear underneath stays unnamed."},{"text":"Say her mother-in-law sounds difficult to deal with","then":"The comment stays surface level, missing how scared she actually felt."},{"text":"Change the subject to avoid making her uncomfortable","then":"The talk moves on, but over tea later she brings it up again, not laughing now."}],"why":"Naming it as frightening, gently, lets her feel seen rather than laughed past."},{"prompt":"She admits, more quietly now, that it happens more than she's said. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask her, without judgement, how often it's been happening","then":"She opens up further, relieved you didn't flinch or lecture her.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her she needs to leave that situation now","then":"She pulls back, not ready for advice that large yet."},{"text":"Say her husband must know and should step in","then":"The conversation shifts away from her and onto someone else's role."},{"text":"Say you're not sure what to say to that","then":"She senses your discomfort, and the conversation trails off."}],"why":"A calm, non-judging question keeps her talking instead of shutting the door."},{"prompt":"She asks if you think she's overreacting by even mentioning it. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it's intimidation, not saas-bahu, and never her fault","then":"She sits with that for a moment. \"I've never heard anyone say it that clearly.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say every household has its own version of this","then":"She hears it as permission to keep letting it slide."},{"text":"Say it's hard to judge from the outside, honestly","then":"She's left without the clarity she was hoping to hear from you."},{"text":"Say maybe it's just a clash of two strong personalities","then":"The framing makes something serious sound like an ordinary disagreement."}],"why":"Naming intimidation clearly, and saying it's never her fault, confirms what she already suspected."},{"prompt":"A few days later, she messages that it happened again and she doesn't know what to do. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Share 181, and offer to go with her to a counsellor","then":"She replies: \"Maybe next week. Just knowing you'd come helps.\"","best":true},{"text":"Advise her to keep it quiet so it doesn't get worse","then":"She replies \"Okay\" and stays silent, and the fear stays with her at home."},{"text":"Offer to have it out with her mother-in-law yourself","then":"She panics. \"Please don't. It'll only be worse for me at home.\""},{"text":"Say it may settle once they all get used to each other","then":"She sends a thumbs up, and doesn't mention it again for weeks."}],"why":"Sharing 181 and offering to go with her gives real help at her pace, without taking over."}],"debrief":"Screaming and throwing out someone's things to frighten them is intimidation, never their fault, not the usual saas-bahu tease.","relearn":"Intimidation isn't 'just saas-bahu'; name it gently and check the person is okay."},
  {"id":"fm-1381","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Reshma","source":"keeping a quiet record","hook":"The control at home is worsening and you're not sure what to do first.","steps":[{"prompt":"Tonight, another small rule gets added to what you're allowed to do. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Quietly save a helpline number on your phone","then":"You save it under a plain name, glad to have it if you need it.","best":true},{"text":"Confront him loudly about the new rule right away","then":"The confrontation raises the tension without actually loosening the rule."},{"text":"Tell yourself it's normal for rules to keep changing","then":"The belief makes the new rule easier to accept, though the pattern keeps growing."},{"text":"Ask his mother to talk him out of the new rule","then":"She says every marriage has rules like this, and nothing changes."}],"why":"A quietly saved number is a small, safe first step when things are still uncertain."},{"prompt":"A cousin calls to catch up, and it feels like a chance to say something. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell her a little, gently, and see how she responds","then":"She listens carefully, and doesn't push you to say more than you're ready to.","best":true},{"text":"Say everything is fine, like you usually do","then":"The call ends warmly, but the truth stays exactly where it was."},{"text":"Tell her, and ask her to have it out with him","then":"She's ready to call him right away, and you have to talk her out of it."},{"text":"Hint at it vaguely, without really saying anything","then":"She senses something's off, but doesn't know enough to help."}],"why":"Telling one trusted person a little, gently, builds support without overwhelming yourself."},{"prompt":"Something happens that upsets you, and you're not sure if it's worth remembering. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Note it briefly somewhere only you can find","then":"The short note takes a minute, and it's there if you ever need it.","best":true},{"text":"Decide it's too small to bother writing down","then":"The small moment fades, and the pattern is harder to see as a whole."},{"text":"Tell yourself you'll remember it without writing it","then":"Details blur over time, and the memory grows less clear."},{"text":"Write down everything in exhausting detail, every single day","then":"The effort becomes its own burden, hard to keep up with."}],"why":"A brief, private note is enough to build a record without it becoming its own weight."},{"prompt":"Your cousin asks, weeks later, how things are going at home. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell her honestly how things have really been","then":"She listens, then gently asks if you've thought about reaching out for support.","best":true},{"text":"Say things have actually gotten better lately","then":"It isn't quite true, and the conversation moves on without the real update."},{"text":"Say it's probably your fault for upsetting him","then":"She's quiet, then says gently that it doesn't sound like your fault."},{"text":"Ask her to stop checking in so often","then":"She steps back, and one source of support quietly falls away."}],"why":"Telling her honestly lets your support grow, at a pace that feels safe to you."},{"prompt":"One quiet afternoon, alone at home, you think about calling a helpline. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call quietly, ready to say what's been happening","then":"The counsellor listens, and helps you think through your next safe step.","best":true},{"text":"Wait for one more sign before finally calling","then":"The waiting continues, and the call keeps getting pushed further out."},{"text":"Tell yourself others have it much worse than you","then":"The comparison makes your worry feel smaller, and the call waits."},{"text":"Tell yourself calling would be betraying the family","then":"The guilt holds the call back, and the control carries on."}],"why":"A quiet call, at a moment that feels safe, turns the small steps into real support."}],"debrief":"Small quiet steps, a saved number, a trusted person, a few notes: these build safety better than one blow-up, and none of this is your fault.","relearn":"Build safety quietly: a saved helpline, a trusted person, a record, not a blow-up."},
  {"id":"fm-1382","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Reshma","source":"the demand returns","hook":"You refused a dowry demand once, but the in-laws raise it again, more insistently.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law says your parents 'can surely manage something' this time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Repeat the same plain 'no' you gave before","then":"She pauses, surprised the answer hasn't shifted at all.","best":true},{"text":"Believe giving a little will finally satisfy them","then":"Paying 'just this once' starts to look like a way out, though it would only reset the ask."},{"text":"Ask your partner to handle this round instead","then":"He tries, but the demand stays exactly as insistent as before."},{"text":"Wonder if your first 'no' was too harsh","then":"The doubt makes the demand feel like something you caused."}],"why":"Repeating the same refusal shows the demand didn't work the first time or this time."},{"prompt":"She says it's expected since 'everyone else in the family did it'. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say plainly that it's illegal regardless of what others did","then":"She has no easy answer to that, and the room goes quiet.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll think about what other families have done","then":"The comment leaves the door open a little wider than it should be."},{"text":"Point out that other families have their own problems","then":"The comment shifts the argument sideways instead of settling it."},{"text":"Believe it can't be illegal if every family does it","then":"The belief makes the demand sound almost reasonable."}],"why":"Naming it as illegal, regardless of family precedent, closes the comparison as an excuse."},{"prompt":"Your partner suggests reaching out to a counsellor about the repeated pressure. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Agree, and call a counsellor together that same week","then":"Making the call together turns the plan into something real, not just talk.","best":true},{"text":"Say you'd rather handle it without outside help","then":"The pressure keeps returning, and you keep facing it alone each time."},{"text":"Say it's not serious enough yet for a counsellor","then":"The repeated demand is treated as smaller than it actually is."},{"text":"Agree, but put off actually making the call","then":"The plan sits unused, and the next demand catches you unprepared again."}],"why":"Adding real support behind your refusal makes it easier to hold the line each time."},{"prompt":"A month later, the demand comes up once more at a family gathering. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hold the same refusal, calmly, with your partner beside you","then":"The demand isn't repeated again after that gathering.","best":true},{"text":"Believe you're the one being unreasonable by still refusing","then":"The self-doubt nearly convinces you to give in this time."},{"text":"Believe the law can't reach a family matter like this","then":"The belief makes the demand feel like something no one can stop."},{"text":"Keep it from your parents so they aren't shamed","then":"Your parents never hear of it, and you face the next ask without them."}],"why":"Holding the same line, together, is what finally makes a repeated demand stop."}],"debrief":"A repeated dowry demand is harassment, never your fault; hold the same refusal and add help. Paying just resets it.","relearn":"Repeat your 'no' to a renewed dowry demand and add 181 or a counsellor. Paying just resets it, and it's never your fault."},
  {"id":"fm-1383","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"branch","persona":"Karthik","source":"asked to choose a side","hook":"Privately, your mother tells you to make your wife 'fall in line' or there'll be trouble.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says it quietly, expecting you to agree with her. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decline to police her; you'll talk to each other as a couple","then":"Your mother looks caught off guard, not expecting you to refuse.","best":true},{"text":"Agree to have a word with your wife about it","then":"You carry the pressure home, unsure how to even raise it."},{"text":"Say you'll think about what she's asking","then":"She takes the maybe as a yes, and expects results soon."},{"text":"Change the subject to avoid answering directly","then":"She reads the dodge as agreement, and drops it for now."}],"why":"Declining to police your wife, plainly, refuses to make you a tool of control. It's never her fault."},{"prompt":"Your wife later asks if your mother said anything to you recently. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell her honestly what your mother asked","then":"She's relieved, and a little surprised, to hear it from you first.","best":true},{"text":"Say it was nothing important","then":"She senses something's being kept from her, even if she can't name it."},{"text":"Say your mother's just worried, without more detail","then":"The vague answer leaves her guessing at what was actually said."},{"text":"Say you'd rather not bring your mother into things","then":"The topic gets avoided, but the pressure hasn't actually gone anywhere."}],"why":"Telling her honestly what was asked of you keeps nothing hidden between you as a team."},{"prompt":"Your mother raises it again weeks later, this time in front of your wife. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say calmly that the two of you decide things together","then":"Your wife relaxes beside you, hearing you say it in front of your mother.","best":true},{"text":"Stay quiet, hoping your wife will handle it herself","then":"Your wife is left to answer alone, without you backing her up."},{"text":"Smooth it over by agreeing with a bit of what she says","then":"The partial agreement leaves your wife unsure where you stand."},{"text":"Pull your mother aside later instead of answering now","then":"The private word helps a little, but your wife didn't get to hear it."}],"why":"Saying it calmly, in front of both of them, makes the team answer hold in public too."}],"debrief":"Being asked to 'make her fall in line' recruits you into control; decline, stand as a couple, and reach a counsellor if threats follow.","relearn":"Don't relay pressure to 'make her comply'; refusing keeps you a team."},
  {"id":"fm-1384","type":"spot","persona":"Reshma","source":"spot the control","hook":"Spot the two lines that are control, not ordinary married life.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Let's plan our weekend together\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You can't keep any money of your own\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I'd like to meet your friends sometime\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Delete his number or face consequences\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Can we share the chores this week?\"","trick":false}],"why":"Banning you from any money and ordering you to cut a contact under threat are control; the rest is normal.","relearn":"Controlling your money and forcing you to cut people off is coercive control.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1385","type":"spot","persona":"Razia","source":"spot the dowry demand","hook":"Spot the two lines that are illegal dowry demands.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"We'd love to host both families for dinner\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Your side must arrange a car before the wedding\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Bring whatever sweets you like\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"No gold set, no marriage\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Let's split the function costs fairly\"","trick":false}],"why":"Demanding a car or gold as a condition of the marriage is an illegal dowry demand; dinners, sweets and fairly shared costs are not.","relearn":"Goods demanded as a condition of the marriage are illegal dowry; shared, freely given hospitality is not.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1386","type":"spot","persona":"Reshma","source":"spot the threat","hook":"Spot the two lines that are threats, not heated words.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm upset and need a minute to cool down\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Say that again and I'll send you back to your parents\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Can we talk about this tomorrow?\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Cross me and you'll regret it\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I disagree, but let's hear each other out\"","trick":false}],"why":"'I'll send you back' and 'you'll regret it' are threats meant to frighten and control; cooling off, pausing and disagreeing calmly are not.","relearn":"Words meant to frighten you into obeying are threats, not just a heated argument.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1387","type":"spot","persona":"Reshma","source":"spot the isolation","hook":"Spot the two moves that are isolation, not just a busy joint household.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"The family eats dinner together most nights","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"You're stopped from calling your parents","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Weekends are full of relatives visiting","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Your friends are forbidden, one by one","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Everyone pitches in for the festival","trick":false}],"why":"Being blocked from your parents and forbidden your friends cuts you off, that's isolation; shared meals and festivals are normal.","relearn":"Being blocked from your own family and friends is isolation; a busy household is not.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1388","type":"spot","persona":"Reshma","source":"spot crossing the line","hook":"Spot the two signs that friction has crossed into abuse.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"You argue sometimes about visiting schedules","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"You're punished with silence and locked rooms","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"You don't always like the same festivals","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"You're afraid to speak in your own home","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"You're still learning each other's habits","trick":false}],"why":"Punishment and living in fear are abuse; disagreeing about visits or festivals and still adjusting are ordinary friction.","relearn":"Living in fear and being punished are abuse; disagreement and adjusting are ordinary friction.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1389","type":"spot","persona":"Reshma","source":"spot the manipulation","hook":"Spot the two lines designed to keep you silent, not to make peace.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Let's both apologise and move on\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"No one will ever believe you\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I was wrong earlier, I'm sorry\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Tell anyone and you'll lose everything\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Can we start fresh tomorrow?\"","trick":false}],"why":"'No one will believe you' and 'you'll lose everything' are made to keep you silent and trapped; the apologies are not.","relearn":"Lines that make you feel unbelievable or that you'll lose everything are made to silence you.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1390","type":"spot","persona":"Reshma","source":"spot economic abuse","hook":"Spot the two money moves that are economic abuse, not shared budgeting.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"You both agree a monthly budget","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"He takes your pay, you beg for bus fare","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"You split bills in a way that suits you both","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"You're banned from any money of your own","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"You each save a little for your own goals","trick":false}],"why":"Seizing your pay and banning you from any money are economic abuse; agreeing budgets, splitting and saving are healthy.","relearn":"Seizing your earnings and banning you from any money of your own is economic abuse.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1391","type":"match","persona":"Razia","source":"route to the right help","hook":"Match each situation to the help route that fits it.","pairs":[{"left":"In-law or dowry harassment","right":"Women Helpline 181"},{"left":"Immediate danger right now","right":"Emergency / police 112"},{"left":"A woman in distress, unsafe","right":"Women in distress 1091"},{"left":"A girl about to be married off","right":"Childline 1098"},{"left":"Naming a pattern and planning","right":"A counsellor"}],"relearn":"181 for dowry/in-law abuse, 112 for danger, 1091 in distress, 1098 for a girl married off, a counsellor to plan.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1392","type":"match","persona":"Reshma","source":"name the behaviour","hook":"Match each behaviour to what it really is.","pairs":[{"left":"Banning you from your parents","right":"Isolation"},{"left":"Taking your salary, rationing you","right":"Economic abuse"},{"left":"\"Send a gold set or else\"","right":"Dowry demand"},{"left":"\"You'll regret crossing me\"","right":"A threat"},{"left":"Disagreeing over visit dates","right":"Ordinary friction"}],"relearn":"Blocking your family is isolation, controlling money is economic abuse, demanding gold is dowry, threats are threats, visit clashes are just friction.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1393","type":"match","persona":"Reshma","source":"friction vs abuse","hook":"Match each line to whether it's friction or abuse.","pairs":[{"left":"Clashing festival customs","right":"Friction to work through"},{"left":"Threats if you don't obey","right":"Abuse, get help"},{"left":"Different ideas on visits","right":"Friction to talk over"},{"left":"Money controlled by force","right":"Abuse, reach 181"},{"left":"Learning each other's habits","right":"Normal adjusting"}],"relearn":"Custom clashes, visit differences and adjusting are friction; threats and money controlled by force are abuse needing help.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1394","type":"match","persona":"Razia","source":"myth to truth","hook":"Match each myth to the truth that replaces it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"A little dowry is tradition\"","right":"Dowry is illegal, even a little"},{"left":"\"Home matters stay private\"","right":"Privacy never covers abuse"},{"left":"\"She should just adjust more\"","right":"Abuse needs help, not adjusting"},{"left":"\"It must be her fault\"","right":"Abuse is never the victim's fault"},{"left":"\"Asking for help is shameful\"","right":"Seeking help is courage"}],"relearn":"Dowry is illegal, privacy doesn't cover abuse, the fix is help not adjusting, it's never your fault, and asking for help is courage.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1395","type":"match","persona":"Reshma","source":"first safe steps","hook":"Match each safe first step to what it gives you.","pairs":[{"left":"Saving a helpline privately","right":"A route ready when you need it"},{"left":"Telling one trusted person","right":"You're no longer alone with it"},{"left":"Noting dates of incidents","right":"A quiet record you can rely on"},{"left":"Calling a counsellor","right":"Help making sense of the pattern"},{"left":"Keeping your own contacts","right":"A link to people outside the home"}],"relearn":"A saved number, a trusted person, a record, a counsellor and your own contacts are quiet first steps that build safety.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1396","type":"match","persona":"Razia","source":"the law in plain words","hook":"Match each plain fact to what the law says.","pairs":[{"left":"Giving or taking dowry","right":"An offence in India"},{"left":"Demanding dowry","right":"Also illegal"},{"left":"Cruelty by husband or in-laws","right":"Can be a crime"},{"left":"Economic, emotional abuse","right":"Recognised harms"},{"left":"A closed front door","right":"Doesn't beat the law"}],"relearn":"Giving, taking and demanding dowry are illegal, in-law cruelty can be a crime, and economic abuse counts.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1397","type":"match","persona":"Reshma","source":"who it's never about","hook":"Match each worry to the reassurance that meets it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Maybe I provoked it\"","right":"Abuse is never your fault"},{"left":"\"It's my private shame\"","right":"There's no shame in being harmed"},{"left":"\"I should manage alone\"","right":"You don't have to carry it alone"},{"left":"\"It isn't bad enough yet\"","right":"You can seek help any time"},{"left":"\"No one will help me\"","right":"181, 112 and counsellors are real"}],"relearn":"It's never your fault, never your shame, never yours to carry alone; you can seek help any time, and real routes exist.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1398","type":"reflect","persona":"Reshma","source":"the clear line","hook":"Lensy: friction you work through kindly; control, threats or a dowry demand are abuse, not boundary problems. Sit with that?","prompt":"What lands for you?","options":["The line is clearer now","That's a real difference","Good to know"],"affirm":"Boundaries handle everyday friction; control, threats and dowry demands are abuse that needs help, not negotiation.","relearn":"Control, threats and dowry demands aren't boundary problems; they're abuse that needs help.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1399","type":"reflect","persona":"Reshma","source":"never your fault","hook":"Lensy: if someone controls, threatens or harms you, that is theirs to own, never something you caused. Hear it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I hear it","Not on me","Thank you"],"affirm":"Control, threats and harm are the choice of the person doing them; nothing you did makes that your fault.","relearn":"Abuse is on the person doing it; nothing you did makes their harm your fault.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1400","type":"reflect","persona":"Reshma","source":"help is courage","hook":"Lensy: reaching for help with a harmful home isn't failing at marriage, it's a brave, ordinary thing to do. Take that in?","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["It helps to hear","It's brave, not failing","I'll remember it"],"affirm":"Asking for help with harm at home is courage, not failure, and not something you have to hold up alone.","relearn":"Seeking help with a harmful home is courage, not a failed marriage.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1401","type":"reflect","persona":"Reshma","source":"you can be unsure","hook":"Lensy: you don't have to be certain it 'counts' before you reach out, uncertainty is reason enough. Okay?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["That's a relief","I don't need proof","Makes sense"],"affirm":"You can seek help while still unsure; a counsellor or 181 can help you make sense of what's happening.","relearn":"You can reach out while unsure; you don't need proof it's 'serious enough' first.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1402","type":"reflect","persona":"Reshma","source":"your money, your link","hook":"Lensy: having your own money and your own people isn't selfish, it's part of a healthy life. Feel that?","prompt":"What comes up?","options":["I feel that","It's not selfish","Good reminder"],"affirm":"Holding some money of your own and keeping your own people are healthy and yours; losing them by force is a warning sign.","relearn":"Your own money and your own people are healthy to keep; losing them by force is a warning sign.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1403","type":"reflect","persona":"Razia","source":"dowry is never yours to owe","hook":"Lensy: no family owes another goods or cash for a marriage, a demand is on the one demanding, not on you. Clear?","prompt":"What's your read?","options":["Clear","It's their demand, not my debt","Noted"],"affirm":"You owe no dowry; a demand is illegal and rests with whoever makes it, never with the family it's aimed at.","relearn":"No family owes dowry; a demand is illegal and belongs to the one making it, not to you.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1404","type":"reflect","persona":"Reshma","source":"believe yourself","hook":"Lensy: if home leaves you small, watched and afraid, that feeling is worth trusting, not arguing down. Sit with it?","prompt":"What stands out?","options":["I can trust it","Worth listening to","Thanks for that"],"affirm":"Feeling watched, controlled and afraid at home is real information; you're allowed to trust it and seek support.","relearn":"Feeling watched, small and afraid at home is worth trusting, and worth seeking support for.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1405","type":"reflect","persona":"Reshma","source":"safety over keeping peace","hook":"Lensy: 'keeping the peace' should never cost your safety, your safety comes first. Take that with you?","prompt":"What lands?","options":["I'll take it","Safety first","That matters"],"affirm":"Keeping the peace is good for friction, but never at the price of your safety; when there's harm, safety and help come first.","relearn":"Keep the peace for friction, but never at the cost of safety; with harm, safety and help come first.","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful"},
  {"id":"fm-1406","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"role-play","persona":"Razia","source":"refuse a dowry list","hook":"An uncle slides you a 'list' of what your family should bring.","setup":"He waits for you to open it, expecting a nod.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"Just have a look. Nothing's fixed yet.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Dowry is illegal, so there's no list.\"","then":"He slides the paper back into his pocket, a little flustered.","best":true},{"text":"\"I suppose it doesn't hurt to just look it over.\"","then":"He watches you scan it, taking the look as real interest."},{"text":"\"My mother always says not to make a fuss.\"","then":"He nods, pleased the advice happens to suit him."},{"text":"\"That's really something for the elders to sort out.\"","then":"He says he might, but keeps the list right where it is."}],"why":"Naming dowry as illegal and declining the list plainly shuts the demand down without leaving a door open."},{"prompt":"He says: \"Every family does a little of this, you know.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's still illegal. We won't take part.\"","then":"He has no real answer, and lets the subject drop for now.","best":true},{"text":"\"I suppose a little wouldn't hurt.\"","then":"He takes it as a small win and keeps the list out on the table."},{"text":"\"I'll need to check with my parents first.\"","then":"He nods. \"Do. I'll wait to hear from them.\""},{"text":"\"I'm sure it'll sort itself out eventually.\"","then":"He assumes the topic will come back around later."}],"why":"Holding to the law, unmoved by 'everyone does it', keeps the illegal demand from taking hold."},{"prompt":"That night, your mother calls and asks: \"Did that uncle say anything about what we should bring?\"","options":[{"text":"\"He showed me a dowry list. I need you with me.\"","then":"She's quiet, then says: \"Then we refuse it together. I'm glad you told me.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing much. Please don't worry about it.\"","then":"\"If you say so,\" she says, and you carry it on your own."},{"text":"\"He says every family does it. Is that true, Ma?\"","then":"She hesitates. \"Some do. Maybe a few things wouldn't hurt.\""},{"text":"\"Leave it to me. I'll sort the uncle out.\"","then":"She sighs. \"Alright. But I don't like you facing him alone.\""}],"why":"Telling your mother means you don't carry it alone, and a dowry demand is never your fault."}],"relearn":"Naming dowry as illegal shuts it down; it's never your fault, and a counsellor or 181 can help if needed."},
  {"id":"fm-1407","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"role-play","persona":"Reshma","source":"reclaim your money","hook":"Your husband controls every rupee you earn.","setup":"On payday, your friend Kavita finds you sitting alone, quiet.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kavita asks: \"You look worried. Is it money at home?\"","options":[{"text":"\"He takes my whole salary. I don't think that's okay.\"","then":"Kavita sits down beside you. \"It isn't. I'm glad you told me.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's nothing. Every husband handles the money.\"","then":"Kavita frowns. \"All of it? That doesn't sound like nothing.\""},{"text":"\"Please don't mention it to anyone. It's shameful.\"","then":"Kavita nods slowly. \"I won't. But the shame isn't yours.\""},{"text":"\"Could you ask his mother to talk to him?\"","then":"Kavita hesitates. \"That might come straight back on you.\""}],"why":"Telling one person you trust takes it out of the house. None of this is your fault."},{"prompt":"Kavita asks: \"What would feel fair to you, with your own salary?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Deciding money together, not handing it all over.\"","then":"Kavita nods. \"Exactly. Sharing isn't the same as surrendering.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe I am careless with money, like he says.\"","then":"Kavita shakes her head. \"Worried isn't careless. It's your money.\""},{"text":"\"His sister says every wife hands it over.\"","then":"Kavita raises an eyebrow. \"Plenty don't. Sharing is different.\""},{"text":"\"I'll confront him tonight and make him stop.\"","then":"Kavita looks worried. \"Alone? Let's get advice before you do that.\""}],"why":"Sharing money is healthy. Being made to hand it all over is control, and it's okay to name it."},{"prompt":"As she leaves, Kavita asks: \"Would it help to talk it through with a counsellor?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes. Will you sit with me while I call 181?\"","then":"Kavita says: \"Of course. Tomorrow afternoon, when you're alone.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"No. Money is between a husband and wife.\"","then":"Kavita says gently: \"Even so, you can ask what's fair.\""},{"text":"\"Counsellors are for serious cases, not money.\"","then":"Kavita says: \"If it's worrying you this much, it's serious.\""},{"text":"\"My mother says I should just adjust for now.\"","then":"Kavita says softly: \"Adjusting won't make it fair. Talking might.\""}],"why":"A counsellor on 181, with a friend beside you, gives you support of your own."}],"relearn":"Sharing money together is healthy; being made to hand over all your earnings is control, and help is there."},
  {"id":"fm-1408","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"role-play","persona":"Reshma","source":"hold your link to family","hook":"Your husband has been steadily limiting your visits to your parents.","setup":"He's refused this weekend too, then gone out, and now your mother is calling.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother asks: \"We haven't seen you in weeks. Is everything alright?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Not really, Ma. He keeps stopping my visits.\"","then":"Your mother goes quiet, then says: \"Thank you for telling me. We're here.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Everything's fine. I've just been busy.\"","then":"She sounds unsure. \"Busy for weeks, beta? You'd tell me if it was more?\""},{"text":"\"It's my fault. I should be managing my home better.\"","then":"She says: \"Running a home doesn't mean giving up your parents.\""},{"text":"\"Married daughters shouldn't visit so often, na?\"","then":"She pauses. \"Who told you that? We just miss you.\""}],"why":"Telling your mother keeps your link to her alive, and being cut off is never your fault."},{"prompt":"Your mother says: \"We don't want to cause you trouble there. Should we call less?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, Ma. I'll always stay in touch. That isn't up for debate.\"","then":"She lets out a breath. \"Good. We're not letting go either.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe a good wife shouldn't need her parents so much.\"","then":"She says quietly: \"A good wife still keeps her mother, beta.\""},{"text":"\"My aunt says I should just adjust for a while.\"","then":"She says: \"Adjusting shouldn't mean losing your own family.\""},{"text":"\"No. I'll have it out with him tonight, once and for all.\"","then":"She sounds worried. \"Please don't face him alone like that.\""}],"why":"Keeping contact isn't negotiable. Cutting you off from family is how control grows."},{"prompt":"Before hanging up, your mother asks: \"Whatever is going on, how can we help?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Help me find a counsellor I can talk to quietly.\"","then":"She says: \"I'll ask around today and message you when it's safe.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing helps. This is just what marriage is like.\"","then":"She says softly: \"It doesn't have to be. Let's find out.\""},{"text":"\"Could you ask his parents to talk sense into him?\"","then":"She hesitates. \"Would that make things harder for you there?\""},{"text":"\"Please don't tell anyone. People will talk.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"I won't. But you shouldn't carry this alone.\""}],"why":"A counsellor gives you support of your own, so keeping your family close isn't a fight you face alone."}],"relearn":"Keeping contact with your parents isn't negotiable; being cut off is control, never your fault, and a counsellor can help."},
  {"id":"fm-1409","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"role-play","persona":"Reshma","source":"name a threat","hook":"Your husband has started using threats when you disagree with him.","setup":"The morning after he said you'd 'regret it', he has left for work and your friend Asha calls.","steps":[{"prompt":"Asha asks: \"You sound shaken. Is everything okay?\"","options":[{"text":"\"He threatened me last night, and I'm scared.\"","then":"Asha's voice softens. \"I'm so glad you told me. You're not alone.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing much. I shouldn't have said no to him.\"","then":"Asha pauses. \"You're allowed to say no. What did he say?\""},{"text":"\"It's fine. Husbands say things when they're angry.\"","then":"Asha hesitates. \"What did he say? You sound frightened.\""},{"text":"\"Please don't ask. What he says at home stays at home.\"","then":"Asha pauses. \"Alright. But you sound scared, and I'm here whenever you want to talk.\""}],"why":"Telling someone you trust means you don't carry a threat alone. It is never your fault."},{"prompt":"Asha rings again that evening while he's out and asks gently: \"Do you think how he acts is just anger, or something more?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's more. It was a threat, not just a bad mood.\"","then":"Asha says: \"Then we take it seriously. Thank you for naming it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"He was tired. He didn't really mean it.\"","then":"Asha says: \"Tired or not, it has scared you.\""},{"text":"\"His mother says all couples fight like this.\"","then":"Asha says: \"Fighting is one thing. Being frightened is another.\""},{"text":"\"Does it matter? It'll blow over by tonight.\"","then":"Asha says: \"It matters. Blowing over isn't the same as okay.\""}],"why":"Naming a threat as a threat, not a mood, refuses to treat it as normal."},{"prompt":"Later in the call, Asha asks: \"If anything like this happens again, can we plan for it now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes. Let's call 181 together and make one.\"","then":"Asha says: \"Yes. Tomorrow afternoon, while he's at work.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Plans are for serious cases. This isn't one.\"","then":"Asha says gently: \"It sounds serious enough to me.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe his father could warn him to stop.\"","then":"Asha frowns. \"He might take that out on you later.\""},{"text":"\"I'll handle it myself if he does it again.\"","then":"Asha says: \"You don't have to face that on your own.\""}],"why":"A plan made with a friend and a helpline keeps you safer than facing a threat alone."}],"relearn":"A threat is abuse, not a bad mood; name it, and reach someone you trust or 181. It's never your fault."},
  {"id":"fm-1410","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"role-play","persona":"Karthik","source":"back your partner","hook":"At dinner, relatives push your wife to accept a 'gift' demand.","setup":"An uncle turns the question toward you both, at the table.","steps":[{"prompt":"The uncle says: \"Surely you two can convince her family to send something nice?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We've decided together: no dowry, and that's final.\"","then":"The table goes quiet. The uncle mutters: \"It's only tradition, beta.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not get into it now. We'll see later.\"","then":"The uncle frowns. \"Later, then. But I'd like to hear from your wife too.\""},{"text":"\"That's really between her family and mine.\"","then":"The uncle says: \"Then I'll speak to her father myself.\""},{"text":"\"I'll have a word with her about it separately.\"","then":"The uncle says: \"Good. Make her see sense.\""}],"why":"A united 'no' shuts down a dowry demand, which is illegal, and backs her as a team, not against family."},{"prompt":"The uncle turns to your wife: \"And what do you say, beta?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Answer if you want to. Either way, I'm with you.\"","then":"Your wife relaxes, then says calmly: \"No. We've decided.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Go on. You tell him. I've already said my piece.\"","then":"Your wife hesitates, left to face the whole table on her own."},{"text":"\"Let me answer for both of us on this one.\"","then":"Your wife stiffens. The uncle nods at you, not at her."},{"text":"\"Can we just move on from this topic now?\"","then":"The uncle shrugs, and the question hangs over the rest of dinner."}],"why":"Backing her while leaving her voice free is a team move, without speaking over her or leaving her exposed."},{"prompt":"Later that night, your wife says: \"That dinner was hard. I hated how they pushed.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It was. Let's agree one answer, and see a counsellor if it goes on.\"","then":"She nods. \"Yes. One answer from both of us, and help if it keeps up.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"He only meant well, you know.\"","then":"She says: \"Meaning well doesn't make it right. It was aimed at me.\""},{"text":"\"It wasn't a big deal, really.\"","then":"She looks away. \"It felt like one to me.\""},{"text":"\"I just didn't want a scene at dinner.\"","then":"She sighs. \"The scene wasn't what worried me.\""}],"why":"The pressure was never her fault. Agreeing one answer ahead means she never faces it alone."}],"relearn":"Back your partner with a united 'no'. That is a team move, not choosing sides. If the pressure goes on, reach a counsellor."},
  {"id":"fm-1411","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"role-play","persona":"Reshma","source":"ask for help out loud","hook":"You finally call a counsellor and your voice shakes.","setup":"She answers warmly on the second ring.","steps":[{"prompt":"She says: \"Hello, you've reached the helpline. How can I help?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm not sure it counts, but I don't feel safe.\"","then":"Gently, she says: \"That's exactly the kind of thing to call about.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Actually, never mind. It's probably nothing. Sorry.\"","then":"She stays on the line. \"Take your time. I'm not going anywhere.\""},{"text":"\"I just have a quick question, nothing serious.\"","then":"She listens patiently, sensing there's more beneath the quick question."},{"text":"\"Someone told me I should call, so, here I am.\"","then":"She gently asks what brought you to actually make the call."}],"why":"Naming the fear plainly, even unsure, is a complete start. None of this is your fault."},{"prompt":"She asks: \"Can you tell me a bit about what's been happening?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Small things, but they've been adding up.\"","then":"She says the small things matter, and asks you to walk through a few.","best":true},{"text":"\"Other people have it worse than I do.\"","then":"She gently says it isn't a competition, and asks about your own days."},{"text":"\"It's probably not as bad as it sounds.\"","then":"She notices the downplaying, and gently asks you to say more anyway."},{"text":"\"I feel silly even explaining it.\"","then":"She reassures you there's nothing silly about calling, and waits."}],"why":"Naming that small things have been adding up opens the door to the real pattern."},{"prompt":"She says: \"It sounds like this has been on your mind for a while.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It has. I've been telling myself it's normal.\"","then":"She says gently, \"A lot of people do, until someone helps them see it clearly.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Not really. Everyone feels like this at home sometimes.\"","then":"She gently says feeling this way is worth talking about, however common it is."},{"text":"\"I don't want to make it sound worse than it is.\"","then":"She gently says there's no need to soften it for her sake."},{"text":"\"I guess so, if you say so.\"","then":"She encourages you to trust your own sense of it too, not just hers."}],"why":"Naming that you'd talked yourself into 'normal' is an honest, important thing to say out loud."},{"prompt":"She asks: \"Is there anyone you can talk to safely, besides me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No one. I don't want to shame the family.\"","then":"She says softly: \"There's no shame in this. Let's think about it together.\""},{"text":"\"I could tell my neighbour, but she talks to everyone.\"","then":"She gently asks what worries you about that before you decide."},{"text":"\"I'm not sure who's actually safe to tell.\"","then":"She helps you think through who might be trustworthy, slowly.","best":true},{"text":"\"Everyone at home already sort of knows.\"","then":"She asks a little more about what 'knowing' has actually looked like."}],"why":"Working out together who's safe to tell starts building support beyond just this call."},{"prompt":"Near the end, she asks: \"What would you like to happen next?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'd like to talk again sometime.\"","then":"She books a time to call again, and it feels like a real, steady start.","best":true},{"text":"\"Talking won't really change anything at home, will it?\"","then":"She says gently: \"It can. Small steps add up, and I can help plan them.\""},{"text":"\"I shouldn't bother you again. Others need you more.\"","then":"She says gently: \"You're not a bother. This line is for you too.\""},{"text":"\"Whatever you think is best for me.\"","then":"She gently says it has to be your own choice, not hers."}],"why":"Asking to keep talking turns one hard call into the start of ongoing support."}],"relearn":"You can ask for help while unsure, and none of it is your fault; 'I'm not sure it counts, but I need to talk' is a brave, complete start."},
  {"id":"fm-1412","cat":"when-it-turns-harmful","type":"role-play","persona":"Razia","source":"decline to police her","hook":"An older relative thinks your wife has too much say in your home.","setup":"He takes you aside, man to man, expecting agreement.","steps":[{"prompt":"He says: \"You need to control your wife a bit more, you know.\"","options":[{"text":"\"She's my partner, not someone I control.\"","then":"He looks surprised, not expecting that kind of answer.","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine, I'll have a word and make her listen.\"","then":"He nods, satisfied, expecting you to follow through."},{"text":"\"She's not that bad, honestly.\"","then":"It sounds like a mild defence rather than a real refusal."},{"text":"\"I'll think about what you're saying.\"","then":"He takes the thinking as agreement waiting to happen."}],"why":"Naming her as a partner, not someone to control, refuses the role he's offering you."},{"prompt":"He adds: \"In my day, the man led the home. That kept families strong.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We lead it together. That's just how we do things.\"","then":"He shakes his head, though he doesn't push it further.","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe you're right. Things have changed a lot.\"","then":"The agreement gives his point more weight than it deserves."},{"text":"\"Every couple does things differently, I suppose.\"","then":"It stays vague enough that he assumes you might come around."},{"text":"\"Let's not get into old arguments.\"","then":"Avoiding it leaves his comment standing unanswered."}],"why":"Naming it as a shared decision, plainly, stands your ground without a fight."},{"prompt":"Later, your wife asks: \"What was he saying to you earlier?\"","options":[{"text":"\"He wants me to control you. I won't do that.\"","then":"She exhales. \"Thank you for telling me straight.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Just the usual old-fashioned talk, nothing much.\"","then":"She senses there's more to it than you're saying."},{"text":"\"He's just set in his ways. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"The comment brushes past what he actually asked of you."},{"text":"\"You know how he can be.\"","then":"The vague answer leaves her without knowing what was actually said."}],"why":"Telling her plainly what he asked, and where you stand, keeps nothing hidden between you."}],"relearn":"Declining to 'control' your partner keeps you a team and refuses to be a channel for control."},
];

